window.PYQ_PHYSICS = {"version":1,"subject":"Physics","class":12,"chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Electric Charges and Fields","unit":"I","unitName":"Electrostatics","unitMarks":16,"count":57},{"n":2,"t":"Electrostatic Potential and Capacitance","unit":"I","unitName":"Electrostatics","unitMarks":16,"count":39},{"n":3,"t":"Current Electricity","unit":"II","unitName":"Current Electricity","unitMarks":16,"count":72},{"n":4,"t":"Moving Charges and Magnetism","unit":"III","unitName":"Magnetic Effects of Current and Magnetism","unitMarks":17,"count":87},{"n":5,"t":"Magnetism and Matter","unit":"III","unitName":"Magnetic Effects of Current and Magnetism","unitMarks":17,"count":36},{"n":6,"t":"Electromagnetic Induction","unit":"IV","unitName":"Electromagnetic Induction and Alternating Currents","unitMarks":17,"count":63},{"n":7,"t":"Alternating Current","unit":"IV","unitName":"Electromagnetic Induction and Alternating Currents","unitMarks":17,"count":57},{"n":8,"t":"Electromagnetic Waves","unit":"V","unitName":"Electromagnetic Waves","unitMarks":18,"count":61},{"n":9,"t":"Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","unit":"VI","unitName":"Optics","unitMarks":18,"count":53},{"n":10,"t":"Wave Optics","unit":"VI","unitName":"Optics","unitMarks":18,"count":30},{"n":11,"t":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","unit":"VII","unitName":"Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter","unitMarks":12,"count":86},{"n":12,"t":"Atoms","unit":"VIII","unitName":"Atoms and Nuclei","unitMarks":12,"count":59},{"n":13,"t":"Nuclei","unit":"VIII","unitName":"Atoms and Nuclei","unitMarks":12,"count":39},{"n":14,"t":"Semiconductor Electronics","unit":"IX","unitName":"Electronic Devices","unitMarks":7,"count":63}],"questions":[{"id":"p0001","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A small object lies at the bottom of a vessel filled with water (refractive index $\\frac{4}{3}$) up to a height $H$. When viewed from a point above the surface of water, the object appears raised by $n$ percent of $H$. The value of $n$ is :","options":{"A":"15","B":"20","C":"25","D":"33"},"answer":"C","hint":"Apparent depth equals real depth divided by refractive index.","explanation":"Apparent depth $=\\frac{3H}{4}$, so the rise is $\\frac{H}{4}=25\\%$ of $H$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",1]]},{"id":"p0002","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron with velocity $\\vec{v}=(v_x\\hat{i}+v_y\\hat{j})$ moves through a magnetic field $\\vec{B}=(B_x\\hat{i}-B_y\\hat{j})$. The force $\\vec{F}$ on the electron is : ($e$ is the magnitude of its charge)","options":{"A":"$-e\\,(v_xB_y-v_yB_x)\\hat{k}$","B":"$e\\,(v_xB_y-v_yB_x)\\hat{k}$","C":"$-e\\,(v_xB_y+v_yB_x)\\hat{k}$","D":"$e\\,(v_xB_y+v_yB_x)\\hat{k}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=q\\,\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}$ with $q=-e$.","explanation":"$\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}=-(v_xB_y+v_yB_x)\\hat{k}$; multiplying by $-e$ gives $+e(v_xB_y+v_yB_x)\\hat{k}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",2],[2023,"C","55/C/2",2]]},{"id":"p0003","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A bar magnet is cut into two equal halves parallel to its magnetic axis. The physical quantity that remains unchanged is :","options":{"A":"pole strength","B":"magnitude of magnetisation","C":"moment of inertia","D":"magnetic moment"},"answer":"B","hint":"Magnetisation is magnetic moment per unit volume.","explanation":"Cutting along the axis halves both the magnetic moment and the volume, so magnetisation $M/V$ is unchanged.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",3]]},{"id":"p0004","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In a series LC circuit connected to an ac source, with the increase in the frequency of the source, the net reactance :","options":{"A":"increases linearly","B":"decreases linearly","C":"first increases to become maximum and then decreases to zero","D":"first decreases to become zero and then increases"},"answer":"D","hint":"Net reactance is $X_L-X_C=\\omega L-\\frac{1}{\\omega C}$.","explanation":"The magnitude of $\\omega L-\\frac{1}{\\omega C}$ falls to zero at resonance and then rises again.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",4],[2023,"C","55/C/3",4]]},{"id":"p0005","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following physical quantities remain the same for X-ray, red light and radio waves when travelling through a medium ?","options":{"A":"Wavelength","B":"Speed","C":"Frequency","D":"Momentum"},"answer":"B","hint":"Think about what all electromagnetic waves share.","explanation":"All electromagnetic waves travel with the same speed (that of light in vacuum); wavelength, frequency and photon momentum differ.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",5]]},{"id":"p0006","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In Young's double-slit experiment, the intensity on the screen is $I_0$ at a point where path difference is $\\lambda$. The intensity at the point where path difference is $\\frac{\\lambda}{4}$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{I_0}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{I_0}{2}$","C":"$I_0$","D":"zero"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $I=I_{max}\\cos^2(\\phi/2)$ with $\\phi=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}\\Delta x$.","explanation":"Path difference $\\lambda$ gives the maximum $I_0$. At $\\frac{\\lambda}{4}$, $\\phi=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ so $I=I_0\\cos^2\\frac{\\pi}{4}=\\frac{I_0}{2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",6],[2023,"C","55/C/2",6],[2023,"C","55/C/3",6]]},{"id":"p0007","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following figures represents the variation of a particle's momentum with the de Broglie wavelength associated with it ?","options":{"A":"Graph (a): straight line through the origin rising with $\\lambda$","B":"Graph (b): decreasing curve (hyperbola) of $P$ against $\\lambda$","C":"Graph (c): straight line falling with $\\lambda$","D":"Graph (d): curve rising with $\\lambda$"},"answer":"B","hint":"de Broglie relation links $p$ and $\\lambda$ inversely.","explanation":"$p=\\frac{h}{\\lambda}$, so $p$ versus $\\lambda$ is a rectangular hyperbola, graph (b).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0007.png","sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",7],[2023,"C","55/C/2",7],[2023,"C","55/C/3",7]]},{"id":"p0008","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"In the nuclear reaction $^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}+{}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}\\longrightarrow X+{}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}$, $X$ represents :","options":{"A":"$^{16}_{7}\\mathrm{O}$","B":"$^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{N}$","C":"$^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}$","D":"$^{16}_{7}\\mathrm{N}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Conserve mass number and atomic number.","explanation":"Mass number $14+4-1=17$, atomic number $7+2-1=8$, so $X={}^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",8]]},{"id":"p0009","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The given figure shows a capacitor $C$ and a resistor $R$ connected in series to an ac source. $V_1$ and $V_2$ are voltmeters and $A$ is an ammeter.\nWhich of the following statements is correct ?","options":{"A":"Current in the circuit lags in phase with voltage shown in $V_2$.","B":"The voltage shown in $V_1$ is ahead in phase with voltage shown in $V_2$.","C":"The current in the circuit and the voltage shown in $V_1$ are always in phase.","D":"The voltage shown in $V_1$ lags behind in phase with the voltage shown in $V_2$."},"answer":"D","hint":"Voltage across $R$ is in phase with current; across $C$ it lags current by $90^\\circ$.","explanation":"$V_2$ (across $R$) is in phase with current while $V_1$ (across $C$) lags the current by $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $V_1$ lags $V_2$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0009.png","sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",9],[2023,"C","55/C/2",11],[2023,"C","55/C/3",10]]},{"id":"p0010","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Light of frequency $1\\cdot5\\,\\nu_0$ is incident on a photosensitive material of threshold frequency $\\nu_0$. If the frequency of the incident radiation is kept constant and intensity is increased, the photo current will :","options":{"A":"increase","B":"decrease","C":"not change","D":"first decrease and then become zero"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photocurrent is proportional to the number of photons per second at fixed frequency.","explanation":"Frequency exceeds threshold, so emission occurs; more intensity means more photoelectrons per second and a larger photocurrent.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",10],[2023,"C","55/C/2",13],[2023,"C","55/C/3",9]]},{"id":"p0011","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The impact parameter for an alpha particle approaching a target nucleus is maximum when the scattering angle ($\\theta$) is :","options":{"A":"$0^\\circ$","B":"$90^\\circ$","C":"$180^\\circ$","D":"$45^\\circ$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$b\\propto\\cot(\\theta/2)$.","explanation":"$b=\\frac{kZe^2}{K}\\cot\\frac{\\theta}{2}$ is largest as $\\theta\\to0^\\circ$ (distant, undeflected particles).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",11],[2023,"C","55/C/2",9],[2023,"C","55/C/3",12]]},{"id":"p0012","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Two nuclei have their mass numbers in the ratio of $1:27$. What is the ratio of their nuclear densities ?","options":{"A":"$1:27$","B":"$1:1$","C":"$1:9$","D":"$1:3$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Nuclear density is independent of mass number.","explanation":"$R=R_0A^{1/3}$ gives $\\rho=\\frac{3m}{4\\pi R_0^3}$, the same for all nuclei, so the ratio is $1:1$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",12],[2023,"C","55/C/2",10],[2023,"C","55/C/3",11]]},{"id":"p0013","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A plane wave is incident on a concave mirror of radius of curvature $R$. The reflected wave is a spherical wave of radius :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{R}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{R}{2}$","C":"$R$","D":"$2R$"},"answer":"B","hint":"A plane wave converges to the focus after reflection.","explanation":"The reflected wavefront converges to the focus at distance $f=\\frac{R}{2}$, so its radius is $\\frac{R}{2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",13],[2023,"C","55/C/2",12],[2023,"C","55/C/3",15]]},{"id":"p0014","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A planar loop is rotated in a magnetic field about an axis perpendicular to the field. The polarity of induced emf changes once in each :","options":{"A":"1 revolution","B":"$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ revolution","C":"$\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ revolution","D":"$\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)$ revolution"},"answer":"B","hint":"Emf varies as $\\sin\\omega t$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=\\varepsilon_0\\sin\\omega t$ changes sign every half cycle, i.e. every half revolution.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",14],[2023,"C","55/C/2",15],[2023,"C","55/C/3",13]]},{"id":"p0015","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In which of the following diagrams is the capacitor 'C' connected correctly to provide smooth output of a half-wave rectifier ?","options":{"A":"Capacitor in series between the source and the diode","B":"Capacitor in parallel with the source, before the diode","C":"Capacitor in parallel with the load resistor, after the diode","D":"Capacitor in series with the load resistor"},"answer":"C","hint":"The filter capacitor must shunt the load after the rectifying diode.","explanation":"A capacitor connected across the load after the diode charges to the peak and discharges slowly, smoothing the output.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0015.png","sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",15],[2023,"C","55/C/2",14],[2023,"C","55/C/3",14]]},{"id":"p0016","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The temperature coefficient of resistance is positive for metals and negative for semi-conductors.\nReason (R): The charge carriers in metals are negatively charged whereas in semi-conductors they are positively charged.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare the actual reason (carrier density change with temperature) with the stated reason.","explanation":"A is true. R is false: semiconductors have both electrons and holes, and the sign of $\\alpha$ arises from carrier density rising with temperature.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",16],[2023,"C","55/C/2",16],[2023,"C","55/C/3",16],[2024,"R","55/4/1",14],[2024,"R","55/4/2",16],[2024,"R","55/4/3",15]]},{"id":"p0017","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A planar loop of irregular shape carrying current is subjected to a magnetic field acting perpendicular to the plane of the loop. If the wire is flexible, the loop takes a circular shape.\nReason (R): The force acting on each point of a current carrying loop, in a magnetic field perpendicular to its plane, is radially outward.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of the direction of $I\\,d\\vec{l}\\times\\vec{B}$ and the tension it produces.","explanation":"Force on each element is radial (outward for the given sense), which stretches the loop to maximise area, a circle; so R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",17]]},{"id":"p0018","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Silicon is preferred over germanium for making semiconductor devices.\nReason (R): The energy gap for germanium is more than the energy gap for silicon.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare band gaps: Si about 1.1 eV, Ge about 0.7 eV.","explanation":"A is true (Si is more stable at higher temperatures, lower leakage). R is false because Ge has the smaller gap.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/1",18],[2023,"C","55/C/2",18],[2023,"C","55/C/3",18]]},{"id":"p0019","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In a series LCR circuit, the capacitance is changed from $C$ to $\\frac{C}{4}$. For the resonant frequency to remain unchanged, the inductance should be changed from $L$ to $nL$, where $n$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","B":"$2$","C":"$4$","D":"$\\frac{1}{4}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Resonant frequency depends on the product $LC$.","explanation":"$\\omega_0=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}$ is constant if $LC$ is constant, so $nL\\cdot\\frac{C}{4}=LC$ gives $n=4$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/2",1]]},{"id":"p0020","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A small bar, when placed near a magnet is repelled by it. This is because the bar is made of :","options":{"A":"Iron","B":"Copper","C":"Aluminium","D":"Nickel"},"answer":"B","hint":"Repulsion by a magnet is a diamagnetic property.","explanation":"Copper is diamagnetic and is repelled; iron and nickel are ferromagnetic and aluminium is paramagnetic, all attracted.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/2",3]]},{"id":"p0021","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"When a negative charge ($-Q$) is brought near one face of a metal cube, the :","options":{"A":"cube becomes positively charged","B":"cube becomes negatively charged","C":"face near the charge becomes positively charged and the opposite face becomes negatively charged","D":"face near the charge becomes negatively charged and the opposite face becomes positively charged"},"answer":"C","hint":"Think of electrostatic induction in a conductor.","explanation":"Free electrons are repelled to the far face, so the near face becomes positive and the far face negative; the cube stays neutral overall.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/2",4]]},{"id":"p0022","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A metal detector is based on :","options":{"A":"Self-induction","B":"Mutual induction","C":"Electrical resonance","D":"Power transmission"},"answer":"C","hint":"The detector coil circuit is tuned to a particular frequency.","explanation":"A metal detector uses an LC circuit at resonance, whose current changes when a metal object alters the coil's inductance.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/2",5]]},{"id":"p0023","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The binding energy per nucleon of $^{16}_{8}\\mathrm{O}$ is $7\\cdot97$ MeV and that of $^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}$ is $7\\cdot75$ MeV. The energy (in MeV) required to remove a neutron from $^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}$ is :","options":{"A":"$0\\cdot42$ MeV","B":"$7\\cdot86$ MeV","C":"$4\\cdot23$ MeV","D":"$3\\cdot64$ MeV"},"answer":"C","hint":"Neutron separation energy is the difference of total binding energies.","explanation":"$17(7.75)-16(7.97)=131.75-127.52=4.23$ MeV.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/2",8]]},{"id":"p0024","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The magnetic field produced by a current carrying solenoid is independent of its length and cross-sectional area.\nReason (R): The magnetic field inside the solenoid is uniform.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall $B=\\mu_0nI$ and what 'uniform' means.","explanation":"A is true ($B=\\mu_0 nI$ for fixed $n$, $I$). R is true but only says the field is the same at all interior points, not why it is independent of length or area.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/2",17]]},{"id":"p0025","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole with dipole moment $\\vec{P}=P_0\\hat{i}-P_0\\hat{j}$ is placed in an electric field $\\vec{E}=E_1\\hat{i}+E_2\\hat{j}$, where $P_0$, $E_1$ and $E_2$ are constants. The torque $\\vec{\\tau}$ acting on the dipole is :","options":{"A":"$P_0(E_2-E_1)\\hat{k}$","B":"$P_0(E_2+E_1)\\hat{k}$","C":"$-P_0(E_2+E_1)\\hat{k}$","D":"$P_0(E_1-E_2)\\hat{k}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\vec{\\tau}=\\vec{P}\\times\\vec{E}$.","explanation":"$\\vec{P}\\times\\vec{E}=(P_0E_2-(-P_0)E_1)\\hat{k}=P_0(E_1+E_2)\\hat{k}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/3",1]]},{"id":"p0026","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"The current $I$ flowing through a conductor of radius $r$ is uniformly distributed across its cross-section. Which of the following graphs represents the variation of magnitude of magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ with distance $x$ from the axis of the conductor ?","options":{"A":"Graph (a): $B$ rises linearly up to $x=r$ then falls as a decaying curve","B":"Graph (b): $B$ falls to zero at $x=r$ then rises","C":"Graph (c): $B$ falls linearly to zero at $x=r$ then rises linearly","D":"Graph (d): $B$ rises as a curve to a sharp peak at $x=r$ then falls"},"answer":"A","hint":"Apply Ampere's law inside and outside a uniformly carrying conductor.","explanation":"Inside, $B\\propto x$; outside, $B=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{2\\pi x}\\propto\\frac{1}{x}$; maximum at $x=r$, graph (a).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0026.png","sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/3",2]]},{"id":"p0027","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A bar magnet of pole strength ($m$) and magnetic moment ($M$) is cut perpendicular to its axis in two equal halves. The new pole strength ($m'$) and magnetic moment ($M'$) of each part are respectively :","options":{"A":"$m$ and $M$","B":"$m$ and $\\frac{M}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{m}{2}$ and $2M$","D":"$2m$ and $\\frac{M}{2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$M=m\\times$ length; pole strength depends on cross-section.","explanation":"Cross-section is unchanged so pole strength stays $m$, while length halves, giving $M'=\\frac{M}{2}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/3",3]]},{"id":"p0028","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following radiations has the highest frequency ?","options":{"A":"Visible light","B":"Infrared rays","C":"Microwaves","D":"X-rays"},"answer":"D","hint":"Order the electromagnetic spectrum by frequency.","explanation":"Frequency order: microwaves < infrared < visible < X-rays, so X-rays are highest.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/3",5]]},{"id":"p0029","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The binding energy per nucleon is lower :","options":{"A":"for light nuclei only","B":"for heavy nuclei only","C":"for nuclei of middle mass numbers only","D":"both for the light nuclei and the heavy nuclei"},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall the binding energy per nucleon versus mass number curve.","explanation":"The curve peaks near $A\\approx56$ and is lower at both low and high mass numbers.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/3",8]]},{"id":"p0030","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric flux through a Gaussian spherical surface enclosing a point charge $q$ is $\\phi$. If the charge is replaced by an electric dipole, magnitude of its dipole moment being $2qa$, the flux through the surface will be :","options":{"A":"$2\\phi$","B":"$\\phi$","C":"$\\frac{\\phi}{2}$","D":"Zero"},"answer":"D","hint":"Gauss's law depends on net enclosed charge.","explanation":"A dipole has zero net charge, so the net flux is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",1]]},{"id":"p0031","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"$10^9$ electrons are transferred to a pith ball with charge $0\\cdot16\\ \\mathrm{nC}$. Its charge now is :","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$-3\\cdot2\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{C}$","C":"$-1\\cdot6\\times10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{C}$","D":"$3\\cdot2\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{C}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Charge of $n$ electrons is $ne$.","explanation":"$10^9\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}=1.6\\times10^{-10}$ C $=0.16$ nC of negative charge added, cancelling the original $+0.16$ nC; net zero.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",2]]},{"id":"p0032","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The amount of charge flowing across a cross-section of a conductor, connected to a battery, in $4\\cdot0\\ \\mathrm{s}$ is $720\\ \\mathrm{mC}$. The current in the circuit is :","options":{"A":"$0\\cdot18\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$1\\cdot8\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$18\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","D":"$18\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$I=\\frac{Q}{t}$.","explanation":"$I=\\frac{0.72}{4}=0.18$ A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",3]]},{"id":"p0033","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two cells, one of emf $2E$ and internal resistance $r$ and the other of emf $E$ and internal resistance $\\frac{r}{2}$ are connected in parallel, by connecting their positive terminals together and their negative terminals together. The equivalent emf of the combination is :","options":{"A":"$3E$","B":"$\\frac{4E}{3}$","C":"$2E$","D":"$\\frac{3E}{4}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$E_{eq}=\\frac{E_1/r_1+E_2/r_2}{1/r_1+1/r_2}$.","explanation":"$E_{eq}=\\frac{2E/r+2E/r}{1/r+2/r}=\\frac{4E}{3}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",4]]},{"id":"p0034","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two long straight parallel conductors carrying steady currents $I_1$ and $I_2$ in the same direction are separated by a distance $d$. The force exerted by one conductor on unit length of the other is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{4\\pi d}$, attractive","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{4\\pi d}$, repulsive","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}$, attractive","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}$, repulsive"},"answer":"C","hint":"Parallel currents attract; force per length is $\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}$.","explanation":"Same-direction currents attract with force per unit length $\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",5]]},{"id":"p0035","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A long solenoid of length $L$ and number of turns $N$ carrying a current $I$ produces a magnetic field $B$ along its axis. If the current in the solenoid is doubled and its number of turns halved, the magnetic field along its axis will be :","options":{"A":"$2B$","B":"$4B$","C":"$\\frac{B}{2}$","D":"$B$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$B=\\mu_0\\frac{N}{L}I$.","explanation":"$B\\propto NI$; $\\frac{N}{2}\\cdot2I=NI$, so $B$ is unchanged.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",6]]},{"id":"p0036","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The speed of electromagnetic waves in vacuum is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$","B":"$\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}$","C":"$\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0$","D":"$\\frac{1}{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Maxwell's relation for $c$.","explanation":"$c=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",7]]},{"id":"p0037","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A thin concave lens of focal length $2f$ is put in contact with a thin convex lens of focal length '$f$'. The focal length of the combination will be :","options":{"A":"$2f$","B":"$\\frac{f}{2}$","C":"$-f$","D":"$f$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $\\frac{1}{F}=\\frac{1}{f_1}+\\frac{1}{f_2}$ with sign.","explanation":"$\\frac{1}{F}=\\frac{1}{f}-\\frac{1}{2f}=\\frac{1}{2f}$, so $F=2f$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",8]]},{"id":"p0038","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In Young's double-slit experiment, the slit separation is made three times and the distance of the screen from the slits is doubled. The fringe width becomes :","options":{"A":"$6$ times","B":"$\\frac{3}{2}$ times","C":"$\\frac{1}{6}$ times","D":"$\\frac{2}{3}$ times"},"answer":"D","hint":"Fringe width $\\beta=\\frac{\\lambda D}{d}$.","explanation":"$\\beta\\propto\\frac{D}{d}=\\frac{2}{3}$ times.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",9]]},{"id":"p0039","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The work function of potassium is $2\\cdot3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ and threshold wavelength for it is $540\\ \\mathrm{nm}$. The threshold wavelength for a metal whose work function is $4\\cdot6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ will be :","options":{"A":"$135\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$270\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$540\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$1080\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$\\lambda_0=\\frac{hc}{\\phi}$, so $\\lambda_0\\propto\\frac{1}{\\phi}$.","explanation":"Work function doubles, so threshold wavelength halves to $270$ nm.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",10]]},{"id":"p0040","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The minimum energy required to free the electron from the ground state of the hydrogen atom in Bohr model is $13\\cdot6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The energy of the electron in its second orbit will be :","options":{"A":"$-6\\cdot8\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$6\\cdot8\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$-3\\cdot4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$3\\cdot4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E_n=-\\frac{13.6}{n^2}$ eV.","explanation":"For $n=2$, $E=-\\frac{13.6}{4}=-3.4$ eV.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",11]]},{"id":"p0041","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following series in hydrogen spectrum lies in its visible region ?","options":{"A":"Lyman series","B":"Balmer series","C":"Paschen series","D":"Pfund series"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall which series ends on $n=2$.","explanation":"The Balmer series (transitions to $n=2$) lies in the visible region.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",12]]},{"id":"p0042","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following dopants in silicon will make it a p-type semiconductor ?","options":{"A":"As","B":"Al","C":"Sb","D":"P"},"answer":"B","hint":"p-type needs a trivalent dopant.","explanation":"Aluminium is trivalent and creates holes in silicon; As, Sb, P are pentavalent.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",13]]},{"id":"p0043","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following has a negative temperature coefficient of resistivity ?","options":{"A":"Silver","B":"Copper","C":"Nichrome","D":"Germanium"},"answer":"D","hint":"Semiconductors behave oppositely to metals.","explanation":"Germanium, a semiconductor, has resistivity that decreases with temperature.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",14]]},{"id":"p0044","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When a p-n junction diode is forward biased :","options":{"A":"the depletion layer width increases and the barrier height is reduced.","B":"the depletion layer width decreases and the barrier height is reduced.","C":"both the depletion layer width and the barrier height increase.","D":"the depletion layer width decreases and the barrier height increases."},"answer":"B","hint":"Forward bias opposes the built-in potential.","explanation":"Forward bias reduces the barrier height and narrows the depletion layer.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",15]]},{"id":"p0045","chapter":5,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The potential energy of a magnetic dipole in a uniform magnetic field is maximum when its magnetic moment and the magnetic field are antiparallel to each other.\nReason (R): A current loop behaves as a magnetic dipole.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"$U=-\\vec{m}\\cdot\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$U=-mB\\cos\\theta$ is maximum $(+mB)$ at $\\theta=180^\\circ$, so A is true; R is true but unrelated to the reason for A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",16]]},{"id":"p0046","chapter":1,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The electrostatic field $\\vec{E}$ is a conservative field.\nReason (R): Line integral of electric field $\\vec{E}$ around a closed path is non zero.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Conservative means zero closed-loop line integral.","explanation":"A is true; R is false since $\\oint\\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{l}=0$ for electrostatic fields.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",17]]},{"id":"p0047","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The photoelectric current increases with increase in intensity of incident radiation, keeping the frequency of the incident radiation and the accelerating potential fixed.\nReason (R): The number of photoelectrons emitted per second is directly proportional to the intensity of incident radiation.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Photocurrent depends on photoelectrons emitted per second.","explanation":"Both true; more intensity means proportionally more photoelectrons per second, hence larger current, so R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"C","55/C/B",18]]},{"id":"p0048","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A point charge situated at a distance '$r$' from a short electric dipole on its axis, experiences a force $\\vec{F}$. If the distance of the charge is '$2r$', the force on the charge will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\vec{F}}{16}$","B":"$\\frac{\\vec{F}}{8}$","C":"$\\frac{\\vec{F}}{4}$","D":"$\\frac{\\vec{F}}{2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Axial field of a short dipole varies as $\\frac{1}{r^3}$.","explanation":"$E\\propto\\frac{1}{r^3}$, so doubling $r$ reduces the force to $\\frac{F}{8}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",1],[2023,"R","55/1/2",15],[2023,"R","55/1/3",9]]},{"id":"p0049","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"For a metallic conductor, the correct representation of variation of resistance $R$ with temperature $T$ is :","options":{"A":"Graph (a): straight line with positive intercept on the $R$ axis, rising with $T$","B":"Graph (b): straight line starting at the origin, rising with $T$","C":"Graph (c): decreasing curve of $R$ with $T$","D":"Graph (d): curve starting at the origin and rising increasingly steeply with $T$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Resistance of a metal increases with temperature.","explanation":"Official key is (d), the curve of $R$ rising with $T$ (nonlinear dependence).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0049.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",2]]},{"id":"p0050","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The potential difference across a cell in an open circuit is $8\\ \\mathrm{V}$. It falls to $4\\ \\mathrm{V}$ when a current of $4\\ \\mathrm{A}$ is drawn from it. The internal resistance of the cell is :","options":{"A":"$4\\ \\Omega$","B":"$3\\ \\Omega$","C":"$2\\ \\Omega$","D":"$1\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$V=\\varepsilon-Ir$.","explanation":"$r=\\frac{8-4}{4}=1\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",3]]},{"id":"p0051","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A steady current flows through a metallic wire whose area of cross-section ($A$) increases continuously from one end of the wire to the other. The magnitude of drift velocity ($v_d$) of the free electrons as a function of '$A$' can be shown by :","options":{"A":"Graph (a): decreasing hyperbola-like curve","B":"Graph (b): straight line decreasing with $A$","C":"Graph (c): horizontal line (constant)","D":"Graph (d): straight line increasing with $A$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $I=neAv_d$ with constant $I$.","explanation":"$v_d=\\frac{I}{neA}\\propto\\frac{1}{A}$, a hyperbola decreasing with $A$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0051.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",4]]},{"id":"p0052","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A diamagnetic substance is brought near the north or south pole of a bar magnet. It will be :","options":{"A":"repelled by both the poles.","B":"attracted by both the poles.","C":"repelled by the north pole and attracted by the south pole.","D":"attracted by the north pole and repelled by the south pole."},"answer":"A","hint":"Diamagnetic materials are weakly repelled by magnetic fields.","explanation":"A diamagnetic substance gets magnetised opposite to the field and is repelled by either pole.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",5],[2023,"R","55/1/2",8],[2023,"R","55/1/3",12]]},{"id":"p0053","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular coil of radius $8.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ and $40$ turns is rotated about its vertical diameter with an angular speed of $\\frac{25}{\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{rad\\ s^{-1}}$ in a uniform horizontal magnetic field of magnitude $3.0\\times10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{T}$. The maximum emf induced in the coil is :","options":{"A":"$0.12\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$0.15\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$0.19\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$0.22\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Maximum emf of a rotating coil is $NBA\\omega$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon_{max}=NB\\pi r^2\\omega=40\\times0.03\\times\\pi(0.08)^2\\times\\frac{25}{\\pi}\\approx0.19\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",6],[2023,"R","55/1/2",9],[2023,"R","55/1/3",7]]},{"id":"p0054","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Figure shows a rectangular conductor PSRQ in which movable arm PQ has a resistance 'r' and resistance of PSRQ is negligible. The magnitude of emf induced when PQ is moved with a velocity $\\vec{v}$ does not depend on :","options":{"A":"magnetic field ($\\vec{B}$)","B":"velocity ($\\vec{v}$)","C":"resistance ($r$)","D":"length of PQ"},"answer":"C","hint":"Motional emf is $Blv$.","explanation":"Emf $=Blv$ depends on $B$, $v$, $l$ but not on the resistance of the arm.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0054.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",7]]},{"id":"p0055","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In the process of charging of a capacitor, the current produced between the plates of the capacitor is :","options":{"A":"$\\mu_0\\frac{d\\phi_E}{dt}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\frac{d\\phi_E}{dt}$","C":"$\\varepsilon_0\\frac{d\\phi_E}{dt}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{d\\phi_E}{dt}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the definition of displacement current.","explanation":"Displacement current is $I_d=\\varepsilon_0\\frac{d\\phi_E}{dt}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",8],[2023,"R","55/1/2",2],[2023,"R","55/1/3",5]]},{"id":"p0056","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"For a concave mirror of focal length 'f', the minimum distance between the object and its real image is :","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$f$","C":"$2f$","D":"$4f$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of the object placed at the centre of curvature.","explanation":"At $u=-2f$ the real image forms at $2f$ too, so object and image coincide and the distance is zero.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",9]]},{"id":"p0057","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The radius of the $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit in Bohr model of hydrogen atom is proportional to :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{n^2}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{n}$","C":"$n^2$","D":"$n$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Bohr radius formula.","explanation":"$r_n=n^2a_0$, so $r_n\\propto n^2$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",10],[2023,"R","55/5/1",15],[2023,"R","55/5/2",15],[2023,"R","55/5/3",15]]},{"id":"p0058","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Hydrogen atom initially in the ground state, absorbs a photon which excites it to $n=5$ level. The wavelength of the photon is :","options":{"A":"$975\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$740\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$523\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$95\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Energy of photon equals $E_5-E_1$.","explanation":"$E=13.6(1-\\frac{1}{25})=13.06\\ \\mathrm{eV}$, $\\lambda=\\frac{1240}{13.06}\\approx95\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",11],[2023,"R","55/1/2",5],[2023,"R","55/1/3",4]]},{"id":"p0059","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The mass density of a nucleus of mass number A is :","options":{"A":"proportional to $A^{1/3}$","B":"proportional to $A^{2/3}$","C":"proportional to $A^{3}$","D":"independent of A"},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare mass with nuclear volume.","explanation":"Mass $\\propto A$ and volume $\\propto A$ (since $R\\propto A^{1/3}$), so density is constant.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",12]]},{"id":"p0060","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"An ac source of voltage is connected in series with a p-n junction diode and a load resistor. The correct option for output voltage across load resistance will be :","options":{"A":"(graph a)","B":"(graph b)","C":"(graph c)","D":"(graph d)"},"answer":"C","hint":"A single diode conducts in only one half cycle.","explanation":"A diode acts as a half-wave rectifier, so only alternate half cycles appear across the load.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0060.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",13],[2023,"R","55/1/2",7],[2023,"R","55/1/3",3]]},{"id":"p0061","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When an intrinsic semiconductor is doped with a small amount of trivalent impurity, then :","options":{"A":"its resistance increases.","B":"it becomes a p-type semiconductor.","C":"there will be more free electrons than holes in the semiconductor.","D":"dopant atoms become donor atoms."},"answer":"B","hint":"Trivalent dopants create holes.","explanation":"Trivalent (acceptor) impurity gives extra holes, making it p-type.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",14],[2023,"R","55/1/2",10],[2023,"R","55/1/3",11]]},{"id":"p0062","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In the energy-band diagram of n-type Si, the gap between the bottom of the conduction band $E_C$ and the donor energy level $E_D$ is of the order of :","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$0.01\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Donor level lies very close to the conduction band.","explanation":"For n-type Si, $E_C-E_D\\approx0.01\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",15],[2023,"R","55/1/2",11],[2023,"R","55/1/3",1]]},{"id":"p0063","chapter":5,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When a bar of copper is placed in an external magnetic field, the field lines get concentrated inside the bar.\nReason (R): Copper is a paramagnetic substance.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Copper is diamagnetic.","explanation":"Copper is diamagnetic, so field lines are expelled, not concentrated: A false; R false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",16],[2023,"R","55/1/2",18],[2023,"R","55/1/3",17]]},{"id":"p0064","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The phase difference between any two points on a wavefront is zero.\nReason (R): All points on a wavefront are at the same distance from the source and thus oscillate in the same phase.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Definition of a wavefront.","explanation":"Both true; equal distance from source means same phase, which explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",17],[2023,"R","55/1/2",16],[2023,"R","55/1/3",18]]},{"id":"p0065","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Photoelectric effect demonstrates the particle nature of light.\nReason (R): Photoelectric current is proportional to intensity of incident radiation for frequencies more than the threshold frequency.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Both statements are standard photoelectric facts, but are they linked?","explanation":"Both true; but proportionality of current to intensity does not explain the particle nature (that comes from instantaneous emission and threshold frequency).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/1",18],[2023,"R","55/1/2",17],[2023,"R","55/1/3",16]]},{"id":"p0066","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Two identical circular coaxial coils A and B, arranged in vertical planes parallel to each other, carry currents in the same direction. If the distance between the coils is decreased at a constant rate, the current :","options":{"A":"increases in A and decreases in B.","B":"decreases in both A and B.","C":"increases in both A and B.","D":"remains same in both A and B."},"answer":"B","hint":"Apply Lenz's law to the increasing flux linked with each coil.","explanation":"Bringing coils closer increases flux through each from the other; induced emf opposes it, so current decreases in both.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/2",1]]},{"id":"p0067","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The minimum distance between an object and its real image formed by a convex lens of focal length f is :","options":{"A":"$f$","B":"$2f$","C":"$3f$","D":"$4f$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Minimise $u+v$ using the lens formula.","explanation":"Minimum separation occurs at $u=v=2f$, giving $4f$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/2",3]]},{"id":"p0068","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The atomic number of an atom represents :","options":{"A":"number of neutrons in nucleus.","B":"total number of protons and electrons in the atom.","C":"number of protons in nucleus.","D":"total number of protons and neutrons in nucleus."},"answer":"C","hint":"Definition of Z.","explanation":"Atomic number $Z$ is the number of protons in the nucleus.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/2",6]]},{"id":"p0069","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A steady current flows through a metallic wire whose area of cross-section (A) increases continuously from one end of the wire to the other. The magnitude of drift velocity ($v_d$) of the free electrons as a function of 'A' can be shown by :","options":{"A":"(graph a)","B":"(graph b)","C":"(graph c)","D":"(graph d)"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $I=nAev_d$ with constant $I$.","explanation":"$v_d=\\frac{I}{neA}\\propto\\frac{1}{A}$, a hyperbola decreasing with A.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0069.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/2",12],[2023,"R","55/1/3",2]]},{"id":"p0070","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the correct representation of variation of conductivity of a conductor with temperature ?","options":{"A":"(graph a)","B":"(graph b)","C":"(graph c)","D":"(graph d)"},"answer":"C","hint":"Resistivity of a conductor rises with T.","explanation":"Resistivity increases roughly linearly with T, so conductivity falls as a decreasing hyperbola-like curve.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0070.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/2",14]]},{"id":"p0071","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"In an experiment, photons of energy $7.5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ are incident on a metal surface. Electrons emitted from the metal surface are stopped by an electrode at a potential of $-4.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$ w.r.t. the metal. The work function of the metal is :","options":{"A":"$3.0\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$4.5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$7.5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$12.0\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Stopping potential gives maximum kinetic energy.","explanation":"$K_{max}=4.5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$, so $\\phi=7.5-4.5=3.0\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/3",6]]},{"id":"p0072","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Two nuclei have their mass numbers in the ratio of $8:1$. The ratio of their nuclear densities is :","options":{"A":"$8:1$","B":"$4:1$","C":"$2:1$","D":"$1:1$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Nuclear density does not depend on A.","explanation":"Nuclear density is the same for all nuclei, so the ratio is $1:1$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/3",8]]},{"id":"p0073","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The number of electrons flowing through a conductor per second is $3.3\\times10^{19}$. The current flowing through the conductor is :","options":{"A":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$3.4\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$4.8\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$5.3\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$I=ne$ per second.","explanation":"$I=3.3\\times10^{19}\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}\\approx5.3\\ \\mathrm{A}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/3",10]]},{"id":"p0074","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following pairs of media has the least value of critical angle ?","options":{"A":"Glass to air","B":"Glass to water","C":"Diamond to water","D":"Diamond to air"},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\sin C=\\frac{n_2}{n_1}$; make it smallest.","explanation":"The ratio $n_{rarer}/n_{denser}$ is smallest for diamond to air, so its critical angle is least.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/3",13]]},{"id":"p0075","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A bar magnet is dropped in a hollow metallic cylinder along its vertical axis. The acceleration of the falling magnet will be :","options":{"A":"zero","B":"equal to $g$","C":"less than $g$","D":"greater than $g$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Eddy currents oppose the motion (Lenz's law).","explanation":"Induced eddy currents exert an upward retarding force, so $a<g$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/3",14]]},{"id":"p0076","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The correct graph showing the variation of the resistance (R) of a cylindrical metal wire as a function of its radius (r), keeping its length and temperature constant, is :","options":{"A":"(graph a)","B":"(graph b)","C":"(graph c)","D":"(graph d)"},"answer":"C","hint":"Resistance varies inversely with area.","explanation":"$R=\\frac{\\rho l}{\\pi r^2}\\propto\\frac{1}{r^2}$, a decreasing curve.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0076.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/1/3",15]]},{"id":"p0077","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnitude of the electric field due to a point charge object at a distance of $4.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ is $9\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$. From the same charged object the electric field of magnitude, $16\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{C}}$ will be at a distance of","options":{"A":"$1\\ \\mathrm{m}$","B":"$2\\ \\mathrm{m}$","C":"$3\\ \\mathrm{m}$","D":"$6\\ \\mathrm{m}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E\\propto1/r^2$.","explanation":"$\\frac{r_2}{r_1}=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}}$, so $r_2=3\\ \\mathrm{m}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",1]]},{"id":"p0078","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A point P lies at a distance $x$ from the mid point of an electric dipole on its axis. The electric potential at point P is proportional to","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{x^2}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{x^3}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{x^4}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{x^{1/2}}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Axial potential of a short dipole.","explanation":"$V=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{p}{x^2}$, so $V\\propto\\frac{1}{x^2}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",2]]},{"id":"p0079","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A current of $0.8\\ \\mathrm{A}$ flows in a conductor of $40\\ \\Omega$ for 1 minute. The heat produced in the conductor will be","options":{"A":"$1445\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$1536\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$1569\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$1640\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Joule heating $I^2Rt$.","explanation":"$H=0.8^2\\times40\\times60=1536\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",3]]},{"id":"p0080","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A cell of emf E is connected across an external resistance R. When current 'I' is drawn from the cell, the potential difference across the electrodes of the cell drops to V. The internal resistance 'r' of the cell is","options":{"A":"$\\left(\\frac{E-V}{E}\\right)R$","B":"$\\left(\\frac{E-V}{R}\\right)$","C":"$\\frac{(E-V)R}{I}$","D":"$\\left(\\frac{E-V}{V}\\right)R$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Terminal voltage $V=E-Ir$ with $I=V/R$.","explanation":"$r=\\frac{E-V}{I}=\\frac{(E-V)R}{V}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",4],[2023,"R","55/2/2",11],[2023,"R","55/2/3",13]]},{"id":"p0081","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Beams of electrons and protons move parallel to each other in the same direction. They","options":{"A":"attract each other.","B":"repel each other.","C":"neither attract nor repel.","D":"force of attraction or repulsion depends upon speed of beams."},"answer":"B","hint":"Compare the Coulomb and magnetic forces.","explanation":"Opposite charges moving in the same direction give opposite currents, so the magnetic force is repulsive (and the electric force is attractive but speed-dependent); the official answer is repel.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",5],[2023,"R","55/2/2",13],[2023,"R","55/2/3",15]]},{"id":"p0082","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A long straight wire of radius 'a' carries a steady current 'I'. The current is uniformly distributed across its area of cross-section. The ratio of magnitude of magnetic field $\\vec{B}_1$ at $\\frac{a}{2}$ and $\\vec{B}_2$ at distance $2a$ is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","B":"$1$","C":"$2$","D":"$4$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use Ampere's law inside and outside the wire.","explanation":"$B_1=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{4\\pi a}$ and $B_2=\\frac{\\mu_0I}{4\\pi a}$, so ratio is 1.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",6],[2023,"R","55/2/2",15],[2023,"R","55/2/3",1]]},{"id":"p0083","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"$\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{B}$ represent the electric and the magnetic field of an electromagnetic wave respectively. The direction of propagation of the wave is along","options":{"A":"$\\vec{B}$","B":"$\\vec{E}$","C":"$\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$","D":"$\\vec{B}\\times\\vec{E}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"EM waves are transverse.","explanation":"The wave propagates along $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",7]]},{"id":"p0084","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of monochromatic light propagating in air, is incident on the surface of water. Which of the following will be the same for the reflected and refracted rays ?","options":{"A":"Energy carried","B":"Speed","C":"Frequency","D":"Wavelength"},"answer":"C","hint":"What does a change of medium not alter?","explanation":"Frequency is fixed by the source; speed, wavelength and energy split change.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",8],[2023,"R","55/2/2",3],[2023,"R","55/2/3",14]]},{"id":"p0085","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A beam of light travels from air into a medium. Its speed and wavelength in the medium are $1.5\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$ and $230\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ respectively. The wavelength of light in air will be","options":{"A":"$230\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$345\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$460\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$690\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$\\lambda_{air}/\\lambda_{med}=n=c/v$.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{3\\times10^8}{1.5\\times10^8}=2$, so $\\lambda_{air}=2\\times230=460\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",9]]},{"id":"p0086","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following metals does not exhibit emission of electrons from its surface when irradiated by visible light ?","options":{"A":"Rubidium","B":"Sodium","C":"Cadmium","D":"Caesium"},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare work functions with visible photon energies.","explanation":"Cadmium has a work function (about $4.1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$) above visible photon energies, unlike alkali metals.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",10]]},{"id":"p0087","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"A hydrogen atom makes a transition from $n=5$ to $n=1$ orbit. The wavelength of photon emitted is $\\lambda$. The wavelength of photon emitted when it makes a transition from $n=5$ to $n=2$ orbit is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{8}{7}\\lambda$","B":"$\\frac{16}{7}\\lambda$","C":"$\\frac{24}{7}\\lambda$","D":"$\\frac{32}{7}\\lambda$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use Rydberg formula for both transitions.","explanation":"$\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\propto\\frac{24}{25}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\lambda'}\\propto\\frac{21}{100}$, so $\\lambda'=\\frac{32}{7}\\lambda$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",11]]},{"id":"p0088","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The curve of binding energy per nucleon as a function of atomic mass number has a sharp peak for helium nucleus. This implies that helium nucleus is","options":{"A":"radioactive","B":"unstable","C":"easily fissionable","D":"more stable nucleus than its neighbours"},"answer":"D","hint":"High binding energy per nucleon means stability.","explanation":"A peak in binding energy per nucleon means helium is more stable than neighbouring nuclei.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",12],[2023,"R","55/2/2",6],[2023,"R","55/2/3",10]]},{"id":"p0089","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In an extrinsic semiconductor, the number density of holes is $4\\times10^{20}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. If the number density of intrinsic carriers is $1.2\\times10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, the number density of electrons in it is","options":{"A":"$1.8\\times10^{9}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$","B":"$2.4\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$","C":"$3.6\\times10^{9}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$","D":"$3.2\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Mass action law $n_en_h=n_i^2$.","explanation":"$n_e=\\frac{(1.2\\times10^{15})^2}{4\\times10^{20}}=3.6\\times10^{9}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",13],[2023,"R","55/2/2",10],[2023,"R","55/2/3",8]]},{"id":"p0090","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Pieces of copper and of silicon are initially at room temperature. Both are heated to temperature T. The conductivity of","options":{"A":"both increases.","B":"both decreases.","C":"copper increases and silicon decreases.","D":"copper decreases and silicon increases."},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare temperature dependence for a metal and a semiconductor.","explanation":"Metals lose conductivity as T rises (more collisions); semiconductors gain carriers.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",14]]},{"id":"p0091","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The formation of depletion region in a p-n junction diode is due to","options":{"A":"movement of dopant atoms","B":"diffusion of both electrons and holes","C":"drift of electrons only","D":"drift of holes only"},"answer":"B","hint":"Think of the initial carrier movement across the junction.","explanation":"Electrons and holes diffuse across the junction, leaving immobile ions that form the depletion region.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",15],[2023,"R","55/2/2",4],[2023,"R","55/2/3",5]]},{"id":"p0092","chapter":5,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Diamagnetic substances exhibit magnetism.\nReason (R): Diamagnetic materials do not have permanent magnetic dipole moment.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Diamagnetism is an induced effect.","explanation":"Both true; but R does not explain A, since magnetism arises from induced moments opposing the field.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",16],[2023,"R","55/2/2",18],[2023,"R","55/2/3",17]]},{"id":"p0093","chapter":2,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Work done in moving a charge around a closed path, in an electric field is always zero.\nReason (R): Electrostatic force is a conservative force.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Conservative forces do zero work over closed loops.","explanation":"Both true; conservative nature of the electrostatic force explains zero work on a closed path.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",17],[2023,"R","55/2/2",16],[2023,"R","55/2/3",18]]},{"id":"p0094","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In Young's double slit experiment all fringes are of equal width.\nReason (R): The fringe width depends upon wavelength of light ($\\lambda$) used, distance of screen from plane of slits (D) and slits separation (d).","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Fringe width $\\beta=\\lambda D/d$.","explanation":"Both true; $\\beta=\\frac{\\lambda D}{d}$ is independent of fringe order, so R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/1",18],[2023,"R","55/2/2",17],[2023,"R","55/2/3",16]]},{"id":"p0095","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the magnitudes of the electric field and magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave is","options":{"A":"$1$","B":"$\\frac{1}{c}$","C":"$c$","D":"$\\frac{1}{c^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Relation between $E_0$ and $B_0$.","explanation":"$\\frac{E}{B}=c$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",1]]},{"id":"p0096","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Specify the transition of electron in the wavelength of the line in the Bohr model of hydrogen atom which gives rise to the spectral line of highest wavelength.","options":{"A":"$n=3$ to $n=1$","B":"$n=3$ to $n=2$","C":"$n=4$ to $n=1$","D":"$n=4$ to $n=2$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Longest wavelength means smallest energy gap.","explanation":"Smallest energy difference among the options is $3\\to2$, hence the highest wavelength.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",2]]},{"id":"p0097","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An isolated point charge particle produces an electric field $\\vec{E}$ at a point $3\\ \\mathrm{m}$ away from it. The distance of the point at which the field is $\\frac{\\vec{E}}{4}$ will be","options":{"A":"$2\\ \\mathrm{m}$","B":"$3\\ \\mathrm{m}$","C":"$4\\ \\mathrm{m}$","D":"$6\\ \\mathrm{m}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$E\\propto1/r^2$.","explanation":"Field falls to a quarter when distance doubles: $6\\ \\mathrm{m}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",5]]},{"id":"p0098","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A steady current of $8\\ \\mathrm{mA}$ flows through a wire. The number of electrons passing through a cross-section of the wire in $10\\ \\mathrm{s}$ is","options":{"A":"$4.0\\times10^{16}$","B":"$5.0\\times10^{17}$","C":"$1.6\\times10^{16}$","D":"$1.0\\times10^{17}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$n=It/e$.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{8\\times10^{-3}\\times10}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}=5.0\\times10^{17}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",7]]},{"id":"p0099","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following elements will require the highest energy to take out an electron from them ? Pb, Ge, C and Si","options":{"A":"Ge","B":"C","C":"Si","D":"Pb"},"answer":"B","hint":"Smaller atoms hold electrons more tightly.","explanation":"Carbon is the smallest of the group, so it has the highest ionisation energy.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",8]]},{"id":"p0100","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A conductor of $10\\ \\Omega$ is connected across a $6\\ \\mathrm{V}$ ideal source. The power supplied by the source to the conductor is","options":{"A":"$1.8\\ \\mathrm{W}$","B":"$2.4\\ \\mathrm{W}$","C":"$3.6\\ \\mathrm{W}$","D":"$7.2\\ \\mathrm{W}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$P=V^2/R$.","explanation":"$P=\\frac{36}{10}=3.6\\ \\mathrm{W}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",9]]},{"id":"p0101","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A photon of wavelength $663\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ is incident on a metal surface. The work function of the metal is $1.50\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photo electrons is","options":{"A":"$3.0\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$6.0\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$4.5\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$9.0\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$K_{max}=hc/\\lambda-\\phi$.","explanation":"Photon energy $=\\frac{1240}{663}=1.87\\ \\mathrm{eV}$; $K_{max}\\approx0.37\\ \\mathrm{eV}\\approx6.0\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",12]]},{"id":"p0102","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of light of wavelength $600\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ propagates from air into a medium. If its wavelength in the medium becomes $400\\ \\mathrm{nm}$, the refractive index of the medium is","options":{"A":"$1.4$","B":"$1.5$","C":"$1.6$","D":"$1.8$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$n=\\lambda_{air}/\\lambda_{med}$.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{600}{400}=1.5$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/2",14]]},{"id":"p0103","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of a photon of wavelength $663\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ is","options":{"A":"$6.64\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$5.18\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$3.0\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$2.0\\times10^{-20}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E=hc/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times3\\times10^{8}}{663\\times10^{-9}}=3.0\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",2]]},{"id":"p0104","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"An electromagnetic wave is produced by a charge","options":{"A":"moving with a constant velocity","B":"moving with a constant speed parallel to a magnetic field","C":"moving with an acceleration","D":"at rest"},"answer":"C","hint":"Radiation requires changing fields.","explanation":"Only accelerated charges radiate electromagnetic waves.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",3]]},{"id":"p0105","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"A semiconductor device is connected in series with a battery, an ammeter and a resisitor. A current flows in the circuit. If the polarity of the battery is reversed, the current in the circuit almost becomes zero. The device is a/an","options":{"A":"intrinsic semiconductor","B":"p-type semiconductor","C":"n-type semiconductor","D":"p-n junction diode"},"answer":"D","hint":"Which device conducts in one bias direction only?","explanation":"Only a p-n junction diode conducts in forward bias and almost not in reverse bias.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",4]]},{"id":"p0106","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The radius of $^{27}_{13}\\mathrm{X}$ nucleus is R. The radius of $^{125}_{53}\\mathrm{Y}$ nucleus will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{5}{3}R$","B":"$\\left(\\frac{13}{53}\\right)^{1/3}R$","C":"$\\left(\\frac{5}{3}R\\right)^{1/3}$","D":"$\\left(\\frac{13}{53}R\\right)^{1/3}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$R\\propto A^{1/3}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{R_Y}{R_X}=\\left(\\frac{125}{27}\\right)^{1/3}=\\frac{5}{3}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",6]]},{"id":"p0107","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole of dipole moment $2\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{C\\text{-}m}$ in a uniform electric field experiences a maximum torque of $6\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{N\\text{-}m}$. The magnitude of electric field is","options":{"A":"$2.2\\times10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$","B":"$1.2\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$","C":"$3.0\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$","D":"$4.2\\times10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$\\tau_{max}=pE$.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{6\\times10^{-4}}{2\\times10^{-8}}=3\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{V/m}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",7]]},{"id":"p0108","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A point charge $q_0$ is moving along a circular path of radius $a$, with a point charge $-Q$ at the centre of the circle. The kinetic energy of $q_0$ is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{q_0Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$","B":"$\\frac{q_0Q}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$","C":"$\\frac{q_0Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a^2}$","D":"$\\frac{q_0Q}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a^2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Equate the Coulomb force to the centripetal force.","explanation":"$\\frac{mv^2}{a}=\\frac{q_0Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0a^2}$, so $\\frac12mv^2=\\frac{q_0Q}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0a}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",9]]},{"id":"p0109","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The current in a device varies with time $t$ as $I = 6t$, where $I$ is in mA and $t$ is in s. The amount of charge that passes through the device during $t = 0\\ \\mathrm{s}$ to $t = 3\\ \\mathrm{s}$ is","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","B":"$18\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","C":"$27\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","D":"$54\\ \\mathrm{mC}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Charge is the time integral of current.","explanation":"$q=\\int_0^3 6t\\,dt=3t^2|_0^3=27$ mC.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",11]]},{"id":"p0110","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of light travels a distance of $12.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ in a transparent sheet in $60\\ \\mathrm{ns}$. The refractive index of the sheet is","options":{"A":"$1.33$","B":"$1.50$","C":"$1.65$","D":"$1.75$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Find speed in the sheet, then use $n=c/v$.","explanation":"$v=12/(60\\times10^{-9})=2\\times10^8$ m/s, so $n=3\\times10^8/2\\times10^8=1.5$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/2/3",12]]},{"id":"p0111","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron experiences a force $(1.6\\times10^{-16}\\ \\mathrm{N})\\hat{i}$ in an electric field $\\vec{E}$. The electric field $\\vec{E}$ is :","options":{"A":"$(1.0\\times10^{3}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{C}})\\hat{i}$","B":"$-(1.0\\times10^{3}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{C}})\\hat{i}$","C":"$(1.0\\times10^{-3}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{C}})\\hat{i}$","D":"$-(1.0\\times10^{-3}\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{C}})\\hat{i}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=q\\vec{E}$ with $q=-e$.","explanation":"$\\vec E=\\vec F/(-e)=-\\frac{1.6\\times10^{-16}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}\\hat i=-10^3\\hat i$ N/C.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",1]]},{"id":"p0112","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is not a scalar quantity ?","options":{"A":"Electric field","B":"Voltage","C":"Resistivity","D":"Power"},"answer":"A","hint":"Identify the quantity having direction.","explanation":"Electric field is a vector; the others are scalars.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",2]]},{"id":"p0113","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The current density due to drift of electrons in a conductor is given by : (symbols have their usual meanings)","options":{"A":"$n e A v_d$","B":"$\\frac{n A v_d}{e}$","C":"$\\frac{n v_d}{e A}$","D":"$n e v_d$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Current density is current per unit area.","explanation":"$I=neAv_d$, so $J=I/A=nev_d$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",3]]},{"id":"p0114","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following graphs correctly represents the variation of the magnitude of the magnetic field outside a straight infinite current carrying wire of radius 'a', as a function of distance 'r' from the centre of the wire ?","options":{"A":"(graph a)","B":"(graph b)","C":"(graph c)","D":"(graph d)"},"answer":"C","hint":"Outside a long straight wire, $B=\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi r}$.","explanation":"Outside the wire $B\\propto 1/r$, a hyperbolic decrease starting from $r=a$ (graph with the dashed line at $a$).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0114.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",4],[2023,"R","55/3/2",15],[2023,"R","55/3/3",8]]},{"id":"p0115","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ moving with a uniform velocity $\\vec{v}=v_{0x}\\hat{i}+v_{0y}\\hat{j}$ enters a region with a magnetic field $\\vec{B}=B_0\\hat{j}$. After some time, an electric field $\\vec{E}=E_0\\hat{j}$ is also switched on in the region. The resulting path described by the particle will be :","options":{"A":"a circle in x-z plane","B":"a parabola in x-y plane","C":"a helix with constant pitch","D":"a helix with increasing pitch"},"answer":"D","hint":"Separate the motion along $\\vec B$ from the motion perpendicular to it.","explanation":"Without $E$ the path is a helix about $y$; $E_0\\hat j$ accelerates the particle along $y$, so the pitch keeps increasing.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",5]]},{"id":"p0116","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An inductor, a capacitor and a resistor are connected in series across an ac source of voltage. If the frequency of the source is decreased gradually, the reactance of :","options":{"A":"both the inductor and the capacitor decreases.","B":"inductor decreases and the capacitor increases.","C":"both the inductor and the capacitor increases.","D":"inductor increases and the capacitor decreases."},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $X_L=\\omega L$ and $X_C=1/\\omega C$.","explanation":"$X_L\\propto\\omega$ decreases and $X_C\\propto1/\\omega$ increases as frequency falls.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",6],[2023,"R","55/3/2",9],[2023,"R","55/3/3",15]]},{"id":"p0117","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electromagnetic radiations used to kill germs in water purifiers are called :","options":{"A":"Infrared waves","B":"X-rays","C":"Gamma rays","D":"Ultraviolet rays"},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall the use of the radiation in the EM spectrum.","explanation":"Ultraviolet rays are used in water purifiers to kill germs.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",7]]},{"id":"p0118","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In the wave picture of light, the intensity $I$ of light is related to the amplitude $A$ of the wave as :","options":{"A":"$I\\propto\\sqrt{A}$","B":"$I\\propto A$","C":"$I\\propto A^2$","D":"$I\\propto\\frac{1}{A^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Energy of a wave goes as the square of its amplitude.","explanation":"Intensity is proportional to the square of the amplitude.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",8],[2023,"R","55/3/2",11],[2023,"R","55/3/3",6]]},{"id":"p0119","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In a single-slit diffraction experiment, the width of the slit is halved. The width of the central maximum, in the diffraction pattern, will become :","options":{"A":"half","B":"twice","C":"four times","D":"one-fourth"},"answer":"B","hint":"Central maximum width is $\\frac{2\\lambda D}{a}$.","explanation":"Width is inversely proportional to slit width $a$, so halving $a$ doubles it.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",9],[2023,"R","55/3/2",14],[2023,"R","55/3/3",12]]},{"id":"p0120","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A graph is plotted between the stopping potential (on y-axis) and the frequency of incident radiation (on x-axis) for a metal. The product of the slope of the straight line obtained and the magnitude of charge on an electron is equal to :","options":{"A":"$h$","B":"$\\frac{h}{c}$","C":"$\\frac{2h}{c}$","D":"$\\frac{h}{2c}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Write $V_0$ as a function of $\\nu$ from Einstein's equation.","explanation":"$eV_0=h\\nu-\\phi$ gives slope $h/e$, so slope $\\times e=h$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",10]]},{"id":"p0121","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Light of frequency $6.4\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ is incident on a metal of work function $2.14\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons is about :","options":{"A":"$0.25\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$0.51\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$1.02\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$0.10\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Convert $h\\nu$ to eV and subtract the work function.","explanation":"$h\\nu=\\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times6.4\\times10^{14}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}\\approx2.65$ eV, so $K_{max}\\approx0.51$ eV.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",11],[2023,"R","55/3/2",6],[2023,"R","55/3/3",9]]},{"id":"p0122","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of maximum frequency and minimum frequency of light emitted in Balmer series of hydrogen spectrum, in Bohr's model is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{11}{9}$","B":"$\\frac{9}{5}$","C":"$\\frac{11}{7}$","D":"$\\frac{16}{7}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Maximum frequency is for $n=\\infty\\to2$, minimum for $3\\to2$.","explanation":"$\\nu_{max}\\propto\\frac14$, $\\nu_{min}\\propto\\frac14-\\frac19=\\frac{5}{36}$, ratio $=\\frac{9}{5}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",12],[2023,"R","55/3/2",2],[2023,"R","55/3/3",13]]},{"id":"p0123","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"At a certain temperature in an intrinsic semiconductor, the electrons and holes concentration is $1.5\\times10^{16}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. When it is doped with a trivalent dopant, hole concentration increases to $4.5\\times10^{22}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$. In the doped semiconductor, the concentration of electrons ($n_e$) will be :","options":{"A":"$3\\times10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$","B":"$5\\times10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$","C":"$5\\times10^{9}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$","D":"$6.75\\times10^{38}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use the mass action law $n_en_h=n_i^2$.","explanation":"$n_e=\\frac{(1.5\\times10^{16})^2}{4.5\\times10^{22}}=5\\times10^{9}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",13],[2023,"R","55/3/2",3],[2023,"R","55/3/3",11]]},{"id":"p0124","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"If a p-n junction diode is reverse biased,","options":{"A":"the potential barrier is lowered.","B":"the potential barrier remains unaffected.","C":"the potential barrier is raised.","D":"the current is mainly due to majority carriers."},"answer":"C","hint":"Reverse bias adds to the built-in junction voltage.","explanation":"Reverse bias raises the potential barrier and widens the depletion layer.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",14]]},{"id":"p0125","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A voltage signal is described by :\n$v = V_0$ for $0 \\le t \\le \\frac{T}{2}$\n$= 0$ for $\\frac{T}{2} \\le t \\le T$\nfor a cycle. Its rms value is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{V_0}{\\sqrt{2}}$","B":"$V_0$","C":"$\\frac{V_0}{2}$","D":"$\\sqrt{2}\\,V_0$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Find the mean of $v^2$ over one full cycle.","explanation":"$v_{rms}^2=\\frac{1}{T}\\cdot V_0^2\\cdot\\frac{T}{2}=\\frac{V_0^2}{2}$, so $v_{rms}=V_0/\\sqrt{2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",15],[2023,"R","55/3/2",1],[2023,"R","55/3/3",10]]},{"id":"p0126","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The internal resistance of a cell is constant.\nReason (R): Ionic concentration of the electrolyte remains same during use of a cell.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Does a cell's electrolyte change as it is used?","explanation":"Internal resistance changes with use because ion concentration in the electrolyte changes; both statements are false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",16],[2023,"R","55/3/2",18],[2023,"R","55/3/3",17]]},{"id":"p0127","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When radius of a circular loop carrying a steady current is doubled, its magnetic moment becomes four times.\nReason (R): The magnetic moment of a circular loop carrying a steady current is proportional to the area of the loop.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Magnetic moment $M=IA=I\\pi r^2$.","explanation":"$M\\propto r^2$, so doubling $r$ gives four times $M$; R is true and explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",17],[2023,"R","55/3/2",16],[2023,"R","55/3/3",18]]},{"id":"p0128","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The nucleus $^{7}_{3}\\mathrm{X}$ is more stable than the nucleus $^{4}_{3}\\mathrm{Y}$.\nReason (R): $^{7}_{3}\\mathrm{X}$ contains more number of protons.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare the proton counts of the two nuclei.","explanation":"Both have 3 protons, so R is false; X has the better neutron-to-proton ratio, so A is true.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/1",18],[2023,"R","55/3/2",17],[2023,"R","55/3/3",16]]},{"id":"p0129","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An infinitely long uniformly charged wire produces an electric field of $18\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,C^{-1}}$ at a distance of $1.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. The linear charge density on the wire :","options":{"A":"$1.12\\times10^{-14}\\ \\mathrm{C\\,m^{-1}}$","B":"$3.08\\times10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{C\\,m^{-1}}$","C":"$1.0\\times10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{C\\,m^{-1}}$","D":"$1.0\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{C\\,m^{-1}}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $E=\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$.","explanation":"$\\lambda=2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 rE=\\frac{0.01\\times18\\times10^4}{2\\times9\\times10^9}=1.0\\times10^{-7}$ C/m.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",4]]},{"id":"p0130","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"At $0\\ \\mathrm{K}$, the resistivity of an intrinsic semiconductor is :","options":{"A":"same as that at $0^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$","B":"same as that at $300\\ \\mathrm{K}$","C":"zero","D":"infinite"},"answer":"D","hint":"Are there any free carriers at absolute zero?","explanation":"At 0 K no electrons are in the conduction band, so it behaves as an insulator with infinite resistivity.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",5]]},{"id":"p0131","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"In an experiment on photoelectric effect, the intensity of incident radiation is increased, keeping the frequency $\\nu$ the same. The number of photoelectrons emitted will :","options":{"A":"increase","B":"decrease","C":"remain same","D":"depend on frequency"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photocurrent depends on photon flux.","explanation":"Higher intensity at fixed frequency means more photons, hence more photoelectrons.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",7]]},{"id":"p0132","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The SI unit of mobility of charge carriers is :","options":{"A":"$\\Omega\\ \\mathrm{s^{-1}}$","B":"$\\mathrm{m^2\\,V^{-1}\\,s^{-1}}$","C":"$\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}\\,V^{-1}}$","D":"$\\Omega\\ \\mathrm{m}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Mobility is drift speed per unit electric field.","explanation":"$\\mu=v_d/E$ has unit $\\frac{\\mathrm{m/s}}{\\mathrm{V/m}}=\\mathrm{m^2V^{-1}s^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",8]]},{"id":"p0133","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"In a moving coil galvanometer, the deflecting torque $\\tau$ acting on the coil is related to the current $I$ flowing through it as :","options":{"A":"$\\tau\\propto I^3$","B":"$\\tau\\propto I^2$","C":"$\\tau\\propto I$","D":"$\\tau\\propto\\sqrt{I}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\tau=NIAB\\sin\\theta$.","explanation":"Torque on the coil is $NIAB$, directly proportional to $I$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",10]]},{"id":"p0134","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Electromagnetic waves of wavelength of the order of a few meters were first produced and detected in the laboratory by :","options":{"A":"J.C. Maxwell","B":"J.C. Bose","C":"H.R. Hertz","D":"G. Marconi"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the history of EM waves.","explanation":"Hertz produced and detected EM waves of a few metres wavelength in 1887.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",12]]},{"id":"p0135","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The emf and internal resistance of a cell are $E$ and $r$ respectively. It is connected across an external resistance $R=2r$. The potential drop across the terminals of the cell will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{E}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{E}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{2}{3}E$","D":"$\\frac{E}{3}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Terminal voltage is the drop across $R$.","explanation":"$I=\\frac{E}{3r}$, so $V=IR=\\frac{2E}{3}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/2",13]]},{"id":"p0136","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A battery of emf $E$ and internal resistance $r$ is connected to an external circuit. The potential drop within the battery is proportional to :","options":{"A":"current in the circuit","B":"total resistance of the circuit","C":"emf of the battery","D":"power dissipated in the circuit"},"answer":"A","hint":"Drop inside the battery is $Ir$.","explanation":"Internal drop $=Ir$ with $r$ fixed, so it is proportional to the current.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",1]]},{"id":"p0137","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The momentum of a photon of wavelength $\\lambda$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{h\\lambda}{c^2}$","B":"$\\frac{h\\lambda}{c}$","C":"$\\frac{h}{\\lambda}$","D":"zero"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $p=E/c$ with $E=hc/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$p=\\frac{h}{\\lambda}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",2]]},{"id":"p0138","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two thin long parallel wires A and B are separated by a distance $r$ and carry current $I$ each in the same direction. The force per unit length exerted by A on wire B is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2r}$, attractive","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi r}$, repulsive","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi r}$, attractive","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi r}$, repulsive"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use the force between parallel currents.","explanation":"$F/l=\\frac{\\mu_0I^2}{2\\pi r}$; parallel currents attract.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",3]]},{"id":"p0139","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two wires of equal length and radii $r$ and $2r$ are connected in series. Their resistivities are $2\\rho$ and $\\rho$ respectively. For the same current passing through the combination, the potential drop across their ends will be in the ratio of :","options":{"A":"$2:1$","B":"$1:2$","C":"$4:1$","D":"$8:1$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Same current, so voltage ratio equals resistance ratio, $R=\\rho l/\\pi r^2$.","explanation":"$R_1:R_2=\\frac{2\\rho}{r^2}:\\frac{\\rho}{4r^2}=8:1$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",4]]},{"id":"p0140","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"A capacitor is charged by a battery. Between the plates, during process of charging :","options":{"A":"only displacement current exists.","B":"only conduction current exists.","C":"both displacement current and conduction current exist.","D":"no current exists."},"answer":"A","hint":"No charge crosses the gap between the plates.","explanation":"Between the plates there is no conduction current; the changing electric field gives displacement current only.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",5]]},{"id":"p0141","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"For the forward biasing of a p-n junction diode, which of the following statements is not correct ?","options":{"A":"The potential barrier decreases.","B":"Minority carrier injection occurs.","C":"Width of depletion layer increases.","D":"Forward current is due to the diffusion of both holes and electrons."},"answer":"C","hint":"Think about what forward bias does to the depletion layer.","explanation":"Forward bias narrows the depletion layer, so statement (c) is incorrect.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",7]]},{"id":"p0142","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two cells of emf $E$ each and resistances $r$ and $2r$ are connected in parallel as shown in the figure. The equivalent emf of the combination is :","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$\\frac{E}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{E}{3}$","D":"$E$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $E_{eq}=\\frac{E_1/r_1+E_2/r_2}{1/r_1+1/r_2}$.","explanation":"With equal emfs, $E_{eq}=\\frac{E/r+E/2r}{1/r+1/2r}=E$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0142.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/3/3",14]]},{"id":"p0143","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two charges $q_1$ and $q_2$ are placed at the centres of two spherical conducting shells of radius $r_1$ and $r_2$ respectively. The shells are arranged such that their centres are $d\\ [>(r_1+r_2)]$ distance apart. The force on $q_2$ due to $q_1$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{q_1q_2}{d^2}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{q_1q_2}{(d-r_1)^2}$","C":"Zero","D":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{q_1q_2}{[d-(r_1+r_2)]^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Think of electrostatic shielding by a conducting shell.","explanation":"Each charge sits inside a closed conducting shell, which screens it from outside fields, so the net force is zero.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",1]]},{"id":"p0144","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron enters a uniform magnetic field with speed $v$. It describes a semicircular path and comes out of the field. The final speed of the electron is :","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$v$","C":"$\\frac{v}{2}$","D":"$2v$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Does the magnetic force do work?","explanation":"Magnetic force is perpendicular to velocity and does no work, so the speed stays $v$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",2]]},{"id":"p0145","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic field lines near a substance are as shown in the figure. The substance is :","options":{"A":"Copper","B":"Iron","C":"Sodium","D":"Aluminium"},"answer":"A","hint":"Field lines are expelled from the material.","explanation":"Lines bend away from the substance, which is diamagnetic; copper is diamagnetic (iron, sodium and aluminium are not).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0145.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",3]]},{"id":"p0146","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows variation of current ($I$) with time ($t$) in four devices P, Q, R and S. The device in which an alternating current flows is :","options":{"A":"P","B":"Q","C":"R","D":"S"},"answer":"D","hint":"An alternating current changes direction periodically.","explanation":"Only S oscillates about zero, reversing direction; P stays positive and Q, R do not oscillate.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0146.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",4],[2023,"R","55/4/2",1],[2023,"R","55/4/3",7]]},{"id":"p0147","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electromagnetic waves used in radar systems are :","options":{"A":"Infrared waves","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"Microwaves","D":"X-rays"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the uses of EM spectrum regions.","explanation":"Radar uses microwaves.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",5],[2023,"R","55/4/2",2]]},{"id":"p0148","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In a Young's double-slit experiment, the fringe width is found to be $\\beta$. If the entire apparatus is immersed in a liquid of refractive index $\\mu$, the new fringe width will be :","options":{"A":"$\\beta$","B":"$\\mu\\beta$","C":"$\\frac{\\beta}{\\mu}$","D":"$\\frac{\\beta}{\\mu^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Wavelength shrinks in a medium.","explanation":"$\\beta=\\lambda D/d$ and $\\lambda'=\\lambda/\\mu$, so $\\beta'=\\beta/\\mu$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",6],[2023,"R","55/4/2",3],[2023,"R","55/4/3",1]]},{"id":"p0149","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Photons of energy $3.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ are incident on a photosensitive surface. If the stopping potential for the emitted electrons is $1.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$, the work function for the surface is :","options":{"A":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$1.7\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$3.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$4.7\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $h\\nu=\\phi+eV_0$.","explanation":"$\\phi=3.2-1.5=1.7$ eV.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",7],[2023,"R","55/4/3",5]]},{"id":"p0150","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is not true for nuclear forces ?","options":{"A":"They are stronger than Coulomb forces.","B":"They have about the same magnitude for different pairs of nucleons.","C":"They are always attractive.","D":"They saturate as the separation between two nucleons increases."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall behaviour of nuclear force at very short range.","explanation":"Nuclear force becomes repulsive at very small separations, so it is not always attractive.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",8],[2023,"R","55/4/2",5],[2023,"R","55/4/3",3]]},{"id":"p0151","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The direction of induced current in the loop abc is :","options":{"A":"along abc if $I$ decreases","B":"along acb if $I$ increases","C":"along abc if $I$ is constant","D":"along abc if $I$ increases"},"answer":"D","hint":"Apply Lenz's law to the flux through the loop from the straight wire.","explanation":"Field of the wire is out of the page at the loop; increasing $I$ increases outward flux, so the induced current is clockwise, i.e. along abc.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0151.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",9],[2023,"R","55/4/2",11],[2023,"R","55/4/3",11]]},{"id":"p0152","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ac voltage $v=v_0\\sin\\omega t$ is applied to a series combination of a resistor $R$ and an element $X$. The instantaneous current in the circuit is $I=I_0\\sin\\left(\\omega t+\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. Then which of the following is correct ?","options":{"A":"$X$ is a capacitor and $X_C=\\sqrt{2}\\,R$","B":"$X$ is an inductor and $X_L=R$","C":"$X$ is an inductor and $X_L=\\sqrt{2}\\,R$","D":"$X$ is a capacitor and $X_C=R$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Current leading voltage means a capacitive circuit; use $\\tan\\phi=X/R$.","explanation":"Current leads by $\\pi/4$, so $X$ is a capacitor and $\\tan45^\\circ=X_C/R=1$, giving $X_C=R$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",10],[2023,"R","55/4/2",12],[2023,"R","55/4/3",12]]},{"id":"p0153","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A proton and an alpha particle have the same kinetic energy. The ratio of de Broglie wavelengths associated with the proton to that with the alpha particle is :","options":{"A":"$1$","B":"$2$","C":"$2\\sqrt{2}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\lambda=\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2mK}}$.","explanation":"$\\lambda\\propto1/\\sqrt{mK}$, so $\\lambda_p/\\lambda_\\alpha=\\sqrt{m_\\alpha/m_p}=\\sqrt4=2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",12],[2023,"R","55/4/2",14],[2023,"R","55/4/3",10]]},{"id":"p0154","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The potential energy of an electron in the second excited state in hydrogen atom is :","options":{"A":"$-3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$-3.02\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$-1.51\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$-6.8\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Second excited state is $n=3$; $U=2E$.","explanation":"$E_3=-1.51$ eV and $U=2E_3=-3.02$ eV.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",13],[2023,"R","55/4/2",15],[2023,"R","55/4/3",14]]},{"id":"p0155","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The difference in mass of $^{7}\\mathrm{X}$ nucleus and total mass of its constituent nucleons is $21.00\\ \\mathrm{u}$. The binding energy per nucleon for this nucleus is equal to the energy equivalent of :","options":{"A":"$3\\ \\mathrm{u}$","B":"$3.5\\ \\mathrm{u}$","C":"$7\\ \\mathrm{u}$","D":"$21\\ \\mathrm{u}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Divide the mass defect by the number of nucleons.","explanation":"Binding energy per nucleon corresponds to $21/7=3$ u.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",14],[2023,"R","55/4/2",9],[2023,"R","55/4/3",15]]},{"id":"p0156","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The threshold voltage for a p-n junction diode used in the circuit is $0.7\\ \\mathrm{V}$. The type of biasing and current in the circuit are :","options":{"A":"Forward biasing, $0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"Reverse biasing, $0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"Forward biasing, $5\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","D":"Reverse biasing, $2\\ \\mathrm{mA}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Compare the applied voltage with the threshold voltage.","explanation":"The diode is forward connected, but $0.5\\ \\mathrm{V}<0.7\\ \\mathrm{V}$, so no current flows.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0156.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",15],[2023,"R","55/4/2",10],[2023,"R","55/4/3",13]]},{"id":"p0157","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When three electric bulbs of power $200\\ \\mathrm{W}$, $100\\ \\mathrm{W}$ and $50\\ \\mathrm{W}$ are connected in series to a source, the power consumed by the $50\\ \\mathrm{W}$ bulb is maximum.\nReason (R): In a series circuit, current is the same through each bulb, but the potential difference across each bulb is different.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"In series $P=I^2R$ and the highest resistance dissipates most.","explanation":"The 50 W bulb has the largest resistance so it consumes most power (A true); R is true but does not itself explain why the 50 W bulb gets most power.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",16],[2023,"R","55/4/2",18],[2023,"R","55/4/3",17]]},{"id":"p0158","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A current carrying square loop made of a wire of length $L$ is placed in a magnetic field. It experiences a torque which is greater than the torque on a circular loop made of the same wire carrying the same current in the same magnetic field.\nReason (R): A square loop occupies more area than a circular loop, both made of wire of the same length.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare the areas for equal perimeter.","explanation":"For the same length a circle encloses the maximum area, so the square has less area and less torque; both statements are false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",17],[2023,"R","55/4/2",16],[2023,"R","55/4/3",18]]},{"id":"p0159","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In 'n' type semiconductor, number density of electrons is greater than the number density of holes but the crystal maintains an overall charge neutrality.\nReason (R): The charge of electrons donated by donor atoms is just equal and opposite to that of the ionised donor.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Each donated electron leaves behind a positive donor ion.","explanation":"Both true; donor ions' positive charge balances the donated electrons, which explains neutrality.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/1",18],[2023,"R","55/4/2",17],[2023,"R","55/4/3",16]]},{"id":"p0160","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A point charge $q$ is kept at a distance $r$ from an infinitely long straight wire with charge density $\\lambda$. The magnitude of the electrostatic force experienced by charge $q$ is :","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$\\frac{q\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$","C":"$\\frac{q\\lambda}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$","D":"$\\frac{q\\lambda}{\\varepsilon_0 r}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Field of a line charge is $\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$.","explanation":"$F=qE=\\frac{q\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/2",6]]},{"id":"p0161","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the magnitude of magnetic moment to that of angular momentum, of an electron revolving around the nucleus in a hydrogen atom is :","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$9.27\\times10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{C/kg}$","C":"$8.8\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{C/kg}$","D":"$6.6\\times10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{C/kg}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\frac{\\mu}{L}=\\frac{e}{2m}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{e}{2m}=\\frac{1.6\\times10^{-19}}{2\\times9.1\\times10^{-31}}\\approx8.8\\times10^{10}$ C/kg.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/2",7]]},{"id":"p0162","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following has negative value of susceptibility?","options":{"A":"Nickel","B":"Aluminium","C":"Lead","D":"Iron"},"answer":"C","hint":"Diamagnetic substances have negative susceptibility.","explanation":"Lead is diamagnetic, so its susceptibility is negative; nickel and iron are ferromagnetic, aluminium is paramagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/2",8]]},{"id":"p0163","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A light of frequency $\\nu$ is incident on a metal surface whose work function is $W_0$. The kinetic energy of emitted electron is K. If the frequency of the incident light is doubled then the kinetic energy of emitted electron will be :","options":{"A":"$2K$","B":"more than $2K$","C":"between $K$ and $2K$","D":"less than $K$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $K=h\\nu-W_0$ for both frequencies.","explanation":"$K'=2h\\nu-W_0=2K+W_0>2K$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/3",2]]},{"id":"p0164","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass m and charge $-q$ is moving with a uniform speed v in a circle of radius r, with another charge q at the centre of the circle. The value of r is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 m}\\left(\\frac{q}{v}\\right)$","B":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 m}\\left(\\frac{q}{v}\\right)^2$","C":"$\\frac{m}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{q}{v}\\right)$","D":"$\\frac{m}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{q}{v}\\right)^2$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Coulomb force provides the centripetal force.","explanation":"$\\frac{mv^2}{r}=\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$ gives $r=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 m}\\left(\\frac{q}{v}\\right)^2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/3",4]]},{"id":"p0165","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following has relative magnetic permeability between 0 and 1 ?","options":{"A":"Aluminium","B":"Alnico","C":"Water","D":"Sodium"},"answer":"C","hint":"Diamagnetic materials have $\\mu_r$ slightly less than 1.","explanation":"Water is diamagnetic ($\\mu_r<1$); aluminium and sodium are paramagnetic and alnico is ferromagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/3",6]]},{"id":"p0166","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Name the electromagnetic waves also known as 'heat waves'.","options":{"A":"Radio waves","B":"Microwaves","C":"X-rays","D":"Infrared waves"},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall which radiation produces heating effect.","explanation":"Infrared waves are called heat waves.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/4/3",8]]},{"id":"p0167","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole of length $2\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ is placed at an angle of $30^\\circ$ with an electric field $2\\times10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$. If the dipole experiences a torque of $8\\times10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{Nm}$, the magnitude of either charge of the dipole is","options":{"A":"$4\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$","B":"$7\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$","C":"$8\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","D":"$2\\ \\mathrm{mC}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Torque $\\tau=qE\\,l\\sin\\theta$.","explanation":"$q=\\frac{8\\times10^{-3}}{2\\times10^{5}\\times0.02\\times0.5}=4\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",1],[2023,"R","55/5/3",1]]},{"id":"p0168","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following has its permeability less than that of free space ?","options":{"A":"Copper","B":"Aluminium","C":"Copper chloride","D":"Nickel"},"answer":"A","hint":"Diamagnetic materials have $\\mu<\\mu_0$.","explanation":"Copper is diamagnetic; aluminium and copper chloride are paramagnetic, nickel is ferromagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",3],[2023,"R","55/5/2",3]]},{"id":"p0169","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A square shaped coil of side $10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, having 100 turns is placed perpendicular to a magnetic field which is increasing at $1\\ \\mathrm{T/s}$. The induced emf in the coil is","options":{"A":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$0.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$0.75\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$1.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"emf $=NA\\,\\frac{dB}{dt}$.","explanation":"$100\\times0.01\\times1=1.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",4],[2023,"R","55/5/2",4],[2023,"R","55/5/3",4]]},{"id":"p0170","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following electromagnetic radiation has the least wavelength ?","options":{"A":"Gamma rays","B":"Microwaves","C":"Visible light","D":"X-rays"},"answer":"A","hint":"Order the spectrum by wavelength.","explanation":"Gamma rays have the highest frequency and hence the smallest wavelength.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",5],[2023,"R","55/5/3",5]]},{"id":"p0171","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In a Young's double-slit experiment, the screen is moved away from the plane of the slits. What will be its effect on the following ?\n(i) Angular separation of the fringes.\n(ii) Fringe-width.","options":{"A":"Both (i) and (ii) remain constant.","B":"(i) remains constant, but (ii) decreases.","C":"(i) remains constant, but (ii) increases.","D":"Both (i) and (ii) increase."},"answer":"C","hint":"Angular fringe width is $\\lambda/d$; fringe width is $\\lambda D/d$.","explanation":"$\\theta=\\lambda/d$ is independent of D while $\\beta=\\lambda D/d$ increases with D.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",6],[2023,"R","55/5/2",6],[2023,"R","55/5/3",6]]},{"id":"p0172","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of a photon of wavelength $\\lambda$ is","options":{"A":"$hc\\lambda$","B":"$hc/\\lambda$","C":"$\\lambda/hc$","D":"$\\lambda h/c$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $E=h\\nu$ with $\\nu=c/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$E=hc/\\lambda$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",7],[2023,"R","55/5/2",7]]},{"id":"p0173","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the nuclear densities of two nuclei having mass numbers 64 and 125 is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{64}{125}$","B":"$\\frac{4}{5}$","C":"$\\frac{5}{4}$","D":"1"},"answer":"D","hint":"Nuclear density does not depend on mass number.","explanation":"Nuclear density is constant for all nuclei, so the ratio is 1.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",8],[2023,"R","55/5/2",8],[2023,"R","55/5/3",8]]},{"id":"p0174","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"During the formation of a p-n junction :","options":{"A":"diffusion current keeps increasing.","B":"drift current remains constant.","C":"both the diffusion current and drift current remain constant.","D":"diffusion current remains almost constant but drift current increases till both currents become equal."},"answer":"D","hint":"Think about how the depletion layer builds up.","explanation":"Diffusion current stays nearly constant while the growing barrier field increases drift current until they balance.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",9],[2023,"R","55/5/2",9]]},{"id":"p0175","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The diagram shows four energy level of an electron in Bohr model of hydrogen atom. Identify the transition in which the emitted photon will have the highest energy.","options":{"A":"I","B":"II","C":"III","D":"IV"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photon energy equals the energy gap between levels.","explanation":"Transition I (n = 3 to n = 1) has the largest gap among the four shown.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0175.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",10],[2023,"R","55/5/2",10],[2023,"R","55/5/3",10]]},{"id":"p0176","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following graphs correctly represents the variation of a particle momentum with its associated de-Broglie wavelength ?","options":{"A":"Straight line with positive slope, p versus $\\lambda$","B":"Curve rising with $\\lambda$ (p increasing with $\\lambda$)","C":"Straight line with negative slope, p versus $\\lambda$","D":"Rectangular hyperbola, p decreasing as $\\lambda$ increases"},"answer":"D","hint":"$p=h/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$p\\propto1/\\lambda$ gives a rectangular hyperbola.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0176.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",11],[2023,"R","55/5/3",11]]},{"id":"p0177","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The capacitors, each of $4\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ are to be connected in such a way that the effective capacitance of the combination is $6\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$. This can be achieved by connecting","options":{"A":"All three in parallel","B":"All three in series","C":"Two of them connected in series and the combination in parallel to the third.","D":"Two of them connected in parallel and the combination in series to the third."},"answer":"C","hint":"Series pair of equal capacitors gives half.","explanation":"Two in series give $2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$; in parallel with the third gives $2+4=6\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",12],[2023,"R","55/5/2",12],[2023,"R","55/5/3",12]]},{"id":"p0178","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements about a series LCR circuit connected to an ac source is correct ?","options":{"A":"If the frequency of the source is increased, the impedance of the circuit first decreases and then increases.","B":"If the net reactance ($X_L-X_C$) of circuit becomes equal to its resistance, then the current leads the voltage by $45^\\circ$.","C":"At resonance, the voltage drop across the inductor is more than that across the capacitor.","D":"At resonance, the voltage drop across the capacitor is more than that across the inductor."},"answer":"A","hint":"Impedance is minimum at resonance.","explanation":"$Z=\\sqrt{R^2+(X_L-X_C)^2}$ falls to a minimum at resonance and then rises; at resonance $V_L=V_C$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",13]]},{"id":"p0179","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"According to Huygens principle, the amplitude of secondary wavelets is","options":{"A":"equal in both the forward and the backward directions.","B":"maximum in the forward direction and zero in the backward direction.","C":"large in the forward direction and small in the backward direction.","D":"small in the forward direction and large in the backward direction."},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall the obliquity factor in Huygens principle.","explanation":"The amplitude is maximum in the forward direction and zero in the backward direction.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",14],[2023,"R","55/5/2",14]]},{"id":"p0180","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The resistance of an intrinsic semiconductor decreases with increase in its temperature.\nReason (R): The number of conduction electrons as well as hole increase in an intrinsic semiconductor with rise in its temperature.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"More carriers means lower resistance.","explanation":"Both true; the increased carrier number is exactly why resistance falls.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",16],[2023,"R","55/5/3",16]]},{"id":"p0181","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The equivalent resistance between points A and B in the given network is 2R.\nReason (R): All the resistors are connected in parallel","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Identify which resistors are shorted or in parallel.","explanation":"The top 2R joins A to the node between the middle and last 2R, giving $R_{AB}=\\frac{6R}{5}$ by my analysis, and R is false; official key says A true, R false.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0181.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",17],[2023,"R","55/5/2",17]]},{"id":"p0182","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The deflecting torque acting on a current carrying loop is zero when its plane is perpendicular to the direction of magnetic field.\nReason (R): The deflecting torque acting on a loop of magnetic moment $\\vec{m}$ in a magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is given by the dot product of $\\vec{m}$ and $\\vec{B}$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Torque is $\\vec{m}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"A is true since $\\vec{m}\\parallel\\vec{B}$ gives zero torque, but R is false because torque is a cross product.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/1",18],[2023,"R","55/5/2",18],[2023,"R","55/5/3",18]]},{"id":"p0183","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A charge Q is placed at the centre of a cube. The electric flux through one if its face is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{Q}{\\varepsilon_0}$","B":"$\\frac{Q}{6\\varepsilon_0}$","C":"$\\frac{Q}{8\\varepsilon_0}$","D":"$\\frac{Q}{3\\varepsilon_0}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Total flux is $Q/\\varepsilon_0$, shared equally by six faces.","explanation":"Each face gets $\\frac{Q}{6\\varepsilon_0}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/2",1]]},{"id":"p0184","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Choose the correct option related to wavelengths ($\\lambda$) of different parts of electromagnetic spectrum.","options":{"A":"$\\lambda_{\\text{x-rays}}<\\lambda_{\\text{micro waves}}<\\lambda_{\\text{radio waves}}<\\lambda_{\\text{visible}}$","B":"$\\lambda_{\\text{visible}}>\\lambda_{\\text{x-rays}}>\\lambda_{\\text{radio waves}}>\\lambda_{\\text{micro waves}}$","C":"$\\lambda_{\\text{radio wave}}>\\lambda_{\\text{micro waves}}>\\lambda_{\\text{visible}}>\\lambda_{\\text{x-rays}}$","D":"$\\lambda_{\\text{visible}}<\\lambda_{\\text{micro waves}}<\\lambda_{\\text{radio waves}}<\\lambda_{\\text{x-rays}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Order the spectrum from radio to X-rays.","explanation":"Wavelength decreases from radio to microwaves to visible to X-rays.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/2",5]]},{"id":"p0185","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Figure shows a plot of stopping potential ($V_0$) versus $\\frac{1}{\\lambda}$, where $\\lambda$ is the wavelength of the radiation causing photoelectric emission from a surface. The slope of the line is equal to","options":{"A":"$\\phi_0$","B":"$\\frac{h}{e}$","C":"$\\frac{hc}{e}$","D":"$\\frac{h^2c}{e^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Write $eV_0=\\frac{hc}{\\lambda}-\\phi_0$.","explanation":"$V_0=\\frac{hc}{e}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\lambda}-\\frac{\\phi_0}{e}$, so slope is $hc/e$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0185.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/2",11]]},{"id":"p0186","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ideal inductor is connected across an AC source of voltage. The current in the circuit","options":{"A":"is ahead of the voltage in phase by $\\pi$.","B":"lags voltage in phase by $\\pi$.","C":"is ahead of voltage in phase by $\\pi/2$.","D":"lags voltage in phase by $\\pi/2$."},"answer":"D","hint":"In a pure inductor voltage leads current.","explanation":"Current lags voltage by $\\pi/2$ in an ideal inductor.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/2",13]]},{"id":"p0187","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In insulators, the forbidden gap is very large.\nReason (R): The valence electrons in an atom of an insulator are very tightly bound to the nucleus.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Tightly bound electrons need a lot of energy to be freed.","explanation":"Both true; tight binding of valence electrons is why the gap is large.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/2",16]]},{"id":"p0188","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following cannot modify an external magnetic field as shown in the figure ?","options":{"A":"Nickel","B":"Silicon","C":"Sodium Chloride","D":"Copper"},"answer":"A","hint":"The figure shows field lines expelled from the material.","explanation":"The lines are expelled, as by a diamagnetic substance; nickel (ferromagnetic) would concentrate the lines instead, so it cannot produce this effect.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0188.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/3",3]]},{"id":"p0189","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"E, c and $\\nu$ represent the energy, velocity and frequency of a photon. Which of the following represents its wavelength ?","options":{"A":"$\\frac{h\\nu}{c^2}$","B":"$h\\nu$","C":"$\\frac{hc}{E}$","D":"$\\frac{h\\nu}{c}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $E=hc/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$\\lambda=hc/E$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/3",7]]},{"id":"p0190","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy required by an electron to jump the forbidden band in silicon at room temperature is about","options":{"A":"$0.01\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$0.05\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$0.7\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$1.1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall the band gap of Si.","explanation":"The band gap of silicon is about $1.1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/3",9]]},{"id":"p0191","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"What is the ratio of inductive and capacitive reactance in an ac circuit ?","options":{"A":"$\\omega^2LC$","B":"$LC^2$","C":"$\\frac{LC}{\\omega^2}$","D":"$\\omega^2L$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$X_L=\\omega L$ and $X_C=1/\\omega C$.","explanation":"$X_L/X_C=\\omega L\\cdot\\omega C=\\omega^2LC$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/3",13]]},{"id":"p0192","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In an interference experiment, third bright fringe is obtained at a point on the screen with a light of $700\\ \\mathrm{nm}$. What should be the wavelength of the light source in order to obtain the fifth bright fringe at the same point ?","options":{"A":"$420\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$750\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$630\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$500\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Path difference $=n\\lambda$ is fixed at the point.","explanation":"$3\\times700=5\\lambda$ gives $\\lambda=420\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/3",14]]},{"id":"p0193","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The given figure does not show a balanced Wheatstone bridge.\nReason (R): For a balanced bridge small current should flow through the galvanometer.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Balance requires $P/Q=R/S$ and zero galvanometer current.","explanation":"$P/Q=2/3\\ne R/S=3/2$ so A is true; at balance no current (not small current) flows, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0193.png","sources":[[2023,"R","55/5/3",17]]},{"id":"p0194","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The charge on a body is $8\\times10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{C}$. It means that the body has :","options":{"A":"lost $8\\times10^{-12}$ electrons","B":"gained $4\\times10^{10}$ electrons","C":"gained $2\\times10^{8}$ electrons","D":"lost $5\\times10^{7}$ electrons"},"answer":"D","hint":"Positive charge means electrons lost; $n=q/e$.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{8\\times10^{-12}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}=5\\times10^{7}$ electrons lost.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",1]]},{"id":"p0195","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A current flows through a series combination of two copper wires of equal length but their radii are in the ratio of 1 : 2. The ratio of drift velocities of free electrons in the wires will be :","options":{"A":"8","B":"4","C":"2","D":"1"},"answer":"B","hint":"Same current: $I=neAv_d$.","explanation":"$v_d\\propto1/A\\propto1/r^2$, so the ratio is $4:1$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",2]]},{"id":"p0196","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A charged particle at rest is subjected to an external magnetic field $\\vec{B}$. If no other force acts on the particle, then it will :","options":{"A":"accelerate along $\\vec{B}$","B":"remain at rest","C":"move in a circular path","D":"move with a constant speed along $\\vec{B}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Magnetic force is $q\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"With $v=0$ the force is zero, so the particle stays at rest.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",3]]},{"id":"p0197","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux (in SI units) through a coil varies with time as $\\phi=3t^2+4t+7$. The ratio of emf induced in the coil at $t=2\\ \\mathrm{s}$ to that at $t=1\\ \\mathrm{s}$ will be :","options":{"A":"2","B":"0.8","C":"1.6","D":"4"},"answer":"C","hint":"emf $=-d\\phi/dt$.","explanation":"$|\\varepsilon|=6t+4$: 16 at 2 s and 10 at 1 s, ratio 1.6.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",4]]},{"id":"p0198","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A current carrying loop is placed in a uniform magnetic field. The torque acting on it does not depend upon the :","options":{"A":"magnetic field","B":"current in the loop","C":"area of the loop","D":"shape of the loop"},"answer":"D","hint":"Torque is $NIAB\\sin\\theta$.","explanation":"Torque depends on area but not on the shape of the loop.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",5]]},{"id":"p0199","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two identical coils carrying equal currents are held concentric with their planes perpendicular to each other. The ratio of the magnitude of magnetic field at the centre of one coil to that of the resultant magnetic field at the centre is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$","B":"$\\sqrt{2}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","D":"2"},"answer":"A","hint":"Perpendicular fields add as vectors.","explanation":"Resultant is $\\sqrt{2}B$, so ratio is $B/\\sqrt2B=1/\\sqrt2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",6]]},{"id":"p0200","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron is revolving around a nucleus in a circular orbit of radius r with a constant speed v. The magnetic moment associated with the circulating current is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{evr}{4}$","B":"$4\\,evr$","C":"$2\\,evr$","D":"$\\frac{evr}{2}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\mu=IA$ with $I=ev/2\\pi r$.","explanation":"$\\mu=\\frac{ev}{2\\pi r}\\cdot\\pi r^2=\\frac{evr}{2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",7]]},{"id":"p0201","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is not correct ?","options":{"A":"Lenz's law is the consequence of the law of conservation of energy.","B":"The magnitude of induced emf in a coil is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux.","C":"Inserting an iron core in a coil decreases its self-inductance.","D":"An emf can be induced in a straight conductor by moving it perpendicularly through a uniform magnetic field."},"answer":"C","hint":"An iron core raises permeability.","explanation":"Inserting an iron core increases self-inductance, so statement (c) is wrong.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",8]]},{"id":"p0202","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In a plane electromagnetic wave, the magnetic field oscillates sinusoidally with a frequency of $3\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$, and amplitude $2.4\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{T}$. The amplitude of the oscillating electric field is :","options":{"A":"$1.6\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$","B":"$3.2\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$","C":"$7.2\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$","D":"$8\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E_0=cB_0$.","explanation":"$E_0=3\\times10^{8}\\times2.4\\times10^{-8}=7.2\\ \\mathrm{V/m}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",9]]},{"id":"p0203","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"An object is placed at a distance of $10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ in front of a concave mirror of radius of curvature $15\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. The nature and position of the image formed is :","options":{"A":"real and inverted, $30\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ in front of the mirror","B":"real and inverted, $15\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ in front of the mirror","C":"virtual and erect, $30\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ behind the mirror","D":"virtual and erect, $15\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ behind the mirror"},"answer":"A","hint":"Mirror formula with $f=R/2$.","explanation":"$f=-7.5$, $u=-10$: $\\frac1v=-\\frac1{7.5}+\\frac1{10}$ gives $v=-30\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, real and inverted.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",10]]},{"id":"p0204","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of a photon in a beam of red light with a wavelength of $6.60\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}$ is close to :","options":{"A":"$4.42\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$4.0\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$2.19\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$3.0\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$E=hc/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{6.6\\times10^{-34}\\times3\\times10^{8}}{6.6\\times10^{-7}}=3.0\\times10^{-19}\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",11]]},{"id":"p0205","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"A photon absorbed by a hydrogen atom excites it from $n=3$ level to $n=5$ level. The energy of the photon is :","options":{"A":"$+0.97\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$-0.97\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$+1.51\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$-0.54\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photon energy equals $E_5-E_3$.","explanation":"$E=13.6\\left(\\frac19-\\frac1{25}\\right)=0.97\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",12]]},{"id":"p0206","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The number of neutrons in $^{237}_{91}\\mathrm{Pa}$ is :","options":{"A":"91","B":"146","C":"237","D":"164"},"answer":"B","hint":"Neutrons $=A-Z$.","explanation":"$237-91=146$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",13]]},{"id":"p0207","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following is not a semiconductor ?","options":{"A":"Ge","B":"Si","C":"Sn","D":"CdS"},"answer":"C","hint":"Tin (grey/white) behaves as a metal/conductor in this context.","explanation":"Ge, Si and CdS are semiconductors; Sn is not.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",14]]},{"id":"p0208","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In an intrinsic semiconductor, the intrinsic carrier concentration ($n_i$), electron concentration ($n_e$) and hole concentration ($n_h$) are related as :","options":{"A":"$n_e+n_h=n_i$","B":"$n_i=\\sqrt{n_e n_h}$","C":"$n_e=n_h=n_i$","D":"$n_e+n_h=\\frac{n_i}{2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Electrons and holes are created in pairs.","explanation":"In an intrinsic semiconductor $n_e=n_h=n_i$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",15]]},{"id":"p0209","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The speed of light decreases when it passes from air into a denser medium.\nReason (R): The speed of light in a denser medium is given by $v=\\frac{c}{\\mu}$, where c is the speed of light in air and $\\mu$ is the refractive index of the denser medium.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Speed in a medium is $c/\\mu$.","explanation":"Both true; $\\mu>1$ makes $v<c$, so R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",16]]},{"id":"p0210","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Silicon is preferred over Germanium for making semiconductor devices.\nReason (R): Silicon can be used at a higher temperature as compared to Germanium.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Compare band gaps of Si and Ge.","explanation":"Both true; larger band gap lets Si work at higher temperature, which is why it is preferred.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",17]]},{"id":"p0211","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The energy (E) and momentum (p) of a photon are related as $p=\\frac{h}{E}$.\nReason (R): Photons behave as a wave.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"For a photon $p=E/c$.","explanation":"Correct relation is $p=E/c$, so A is false; official key marks R false as well.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2023,"R","55/B/5",18]]},{"id":"p0212","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Three points charges $+q$, $-4q$ and $+2q$ are kept at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side 'a'. The electrostatic potential energy of this system of charges is :","options":{"A":"$-\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{q^2}{a^2}\\right)$","B":"$+\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{8q^2}{a^2}\\right)$","C":"$+\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{6q^2}{a}\\right)$","D":"$-\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(\\frac{10q^2}{a}\\right)$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Sum $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{q_iq_j}{a}$ over the three pairs.","explanation":"$U=\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}(-4+2-8)=-\\frac{10q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",1]]},{"id":"p0213","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The element of a heater is cut into two equal parts. Now only one part is used in the heater. The heat generated now as compared to the original rating of the heater will be :","options":{"A":"halved","B":"doubled","C":"four times","D":"same"},"answer":"B","hint":"Power $=V^2/R$ and R is proportional to length.","explanation":"Resistance halves, so $P=V^2/R$ doubles.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",2]]},{"id":"p0214","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A bar magnet of magnetic moment $4\\ \\mathrm{JT^{-1}}$ is kept along the direction of magnetic field of $0.4\\ \\mathrm{T}$. The work done to rotate the magnet through an angle of $90^\\circ$ is :","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$-1.6\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$-0.2\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$1.6\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$W=mB(\\cos\\theta_1-\\cos\\theta_2)$.","explanation":"$W=mB(1-\\cos90^\\circ)=4\\times0.4=1.6\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",3]]},{"id":"p0215","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The dimensions of permeability of a medium are :","options":{"A":"$[\\mathrm{MLT^{-1}A^{-1}}]$","B":"$[\\mathrm{MLT^{-2}A^{-2}}]$","C":"$[\\mathrm{ML^{-2}T^{-2}A^{-1}}]$","D":"$[\\mathrm{MLT^{-2}A^{-1}}]$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $F/l=\\frac{\\mu_0I_1I_2}{2\\pi d}$.","explanation":"$\\mu_0=\\frac{F}{I^2}$ has dimensions $\\mathrm{MLT^{-2}A^{-2}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",4]]},{"id":"p0216","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two parallel straight wires carrying currents $I_1$ and $I_2$ are separated by a distance x. The current in the first wire is increased from $I_1$ to $2I_1$ and their separation is reduced to $\\frac{x}{2}$. The value of force between the wires will :","options":{"A":"become four times","B":"become two times","C":"becomes one-fourth","D":"remain the same"},"answer":"A","hint":"$F\\propto I_1I_2/x$.","explanation":"$F\\propto\\frac{2}{1/2}=4$, so the force becomes four times.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",5]]},{"id":"p0217","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A bar magnet is thrown from a distance into a closed loop with its north pole towards the loop and passes through it. The induced current in the loop is :","options":{"A":"first clockwise and then anticlockwise","B":"first anticlockwise and then clockwise","C":"clockwise only","D":"anticlockwise only"},"answer":"B","hint":"Apply Lenz's law as flux first rises then falls.","explanation":"Approaching N pole gives anticlockwise current (seen from magnet side); after passing, the direction reverses to clockwise.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",6]]},{"id":"p0218","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The speed of electromagnetic waves in a medium depends upon :","options":{"A":"properties of the medium","B":"frequency of the waves","C":"wavelength of the waves","D":"intensity of the waves"},"answer":"A","hint":"$v=1/\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}$.","explanation":"Speed is set by the permittivity and permeability of the medium.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",7]]},{"id":"p0219","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"The phase difference between any two points on a wavefront of a wave is :","options":{"A":"$\\pi$","B":"$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","C":"Zero","D":"$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"All points on a wavefront are in the same phase.","explanation":"Points on a wavefront oscillate in phase, so the difference is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",8]]},{"id":"p0220","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the de Broglie wavelengths associated with an electron of mass m and of proton of mass M when both are accelerated through a potential difference V is :","options":{"A":"$\\left(\\frac{M}{m}\\right)^{3/2}$","B":"$\\frac{m}{M}$","C":"$\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{m}}$","D":"$\\sqrt{\\frac{m}{M}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$\\lambda=\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2mqV}}$.","explanation":"Same charge and V give $\\lambda\\propto1/\\sqrt m$, so $\\lambda_e/\\lambda_p=\\sqrt{M/m}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",9]]},{"id":"p0221","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The electron of a hydrogen atom is in the second excited level. During its de-excitation to the ground state, the number of possible spectral lines that can be emitted is :","options":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"6"},"answer":"C","hint":"Count transitions among n = 3, 2, 1.","explanation":"Second excited level is n = 3, giving $\\frac{3\\cdot2}{2}=3$ lines.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",10]]},{"id":"p0222","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The majority and the minority charge carriers inside a p-type semiconductor are respectively :","options":{"A":"electrons, holes","B":"holes, electrons","C":"holes, holes","D":"electrons, electrons"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall what doping with a trivalent impurity creates.","explanation":"In a p-type semiconductor holes are the majority carriers and electrons the minority carriers.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",11]]},{"id":"p0223","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The output of a full-wave rectifier is :","options":{"A":"steady","B":"continuous with ripple","C":"continuous without any ripple","D":"zero during alternate half cycles of input"},"answer":"B","hint":"Rectifier output is unidirectional but not constant.","explanation":"Full-wave rectification gives unidirectional pulsating output that is continuous but has ripple, removed only by a filter.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",12]]},{"id":"p0224","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons depends upon the work function of the surface.\nReason (R): The maximum value of kinetic energy of photoelectrons is the difference of energy of incident photons and work function of the surface.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Use Einstein's photoelectric equation.","explanation":"$K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi_0$, so A is true and R is true and explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",13]]},{"id":"p0225","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): At a very high frequency, a capacitor can behave as a conductor.\nReason (R): The capacitance of a capacitor depends only on its geometrical configuration and nature of dielectric used.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Think of capacitive reactance versus frequency.","explanation":"$X_C=\\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\to 0$ at high frequency so A is true; R is true but does not explain the behaviour at high frequency.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",14]]},{"id":"p0226","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A magnetic field does not interact with a moving charge.\nReason (R): A moving charge does not produce a magnetic field.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"A moving charge both feels and produces a magnetic field.","explanation":"A is false since force $q\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}$ acts on a moving charge; R is also false since moving charges produce magnetic fields.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",15]]},{"id":"p0227","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In optical fibres, the refractive index of cladding must be lesser than that of the core.\nReason (R): Total internal reflection takes place only when the ray passes from denser to rarer medium.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Condition for total internal reflection.","explanation":"TIR needs a ray going from denser to rarer medium, so the cladding must have lower index than the core; R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55(B)/S",16]]},{"id":"p0228","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Two particles A and B of the same mass but having charges q and 4q respectively, are accelerated from rest through different potential differences $V_A$ and $V_B$ such that they attain same kinetic energies. The value of $\\left(\\frac{V_A}{V_B}\\right)$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"$2$","D":"$4$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Kinetic energy gained equals charge times potential difference.","explanation":"$qV_A=4qV_B$ gives $V_A/V_B=4$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",1],[2024,"C","55/S/3",8]]},{"id":"p0229","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A coil of resistance $20\\ \\Omega$ and self-inductance $10\\ \\mathrm{mH}$ is connected to an ac source of frequency $1000/\\pi\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$. The phase difference between current in the circuit and the source voltage is :","options":{"A":"$30^\\circ$","B":"$60^\\circ$","C":"$75^\\circ$","D":"$45^\\circ$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\tan\\phi=X_L/R$.","explanation":"$X_L=2\\pi fL=2000\\times10^{-2}=20\\ \\Omega=R$, so $\\tan\\phi=1$ and $\\phi=45^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",2]]},{"id":"p0230","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Isotones are nuclides having :","options":{"A":"same number of neutrons but different number of protons","B":"same number of protons but different number of neutrons","C":"same number of protons and also same number of neutrons","D":"different number of protons and also different number of neutrons"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall isotopes, isobars and isotones definitions.","explanation":"Isotones have equal neutron number $N$ but different proton number $Z$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",3],[2024,"C","55/S/2",5]]},{"id":"p0231","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A bulb is rated $(100\\ \\mathrm{W},\\ 110\\ \\mathrm{V})$. It is operated by current of $1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$ supplied by a step down transformer. If the input voltage and efficiency of the transformer are $220\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $0.9$ respectively, the input current drawn from the mains is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$\\frac{3}{8}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$\\frac{5}{9}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$\\frac{4}{7}\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Efficiency is output power over input power.","explanation":"Output power $=110\\times1=110\\ \\mathrm{W}$; $220\\,I_p\\times0.9=110$ gives $I_p=5/9\\ \\mathrm{A}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",4]]},{"id":"p0232","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following substances has relative magnetic permeability $\\mu_r \\gg 1$ ?","options":{"A":"Aluminium","B":"Copper chloride","C":"Nickel","D":"Sodium chloride"},"answer":"C","hint":"Which of these is ferromagnetic?","explanation":"Nickel is ferromagnetic with $\\mu_r\\gg1$; the others are para- or diamagnetic with $\\mu_r\\approx1$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",5]]},{"id":"p0233","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is correct for alpha particle scattering experiment ?","options":{"A":"For angle of scattering $\\theta \\approx 0$, the impact parameter is small.","B":"For angle of scattering $\\theta \\simeq \\pi$, the impact parameter is large.","C":"The number of alpha particles undergoing head-on collision is small.","D":"The experiment provides an estimate of the upper limit to the size of target atom."},"answer":"C","hint":"Relate impact parameter to scattering angle and to the nucleus size.","explanation":"Large deflection needs small impact parameter and few particles collide head-on; the experiment bounds nuclear size, not atom size.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",6],[2024,"C","55/S/2",8],[2024,"C","55/S/3",9]]},{"id":"p0234","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A straight wire of length $1.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ is placed along x-axis, in a region with magnetic field $\\vec{B}=(3\\hat{i}+2\\hat{j})\\ \\mathrm{T}$. A current of $2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$ flows in the wire along $+x$ direction. The magnetic force acting on the wire is :","options":{"A":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{N}$, along z-axis","B":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{N}$, along $-$z-axis","C":"$4.0\\ \\mathrm{N}$, along z-axis","D":"$4.0\\ \\mathrm{N}$, along $-$z-axis"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=I\\vec{L}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$\\vec{F}=2\\,\\hat{i}\\times(3\\hat{i}+2\\hat{j})=4\\hat{k}$ N.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",7]]},{"id":"p0235","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field E associated with an electromagnetic wave is represented by\n$E_y=E_0\\sin(kx-\\omega t)$\nWhich of the following statements is correct ?","options":{"A":"The wave is propagating along $+x$-axis.","B":"The wave is propagating along $+z$-axis.","C":"The magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ of the wave is acting along $+y$-axis.","D":"The magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ of the wave is acting along $-x$-axis."},"answer":"A","hint":"The sign between $kx$ and $\\omega t$ gives direction; $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$ is along propagation.","explanation":"$kx-\\omega t$ means propagation along $+x$; $\\vec{B}$ is perpendicular to both, along $z$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",8],[2024,"C","55/S/3",1]]},{"id":"p0236","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A point object is placed in air at a distance of $4R$ on the principal axis of a convex spherical surface of radius of curvature $R$ separating two mediums, air and glass. As the object is moved towards the surface, the image formed is :","options":{"A":"always real","B":"always virtual","C":"first virtual and then real","D":"first real and then virtual"},"answer":"D","hint":"Apply refraction at a spherical surface and see when the object crosses the first focus.","explanation":"Far away the image is real; once the object is inside the first focal point the image becomes virtual.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",9],[2024,"C","55/S/2",10],[2024,"C","55/S/3",6]]},{"id":"p0237","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is not true for a p-n junction diode under reverse bias ?","options":{"A":"The current is almost independent of the applied voltage.","B":"Holes flow from p-side to n-side.","C":"Electric field in the depletion region increases.","D":"n-side of the junction is connected to $+$ve terminal and p-side to $-$ve terminal of the battery."},"answer":"B","hint":"Think about majority-carrier flow under reverse bias.","explanation":"Majority carrier flow stops under reverse bias; only a small minority-carrier current flows, so B is false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",11],[2024,"C","55/S/2",11]]},{"id":"p0238","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A parallel plate capacitor is charged by a battery. The battery is then disconnected and the plates of the charged capacitor are then moved farther apart. In the process :","options":{"A":"the charge on the capacitor increases.","B":"the potential difference across the plates decreases.","C":"the capacitance of the capacitor increases.","D":"the electrostatic energy stored in the capacitor increases."},"answer":"D","hint":"Charge stays constant when isolated; check how $C$ changes with $d$.","explanation":"$Q$ is fixed, $C\\propto1/d$ decreases, $V=Q/C$ and $U=Q^2/2C$ increase.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",12]]},{"id":"p0239","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The current density ($\\vec{J}$) at a point in a conducting wire is in the direction of electric field ($\\vec{E}$) at that point.\nReason (R): A conducting wire obeys Ohm's law.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Relate $\\vec{J}=\\sigma\\vec{E}$ to the direction of E.","explanation":"A is true and R is true, but the direction of $\\vec{J}$ along $\\vec{E}$ holds for charge-carrier motion in general, not because of Ohm's law.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",13],[2024,"C","55/S/2",15],[2024,"C","55/S/3",14]]},{"id":"p0240","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The torque acting on a current carrying coil is maximum when it is suspended in a radial magnetic field.\nReason (R): The torque tends to rotate the coil on its own axis.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"In a radial field the plane of the coil is always parallel to B.","explanation":"Radial field keeps $\\sin\\theta=1$ so torque is maximum (A true); R is also true but does not explain A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",14],[2024,"C","55/S/2",16],[2024,"C","55/S/3",16]]},{"id":"p0241","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Although the surfaces of a goggle lens are curved, it does not have any power.\nReason (R): In case of goggles, both the curved surfaces are curved on the same side and have equal radii of curvature.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Use the lens maker's formula with $R_1=R_2$ on the same side.","explanation":"With equal radii on the same side $\\frac{1}{R_1}-\\frac{1}{R_2}=0$, so power is zero; R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",15],[2024,"C","55/S/2",13],[2024,"C","55/S/3",13]]},{"id":"p0242","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Nuclear fission reactions are responsible for energy generation in the Sun.\nReason (R): Light nuclei fuse together in the nuclear fission reactions.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Which process powers the Sun?","explanation":"The Sun's energy comes from fusion, not fission, and fusion is what joins light nuclei, so both statements are false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/1",16],[2024,"C","55/S/2",14],[2024,"C","55/S/3",15]]},{"id":"p0243","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The relative magnetic permeability of a medium is $0.075$. Its magnetic susceptibility will be :","options":{"A":"$0.925$","B":"$-0.925$","C":"$1.075$","D":"$-1.075$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\chi=\\mu_r-1$.","explanation":"$\\chi=0.075-1=-0.925$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",1]]},{"id":"p0244","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two circular loops of areas $A$ and $4A$ carry currents $2I$ and $I$ respectively. The magnetic fields at their centres will be in the ratio of :","options":{"A":"$3:1$","B":"$4:1$","C":"$1:1$","D":"$1:2$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Radius scales as the square root of area; $B=\\mu_0I/2R$.","explanation":"Radii are in ratio $1:2$, so $B_1/B_2=\\frac{2I/R}{I/2R}=4$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",2]]},{"id":"p0245","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A conducting sphere of radius R is given a charge Q. Consider three points A, B and C — A at the centre, B at a distance $R/2$ from the centre and C on the surface of the sphere. The electric potentials at these points are such that :","options":{"A":"$V_A=V_B=V_C$","B":"$V_A=V_B\\neq V_C$","C":"$V_A\\neq V_B=V_C$","D":"$V_A\\neq V_B\\neq V_C$"},"answer":"A","hint":"A conductor is an equipotential.","explanation":"Inside and on the surface of a charged conductor the potential is constant.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",3]]},{"id":"p0246","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor is $10\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ when the distance between its plates is $8\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. If the distance between the plates is halved, the capacitance will become :","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$","B":"$15\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$","C":"$20\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$","D":"$40\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$C\\propto 1/d$.","explanation":"Halving $d$ doubles $C$ to $20\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",4]]},{"id":"p0247","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic field of an electromagnetic wave is represented as\n$B_x=B_0\\sin(ky-\\omega t)$. It means that the wave propagation direction and wave vector k are respectively :","options":{"A":"$+z$ axis, $\\frac{2\\pi}{v}$","B":"$-z$ axis, $\\frac{2\\pi}{T}$","C":"$+y$ axis, $\\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda}$","D":"$-y$ axis, $\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"The argument $ky-\\omega t$ fixes direction; $k=2\\pi/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$ky-\\omega t$ means propagation along $+y$ and $k=2\\pi/\\lambda$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",6]]},{"id":"p0248","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An inductor of resistance $5\\ \\Omega$ and self-inductance $25\\ \\mathrm{mH}$ is connected across an ac source of angular frequency $200\\ \\mathrm{rad\\ s^{-1}}$. The phase angle between the voltage and the current is :","options":{"A":"$30^\\circ$","B":"$45^\\circ$","C":"$60^\\circ$","D":"$90^\\circ$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$\\tan\\phi=\\omega L/R$.","explanation":"$\\omega L=200\\times0.025=5\\ \\Omega=R$, so $\\phi=45^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",7]]},{"id":"p0249","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The ionisation energy of the hydrogen atom, in Bohr model, is :","options":{"A":"$-3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$-13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Energy needed to take the electron from the ground state to infinity.","explanation":"Ground state energy is $-13.6$ eV, so ionisation energy is $+13.6$ eV.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",9]]},{"id":"p0250","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"The current sensitivity of a galvanometer does not depend on the :","options":{"A":"magnetic field in which the coil is suspended.","B":"current flowing in the coil.","C":"torsional constant of the spring.","D":"area of the coil."},"answer":"B","hint":"Current sensitivity is deflection per unit current.","explanation":"$\\frac{\\phi}{I}=\\frac{NAB}{k}$, which does not involve the current itself.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/2",12]]},{"id":"p0251","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A capacitor of $5\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ is connected to an ac source of $200\\ \\mathrm{V}$, $\\frac{1000}{\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ through a resistor of $100\\ \\Omega$. The phase difference between the voltage (V) applied and current (I) is :","options":{"A":"$120^\\circ$","B":"$90^\\circ$","C":"$60^\\circ$","D":"$45^\\circ$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\tan\\phi=X_C/R$.","explanation":"$X_C=\\frac{1}{2\\pi fC}=100\\ \\Omega=R$, so $\\phi=45^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",2]]},{"id":"p0252","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"m, $m_n$ and $m_p$ represents masses of $^{A}_{Z}\\mathrm{X}$ nucleus, a neutron and a proton, respectively. Then :","options":{"A":"$m<(A-Z)m_n+Zm_p$","B":"$m=(A-Z)m_n+Zm_p$","C":"$m=(A-Z)m_p+Zm_n$","D":"$m>(A-Z)m_n+Zm_p$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of mass defect.","explanation":"The nuclear mass is less than the sum of its nucleons' masses because of binding energy.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",3]]},{"id":"p0253","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A rod PQ of resistance R lies across frictionless conducting rails in a constant uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$, as shown in the figure. Assuming that the rails have negligible resistance, the force required to pull the rod to the right at constant velocity $\\vec{v}$ is :","options":{"A":"$0$","B":"$Bl v$","C":"$\\frac{Blv}{R}$","D":"$\\frac{B^2l^2v}{R}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Constant velocity means applied force balances magnetic force $BIl$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=Blv$, $I=Blv/R$, so $F=BIl=B^2l^2v/R$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0253.png","sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",4]]},{"id":"p0254","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"When a ferromagnetic substance is heated to a temperature above its Curie temperature, it will :","options":{"A":"behave like a diamagnetic material.","B":"behave like a paramagnetic material.","C":"permanently demagnetise.","D":"remain a ferromagnetic."},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall what Curie temperature signifies.","explanation":"Above the Curie temperature ferromagnetism is lost and the substance becomes paramagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",5]]},{"id":"p0255","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A current of $5\\ \\mathrm{A}$ is flowing in a wire of length $1.5\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. A force of $7.5\\ \\mathrm{mN}$ acts on it when it is placed in a uniform magnetic field of $0.2\\ \\mathrm{T}$. The angle between the magnetic field and the direction of current is :","options":{"A":"$30^\\circ$","B":"$45^\\circ$","C":"$60^\\circ$","D":"$90^\\circ$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $F=BIl\\sin\\theta$.","explanation":"$BIl=0.2\\times5\\times0.015=15$ mN, so $\\sin\\theta=0.5$ and $\\theta=30^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",7]]},{"id":"p0256","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the given graphs correctly represents the variation of photoelectric current with the intensity of incident radiation, keeping other parameters fixed ?","options":{"A":"Current rises with intensity along a curve that bends upward (starting at the origin)","B":"Straight line with a positive intercept on the current axis","C":"Straight line through the origin","D":"Curve rising from the origin and flattening to a saturation value"},"answer":"C","hint":"Photocurrent is proportional to the number of photoelectrons emitted per second.","explanation":"Photocurrent is directly proportional to intensity, giving a straight line through the origin.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0256.png","sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",10]]},{"id":"p0257","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When intrinsic silicon semiconductor is doped with Al atom, then it :","options":{"A":"decreases the number of holes in the conduction band.","B":"increases the number of holes in the valence band.","C":"increases the energy gap value.","D":"increases the number of electrons in the valence band."},"answer":"B","hint":"Aluminium is trivalent.","explanation":"Al is an acceptor impurity, producing p-type silicon with extra holes in the valence band.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",11]]},{"id":"p0258","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two cells of emf $2\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $3\\ \\mathrm{V}$, and internal resistance $1\\ \\Omega$ and $2\\ \\Omega$, respectively are connected in parallel. The effective emf and internal resistance of the combination cell are respectively :","options":{"A":"$3\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ 1\\ \\Omega$","B":"$5\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ 3\\ \\Omega$","C":"$\\frac{7}{3}\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\ \\Omega$","D":"$\\frac{6}{5}\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"C","hint":"For parallel cells $\\varepsilon_{eq}=\\frac{\\varepsilon_1/r_1+\\varepsilon_2/r_2}{1/r_1+1/r_2}$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon_{eq}=\\frac{2+1.5}{1.5}=\\frac{7}{3}$ V and $r_{eq}=\\frac{1\\times2}{1+2}=\\frac{2}{3}\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"C","55/S/3",12]]},{"id":"p0259","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"When a glass rod is rubbed with a silk cloth, it acquires a positive charge of $4.8\\times10^{-17}\\ \\mathrm{C}$. In the process, the number of electrons and protons transferred are respectively :","options":{"A":"0, 300","B":"0, 225","C":"150, 150","D":"300, 0"},"answer":"D","hint":"Only electrons move; $n=q/e$.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{4.8\\times10^{-17}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}=300$ electrons leave the rod; no protons are transferred.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",1]]},{"id":"p0260","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The emf and internal resistance of a cell are $3\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $0.2\\ \\Omega$ respectively. It is connected to an external resistor of $5.8\\ \\Omega$. The potential difference across the cell will be :","options":{"A":"$1.2\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$2.9\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$3.2\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Terminal voltage $V=\\varepsilon-Ir$.","explanation":"$I=3/6=0.5$ A, so $V=3-0.5\\times0.2=2.9$ V.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",2]]},{"id":"p0261","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic field due to a current carrying circular coil at a point far off on its axis at a distance 'r' from the centre of the coil is proportional to :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{r}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{r^2}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{r^{3/2}}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{r^3}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"For $r\\gg R$ the axial field is $\\frac{\\mu_0 \\cdot 2m}{4\\pi r^3}$ (dipole).","explanation":"For $r\\gg R$, $B=\\frac{\\mu_0IR^2}{2r^3}\\propto\\frac{1}{r^3}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",3]]},{"id":"p0262","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic field B within the solenoid of length L having N turns and carrying current I will be :","options":{"A":"$\\mu_0NIL$","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0NI}{L}$","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0NI}{4\\pi L}$","D":"$\\mu_0NI$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Field inside an ideal solenoid is $\\mu_0 n I$.","explanation":"$n=N/L$, so $B=\\mu_0NI/L$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",4]]},{"id":"p0263","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular loop of radius 'a' carries a current 3I through it. Another concentric circular loop of radius 2a carrying a current I is placed perpendicular to the first loop. The dipole moment of the equivalent magnetic dipole for the system is :","options":{"A":"$\\pi Ia^2$","B":"$5Ia$","C":"$5\\pi Ia^2$","D":"$7\\pi Ia^2$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Magnetic moments are vectors; the loops are perpendicular.","explanation":"$m_1=3\\pi Ia^2$, $m_2=4\\pi Ia^2$ at right angles; $m=\\sqrt{m_1^2+m_2^2}=5\\pi Ia^2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",5]]},{"id":"p0264","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux $\\phi$ (in Wb) through a closed loop of resistance $3.0\\ \\Omega$ varies with time $t$ (s) as $\\phi=6t^2+3.0t+5$. The value of the induced current in the loop at $t=1.0\\ \\mathrm{s}$ is :","options":{"A":"$15.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$3.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$4.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$5.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Find $\\varepsilon=|d\\phi/dt|$ then $I=\\varepsilon/R$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=12t+3=15$ V at $t=1$ s, so $I=15/3=5.0$ A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",6]]},{"id":"p0265","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The frequencies of ultraviolet rays, gamma-rays and microwaves are $\\nu_1,\\nu_2$ and $\\nu_3$ respectively. Then :","options":{"A":"$\\nu_1=\\nu_2=\\nu_3$","B":"$\\nu_1>\\nu_2>\\nu_3$","C":"$\\nu_2>\\nu_1>\\nu_3$","D":"$\\nu_3>\\nu_2>\\nu_1$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the order of the EM spectrum by frequency.","explanation":"Frequency order: gamma > ultraviolet > microwaves.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",7]]},{"id":"p0266","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"In the photoelectric effect, electrons are emitted from a given metal surface when the incident radiation has a certain minimum value of :","options":{"A":"frequency","B":"intensity","C":"wavelength","D":"angle of incidence"},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of the threshold condition.","explanation":"Emission requires frequency at least the threshold frequency.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",8]]},{"id":"p0267","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of a hydrogen atom, the increase in the angular momentum of the electron, when it is excited from n = 1 to n = 3 orbit, is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{h}{2\\pi}$","B":"$\\frac{2h}{\\pi}$","C":"$\\frac{3h}{2\\pi}$","D":"$\\frac{h}{\\pi}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Angular momentum is quantised as $nh/2\\pi$.","explanation":"$\\Delta L=(3-1)\\frac{h}{2\\pi}=\\frac{h}{\\pi}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",9]]},{"id":"p0268","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"A positively charged particle of mass m, moving with velocity v is bombarded on a heavy nucleus of charge Ze. Its distance of closest approach from the nucleus is proportional to :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{Z}{mv^2}$","B":"$\\frac{v^2}{Zm}$","C":"$v^2$","D":"$\\frac{Zv^2}{m}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Equate initial kinetic energy to potential energy at closest approach.","explanation":"$\\frac12mv^2=\\frac{kZe\\cdot q}{r_0}$ gives $r_0\\propto\\frac{Z}{mv^2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",10]]},{"id":"p0269","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The effect on width of depletion layer and height of the potential barrier of a p-n junction, when it is forward biased, are respectively :","options":{"A":"decreases, decreases","B":"increases, increases","C":"decreases, increases","D":"increases, decreases"},"answer":"A","hint":"Forward bias opposes the built-in field.","explanation":"Forward bias narrows the depletion layer and lowers the barrier.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",11]]},{"id":"p0270","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The doping of a semiconductor with donor impurity results in :","options":{"A":"formation of a p-type semiconductor","B":"formation of a new energy level just above the filled valence band","C":"decrease in the concentration of electrons","D":"formation of a new energy level just below the conduction band"},"answer":"D","hint":"Donor levels sit close to the conduction band.","explanation":"Donor impurity gives n-type material with a donor level just below the conduction band.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",12]]},{"id":"p0271","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Work functions of different metals are different.\nReason (R): Metals differ in their conductivity.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Work function depends on the metal's surface and electron binding.","explanation":"Both true, but conductivity differences do not explain different work functions.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",13]]},{"id":"p0272","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The capacitive reactance increases with the increase of the frequency ($\\omega$) of applied ac voltage.\nReason (R): The value of capacitive reactance ($X_C$) is $\\omega C$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall $X_C=1/\\omega C$.","explanation":"$X_C=1/\\omega C$ decreases with frequency, so A is false; R is also false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",14]]},{"id":"p0273","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When current is passed through a spring, two adjacent turns move towards each other.\nReason (R): There is a force between any two adjacent turns of a current carrying spring.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Parallel currents attract.","explanation":"Adjacent turns carry parallel currents and attract; this force is the reason for the turns moving together.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",15]]},{"id":"p0274","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Signals through an optical fibre can be transmitted without any appreciable loss of energy.\nReason (R): An optical fibre works on the principle of total internal reflection.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Total internal reflection is lossless in principle.","explanation":"Both true; repeated total internal reflection keeps energy loss negligible.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55(B)",16]]},{"id":"p0275","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A thin plastic rod is bent into a circular ring of radius R. It is uniformly charged with charge density $\\lambda$. The magnitude of the electric field at its centre is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\varepsilon_0R}$","B":"Zero","C":"$\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0R}$","D":"$\\frac{\\lambda}{4\\varepsilon_0R}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use symmetry of diametrically opposite elements.","explanation":"Opposite elements produce equal and opposite fields at the centre, so the net field is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",1],[2024,"R","55/1/2",1],[2024,"R","55/1/3",1]]},{"id":"p0276","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Ten capacitors, each of capacitance $1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$, are connected in parallel to a source of $100\\ \\mathrm{V}$. The total energy stored in the system is equal to :","options":{"A":"$10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$0.5\\times10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$5.0\\times10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Parallel capacitances add; $U=\\frac12CV^2$.","explanation":"$C=10\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$, $U=\\frac12\\times10^{-5}\\times10^4=5\\times10^{-2}$ J.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",2]]},{"id":"p0277","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider the circuit shown in the figure. The potential difference between points A and B is :","options":{"A":"$6\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$8\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$9\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$12\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Two branches in parallel between A and B; use the parallel-cell formula.","explanation":"$V=\\frac{12/1+6/0.5}{1/1+1/0.5}=\\frac{24}{3}=8$ V.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0277.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",3]]},{"id":"p0278","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A loop carrying a current I clockwise is placed in x$-$y plane, in a uniform magnetic field directed along z-axis. The tendency of the loop will be to :","options":{"A":"move along x-axis","B":"move along y-axis","C":"shrink","D":"expand"},"answer":"C","hint":"Find the direction of force on a current element, $I\\,d\\vec{l}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"Net force is zero but each element feels a force pointing toward the centre, so the loop tends to shrink.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",4]]},{"id":"p0279","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A $10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ long wire lies along y-axis. It carries a current of $1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$ in positive y-direction. A magnetic field $\\vec{B}=(5\\ \\mathrm{mT})\\hat{j}-(8\\ \\mathrm{mT})\\hat{k}$ exists in the region. The force on the wire is :","options":{"A":"$(0.8\\ \\mathrm{mN})\\hat{i}$","B":"$-(0.8\\ \\mathrm{mN})\\hat{i}$","C":"$(80\\ \\mathrm{mN})\\hat{i}$","D":"$-(80\\ \\mathrm{mN})\\hat{i}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=I\\vec{L}\\times\\vec{B}$ with $\\hat{j}\\times\\hat{k}=\\hat{i}$.","explanation":"$\\vec{F}=0.1\\,\\hat{j}\\times(-8\\times10^{-3}\\hat{k})=-0.8\\ \\mathrm{mN}\\,\\hat{i}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",5],[2024,"R","55/1/2",5],[2024,"R","55/1/3",6]]},{"id":"p0280","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer of resistance $G\\ \\Omega$ is converted into an ammeter of range $0$ to $I$ A. If the current through the galvanometer is $0.1\\%$ of $I$ A, the resistance of the ammeter is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{G}{999}\\ \\Omega$","B":"$\\frac{G}{1000}\\ \\Omega$","C":"$\\frac{G}{1001}\\ \\Omega$","D":"$\\frac{G}{100\\cdot1}\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Shunt in parallel with the galvanometer; ammeter resistance is their parallel combination.","explanation":"$S=\\frac{G\\,I_g}{I-I_g}=\\frac{G}{999}$, and $R_A=\\frac{GS}{G+S}=\\frac{G}{1000}$ (equivalently $G I_g/I$).","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",6],[2024,"R","55/1/2",6],[2024,"R","55/1/3",5]]},{"id":"p0281","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The reactance of a capacitor of capacitance C connected to an ac source of frequency $\\omega$ is 'X'. If the capacitance of the capacitor is doubled and the frequency of the source is tripled, the reactance will become :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{X}{6}$","B":"$6X$","C":"$\\frac{2}{3}X$","D":"$\\frac{3}{2}X$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$X_C=\\frac{1}{\\omega C}$.","explanation":"The product $\\omega C$ becomes six times larger, so the reactance is $X/6$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",7]]},{"id":"p0282","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In the four regions, I, II, III and IV, the electric fields are described as :\nRegion I : $E_x=E_0\\sin(kz-\\omega t)$\nRegion II : $E_x=E_0$\nRegion III : $E_x=E_0\\sin kz$\nRegion IV : $E_x=E_0\\cos kz$\nThe displacement current will exist in the region :","options":{"A":"I","B":"IV","C":"II","D":"III"},"answer":"A","hint":"Displacement current needs a time-varying electric field.","explanation":"Only region I has an electric field that changes with time, so $\\varepsilon_0\\,d\\Phi_E/dt\\neq0$ there.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",8]]},{"id":"p0283","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The transition of electron that gives rise to the formation of the second spectral line of the Balmer series in the spectrum of hydrogen atom corresponds to :","options":{"A":"$n_f=2$ and $n_i=3$","B":"$n_f=3$ and $n_i=4$","C":"$n_f=2$ and $n_i=4$","D":"$n_f=2$ and $n_i=\\infty$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Balmer series ends on $n=2$; the first line starts from $n=3$.","explanation":"Balmer lines have $n_f=2$; the first is from 3, so the second is from $n_i=4$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",9],[2024,"R","55/1/2",12],[2024,"R","55/1/3",11]]},{"id":"p0284","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Ge is doped with As. Due to doping,","options":{"A":"the structure of Ge lattice is distorted.","B":"the number of conduction electrons increases.","C":"the number of holes increases.","D":"the number of conduction electrons decreases."},"answer":"B","hint":"Arsenic is a pentavalent donor impurity.","explanation":"As donates extra electrons, giving n-type Ge with more conduction electrons.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",10],[2024,"R","55/1/2",11],[2024,"R","55/1/3",12]]},{"id":"p0285","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Two beams, A and B whose photon energies are $3.3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ and $11.3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ respectively, illuminate a metallic surface (work function $2.3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$) successively. The ratio of maximum speed of electrons emitted due to beam A to that due to beam B is :","options":{"A":"$3$","B":"$9$","C":"$\\frac{1}{3}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{9}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$K_{max}=E-\\phi$ and $v\\propto\\sqrt{K}$.","explanation":"$K_A=1$ eV, $K_B=9$ eV, so $v_A/v_B=\\sqrt{1/9}=1/3$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",11],[2024,"R","55/1/2",9],[2024,"R","55/1/3",10]]},{"id":"p0286","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The waves associated with a moving electron and a moving proton have the same wavelength $\\lambda$. It implies that they have the same :","options":{"A":"momentum","B":"angular momentum","C":"speed","D":"energy"},"answer":"A","hint":"de Broglie relation $\\lambda=h/p$.","explanation":"Equal wavelength means equal momentum $p=h/\\lambda$; speed and energy differ because masses differ.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",12],[2024,"R","55/1/2",10],[2024,"R","55/1/3",9]]},{"id":"p0287","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In photoelectric effect, the kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons increases with increase in the intensity of the incident light.\nReason (R): Photoelectric current depends on the wavelength of the incident light.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Does K.E. depend on intensity or on frequency?","explanation":"A is false since the maximum kinetic energy depends on frequency, not intensity; marked D, R treated as false by the scheme.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",13],[2024,"R","55/1/2",15],[2024,"R","55/1/3",16]]},{"id":"p0288","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The mutual inductance between two coils is maximum when the coils are wound on each other.\nReason (R): The flux linkage between two coils is maximum when they are wound on each other.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"$M=N_2\\Phi_2/I_1$.","explanation":"Coaxial coils wound on each other have maximum flux linkage, hence maximum $M$; R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",14],[2024,"R","55/1/2",16],[2024,"R","55/1/3",15]]},{"id":"p0289","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Two long parallel wires, freely suspended and connected in series to a battery, move apart.\nReason (R): Two wires carrying current in opposite directions repel each other.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"In a series connection the currents in the two wires run opposite.","explanation":"Series connection makes the currents antiparallel and antiparallel currents repel; R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",15],[2024,"R","55/1/2",14],[2024,"R","55/1/3",13]]},{"id":"p0290","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Plane and convex mirrors cannot produce real images under any circumstance.\nReason (R): A virtual image cannot serve as an object to produce a real image.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Consider a converging beam falling on a plane mirror.","explanation":"A converging beam acts as a virtual object and gives a real image in a plane or convex mirror, so A is false; R is also false.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/1",16],[2024,"R","55/1/2",13],[2024,"R","55/1/3",14]]},{"id":"p0291","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A charged sphere of radius r has surface charge density $\\sigma$. The electric field on its surface is E. If the radius of the sphere is doubled, keeping charge density the same, the ratio of the electric field on the old sphere to that on the new sphere will be :","options":{"A":"$1$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","D":"$4$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Field at the surface of a conductor is $\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$.","explanation":"$E=\\sigma/\\varepsilon_0$ is independent of radius, so the ratio is 1.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/2",2]]},{"id":"p0292","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A student is asked to connect four cells, each of emf $E$ and internal resistance $r$, in series. But she/he connects one cell wrongly in series with the other cells. The equivalent emf and the equivalent internal resistance of the combination will be :","options":{"A":"$4E$ and $2r$","B":"$4E$ and $3r$","C":"$3E$ and $4r$","D":"$2E$ and $4r$"},"answer":"D","hint":"A reversed cell subtracts its emf but its internal resistance still adds.","explanation":"Net emf $=3E-E=2E$; internal resistances always add, giving $4r$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/2",3]]},{"id":"p0293","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A piece of wire bent in the form of a circular loop A carries a current $I$. The wire is then bent into a circular loop B of two turns and carries the same current. The ratio of magnetic fields at the centre of loop A to that of loop B will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{16}$","B":"$16$","C":"$4$","D":"$\\frac{1}{4}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Same wire length: two turns means half the radius; use $B=N\\mu_0 I/2R$.","explanation":"Loop B has $R/2$ and $N=2$, so $B_B=\\frac{2\\mu_0 I}{2(R/2)}=4B_A$. Ratio $B_A/B_B=\\frac14$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/2",4]]},{"id":"p0294","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A conducting circular loop is placed in a uniform magnetic field $B = 50\\ \\mathrm{mT}$ with its plane perpendicular to the magnetic field. The radius of the loop is made to shrink at a constant rate of $1\\ \\mathrm{mm\\,s^{-1}}$. At the instant the radius of the loop is $4\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, the induced emf in the loop is :","options":{"A":"$\\pi\\ \\mu\\mathrm{V}$","B":"$2\\pi\\ \\mu\\mathrm{V}$","C":"$4\\pi\\ \\mu\\mathrm{V}$","D":"$8\\pi\\ \\mu\\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\varepsilon = B\\,\\frac{dA}{dt}$ with $A=\\pi r^2$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon = B\\cdot 2\\pi r\\,\\frac{dr}{dt}=0.05\\times2\\pi\\times0.04\\times10^{-3}=4\\pi\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/2",7]]},{"id":"p0295","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric and magnetic fields of electromagnetic waves are :","options":{"A":"In the same phase and perpendicular to each other.","B":"In the same phase and not perpendicular to each other.","C":"Not in the same phase but are perpendicular to each other.","D":"Neither in the same phase nor perpendicular to each other."},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the structure of a plane EM wave.","explanation":"$\\vec E$ and $\\vec B$ oscillate in phase and are mutually perpendicular, both perpendicular to propagation.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/2",8]]},{"id":"p0296","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Three small charged spheres X, Y and Z carrying charges $+q$, $-q$ and $+q$ respectively are placed equidistant from each other, as shown in the figure. The spheres Y and Z are held in place. Initially X is also held in place, but is otherwise free to move. When X is released, the path followed by it will be :","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"answer":"B","hint":"Find the net Coulomb force on X initially and see how it changes as X moves.","explanation":"Net force on X starts along A (attraction to Y plus repulsion from Z), but as X moves the attraction to Y grows, so it curves toward Y along path B.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0296.png","note":"Official answer B (curved path towards Y).","sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/3",2]]},{"id":"p0297","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"In a uniform straight wire, conduction electrons move along $+x$ direction. Let $\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{j}$ be the electric field and current density in the wire, respectively. Then :","options":{"A":"$\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{j}$ both are along $+x$ direction.","B":"$\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{j}$ both are along $-x$ direction.","C":"$\\vec{E}$ is along $+x$ direction, but $\\vec{j}$ is along $-x$ direction.","D":"$\\vec{E}$ is along $-x$ direction, but $\\vec{j}$ is along $+x$ direction."},"answer":"B","hint":"Electrons have negative charge; $\\vec j$ is along $\\vec E$ for the drift of charges.","explanation":"Electrons drift along $+x$, so conventional current density is along $-x$; force on electrons is opposite to $\\vec E$, so $\\vec E$ is also along $-x$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/3",3]]},{"id":"p0298","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two charged particles, P and Q, each having charge $q$ but of masses $m_1$ and $m_2$, are accelerated through the same potential difference $V$. They enter a region of magnetic field $\\vec{B}\\ (\\perp \\vec{v})$ and describe the circular paths of radii $a$ and $b$ respectively. Then $\\left(\\frac{m_1}{m_2}\\right)$ is equal to :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{a}{b}$","B":"$\\frac{b}{a}$","C":"$\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)^2$","D":"$\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right)^2$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Combine $qV=\\frac12mv^2$ with $r=mv/qB$.","explanation":"$r=\\frac{1}{B}\\sqrt{\\frac{2mV}{q}}$, so $r^2\\propto m$ and $m_1/m_2=(a/b)^2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/3",4]]},{"id":"p0299","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The primary and secondary coils of a transformer have 500 turns and 5000 turns respectively. The primary coil is connected to an ac source of $220\\ \\mathrm{V} - 50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$. The output across the secondary coil is :","options":{"A":"$220\\ \\mathrm{V} - 50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","B":"$1100\\ \\mathrm{V} - 50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","C":"$2200\\ \\mathrm{V} - 5\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","D":"$2200\\ \\mathrm{V} - 50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Voltage scales with turns ratio; frequency is unchanged.","explanation":"$V_s=220\\times\\frac{5000}{500}=2200\\ \\mathrm{V}$, frequency stays $50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/3",7]]},{"id":"p0300","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The first scientist who produced and observed electromagnetic waves of wavelengths in the range $25\\ \\mathrm{mm} - 5\\ \\mathrm{mm}$ was :","options":{"A":"J.C. Maxwell","B":"H.R. Hertz","C":"J.C. Bose","D":"G. Marconi"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall who produced millimetre waves in 1895.","explanation":"J.C. Bose produced and observed EM waves of about 5 mm to 25 mm wavelength.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/1/3",8]]},{"id":"p0301","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two charged particles P and Q, having the same charge but different masses $m_P$ and $m_Q$, start from rest and travel equal distances in a uniform electric field $\\vec{E}$ in time $t_P$ and $t_Q$ respectively. Neglecting the effect of gravity, the ratio $\\left(\\frac{t_P}{t_Q}\\right)$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{m_P}{m_Q}$","B":"$\\frac{m_Q}{m_P}$","C":"$\\sqrt{\\frac{m_P}{m_Q}}$","D":"$\\sqrt{\\frac{m_Q}{m_P}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $d=\\frac12at^2$ with $a=qE/m$.","explanation":"$t=\\sqrt{2dm/qE}\\propto\\sqrt m$, so $t_P/t_Q=\\sqrt{m_P/m_Q}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",1]]},{"id":"p0302","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Electrons drift with speed $v_d$ in a conductor with potential difference $V$ across its ends. If $V$ is reduced to $\\left(\\frac{V}{2}\\right)$, their drift speed will become :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{v_d}{2}$","B":"$v_d$","C":"$2v_d$","D":"$4v_d$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Drift speed is proportional to the electric field, which is $V/l$.","explanation":"$v_d=\\frac{eE\\tau}{m}\\propto V$, so halving $V$ halves $v_d$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",2],[2024,"R","55/2/2",2],[2024,"R","55/2/3",2]]},{"id":"p0303","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A wire of length $4{\\cdot}4\\ \\mathrm{m}$ is bent round in the shape of a circular loop and carries a current of $1{\\cdot}0\\ \\mathrm{A}$. The magnetic moment of the loop will be :","options":{"A":"$0{\\cdot}7\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","B":"$1{\\cdot}54\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","C":"$2{\\cdot}10\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","D":"$3{\\cdot}5\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Get the radius from the circumference, then use $m=IA$.","explanation":"$2\\pi r=4.4\\Rightarrow r=0.7\\ \\mathrm{m}$; $m=I\\pi r^2=\\pi(0.49)\\approx1.54\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",3]]},{"id":"p0304","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular coil of radius $10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ is placed in a magnetic field $\\vec{B} = (1{\\cdot}0\\,\\hat{i} + 0{\\cdot}5\\,\\hat{j})\\ \\mathrm{mT}$ such that the outward unit vector normal to the surface of the coil is $(0{\\cdot}6\\,\\hat{i} + 0{\\cdot}8\\,\\hat{j})$. The magnetic flux linked with the coil is :","options":{"A":"$0{\\cdot}314\\ \\mu\\mathrm{Wb}$","B":"$3{\\cdot}14\\ \\mu\\mathrm{Wb}$","C":"$31{\\cdot}4\\ \\mu\\mathrm{Wb}$","D":"$1{\\cdot}256\\ \\mu\\mathrm{Wb}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Flux is $\\vec B\\cdot\\vec A$ with $\\vec A=A\\hat n$.","explanation":"$\\vec B\\cdot\\hat n=(0.6+0.4)\\ \\mathrm{mT}=1\\ \\mathrm{mT}$; $\\phi=10^{-3}\\times\\pi(0.1)^2=31.4\\ \\mu\\mathrm{Wb}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",4],[2024,"R","55/2/2",4],[2024,"R","55/2/3",4]]},{"id":"p0305","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following quantity/quantities remains same in primary and secondary coils of an ideal transformer ?\nCurrent, Voltage, Power, Magnetic flux","options":{"A":"Current only","B":"Voltage only","C":"Power only","D":"Magnetic flux and Power both"},"answer":"D","hint":"Ideal transformer: no flux leakage and no losses.","explanation":"Same core flux links both coils, and $P_{in}=P_{out}$; voltage and current change with turns ratio.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",5],[2024,"R","55/2/2",5],[2024,"R","55/2/3",5]]},{"id":"p0306","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A resistor and an ideal inductor are connected in series to a $100\\sqrt{2}\\ \\mathrm{V}$, $50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ ac source. When a voltmeter is connected across the resistor or the inductor, it shows the same reading. The reading of the voltmeter is :","options":{"A":"$100\\sqrt{2}\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$100\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$50\\sqrt{2}\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$50\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$V_R$ and $V_L$ are in quadrature, so $V^2=V_R^2+V_L^2$.","explanation":"With $V_R=V_L=V'$, $\\sqrt{2}V'=100\\sqrt2$, so $V'=100\\ \\mathrm{V}$ (source value is rms).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",6]]},{"id":"p0307","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Electromagnetic waves with wavelength $10\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ are called :","options":{"A":"Infrared waves","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"Gamma rays","D":"X-rays"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall the EM spectrum wavelength ranges.","explanation":"Wavelength of $10\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ falls in the ultraviolet range as per NCERT ($\\approx 400$ nm to 1 nm).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",7]]},{"id":"p0308","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The work function for a photosensitive surface is $3{\\cdot}315\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The cut-off wavelength for photoemission of electrons from this surface is :","options":{"A":"$150\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$200\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$375\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$500\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Threshold wavelength $\\lambda_0=hc/\\phi_0$.","explanation":"$\\lambda_0=\\frac{1240\\ \\mathrm{eV\\,nm}}{3.315\\ \\mathrm{eV}}\\approx375\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",8]]},{"id":"p0309","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Energy levels A, B and C of an atom correspond to increasing values of energy i.e. $E_A < E_B < E_C$. Let $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$ and $\\lambda_3$ be the wavelengths of radiation corresponding to the transitions C to B, B to A and C to A, respectively. The correct relation between $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$ and $\\lambda_3$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\lambda_1^2+\\lambda_2^2=\\lambda_3^2$","B":"$\\frac{1}{\\lambda_1}+\\frac{1}{\\lambda_2}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda_3}$","C":"$\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2+\\lambda_3=0$","D":"$\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Photon energy is $hc/\\lambda$ and energy differences add.","explanation":"$E_C-E_A=(E_C-E_B)+(E_B-E_A)$ gives $\\frac{1}{\\lambda_3}=\\frac{1}{\\lambda_1}+\\frac{1}{\\lambda_2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",9],[2024,"R","55/2/2",9],[2024,"R","55/2/3",10]]},{"id":"p0310","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"An alpha particle approaches a gold nucleus in Geiger-Marsden experiment with kinetic energy $K$. It momentarily stops at a distance $d$ from the nucleus and reverses its direction. Then $d$ is proportional to :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{K}}$","B":"$\\sqrt{K}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{K}$","D":"$K$"},"answer":"C","hint":"At closest approach all kinetic energy becomes electrostatic potential energy.","explanation":"$K=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{(2e)(79e)}{d}$, so $d\\propto\\frac1K$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",10],[2024,"R","55/2/2",10],[2024,"R","55/2/3",12]]},{"id":"p0311","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"An n-type semiconducting Si is obtained by doping intrinsic Si with :","options":{"A":"Al","B":"B","C":"P","D":"In"},"answer":"C","hint":"n-type needs a pentavalent donor.","explanation":"Phosphorus (group 15) is pentavalent and gives n-type Si; Al, B, In are trivalent.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",11],[2024,"R","55/2/2",11],[2024,"R","55/2/3",9]]},{"id":"p0312","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When a p-n junction diode is subjected to reverse biasing :","options":{"A":"the barrier height decreases and the depletion region widens.","B":"the barrier height increases and the depletion region widens.","C":"the barrier height decreases and the depletion region shrinks.","D":"the barrier height increases and the depletion region shrinks."},"answer":"B","hint":"Reverse bias adds to the built-in potential.","explanation":"Reverse bias raises the barrier and widens the depletion region.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",12],[2024,"R","55/2/2",12],[2024,"R","55/2/3",11]]},{"id":"p0313","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Photoelectric current increases with an increase in intensity of incident radiation, for a given frequency of incident radiation and the accelerating potential.\nReason (R): Increase in the intensity of incident radiation results in an increase in the number of photoelectrons emitted per second and hence an increase in the photocurrent.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Intensity is proportional to photons per second.","explanation":"Both true; more photons per second eject more photoelectrons per second, so R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",13],[2024,"R","55/2/2",16],[2024,"R","55/2/3",16]]},{"id":"p0314","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Lenz's law is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy.\nReason (R): There is no power loss in an ideal inductor.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Check whether R actually justifies Lenz's law.","explanation":"A is true and R is true, but R concerns average power in an ideal inductor and does not explain Lenz's law.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",14],[2024,"R","55/2/2",15],[2024,"R","55/2/3",15]]},{"id":"p0315","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): An electron and a proton enter with the same momentum $\\vec{p}$ in a magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ such that $\\vec{p}\\perp\\vec{B}$. Then both describe a circular path of the same radius.\nReason (R): The radius of the circular path described by the charged particle (charge $q$, mass $m$) moving in the magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is given by $r=\\frac{mv}{qB}$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Write $r=p/(qB)$.","explanation":"$r=\\frac{p}{|q|B}$ is the same for equal $p$ and equal $|q|$; R gives this directly.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",15],[2024,"R","55/2/2",14],[2024,"R","55/2/3",13]]},{"id":"p0316","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The magnifying power of a compound microscope is negative.\nReason (R): The final image formed is erect with respect to the object.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Negative magnification means an inverted image.","explanation":"A is true (final image is inverted), but R is false since the image is inverted, not erect.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/1",16],[2024,"R","55/2/2",13],[2024,"R","55/2/3",14]]},{"id":"p0317","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The capacitance of a parallel plate capacitor having a medium of dielectric constant $K = 4$ in between the plates is $C$. If this medium is removed, then the capacitance of the capacitor becomes :","options":{"A":"$4C$","B":"$C$","C":"$\\frac{C}{4}$","D":"$2C$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$C=KC_0$.","explanation":"Removing the dielectric divides the capacitance by $K=4$, giving $C/4$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/2",1]]},{"id":"p0318","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A conducting loop is placed in a magnetic field, normal to its plane. The magnitude of the magnetic field varies with time as shown in the figure. If $\\varepsilon_1$, $\\varepsilon_2$ and $\\varepsilon_3$ are magnitudes of induced emfs during periods $0 \\le t \\le T$, $T \\le t \\le 2T$ and $2T \\le t \\le 3T$, then :","options":{"A":"$\\varepsilon_1>\\varepsilon_2>\\varepsilon_3$","B":"$\\varepsilon_2>\\varepsilon_3>\\varepsilon_1$","C":"$\\varepsilon_3>\\varepsilon_1>\\varepsilon_2$","D":"$\\varepsilon_1>\\varepsilon_3>\\varepsilon_2$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Induced emf magnitude is $A\\,|dB/dt|$, the slope of the graph.","explanation":"Slope is steepest in $0$ to $T$, zero in $T$ to $2T$, and smaller non-zero in $2T$ to $3T$, so $\\varepsilon_1>\\varepsilon_3>\\varepsilon_2$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0318.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/2",3]]},{"id":"p0319","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A series LCR circuit ($L = 2\\ \\mathrm{mH}$, $C = 0{\\cdot}2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ and $R = 30\\ \\Omega$) is connected to an ac source of variable frequency. The impedance of this circuit will be minimum at a frequency of :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{10^5}{4\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","B":"$\\frac{10^5}{2\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","C":"$\\frac{10^4}{4\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","D":"$\\frac{10^4}{2\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Impedance is minimum at resonance, $f=\\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{LC}}$.","explanation":"$\\sqrt{LC}=\\sqrt{4\\times10^{-10}}=2\\times10^{-5}$, so $f=\\frac{10^5}{4\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/2",6]]},{"id":"p0320","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Welders wear special glass goggles or face masks with glass windows to protect their eyes from radiations produced by welding arcs. These radiations are :","options":{"A":"X-rays","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"Infrared waves","D":"Gamma rays"},"answer":"B","hint":"Glass absorbs the harmful radiation from arcs.","explanation":"Welding arcs emit intense ultraviolet radiation, which glass goggles absorb.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/2",7]]},{"id":"p0321","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A photosensitive surface has a work function of $2{\\cdot}00\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The maximum kinetic energy of electrons ejected from this surface by radiation of wavelength $300\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ is :","options":{"A":"$0{\\cdot}54\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$1{\\cdot}07\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$1{\\cdot}61\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$2{\\cdot}14\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Einstein's equation $K_{max}=hc/\\lambda-\\phi_0$.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{1240}{300}=4.14\\ \\mathrm{eV}$; $K_{max}=4.14-2.00=2.14\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/2",8]]},{"id":"p0322","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Coulomb force $F$ versus $\\left(\\frac{1}{r^2}\\right)$ graphs for two pairs of point charges ($q_1$ and $q_2$) and ($q_2$ and $q_3$) are shown in the figure. The ratio of charges $\\left(\\frac{q_1}{q_3}\\right)$ is :","options":{"A":"$\\sqrt{3}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","C":"$3$","D":"$\\frac{1}{3}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Slope of $F$ versus $1/r^2$ is $kq_aq_b$.","explanation":"Slopes: $kq_1q_2\\propto\\tan30^\\circ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$ and $kq_2q_3\\propto\\tan60^\\circ=\\sqrt3$ (magnitudes), so $|q_1/q_3|=\\frac13$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0322.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/3",1]]},{"id":"p0323","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The emf induced in a coil rotating in a magnetic field does not depend upon the following :","options":{"A":"Area of the coil","B":"Resistance of the coil","C":"Number of turns in the coil","D":"Angular speed of rotation of the coil"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall $\\varepsilon=NBA\\omega\\sin\\omega t$.","explanation":"Induced emf depends on $N$, $B$, $A$ and $\\omega$, not on the resistance of the coil.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/3",3]]},{"id":"p0324","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In an ac circuit, the instantaneous values of current (in A) and voltage (in V) are $I = 5\\sin\\omega t$ and $E = 200\\cos\\left(\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ respectively. The phase difference between voltage and current at any instant is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$","B":"$\\frac{5\\pi}{4}$","C":"$\\frac{5\\pi}{2}$","D":"$\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Convert the cosine to a sine before comparing phases.","explanation":"$E=200\\sin(\\omega t+\\frac{\\pi}{3}+\\frac{\\pi}{2})=200\\sin(\\omega t+\\frac{5\\pi}{6})$, so phase difference is $\\frac{5\\pi}{6}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/3",6]]},{"id":"p0325","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In four regions I, II, III and IV, the magnetic field is given by :\nI. $B_y = B_0\\sin kz$\nII. $B_y = B_0\\cos kz$\nIII. $B_y = B_0\\sin(kz-\\omega t)$\nIV. $B_y = B_0\\sin kz + B_0\\cos kz$\nThe electromagnetic wave will exist in the region :","options":{"A":"IV","B":"I","C":"III","D":"II"},"answer":"C","hint":"A travelling wave depends on both position and time through $kz-\\omega t$.","explanation":"Only region III is a function of both $z$ and $t$ as $kz-\\omega t$, i.e. a propagating wave.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/3",7]]},{"id":"p0326","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The momentum (in SI units) associated with a photon of energy $1{\\cdot}5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ is :","options":{"A":"$4\\times10^{-27}$","B":"$8\\times10^{-28}$","C":"$2\\times10^{-30}$","D":"$6\\times10^{-29}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Photon momentum $p=E/c$.","explanation":"$p=\\frac{1.5\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}}{3\\times10^{8}}=8\\times10^{-28}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/2/3",8]]},{"id":"p0327","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider a group of charges $q_1, q_2, q_3, \\ldots$ such that $\\Sigma q \\neq 0$. Then equipotentials at a large distance, due to this group are approximately :","options":{"A":"Plane","B":"Spherical surface","C":"Paraboloidal surface","D":"Ellipsoidal surface"},"answer":"B","hint":"At large distance the group looks like a point charge $\\Sigma q$.","explanation":"Far away the potential is $\\approx\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{\\Sigma q}{r}$, so equipotentials are spheres.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",1]]},{"id":"p0328","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A proton is taken from point $P_1$ to point $P_2$, both located in an electric field. The potentials at points $P_1$ and $P_2$ are $-5\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $+5\\ \\mathrm{V}$ respectively. Assuming that kinetic energies of the proton at points $P_1$ and $P_2$ are zero, the work done on the proton is :","options":{"A":"$-1{\\cdot}6\\times10^{-18}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$1{\\cdot}6\\times10^{-18}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"Zero","D":"$0{\\cdot}8\\times10^{-18}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Work done on the charge (by external agent) equals $q\\Delta V$ when KE is unchanged.","explanation":"$W=q(V_2-V_1)=1.6\\times10^{-19}\\times10=1.6\\times10^{-18}\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",2],[2024,"R","55/3/2",2],[2024,"R","55/3/3",2]]},{"id":"p0329","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A $2{\\cdot}0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ segment of wire, carrying $5{\\cdot}0\\ \\mathrm{A}$ current in positive $y$-direction lies along $y$-axis, as shown in the figure. The magnetic field at a point $(3\\ \\mathrm{m}, 4\\ \\mathrm{m}, 0)$ due to this segment (part of a circuit) is :","options":{"A":"$(0{\\cdot}12\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{j}$","B":"$-(0{\\cdot}10\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{j}$","C":"$-(0{\\cdot}24\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{k}$","D":"$(0{\\cdot}24\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{k}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use Biot-Savart: $d\\vec B=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{I\\,d\\vec l\\times\\vec r}{r^3}$.","explanation":"$d\\vec l\\times\\vec r\\propto\\hat j\\times(3\\hat i+4\\hat j)=-3\\hat k$; $B=10^{-7}\\times5\\times0.02\\times3/125=0.24\\ \\mathrm{nT}$ along $-\\hat k$.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0329.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",3],[2024,"R","55/3/2",3],[2024,"R","55/3/3",3]]},{"id":"p0330","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular loop of wire, carrying a current 'I' is lying in $xy$-plane with its centre coinciding with the origin. It is subjected to a uniform magnetic field pointing along $+z$-axis. The loop will :","options":{"A":"move along $x$-axis","B":"move along $-y$-axis","C":"move along $z$-axis","D":"remain stationary"},"answer":"D","hint":"Check net force and net torque on a loop in a uniform field.","explanation":"Uniform field gives zero net force; magnetic moment is parallel to $\\vec B$ so torque is zero; the loop stays stationary.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",4]]},{"id":"p0331","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A current carrying circular loop of magnetic moment $\\vec{M}$ is suspended in a vertical plane in an external magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ such that its plane is normal to $\\vec{B}$. The work done in rotating this loop by $45^\\circ$ about an axis perpendicular to $\\vec{B}$ is closest to :","options":{"A":"$-0{\\cdot}3\\ MB$","B":"$0{\\cdot}3\\ MB$","C":"$-1{\\cdot}7\\ MB$","D":"$1{\\cdot}7\\ MB$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Work equals change in $U=-MB\\cos\\theta$.","explanation":"$W=MB(\\cos0^\\circ-\\cos45^\\circ)=MB(1-0.707)\\approx0.3\\,MB$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",5],[2024,"R","55/3/2",5],[2024,"R","55/3/3",5]]},{"id":"p0332","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The current in a coil of $15\\ \\mathrm{mH}$ increases uniformly from zero to $4\\ \\mathrm{A}$ in $0{\\cdot}004\\ \\mathrm{s}$. The emf induced in the coil will be :","options":{"A":"$22{\\cdot}5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$17{\\cdot}5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$15{\\cdot}0\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$12{\\cdot}5\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\varepsilon=L\\,|dI/dt|$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=15\\times10^{-3}\\times\\frac{4}{0.004}=15\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",6]]},{"id":"p0333","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider a solenoid of length $l$ and area of cross-section $A$ with fixed number of turns. The self-inductance of the solenoid will increase if :","options":{"A":"both $l$ and $A$ are increased","B":"$l$ is decreased and $A$ is increased","C":"$l$ is increased and $A$ is decreased","D":"both $l$ and $A$ are decreased"},"answer":"B","hint":"$L=\\mu_0N^2A/l$.","explanation":"$L\\propto A/l$, so it increases when $A$ increases and $l$ decreases.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",7],[2024,"R","55/3/2",7],[2024,"R","55/3/3",7]]},{"id":"p0334","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following has the highest frequency ?","options":{"A":"Infrared rays","B":"Gamma rays","C":"Radio waves","D":"Microwaves"},"answer":"B","hint":"Order the EM spectrum by frequency.","explanation":"Gamma rays lie at the highest-frequency end of the spectrum.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",8]]},{"id":"p0335","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"A proton and an alpha particle having equal velocities approach a target nucleus. They come momentarily to rest and then reverse their directions. The ratio of the distance of closest approach of the proton to that of the alpha particle will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","B":"$2$","C":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","D":"$4$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$d\\propto\\frac{q}{mv^2}$ from energy conservation at closest approach.","explanation":"$d=\\frac{Zq e}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 mv^2}\\propto\\frac{q}{m}$; proton $1$, alpha $\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac12$, so ratio is $2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",9],[2024,"R","55/3/2",9],[2024,"R","55/3/3",9]]},{"id":"p0336","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following is the correct graph between the maximum kinetic energy ($K_m$) of the emitted photoelectrons and the frequency of incident radiation ($\\nu$) for a given photosensitive surface ?","options":{"A":"Graph A","B":"Graph B","C":"Graph C","D":"Graph D"},"answer":"C","hint":"$K_m=h\\nu-\\phi_0$ is linear with a threshold frequency.","explanation":"$K_m=h\\nu-\\phi_0$: zero below threshold, then a straight line of positive slope, as in graph C.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0336.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",10]]},{"id":"p0337","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron makes a transition from $n = 2$ level to $n = 1$ level in the Bohr model of a hydrogen atom. Its period of revolution :","options":{"A":"increases by $87{\\cdot}5\\%$","B":"decreases by $87{\\cdot}5\\%$","C":"increases by $43{\\cdot}75\\%$","D":"decreases by $43{\\cdot}75\\%$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Period of revolution scales as $n^3$.","explanation":"$T\\propto n^3$, so $T_1/T_2=1/8$: a decrease of $87.5\\%$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",11],[2024,"R","55/3/2",11],[2024,"R","55/3/3",11]]},{"id":"p0338","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Si is doped with a pentavalent element. The energy required to set the additional electron free is about :","options":{"A":"$0.01\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$0.05\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$0.72\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$1.1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Donor level lies very close to the conduction band.","explanation":"The donor energy level is about 0.05 eV below the conduction band in Si, so about 0.05 eV frees the extra electron.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",12],[2024,"R","55/3/2",12],[2024,"R","55/3/3",12]]},{"id":"p0339","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a semiconductor, the electrons in the conduction band have lesser energy than those in the valence band.\nReason (R): Donor energy level is just above the valence band in a semiconductor.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare the energy ordering of the conduction and valence bands.","explanation":"Conduction band electrons have higher energy, so A is false. The donor level is just below the conduction band, so R is false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",13],[2024,"R","55/3/2",13],[2024,"R","55/3/3",13]]},{"id":"p0340","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Photoelectric effect demonstrates the particle nature of light.\nReason (R): Photoelectric current is proportional to frequency of incident radiation.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Photocurrent depends on intensity, not frequency.","explanation":"A is true (photon picture). Photocurrent is proportional to intensity, not frequency, so R is false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",14],[2024,"R","55/3/2",14],[2024,"R","55/3/3",14]]},{"id":"p0341","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A proton and an electron enter a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ with the same momentum $\\vec{p}$ such that $\\vec{p}$ is perpendicular to $\\vec{B}$. They describe circular paths of the same radius.\nReason (R): In a magnetic field, orbital radius $r$ is equal to $\\frac{p}{qB}$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $r=p/(qB)$.","explanation":"Since $r=p/(qB)$ and $p$, $|q|$, $B$ are the same, the radii are equal. R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",15],[2024,"R","55/3/2",15],[2024,"R","55/3/3",15]]},{"id":"p0342","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A convex lens, when immersed in a liquid, disappears.\nReason (R): The refractive indices of material of the lens and the liquid are equal.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of the lensmaker's formula when $\\mu_{lens}=\\mu_{liquid}$.","explanation":"If the indices are equal, there is no refraction and the focal length is infinite, so the lens becomes invisible. R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/1",16],[2024,"R","55/3/2",16],[2024,"R","55/3/3",16]]},{"id":"p0343","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An isolated conductor, with a cavity, has a net charge $+Q$. A point charge $+q$ is inside the cavity. The charges on the cavity wall and the outer surface are respectively :","options":{"A":"$0$ and $Q$","B":"$-q$ and $Q-q$","C":"$-q$ and $Q+q$","D":"$0$ and $Q-q$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Induced charge on the cavity wall cancels the enclosed charge.","explanation":"Cavity wall carries $-q$ (Gauss's law); the total charge $Q$ stays on the conductor, so the outer surface has $Q+q$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/2",1]]},{"id":"p0344","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two thin long parallel wires separated by a distance 'a' carry current 'I' in opposite directions. The wires will :","options":{"A":"Repel each other with a force $\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi a^2}$, per unit length.","B":"Attract each other with a force $\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi a^2}$, per unit length.","C":"Attract each other with a force $\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi a}$, per unit length.","D":"Repel each other with a force $\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi a}$, per unit length."},"answer":"D","hint":"Antiparallel currents repel; use $F/l=\\mu_0 I_1I_2/2\\pi a$.","explanation":"Opposite currents repel with $F/l=\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi a}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/2",4]]},{"id":"p0345","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The mutual inductance of two coils $C_1$ and $C_2$ is $20\\ \\mathrm{mH}$. In coil $C_1$, the current changes from $4\\ \\mathrm{A}$ to zero in $0.2\\ \\mathrm{s}$. If the resistance of coil $C_2$ is $4\\ \\Omega$, then the charge that flows through it per second will be :","options":{"A":"$4.0\\ \\mathrm{C}$","B":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{C}$","C":"$0.05\\ \\mathrm{C}$","D":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{C}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"emf $=M\\,\\Delta I/\\Delta t$, current $=\\mathrm{emf}/R$.","explanation":"emf $=20\\times10^{-3}\\times4/0.2=0.4\\ \\mathrm{V}$; $I=0.4/4=0.1\\ \\mathrm{A}$, so 0.1 C flows per second.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/2",6]]},{"id":"p0346","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Electromagnetic waves with frequency $1.0\\times10^{18}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ are known as :","options":{"A":"Infrared rays","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"X-rays","D":"Gamma rays"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the frequency ranges of the EM spectrum.","explanation":"Frequencies around $10^{18}$ Hz lie in the X-ray band.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/2",8]]},{"id":"p0347","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows the variation of stopping potential $V_0$ with frequency $\\nu$ of incident radiation, for three materials $M_1$, $M_2$ and $M_3$ with work functions $\\phi_1$, $\\phi_2$ and $\\phi_3$ respectively. Then :","options":{"A":"$\\phi_1>\\phi_2>\\phi_3$","B":"$\\phi_2>\\phi_3>\\phi_1$","C":"$\\phi_3>\\phi_2>\\phi_1$","D":"$\\phi_2>\\phi_1>\\phi_3$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Work function $=h\\nu_0$, read the threshold frequency intercepts.","explanation":"Threshold frequency increases from $M_1$ to $M_3$, so $\\phi_3>\\phi_2>\\phi_1$.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0347.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/2",10]]},{"id":"p0348","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The plates $P_1$ and $P_2$ of a $2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ capacitor are at potentials $25\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $-25\\ \\mathrm{V}$ respectively. The charge on plate $P_1$ will be :","options":{"A":"$0.02\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","B":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","C":"$0.1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$","D":"$1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $Q=C\\,\\Delta V$ with the potential difference.","explanation":"$\\Delta V=50\\ \\mathrm{V}$, so $Q=2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}\\times50=100\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}=0.1\\ \\mathrm{mC}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/3",1]]},{"id":"p0349","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following is a diamagnetic substance ?","options":{"A":"Gadolinium","B":"Sodium","C":"Copper chloride","D":"Sodium chloride"},"answer":"D","hint":"Diamagnets have no unpaired electron spins.","explanation":"Gadolinium is ferromagnetic; sodium and copper chloride are paramagnetic; sodium chloride is diamagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/3",4]]},{"id":"p0350","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The average value of the alternating voltage $v=(157\\ \\mathrm{V})\\sin\\omega t$ over its first half-cycle is :","options":{"A":"$157\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$\\frac{157}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$78.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$100\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Half-cycle average is $2V_0/\\pi$.","explanation":"$V_{avg}=2V_0/\\pi=2\\times157/3.14=100\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/3",6]]},{"id":"p0351","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"A plane electromagnetic wave is travelling in air in $+x$ direction. At a particular moment, its electric field $\\vec{E}$ is along $+y$ direction. At that moment, the magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is along :","options":{"A":"$+z$ direction and in phase with $\\vec{E}$","B":"$-z$ direction and in phase with $\\vec{E}$","C":"$+z$ direction and out of phase with $\\vec{E}$","D":"$-z$ direction and out of phase with $\\vec{E}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Propagation direction is along $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$\\hat{y}\\times\\hat{z}=\\hat{x}$, so $\\vec{B}$ is along $+z$; $E$ and $B$ are always in phase.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/3",8]]},{"id":"p0352","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The wavelength of matter wave associated with an electron of kinetic energy $K$ is $\\lambda$. If the kinetic energy of the electron is doubled, the associated wavelength becomes :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\lambda}{\\sqrt{2}}$","B":"$\\frac{\\lambda}{2}$","C":"$\\sqrt{2}\\,\\lambda$","D":"$2\\lambda$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$\\lambda=h/\\sqrt{2mK}$.","explanation":"$\\lambda\\propto K^{-1/2}$, so doubling $K$ gives $\\lambda/\\sqrt{2}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/3/3",10]]},{"id":"p0353","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Two charges $+q$ each are kept '$2a$' distance apart. A third charge $-2q$ is placed midway between them. The potential energy of the system is -","options":{"A":"$\\frac{q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$","B":"$-\\frac{6q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$","C":"$\\frac{-7q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$","D":"$\\frac{9q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Add the pairwise potential energies of all three pairs.","explanation":"$U=\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0(2a)}-2\\cdot\\frac{2q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}=\\frac{-7q^2}{8\\pi\\varepsilon_0 a}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",1]]},{"id":"p0354","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two identical small conducting balls $B_1$ and $B_2$ are given $-7\\ \\mathrm{pC}$ and $+4\\ \\mathrm{pC}$ charges respectively. They are brought in contact with a third identical ball $B_3$ and then separated. If the final charge on each ball is $-2\\ \\mathrm{pC}$, the initial charge on $B_3$ was","options":{"A":"$-2\\ \\mathrm{pC}$","B":"$-3\\ \\mathrm{pC}$","C":"$-5\\ \\mathrm{pC}$","D":"$-15\\ \\mathrm{pC}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Total charge is conserved and shared equally.","explanation":"$(-7+4+x)/3=-2$ gives $x=-3\\ \\mathrm{pC}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",2]]},{"id":"p0355","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The quantum nature of light explains the observations on photoelectric effect as -","options":{"A":"there is a minimum frequency of incident radiation below which no electrons are emitted.","B":"the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons depends only on the frequency of incident radiation.","C":"when the metal surface is illuminated, electrons are ejected from the surface after sometime.","D":"the photoelectric current is independent of the intensity of incident radiation."},"answer":"A","hint":"Photon energy must exceed the work function.","explanation":"Official answer is A (threshold frequency). B is also a true consequence of $K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi$, but the scheme gives A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",3],[2024,"R","55/4/2",6],[2024,"R","55/4/3",10]]},{"id":"p0356","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The radius ($r_n$) of $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit in Bohr model of hydrogen atom varies with $n$ as","options":{"A":"$r_n\\propto n$","B":"$r_n\\propto\\frac{1}{n}$","C":"$r_n\\propto n^2$","D":"$r_n\\propto\\frac{1}{n^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Bohr radius formula.","explanation":"$r_n=n^2 a_0$, so $r_n\\propto n^2$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",4],[2024,"R","55/4/2",8],[2024,"R","55/4/3",12]]},{"id":"p0357","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A straight wire is kept horizontally along east-west direction. If a steady current flows in wire from east to west, the magnetic field at a point above the wire will point towards","options":{"A":"East","B":"West","C":"North","D":"South"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use the right-hand thumb rule.","explanation":"Current towards west, point above: $\\hat{(-x)}\\times\\hat{z}=+\\hat{y}$, i.e. north.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",5]]},{"id":"p0358","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic susceptibility for a diamagnetic material is","options":{"A":"small and negative","B":"small and positive","C":"large and negative","D":"large and positive"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall susceptibility values of diamagnets.","explanation":"Diamagnetic materials have small negative susceptibility.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",6],[2024,"R","55/4/2",3],[2024,"R","55/4/3",11]]},{"id":"p0359","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer of resistance $100\\ \\Omega$ is converted into an ammeter of range $(0-1\\ \\mathrm{A})$ using a resistance of $0.1\\ \\Omega$. The ammeter will show full scale deflection for a current of about","options":{"A":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","B":"$1\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","C":"$10\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","D":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Shunt: $I_gG=(I-I_g)S$.","explanation":"$I_g\\times100=(1-I_g)\\times0.1$ gives $I_g\\approx1\\ \\mathrm{mA}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",7],[2024,"R","55/4/2",10],[2024,"R","55/4/3",8],[2026,"R","55/2/1",7]]},{"id":"p0360","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular loop A of radius $R$ carries a current $I$. Another circular loop B of radius $r\\left(=\\frac{R}{20}\\right)$ is placed concentrically in the plane of A. The magnetic flux linked with loop B is proportional to","options":{"A":"$R$","B":"$\\sqrt{R}$","C":"$R^{3/2}$","D":"$R^2$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Field at centre of A times area of B.","explanation":"$B=\\mu_0I/2R$ and area $\\pi R^2/400$, so $\\phi\\propto R$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",8],[2024,"R","55/4/2",4],[2024,"R","55/4/3",7]]},{"id":"p0361","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Figure shows the variation of inductive reactance $X_L$ of two ideal inductors of inductance $L_1$ and $L_2$, with angular frequency $\\omega$. The value of $\\frac{L_1}{L_2}$ is","options":{"A":"$\\sqrt{3}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","C":"$3$","D":"$\\frac{1}{3}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Slope of the $X_L$-$\\omega$ line equals $L$.","explanation":"$L_1/L_2=\\tan30^\\circ/\\tan60^\\circ=\\frac{1/\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt3}=\\frac13$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0361.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",9]]},{"id":"p0362","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The phase difference between electric field $\\vec{E}$ and magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ in an electromagnetic wave propagating along z-axis is -","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$\\pi$","C":"$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","D":"$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"E and B oscillate together.","explanation":"In an EM wave $E$ and $B$ are in phase.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",10],[2024,"R","55/4/2",7],[2024,"R","55/4/3",3]]},{"id":"p0363","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Balmer series of hydrogen atom, as the wavelength of spectral lines decreases, they appear","options":{"A":"equally spaced and equally intense.","B":"further apart and stronger in intensity.","C":"closer together and stronger in intensity.","D":"closer together and weaker in intensity."},"answer":"D","hint":"Lines converge towards the series limit.","explanation":"Towards shorter wavelengths the Balmer lines crowd together and become fainter.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",12],[2024,"R","55/4/2",11],[2024,"R","55/4/3",4]]},{"id":"p0364","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Electrons are ejected from the surface of zinc when it is irradiated by yellow light.\nReason (R): Energy associated with a photon of yellow light is more than the work function of zinc.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare yellow photon energy (~2 eV) with zinc work function (~4.3 eV).","explanation":"Yellow photons have less energy than zinc's work function, so no emission: A false, R false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",13],[2024,"R","55/4/2",15],[2024,"R","55/4/3",16]]},{"id":"p0365","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When electrons drift in a conductor, it does not mean that all free electrons in the conductor are moving in the same direction.\nReason (R): The drift velocity is superposed over large random velocities of electrons.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Drift is a small average velocity on top of random thermal motion.","explanation":"Both true; random thermal velocities dominate, so electrons move in all directions, and drift is only superposed on this.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",15],[2024,"R","55/4/2",14],[2024,"R","55/4/3",13]]},{"id":"p0366","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In interference and diffraction of light, light energy reduces in one region producing a dark fringe. It increases in another region and produces a bright fringe.\nReason (R): This happens because energy is not conserved in the phenomena of interference and diffraction.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Energy is redistributed, not created or destroyed.","explanation":"A is true (redistribution of energy). R is false since energy is conserved.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/1",16],[2024,"R","55/4/2",13],[2024,"R","55/4/3",14]]},{"id":"p0367","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Three point charges, each of charge $q$ are placed on vertices of a triangle ABC, with $AB=AC=5L$, $BC=6L$. The electrostatic potential at midpoint of side BC will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{11}{48}\\frac{q}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L}$","B":"$\\frac{8q}{36\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L}$","C":"$\\frac{5q}{24\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{16}\\frac{q}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 L}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Add potentials of the three charges at the midpoint; find the distance to A by Pythagoras.","explanation":"Distances are $3L$, $3L$, $4L$: $V=\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0L}\\left(\\frac13+\\frac13+\\frac14\\right)=\\frac{11q}{48\\pi\\varepsilon_0L}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/2",1]]},{"id":"p0368","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The Coulomb force $F$ versus $(1/r^2)$ graphs for two pairs of point charges ($q_1$ and $q_2$) and ($q_2$ and $q_3$) are shown in figure. The charge $q_2$ is positive and has least magnitude. Then","options":{"A":"$q_1>q_2>q_3$","B":"$q_1>q_3>q_2$","C":"$q_3>q_2>q_1$","D":"$q_3>q_1>q_2$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Slope of $F$ vs $1/r^2$ is $kq_aq_b$; compare slope magnitudes.","explanation":"$q_1$ is positive and $q_3$ negative; the $(q_2,q_3)$ line is steeper, so $|q_3|>|q_1|>|q_2|$.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0368.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/2",2]]},{"id":"p0369","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ is moving with velocity $\\vec{v}=v_x\\hat{i}+v_y\\hat{j}$. If it is subjected to a magnetic field $\\vec{B}=B_0\\hat{i}$, it will move in a -","options":{"A":"straight line path","B":"circular path","C":"helical path","D":"parabolic path"},"answer":"C","hint":"Resolve velocity parallel and perpendicular to $\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$v_x$ (parallel to $B$) is unchanged while $v_y$ gives circular motion, so the path is helical.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/2",5]]},{"id":"p0370","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ac source $V=282\\sin(100\\,t)$ volt is connected across a $1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ capacitor. The rms value of current in the circuit will be (take $\\sqrt{2}=1.41$)","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","B":"$20\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","C":"$40\\ \\mathrm{mA}$","D":"$80\\ \\mathrm{mA}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$X_C=1/\\omega C$, $I_{rms}=V_{rms}/X_C$.","explanation":"$X_C=10^4\\ \\Omega$, $V_{rms}=200\\ \\mathrm{V}$, so $I_{rms}=20\\ \\mathrm{mA}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/2",9]]},{"id":"p0371","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole of dipole moment $\\vec{p}$ is kept in a uniform electric field $\\vec{E}$. The amount of work done to rotate it from the position of stable equilibrium to that of unstable equilibrium will be","options":{"A":"$2pE$","B":"$-2pE$","C":"$pE$","D":"zero"},"answer":"A","hint":"$W=pE(\\cos\\theta_1-\\cos\\theta_2)$.","explanation":"$W=U(180^\\circ)-U(0^\\circ)=pE-(-pE)=2pE$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/3",1]]},{"id":"p0372","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An infinite long straight wire having a charge density $\\lambda$ is kept along $y'y$ axis in $x$-$y$ plane. The Coulomb force on a point charge $q$ at a point $P\\,(x,0)$ will be","options":{"A":"attractive and $\\frac{q\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x}$","B":"repulsive and $\\frac{q\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x}$","C":"attractive and $\\frac{q\\lambda}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x}$","D":"repulsive and $\\frac{q\\lambda}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Field of a line charge is $\\lambda/2\\pi\\varepsilon_0x$.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0x}$, so $F=qE=\\frac{q\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0x}$; like charges repel.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/3",2]]},{"id":"p0373","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The r.m.s. value of a current given by $i=(i_1\\cos\\omega t+i_2\\sin\\omega t)$ is -","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i_1+i_2)$","B":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i_1-i_2)$","C":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\sqrt{i_1^2+i_2^2}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i_1^2+i_2^2)$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Combine into a single sinusoid of amplitude $\\sqrt{i_1^2+i_2^2}$.","explanation":"Peak value is $\\sqrt{i_1^2+i_2^2}$, so $i_{rms}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\sqrt{i_1^2+i_2^2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/4/3",9]]},{"id":"p0374","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A battery supplies $0.9\\ \\mathrm{A}$ current through a $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor and $0.3\\ \\mathrm{A}$ current through a $7\\ \\Omega$ resistor when connected one by one. The internal resistance of the battery is :","options":{"A":"$2\\ \\Omega$","B":"$1.2\\ \\Omega$","C":"$1\\ \\Omega$","D":"$0.5\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Equate $\\varepsilon=I(R+r)$ for both cases.","explanation":"$0.9(2+r)=0.3(7+r)$ gives $r=0.5\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",1]]},{"id":"p0375","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ describes a circular path of radius $R$ in a magnetic field. If its mass and charge were $2m$ and $\\frac{q}{2}$ respectively, the radius of its path would be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{R}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{R}{2}$","C":"$2R$","D":"$4R$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$r=mv/(qB)$, so $r\\propto m/q$ at fixed speed.","explanation":"$m/q$ becomes 4 times, so the radius becomes $4R$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",2]]},{"id":"p0376","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following pairs is that of paramagnetic materials ?","options":{"A":"Copper and Aluminium","B":"Sodium and Calcium","C":"Lead and Iron","D":"Nickel and Cobalt"},"answer":"B","hint":"Copper and lead are diamagnetic; Ni, Co, Fe are ferromagnetic.","explanation":"Sodium and calcium are both paramagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",3]]},{"id":"p0377","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer of resistance $50\\ \\Omega$ is converted into a voltmeter of range $(0-2\\ \\mathrm{V})$ using a resistor of $1.0\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega$. If it is to be converted into a voltmeter of range $(0-10\\ \\mathrm{V})$, the resistance required will be","options":{"A":"$4.8\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega$","B":"$5.0\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega$","C":"$5.2\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega$","D":"$5.4\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Full-scale galvanometer current is fixed; $V=I_g(G+R)$.","explanation":"$I_g=2/1050$ A, so $R=10\\times1050/2-50=5200\\ \\Omega=5.2\\ \\mathrm{k}\\Omega$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",4]]},{"id":"p0378","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Two coils are placed near each other. When the current in one coil is changed at the rate of $5\\ \\mathrm{A/s}$, an emf of $2\\ \\mathrm{mV}$ is induced in the other. The mutual inductance of the two coils is","options":{"A":"$0.4\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","B":"$2.5\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","C":"$10\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","D":"$2.5\\ \\mathrm{H}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$\\varepsilon=M\\,dI/dt$.","explanation":"$M=2\\ \\mathrm{mV}/5\\ \\mathrm{A/s}=0.4\\ \\mathrm{mH}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",5]]},{"id":"p0379","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electromagnetic waves used to purify water are","options":{"A":"Infrared rays","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"X-rays","D":"Gamma rays"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall uses of the EM spectrum.","explanation":"Ultraviolet rays are used in water purifiers to kill germs.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",6],[2024,"R","55/5/2",11],[2024,"R","55/5/3",9]]},{"id":"p0380","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The focal lengths of the objective and the eyepiece of a compound microscope are $1\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ and $2\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ respectively. If the tube length of the microscope is $10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, the magnification obtained by the microscope for most suitable viewing by relaxed eye is :","options":{"A":"$250$","B":"$200$","C":"$150$","D":"$125$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Relaxed eye: $m=\\frac{L}{f_o}\\cdot\\frac{D}{f_e}$.","explanation":"$m=\\frac{10}{1}\\times\\frac{25}{2}=125$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",7],[2024,"R","55/5/2",12],[2024,"R","55/5/3",11]]},{"id":"p0381","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The variation of the stopping potential ($V_0$) with the frequency ($\\nu$) of the incident radiation for four metals A, B, C and D is shown in the figure. For the same frequency of incident radiation producing photo-electrons in all metals, the kinetic energy of photo-electrons will be maximum for metal","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"answer":"A","hint":"$K_{max}=h(\\nu-\\nu_0)$, so smallest threshold gives largest $K$.","explanation":"A has the lowest threshold frequency (smallest work function), so its photoelectrons have maximum kinetic energy.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0381.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",8],[2024,"R","55/5/2",9],[2024,"R","55/5/3",10]]},{"id":"p0382","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of an electron in the ground state of hydrogen atom is $-13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The kinetic and potential energy of the electron in the first excited state will be","options":{"A":"$-13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV},\\ 27.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$-6.8\\ \\mathrm{eV},\\ 13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV},\\ -6.8\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$6.8\\ \\mathrm{eV},\\ -3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E_n=-13.6/n^2$, $K=-E$, $U=2E$.","explanation":"For $n=2$, $E=-3.4$ eV, so $K=3.4$ eV and $U=-6.8$ eV.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",9],[2024,"R","55/5/2",10],[2024,"R","55/5/3",8]]},{"id":"p0383","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"A Young's double-slit experimental set up is kept in a medium of refractive index $\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$. Which maximum in this case will coincide with the $6^{\\mathrm{th}}$ maximum obtained if the medium is replaced by air ?","options":{"A":"$4^{\\mathrm{th}}$","B":"$6^{\\mathrm{th}}$","C":"$8^{\\mathrm{th}}$","D":"$10^{\\mathrm{th}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Fringe width scales as $\\lambda/\\mu$.","explanation":"$n\\lambda/\\mu=6\\lambda$ gives $n=6\\times\\frac43=8$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",10]]},{"id":"p0384","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The potential energy between two nucleons inside a nucleus is minimum at a distance of about","options":{"A":"$0.8\\ \\mathrm{fm}$","B":"$1.6\\ \\mathrm{fm}$","C":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{fm}$","D":"$2.8\\ \\mathrm{fm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the potential energy vs separation curve for nucleons.","explanation":"The nucleon-nucleon potential energy has its minimum at about 0.8 fm.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",11],[2024,"R","55/5/2",7],[2024,"R","55/5/3",6]]},{"id":"p0385","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"A pure Si crystal having $5\\times10^{28}$ atoms $\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$ is doped with 1 ppm concentration of antimony. If the concentration of holes in the doped crystal is found to be $4.5\\times10^{9}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, the concentration (in $\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$) of intrinsic charge carriers in Si crystal is about","options":{"A":"$1.2\\times10^{15}$","B":"$1.5\\times10^{16}$","C":"$3.0\\times10^{15}$","D":"$2.0\\times10^{16}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Mass action law: $n_en_h=n_i^2$.","explanation":"$n_e=5\\times10^{22}$, so $n_i^2=5\\times10^{22}\\times4.5\\times10^9=2.25\\times10^{32}$ and $n_i=1.5\\times10^{16}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-3}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",12],[2024,"R","55/5/2",6],[2024,"R","55/5/3",7]]},{"id":"p0386","chapter":1,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Equal amount of positive and negative charges are distributed uniformly on two halves of a thin circular ring as shown in figure. The resultant electric field at the centre O of the ring is along OC.\nReason (R): It is so because the net potential at O is not zero.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Potential at the centre is the algebraic sum of equal and opposite contributions.","explanation":"The field at O points from the positive half towards the negative half, along OC, so A is true. Net potential at O is zero, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0386.png","sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",13],[2024,"R","55/5/2",15],[2024,"R","55/5/3",15]]},{"id":"p0387","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The energy of a charged particle moving in a magnetic field does not change.\nReason (R): It is because the work done by the magnetic force on the charge moving in a magnetic field is zero.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Magnetic force is perpendicular to velocity.","explanation":"Force is perpendicular to displacement, so work is zero and kinetic energy is constant. R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",14],[2024,"R","55/5/2",16],[2024,"R","55/5/3",16]]},{"id":"p0388","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a Young's double-slit experiment, interference pattern is not observed when two coherent sources are infinitely close to each other.\nReason (R): The fringe width is proportional to the separation between the two sources.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Fringe width $\\beta=\\lambda D/d$.","explanation":"As $d\\to0$ the fringe width becomes very large and no pattern is seen, so A is true. $\\beta\\propto1/d$, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",15],[2024,"R","55/5/2",14],[2024,"R","55/5/3",13]]},{"id":"p0389","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): An alpha particle is moving towards a gold nucleus. The impact parameter is maximum for the scattering angle of $180^\\circ$.\nReason (R): The impact parameter in an alpha particle scattering experiment does not depend upon the atomic number of the target nucleus.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"$b\\propto Z\\cot(\\theta/2)$.","explanation":"Head-on (zero impact parameter) gives $180^\\circ$, so A is false. $b$ depends on $Z$, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/1",16],[2024,"R","55/5/2",13],[2024,"R","55/5/3",14]]},{"id":"p0390","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"An ammeter and a voltmeter are connected in series to a battery. Their readings are noted as 'A' and 'V' respectively. If a resistor is connected in parallel with the voltmeter, then","options":{"A":"A will increase, V will decrease.","B":"A will decrease, V will increase.","C":"Both A and V will decrease.","D":"Both A and V will increase."},"answer":"A","hint":"Parallel resistor lowers the total resistance in the circuit.","explanation":"Total resistance falls so current A rises; the voltmeter, parallel to a smaller resistance, reads less.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/2",1]]},{"id":"p0391","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ac voltage is applied across an ideal inductor. The current in it","options":{"A":"leads the voltage by $\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ cycle.","B":"lags the voltage by $\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ cycle.","C":"leads the voltage by $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ cycle.","D":"lags the voltage by $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ cycle."},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall the phase relation between current and voltage in a purely inductive circuit.","explanation":"In an ideal inductor the current lags the voltage by $\\pi/2$, i.e. one-quarter cycle.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/2",2]]},{"id":"p0392","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"An iron needle is kept near a strong bar magnet. It will experience","options":{"A":"a force of attraction and no torque.","B":"a force of attraction and a torque.","C":"a torque and no force.","D":"neither a force nor a torque."},"answer":"B","hint":"Iron is ferromagnetic, so it gets magnetised, and the field is non-uniform.","explanation":"The needle is magnetised and aligns (torque) and, in the non-uniform field, is attracted toward the magnet (force).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/2",3]]},{"id":"p0393","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer shows full scale deflection for a current $I_g$. If a shunt of resistance $S_1$ is connected to the galvanometer, it gets converted into an ammeter of range $(0-I)$. When resistance of the shunt is made $S_2$, its range becomes $(0-2I)$. Then $\\left(\\frac{S_1}{S_2}\\right)$ is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{I+I_g}{I-I_g}$","B":"$\\frac{I-I_g}{I+I_g}$","C":"$\\frac{2I-I_g}{I-I_g}$","D":"$\\frac{I-I_g}{2I-I_g}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Shunt resistance is $S=\\frac{I_gG}{I-I_g}$ for range $I$.","explanation":"$S_1=\\frac{I_gG}{I-I_g}$ and $S_2=\\frac{I_gG}{2I-I_g}$, so $S_1/S_2=\\frac{2I-I_g}{I-I_g}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/2",4]]},{"id":"p0394","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A coil of area of cross-section $0.5\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ is placed in a magnetic field acting normally to its plane. The field varies as $B=0.5t^2+2t$, where $B$ is in tesla and $t$ in seconds. The emf induced in the coil at $t=1\\ \\mathrm{s}$ is","options":{"A":"$0.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$1.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$3.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\varepsilon=A\\,\\frac{dB}{dt}$.","explanation":"$dB/dt=t+2=3\\ \\mathrm{T/s}$ at $t=1$ s, so $\\varepsilon=0.5\\times3=1.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/2",5]]},{"id":"p0395","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In a Young's double-slit experiment in air, the fringe width is found to be $0.44\\ \\mathrm{mm}$. If the entire setup is immersed in water $\\left(n=\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$, the fringe width will be","options":{"A":"$0.88\\ \\mathrm{mm}$","B":"$0.59\\ \\mathrm{mm}$","C":"$0.33\\ \\mathrm{mm}$","D":"$0.44\\ \\mathrm{mm}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Wavelength reduces by factor $n$ in a medium.","explanation":"$\\beta'=\\beta/n=0.44\\times\\frac{3}{4}=0.33\\ \\mathrm{mm}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/2",8]]},{"id":"p0396","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A heater coil rated as $(P, V)$ is cut into two equal parts. One of the parts is then connected to a battery of $V$ volt. The power consumed by it will be","options":{"A":"$P$","B":"$\\frac{P}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{P}{4}$","D":"$2P$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $P=V^2/R$ and note resistance is proportional to length.","explanation":"Half the coil has resistance $R/2$, so $P'=V^2/(R/2)=2P$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/3",1]]},{"id":"p0397","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two insulated concentric coils, each of radius $R$, placed at right angles to each other, carry currents $I$ and $\\sqrt{3}\\,I$ respectively. The magnitude of the net magnetic field at their common centre will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{R}$","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2R}$","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4R}$","D":"$\\frac{2\\mu_0 I}{R}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Fields at the centre are perpendicular, so add in quadrature.","explanation":"$B_1=\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2R}$, $B_2=\\frac{\\sqrt3\\mu_0 I}{2R}$; $B=\\sqrt{B_1^2+B_2^2}=\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{R}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/3",2]]},{"id":"p0398","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following material has its magnetic susceptibility $x$ in the range $0<x<\\varepsilon$, where $\\varepsilon$ is positive and small ?","options":{"A":"Aluminium","B":"Water","C":"Gadolinium","D":"Bismuth"},"answer":"A","hint":"Small positive susceptibility means paramagnetic.","explanation":"Aluminium is paramagnetic; water and bismuth are diamagnetic and gadolinium is ferromagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/3",3]]},{"id":"p0399","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer of resistance $100\\ \\Omega$ gives full scale deflection for a current of $1.0\\ \\mathrm{mA}$. It is converted into an ammeter of range $(0-1\\ \\mathrm{A})$. The resistance of the ammeter will be close to","options":{"A":"$0.1\\ \\Omega$","B":"$0.8\\ \\Omega$","C":"$1.0\\ \\Omega$","D":"$10\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Ammeter resistance is $G$ in parallel with the shunt.","explanation":"$S=\\frac{I_gG}{I-I_g}\\approx0.1\\ \\Omega$; the combination is $\\approx0.1\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/3",4]]},{"id":"p0400","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The mutual inductance of two coils, in a given orientation is $50\\ \\mathrm{mH}$. If the current in one of the coils changes as $i=1.0\\sin\\left(100\\pi t+\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\ \\mathrm{A}$, the peak value of emf (in volt) induced in the other coil will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\pi}{5}$","B":"$5\\pi$","C":"$0.5\\pi$","D":"$0.05\\pi$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Peak emf is $M\\,\\omega I_0$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon_0=M\\omega I_0=0.05\\times100\\pi\\times1=5\\pi\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/3",5]]},{"id":"p0401","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A point object is kept $60\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ in front of a spherical convex surface ($n=1.5$, radius of curvature $40\\ \\mathrm{cm}$). The image formed is","options":{"A":"real, at a distance $1.8\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from the surface.","B":"virtual, at a distance $1.8\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from the surface.","C":"real, at a distance $3.6\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from the surface.","D":"virtual, at a distance $3.6\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from the surface."},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\frac{n_2}{v}-\\frac{n_1}{u}=\\frac{n_2-n_1}{R}$ with sign convention.","explanation":"$\\frac{1.5}{v}=\\frac{0.5}{40}-\\frac{1}{60}=-\\frac{1}{240}$, so $v=-360$ cm: virtual, 3.6 m.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2024,"R","55/5/3",12]]},{"id":"p0402","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements about electric field $E$ is incorrect ?","options":{"A":"The magnitude of the electric field due to a point charge is proportional to $\\frac{1}{r^2}$, where $r$ is the distance of the point from the charge.","B":"The magnitude of the electric field is zero at all points in a region where the potential is zero.","C":"Electric field is a vector quantity.","D":"The magnitude of the electric field at a point is a measure of the force experienced by a unit charge at that point."},"answer":"B","hint":"Field is the negative gradient of potential, not the potential itself.","explanation":"Zero potential does not imply zero field (e.g. the mid-point of a dipole).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",1]]},{"id":"p0403","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A battery of emf $6.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and internal resistance $0.40\\ \\Omega$ is connected to an external resistor of resistance $11.6\\ \\Omega$. The power dissipated in the external resistance is :","options":{"A":"$1.45\\ \\mathrm{W}$","B":"$2.9\\ \\mathrm{W}$","C":"$5.8\\ \\mathrm{W}$","D":"$11.6\\ \\mathrm{W}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Find the current then $P=I^2R$.","explanation":"$I=6/12=0.5$ A; $P=0.25\\times11.6=2.9$ W.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",2]]},{"id":"p0404","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"The acceleration of a particle of mass 'm' and charge 'q' moving with uniform speed $v$ at right angles to a magnetic field $B$ is given by :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{qB}{vm}$","B":"$\\frac{qBv}{m}$","C":"$\\frac{qv}{Bm}$","D":"$Bqvm$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Magnetic force is $qvB$ for perpendicular motion.","explanation":"$a=F/m=qvB/m$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",3]]},{"id":"p0405","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A current of $5.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$ in one coil is reduced to zero in $10\\ \\mathrm{ms}$ inducing an emf of $9.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$ in the neighbouring coil. The mutual inductance of the two coils is :","options":{"A":"$18\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","B":"$1.8\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","C":"$0.18\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","D":"$0.18\\ \\mathrm{H}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $\\varepsilon=M\\,\\left|\\frac{dI}{dt}\\right|$.","explanation":"$M=\\frac{9}{5/0.01}=0.018\\ \\mathrm{H}=18\\ \\mathrm{mH}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",4]]},{"id":"p0406","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements about transformers is not correct ?","options":{"A":"For ideal transformers, the output power is equal to the input power.","B":"A transformer can work for both alternating and direct voltages.","C":"For a step-up transformer, the voltage across the secondary coil is higher but the current in it is lower.","D":"For an ideal transformer, the magnetic flux linked with the secondary is equal to that linked with the primary."},"answer":"B","hint":"Transformers rely on changing flux.","explanation":"DC gives constant flux and no induced emf, so a transformer works only with ac.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",5]]},{"id":"p0407","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following will not increase the output voltage in a generator ?","options":{"A":"Rotating the coil rapidly","B":"Changing the material of the coil","C":"Increasing the area of coil","D":"Increasing the magnetic field through the coil"},"answer":"B","hint":"Peak emf is $NBA\\omega$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon_0=NBA\\omega$ does not depend on the coil's material.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",6]]},{"id":"p0408","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In free space, which of the following quantities is always larger for X-rays than for a radio wave ?","options":{"A":"Speed","B":"Frequency","C":"Amplitude","D":"Wavelength"},"answer":"B","hint":"All EM waves travel at $c$ in free space.","explanation":"X-rays have higher frequency (shorter wavelength); speed is the same and amplitude is not fixed.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",7]]},{"id":"p0409","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The depth of a swimming pool (refractive index of water $=\\frac{4}{3}$) is $1\\ \\mathrm{m}$. If one looks straight vertically down, the depth of the pool will appear to be :","options":{"A":"$0.25\\ \\mathrm{m}$","B":"$0.75\\ \\mathrm{m}$","C":"$0.95\\ \\mathrm{m}$","D":"$1.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Apparent depth $=$ real depth$/n$.","explanation":"$1/(4/3)=0.75$ m.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",8]]},{"id":"p0410","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A beam of red light and a beam of blue light have equal intensities. It implies that :","options":{"A":"Red light beam has higher number of photons than that in blue light beam.","B":"Blue light beam has higher number of photons than that in red light beam.","C":"Both beams contain the same number of photons.","D":"The momentum of a photon of red light beam is more than that of a photon of blue light beam."},"answer":"A","hint":"Intensity $=$ (photon rate)$\\times h\\nu$.","explanation":"Red photons have lower energy, so equal intensity needs more red photons.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",9]]},{"id":"p0411","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"If $1.0$ milligram of mass is converted into energy, the energy released will be :","options":{"A":"$9\\times10^{16}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$3\\times10^{12}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$9\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$3\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $E=mc^2$ with mass in kg.","explanation":"$E=10^{-6}\\times(3\\times10^8)^2=9\\times10^{10}$ J.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",10]]},{"id":"p0412","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following impurity atoms, when doped in silicon, would produce an n-type semiconductor ?","options":{"A":"Arsenic","B":"Aluminium","C":"Boron","D":"Indium"},"answer":"A","hint":"n-type needs a pentavalent donor.","explanation":"Arsenic is pentavalent; the others are trivalent (p-type).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",11]]},{"id":"p0413","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is correct during formation of a p-n junction ?","options":{"A":"Initially the diffusion current is small and the drift current is large.","B":"Initially the diffusion current and the drift current are equal to each other.","C":"Finally, the diffusion current and the drift current are equal to each other.","D":"Electrons move from p-region to n-region."},"answer":"C","hint":"Think of the equilibrium condition.","explanation":"At equilibrium the two currents balance; initially diffusion dominates.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",12]]},{"id":"p0414","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Nuclear forces are always attractive in nature.\nReason (R): Nuclear forces are charge-dependent forces.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Nuclear force is repulsive at very short range and charge independent.","explanation":"A is false (repulsive at very short distances), R is false (charge independent).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",13]]},{"id":"p0415","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): If the maximum kinetic energy of an electron in a photoelectric experiment is one-fourth the energy of a photon of wavelength $\\lambda$ incident on a surface, the work function of the surface is $\\frac{3}{4}\\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$.\nReason (R): Photoelectric emission can be explained by Einstein's photoelectric equation.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi$.","explanation":"$\\phi=E-E/4=\\frac34\\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$, so A is true; R is true and is the basis of this result.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",14]]},{"id":"p0416","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a Young's double-slit experiment, interference pattern is obtained by using yellow light. If yellow light is replaced by red light, the fringe width increases.\nReason (R): Fringe width is inversely proportional to the distance of the screen from the slits.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall $\\beta=\\lambda D/d$.","explanation":"Red has longer $\\lambda$ so $\\beta$ increases (A true); $\\beta\\propto D$, not $1/D$, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",15]]},{"id":"p0417","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The reactance of an inductor decreases with the increase in the frequency of alternating voltage applied.\nReason (R): The reactance of an inductor $X_L=\\frac{1}{\\omega L}$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall $X_L=\\omega L$.","explanation":"$X_L=\\omega L$ increases with frequency, so both A and R are false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55(B)/S",16]]},{"id":"p0418","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A small metallic sphere S of charge $+q$ is placed exactly at a point midway between two-point charges A $(+Q)$ and B $(+Q)$ where $Q\\gg q$. If the sphere is slightly displaced towards charge A and released, then :","options":{"A":"it will move further towards A","B":"it will move towards B","C":"it will oscillate about its original position","D":"it will not move at all"},"answer":"C","hint":"Check whether the net force on displacement is restoring.","explanation":"Near A the repulsion from A exceeds that from B, pushing it back; the midpoint is a stable equilibrium so it oscillates.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",1]]},{"id":"p0419","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In a Rutherford scattering experiment, when an alpha particle of mass $m_1$ approaches a target nucleus of charge $Ze$ and mass $m_2$, the distance of the closest approach is $d_0$. The energy of the particle is :","options":{"A":"directly proportional to $Z$","B":"inversely proportional to $Z$","C":"directly proportional to mass $m_2$","D":"directly proportional to $m_1m_2$"},"answer":"A","hint":"At closest approach all kinetic energy becomes electrostatic potential energy.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{2e\\cdot Ze}{d_0}\\propto Z$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",2]]},{"id":"p0420","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The potential energy of a pair of nucleons is minimum when they are separated by about :","options":{"A":"$0.8\\times10^{-15}\\ \\mathrm{m}$","B":"$0.8\\times10^{-10}\\ \\mathrm{m}$","C":"$2.0\\times10^{-13}\\ \\mathrm{m}$","D":"$2.0\\times10^{-14}\\ \\mathrm{m}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the nucleon potential energy curve.","explanation":"The minimum occurs at about $0.8$ fm $=0.8\\times10^{-15}$ m.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",3],[2025,"C","55/S/2",7],[2025,"C","55/S/3",10]]},{"id":"p0421","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows the variation of maximum kinetic energy ($K_{max}$) of emitted electrons as a function of the frequency $\\upsilon$ of radiation incident on a photosensitive surface. The slope of the curve is :","options":{"A":"$h$","B":"$\\frac{h}{\\upsilon}$","C":"$\\frac{h}{e}$","D":"$he$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Write $K_{max}$ as a linear function of $\\upsilon$.","explanation":"$K_{max}=h\\upsilon-\\phi$, so the slope is $h$.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0421.png","sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",4],[2025,"C","55/S/2",3],[2025,"C","55/S/3",8]]},{"id":"p0422","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Whenever a magnet is moved either towards or away from a conducting coil, an emf is induced whose magnitude is independent of the :","options":{"A":"number of turns in the coil","B":"resistance of the coil","C":"speed with which the magnet is moved","D":"area of the coil"},"answer":"B","hint":"Induced emf is $-N\\,d\\phi/dt$.","explanation":"The emf depends on $N$, $A$ and speed but not on the coil's resistance.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",5],[2025,"C","55/S/2",10],[2025,"C","55/S/3",12]]},{"id":"p0423","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Electromagnetic radiation used to kill germs in water purifiers is :","options":{"A":"Microwaves","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"Gamma rays","D":"Radio waves"},"answer":"B","hint":"Think of the germicidal radiation.","explanation":"Ultraviolet rays sterilise water.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",6]]},{"id":"p0424","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer having a coil resistance of $48\\ \\Omega$ shows full-scale deflection for a current of $2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$. It can be converted into an ammeter of range $(0-10\\ \\mathrm{A})$ by connecting :","options":{"A":"in series a resistance of $8\\ \\Omega$","B":"in parallel a resistance of $8\\ \\Omega$","C":"in parallel a resistance of $12\\ \\Omega$","D":"in series a resistance of $12\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"C","hint":"A shunt is connected in parallel: $S=\\frac{I_gG}{I-I_g}$.","explanation":"$S=\\frac{2\\times48}{10-2}=12\\ \\Omega$ in parallel.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",7],[2025,"C","55/S/2",5],[2025,"C","55/S/3",3]]},{"id":"p0425","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A rectangular loop of area vector $\\vec{A}=[\\hat{i}+\\sqrt{3}\\,\\hat{j}]\\times10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ is placed in a magnetic field $\\vec{B}=[4.0t^2+2.0]\\hat{i}$, where $B$ is in tesla and $t$ in seconds. At $t=1.0\\ \\mathrm{s}$, the magnitude of induced emf in the loop is :","options":{"A":"$80\\ \\mathrm{mV}$","B":"$0.11\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$0.22\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$0.8\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Flux is $\\vec B\\cdot\\vec A$; only the $\\hat i$ component of $\\vec A$ matters.","explanation":"$\\phi=(4t^2+2)\\times10^{-2}$; $\\varepsilon=8t\\times10^{-2}=0.08$ V $=80$ mV.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",8],[2025,"C","55/S/2",12],[2025,"C","55/S/3",5]]},{"id":"p0426","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A charge Q is enclosed by a spherical Gaussian surface of radius R. If the radius is doubled, then the total electric flux through the surface :","options":{"A":"becomes four times","B":"remains the same","C":"becomes half","D":"becomes twice"},"answer":"B","hint":"Apply Gauss's law.","explanation":"Flux $=Q/\\varepsilon_0$, independent of radius.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",9],[2025,"C","55/S/2",4],[2025,"C","55/S/3",4]]},{"id":"p0427","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The average value of voltage of $220\\ \\mathrm{V}$ ac mains during its positive half cycle will be :","options":{"A":"$156\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$198\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$220\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"zero"},"answer":"B","hint":"Half-cycle average is $2V_0/\\pi$.","explanation":"$V_0=220\\sqrt2\\approx311$ V; average $=2\\times311/\\pi\\approx198$ V.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",10]]},{"id":"p0428","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following assumptions has been used to obtain the relation $\\frac{v_s}{v_p} = \\frac{N_s}{N_p}$ for a transformer?","options":{"A":"The resistance of the primary coil is large.","B":"The same flux links both the primary and the secondary coils.","C":"The resistance of secondary coil is large.","D":"The transformer is 100% efficient."},"answer":"B","hint":"Think about what the induced emf per turn depends on.","explanation":"The ratio of emfs equals the turns ratio only if the same flux per turn links both coils (no flux leakage), with negligible coil resistance.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",12],[2025,"C","55/S/2",8],[2025,"C","55/S/3",7]]},{"id":"p0429","chapter":2,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Electrostatic force is a conservative force.\nReason (R): In an electrostatic field, the work done between two points per unit positive charge depends on the path followed.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the defining property of a conservative force.","explanation":"A is true. R is false because work done in an electrostatic field is path independent.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",13],[2025,"C","55/S/2",16],[2025,"C","55/S/3",15]]},{"id":"p0430","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The image of an object placed between f and 2f from a convex lens can be seen on a screen. If the screen is removed, image is not formed.\nReason (R): Rays from a given point on the object placed between f and 2f, after passing through a convex lens, do not converge on a point in space.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"A real image exists in space whether or not a screen is present.","explanation":"Between f and 2f the lens forms a real image where refracted rays converge, even without a screen. Both A and R are false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",14],[2025,"C","55/S/2",15],[2025,"C","55/S/3",16]]},{"id":"p0431","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The electrical conductivity of a pure Ge crystal increases with increase in its temperature.\nReason (R): The number of electrons excited by thermal excitation from the valence band to the conduction band, in a semiconductor, increases with increase in temperature.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Conductivity depends on carrier density in an intrinsic semiconductor.","explanation":"More electrons cross the gap at higher temperature, raising carrier density and conductivity. A and R true, R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/1",16],[2025,"C","55/S/2",13],[2025,"C","55/S/3",14]]},{"id":"p0432","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A point charge of $10\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ is fixed at the origin of x-y plane. A second point charge of $-20\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ is moved from point $(5\\ \\mathrm{cm}, 0)$ to point $(0, 5\\ \\mathrm{cm})$. The change in the potential energy of the system of the two charges is:","options":{"A":"$36\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$","B":"$-36\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$","C":"$72\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$","D":"$0$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare the distances of the moving charge from the origin at start and end.","explanation":"Both points are 5 cm from the origin, so $U=\\frac{kq_1q_2}{r}$ is unchanged and $\\Delta U=0$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/2",1]]},{"id":"p0433","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of the hydrogen atom, the potential energy of electron in the ground state is:","options":{"A":"$13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$-13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$27.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$-27.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Relate potential energy to total energy in a Coulomb orbit.","explanation":"$U=2E=2(-13.6)=-27.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/2",2],[2026,"R","55(B)",10]]},{"id":"p0434","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Welders wear special glass goggles or face masks with glass windows to protect their eyes from:","options":{"A":"UV rays","B":"X-rays","C":"IR rays","D":"Gamma rays"},"answer":"A","hint":"Glass absorbs one part of the spectrum strongly that welding arcs emit.","explanation":"Welding arcs emit intense ultraviolet radiation, which the glass absorbs.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/2",6]]},{"id":"p0435","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field due to an infinitely long, straight, uniformly and positively charged wire at a point at distance r from the wire is:","options":{"A":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r}$ and is directed outward","B":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ and is directed outward","C":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r}$ and is directed inward","D":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ and is directed inward"},"answer":"A","hint":"Apply Gauss's law with a cylindrical surface.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r}$, radially outward for positive charge.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/2",9]]},{"id":"p0436","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider two long, straight, parallel conductors A and B separated by a distance d. They carry steady current I in the same direction. The force acting on unit length of conductor B due to conductor A is:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{2\\pi}\\frac{I^2}{d}$ and is attractive","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{2\\pi}\\frac{I^2}{d}$ and is repulsive","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{I^2}{d}$ and is attractive","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{I^2}{d}$ and is repulsive"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the force per unit length between parallel currents.","explanation":"$F/L=\\frac{\\mu_0 I^2}{2\\pi d}$, and parallel currents attract.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/2",11]]},{"id":"p0437","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A point charge of $20\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ is fixed at the origin of x-y plane. The work done in taking a second point charge of $-50\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ from point $(4\\ \\mathrm{cm}, 0)$ to point $(0, 5\\ \\mathrm{cm})$ is:","options":{"A":"$45\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$","B":"$-45\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$","C":"$90\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$","D":"$-90\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Work equals the change in potential energy $kq_1q_2(1/r_f-1/r_i)$.","explanation":"$W=kq_1q_2\\left(\\frac{1}{0.05}-\\frac{1}{0.04}\\right)=(-9\\times10^{-6})(-5)=45\\ \\mu\\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/3",1]]},{"id":"p0438","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of the hydrogen atom, the kinetic energy of an electron in $n = 2$ orbit is:","options":{"A":"$1.7\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$6.8\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$10.2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Kinetic energy equals the magnitude of total energy.","explanation":"$K=\\frac{13.6}{n^2}=\\frac{13.6}{4}=3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/3",2]]},{"id":"p0439","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following electromagnetic waves are known as 'heat waves'?","options":{"A":"UV rays","B":"IR rays","C":"X-rays","D":"Gamma rays"},"answer":"B","hint":"Which radiation is readily absorbed and heats bodies?","explanation":"Infrared rays are called heat waves.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/3",6]]},{"id":"p0440","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field due to a uniformly and negatively charged thin spherical shell at a point outside the shell at a distance r from its centre is:","options":{"A":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r}$ and is directed outward","B":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r}$ and is directed inward","C":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ and is directed outward","D":"proportional to $\\frac{1}{r^2}$ and is directed inward"},"answer":"D","hint":"Outside the shell it acts like a point charge at the centre.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{kq}{r^2}$, directed toward the centre since the charge is negative.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/3",9]]},{"id":"p0441","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two long, straight, parallel conductors carry the same current and attract each other with a force F per unit length. If the current is doubled in each conductor and their separation is made half, the force per unit length becomes:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{F}{2}$","B":"$2F$","C":"$4F$","D":"$8F$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Force per unit length scales as $I_1I_2/d$.","explanation":"$F\\propto I^2/d$, so the factor is $2^2\\times2=8$, giving $8F$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"C","55/S/3",11]]},{"id":"p0442","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A charge of $-1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ on a body represents","options":{"A":"loss of $6.25\\times10^{12}$ electrons by the body.","B":"gain of $6.25\\times10^{12}$ electrons by the body.","C":"gain of $1.6\\times10^{13}$ electrons by the body.","D":"loss of $1.6\\times10^{13}$ electrons by the body."},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $q=ne$; negative charge means excess electrons.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{10^{-6}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}=6.25\\times10^{12}$ electrons gained.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",1]]},{"id":"p0443","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The resistance of a wire at $20\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$ is $50\\ \\Omega$. When it is heated to $120\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$, the resistance becomes $51\\ \\Omega$. The temperature coefficient of resistance of the material of the wire is","options":{"A":"$2\\times10^{-4}\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}^{-1}$","B":"$1\\times10^{-4}\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}^{-1}$","C":"$5\\times10^{-4}\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}^{-1}$","D":"$1\\times10^{-3}\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}^{-1}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $R_2=R_1[1+\\alpha\\Delta T]$.","explanation":"$\\alpha=\\frac{1}{50\\times100}=2\\times10^{-4}\\ ^\\circ\\mathrm{C}^{-1}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",2]]},{"id":"p0444","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass m and charge q moving with a velocity $\\vec{V}$ at right angle to a magnetic field $\\vec{B}$, experiences a force $\\vec{F}$. Another particle of mass 2m and charge 2q enters the same field $\\vec{B}$ with a velocity $\\frac{\\vec{V}}{2}$, in the same direction. This particle will experience a force","options":{"A":"$4\\vec{F}$","B":"$2\\vec{F}$","C":"$\\vec{F}$","D":"$\\frac{\\vec{F}}{2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Force is $qvB$ for perpendicular motion; mass does not matter.","explanation":"$F'=(2q)(v/2)B=qvB=F$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",3]]},{"id":"p0445","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A $5.0\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ long wire carrying $1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$ current is kept in a region in which a uniform magnetic field of $0.3\\ \\mathrm{T}$ is applied perpendicular to the length of the wire. The magnitude of the force acting on the wire is -","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$0.45\\ \\mathrm{N}$","C":"$0.03\\ \\mathrm{N}$","D":"$0.015\\ \\mathrm{N}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $F=BIl$ for a perpendicular wire.","explanation":"$F=0.3\\times1.0\\times0.05=0.015\\ \\mathrm{N}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",4]]},{"id":"p0446","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A step up transformer -","options":{"A":"decreases the current to transmit power over short distances with minimum loss.","B":"increases the current to transmit power over short distances with minimum loss.","C":"increases the voltage to transmit power over short distances with minimum loss.","D":"increases the voltage to transmit power over long distances with minimum loss."},"answer":"D","hint":"Power loss in lines is $I^2R$.","explanation":"Stepping up the voltage lowers the current and hence the line loss, which matters for long-distance transmission.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",5],[2025,"R","55(B)",11]]},{"id":"p0447","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux through a loop changes from $3.6\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$ to $1.6\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$ in $0.4\\ \\mathrm{s}$. The magnitude of the induced emf in the loop is -","options":{"A":"$5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$2.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$0.5\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Faraday's law: emf equals rate of change of flux.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=\\frac{3.6-1.6}{0.4}=5\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",6]]},{"id":"p0448","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electromagnetic radiation used to kill germs in water purifiers is","options":{"A":"Ultraviolet rays","B":"Infrared waves","C":"Visible rays","D":"$\\gamma$-rays"},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of germicidal lamps.","explanation":"Ultraviolet radiation is used to disinfect water.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",7]]},{"id":"p0449","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Two thin lenses of focal lengths $+40\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ and $-20\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ and placed coaxially in contact. An object is placed at infinity, in front of the combination. The image formed by the combination will lie -","options":{"A":"at a distance of $60\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, in front of the combination.","B":"at a distance of $40\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, in front of the combination.","C":"at a distance of $20\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, behind the combination.","D":"at infinity"},"answer":"B","hint":"Add powers of lenses in contact.","explanation":"$P=\\frac{1}{0.4}-\\frac{1}{0.2}=-2.5\\ \\mathrm{D}$, so $f=-40\\ \\mathrm{cm}$; a virtual image forms 40 cm in front.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",8]]},{"id":"p0450","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The de-Broglie wavelengths associated with an electron (mass $m_e$) and a proton (mass $m_p$) moving with the same velocity are $\\lambda_e$ and $\\lambda_p$ respectively. The value of $(\\lambda_e/\\lambda_p)$ is -","options":{"A":"$\\sqrt{\\frac{m_p}{m_e}}$","B":"$\\frac{m_p}{m_e}$","C":"$\\frac{m_e}{m_p}$","D":"$\\sqrt{\\frac{m_e}{m_p}}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\lambda=h/mv$.","explanation":"At equal speed, $\\lambda\\propto 1/m$, so $\\lambda_e/\\lambda_p=m_p/m_e$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",9]]},{"id":"p0451","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one out of the following transitions of an electron in Bohr's model of hydrogen atom will emit a photon with the greatest frequency?","options":{"A":"n = 3 to n = 1","B":"n = 3 to n = 2","C":"n = 4 to n = 3","D":"n = 4 to n = 2"},"answer":"A","hint":"Greatest frequency means greatest energy gap.","explanation":"Energy gaps: 12.1, 1.89, 0.66, 2.55 eV; n=3 to 1 is largest.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",10]]},{"id":"p0452","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following impurity atoms when doped in silicon, would produce a p-type semiconductor?","options":{"A":"Phosphorus","B":"Arsenic","C":"Antimony","D":"Boron"},"answer":"D","hint":"P-type needs a trivalent dopant.","explanation":"Boron has three valence electrons and acts as an acceptor.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",12]]},{"id":"p0453","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Only a continuous change of magnetic flux will maintain an induced emf in a coil.\nReason (R): An induced current has a direction such that the magnetic field due to the current opposes the change in the magnetic field that induces the current.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Does Lenz's law say why the emf needs continuous change?","explanation":"A is true (emf exists only while flux changes). R is Lenz's law, true, but gives direction, not why change must be continuous.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",13]]},{"id":"p0454","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): At resonance the impedance of a series LCR circuit is Z = R.\nReason (R): $Z=\\sqrt{R^2+(X_C-X_L)^2}$ and at resonance the inductive reactance ($X_L$) is equal to the capacitive reactance ($X_C$).","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Substitute $X_L=X_C$ in the impedance formula.","explanation":"At resonance $X_C-X_L=0$ so $Z=R$; R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",14]]},{"id":"p0455","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When two coherent sources in Young's double-slit experiment (YDSE) are infinitely close to each other, interference pattern is observed on a screen.\nReason (R): The fringe width in YDSE does not depend on separation between the two slits.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Fringe width is $\\beta=\\lambda D/d$.","explanation":"As $d\\to0$, $\\beta\\to\\infty$ so no pattern is seen (A false), and $\\beta$ depends on $d$ (R false).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",15]]},{"id":"p0456","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A photon behaves as a particle.\nReason (R): A photon possesses both energy and momentum.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Particle nature is shown by energy and momentum.","explanation":"Photon has $E=h\\nu$ and $p=h/\\lambda$, which explains its particle behaviour. Both true, R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55(B)",16]]},{"id":"p0457","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Figure shows variation of Coulomb force (F) acting between two point charges with $\\frac{1}{r^2}$, r being the separation between the two charges $(q_1, q_2)$ and $(q_2, q_3)$. If $q_2$ is positive and least in magnitude, then the magnitudes of $q_1$, $q_2$ and $q_3$ are such that","options":{"A":"$q_2 < q_3 < q_1$","B":"$q_3 < q_1 < q_2$","C":"$q_1 < q_2 < q_3$","D":"$q_2 < q_1 < q_3$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Slope of F against $1/r^2$ is $k q q'$ in magnitude.","explanation":"The $(q_1,q_2)$ line is steeper, so $|q_1|>|q_3|$; with $q_2$ least, $q_2<q_3<q_1$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0457.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",1]]},{"id":"p0458","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two wires P and Q are made of the same material. The wire Q has twice the diameter and half the length as that of wire P. If the resistance of wire P is R, the resistance of the wire Q will be","options":{"A":"$R$","B":"$\\frac{R}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{R}{8}$","D":"$2R$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $R=\\rho l/A$ with $A\\propto d^2$.","explanation":"$R_Q=R\\times\\frac{1}{2}\\times\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{R}{8}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",2]]},{"id":"p0459","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A $1\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ segment of a wire lying along x-axis carries current of $0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$ along $+x$ direction. A magnetic field $\\vec{B} = (0.4\\ \\mathrm{mT})\\hat{j} + (0.6\\ \\mathrm{mT})\\hat{k}$ is switched on, in the region. The force acting on the segment is","options":{"A":"$(2\\hat{j} + 3\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{mN}$","B":"$(-3\\hat{j} + 2\\hat{k})\\ \\mu\\mathrm{N}$","C":"$(6\\hat{j} + 4\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{mN}$","D":"$(-4\\hat{j} + 6\\hat{k})\\ \\mu\\mathrm{N}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=I\\vec{l}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j}=\\hat{k}$, $\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{k}=-\\hat{j}$, so $F=0.005(0.4\\hat{k}-0.6\\hat{j})\\times10^{-3}=(-3\\hat{j}+2\\hat{k})\\ \\mu\\mathrm{N}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",3],[2025,"R","55/1/2",3],[2025,"R","55/1/3",3]]},{"id":"p0460","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A coil has 100 turns, each of area $0.05\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ and total resistance $1.5\\ \\Omega$. It is inserted at an instant in a magnetic field of $90\\ \\mathrm{mT}$, with its axis parallel to the field. The charge induced in the coil at that instant is:","options":{"A":"$3.0\\ \\mathrm{mC}$","B":"$0.30\\ \\mathrm{C}$","C":"$0.45\\ \\mathrm{C}$","D":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{C}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Induced charge is $\\Delta\\phi$ times turns over resistance.","explanation":"$q=\\frac{NBA}{R}=\\frac{100\\times0.09\\times0.05}{1.5}=0.30\\ \\mathrm{C}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",4]]},{"id":"p0461","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"You are required to design an air-filled solenoid of inductance $0.016\\ \\mathrm{H}$ having a length $0.81\\ \\mathrm{m}$ and radius $0.02\\ \\mathrm{m}$. The number of turns in the solenoid should be","options":{"A":"2592","B":"2866","C":"2976","D":"3140"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $L=\\mu_0N^2A/l$.","explanation":"$N=\\sqrt{\\frac{Ll}{\\mu_0\\pi r^2}}\\approx2866$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",5],[2025,"R","55/1/2",5],[2025,"R","55/1/3",5]]},{"id":"p0462","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A voltage $v = v_0\\sin\\omega t$ applied to a circuit drives a current $i = i_0\\sin(\\omega t + \\phi)$ in the circuit. The average power consumed in the circuit over a cycle is","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$i_0v_0\\cos\\phi$","C":"$\\frac{i_0v_0}{2}$","D":"$\\frac{i_0v_0}{2}\\cos\\phi$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Average of $vi$ over a cycle.","explanation":"$P=V_{rms}I_{rms}\\cos\\phi=\\frac{i_0v_0}{2}\\cos\\phi$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",6],[2025,"R","55/1/2",6],[2025,"R","55/1/3",6]]},{"id":"p0463","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The given diagram exhibits the relationship between the wavelength of the electromagnetic waves and the energy of photon associated with them. The three points P, Q and R marked on the diagram may correspond respectively to:","options":{"A":"X-rays, microwaves, UV radiation","B":"X-rays, UV radiation, microwaves","C":"UV radiation, microwaves, X-rays","D":"Microwaves, UV radiation, X-rays"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photon energy is inversely proportional to wavelength.","explanation":"P has the highest energy (X-rays), Q the lowest (microwaves), R in between (UV).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0463.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",7]]},{"id":"p0464","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A beaker is filled with water (refractive index $\\frac{4}{3}$) upto a height H. A coin is placed at its bottom. The depth of the coin, when viewed along the near normal direction, will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{H}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{3H}{4}$","C":"$H$","D":"$\\frac{4H}{3}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Apparent depth equals real depth divided by refractive index.","explanation":"$d'=H/n=\\frac{3H}{4}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",8]]},{"id":"p0465","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The stopping potential $V_0$ measured in a photoelectric experiment for a metal surface is plotted against frequency $\\nu$ of the incident radiation. Let m be the slope of the straight line so obtained. Then the value of charge of an electron is given by (h is the Planck's constant.)","options":{"A":"$mh$","B":"$\\frac{m}{h}$","C":"$\\frac{h}{m}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{mh}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Write $V_0$ as a linear function of $\\nu$.","explanation":"$V_0=\\frac{h}{e}\\nu-\\frac{\\phi}{e}$, so $m=h/e$ and $e=h/m$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",9]]},{"id":"p0466","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Let $\\lambda_e$, $\\lambda_p$ and $\\lambda_d$ be the wavelengths associated with an electron, a proton and a deuteron, all moving with the same speed. Then the correct relation between them is","options":{"A":"$\\lambda_d > \\lambda_p > \\lambda_e$","B":"$\\lambda_e > \\lambda_p > \\lambda_d$","C":"$\\lambda_p > \\lambda_e > \\lambda_d$","D":"$\\lambda_e = \\lambda_p = \\lambda_d$"},"answer":"B","hint":"At equal speed, $\\lambda\\propto 1/m$.","explanation":"Mass order is $m_e<m_p<m_d$, so $\\lambda_e>\\lambda_p>\\lambda_d$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",10],[2025,"R","55/1/2",12],[2025,"R","55/1/3",11]]},{"id":"p0467","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following figures correctly represent the shape of curve of binding energy per nucleon as a function of mass number?","options":{"A":"Graph A (as shown in figure)","B":"Graph B (as shown in figure)","C":"Graph C (as shown in figure)","D":"Graph D (as shown in figure)"},"answer":"A","hint":"Binding energy per nucleon peaks near $A=56$ and falls slowly after.","explanation":"The curve rises steeply, peaks near $A=56$ and decreases gently for heavy nuclei, as in (A).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0467.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",11],[2025,"R","55/1/2",10]]},{"id":"p0468","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When a p-n junction diode is forward biased","options":{"A":"the barrier height and the depletion layer width both increase.","B":"the barrier height increases and the depletion layer width decreases.","C":"the barrier height and the depletion layer width both decrease.","D":"the barrier height decreases and the depletion layer width increases."},"answer":"C","hint":"Forward bias opposes the built-in potential.","explanation":"Forward bias reduces the barrier and narrows the depletion layer.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",12],[2025,"R","55/1/2",11],[2025,"R","55/1/3",10]]},{"id":"p0469","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): It is difficult to move a magnet into a coil of large number of turns when the circuit of the coil is closed.\nReason (R): The direction of induced current in a coil with its circuit closed, due to motion of a magnet, is such that it opposes the cause.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of Lenz's law and the work done against opposition.","explanation":"R is Lenz's law, so a larger number of turns gives larger opposing force, which explains A. Both true, R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",13],[2025,"R","55/1/2",16],[2025,"R","55/1/3",15]]},{"id":"p0470","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The deflection in a galvanometer is directly proportional to the current passing through it.\nReason (R): The coil of a galvanometer is suspended in a uniform radial magnetic field.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Radial field keeps torque independent of angle.","explanation":"Radial field gives $NIAB=k\\theta$, so $\\theta\\propto I$. Both true, R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",14],[2025,"R","55/1/2",15],[2025,"R","55/1/3",16]]},{"id":"p0471","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): We cannot form a p-n junction diode by taking a slab of a p-type semiconductor and physically joining it to another slab of a n-type semiconductor.\nReason (R): In a p-type semiconductor $\\eta_e \\gg \\eta_h$ while in a n-type semiconductor $\\eta_h \\gg \\eta_e$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Which carriers dominate in p-type and n-type?","explanation":"A is true (surface roughness and gaps prevent proper junction). R is false because in p-type $\\eta_h\\gg\\eta_e$ and in n-type $\\eta_e\\gg\\eta_h$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",15],[2025,"R","55/1/2",14],[2025,"R","55/1/3",13]]},{"id":"p0472","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The potential energy of an electron revolving in any stationary orbit in a hydrogen atom is positive.\nReason (R): The total energy of a charged particle is always positive.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Electron is bound to the nucleus.","explanation":"Potential energy is negative ($-\\frac{ke^2}{r}$) and the total energy of a bound electron is negative, so both are false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/1",16],[2025,"R","55/1/2",13],[2025,"R","55/1/3",14]]},{"id":"p0473","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"In the figure curved lines represent equipotential surfaces. A charge Q is moved along different paths A, B, C and D. The work done on the charge will be maximum along the path","options":{"A":"A","B":"B","C":"C","D":"D"},"answer":"C","hint":"Work depends only on the potential difference between end points.","explanation":"$W=Q\\Delta V$; path C ends on the equipotential with the largest potential difference from Q's start.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0473.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/2",1]]},{"id":"p0474","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The resistance of a wire of length L and radius r is R. Which one of the following would provide a wire of the same material of resistance $\\frac{R}{2}$?","options":{"A":"Using a wire of same radius and twice the length.","B":"Using a wire of same radius and half length.","C":"Using a wire of same length and twice the radius.","D":"Using a wire of same length and half the radius."},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $R=\\rho l/(\\pi r^2)$.","explanation":"Halving length at same radius halves R; doubling radius gives $R/4$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/2",2]]},{"id":"p0475","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular coil of diameter $15\\ \\mathrm{mm}$ having 300 turns is placed in a magnetic field of $30\\ \\mathrm{mT}$ such that the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the direction of the magnetic field. The magnetic field is reduced uniformly to zero in $20\\ \\mathrm{ms}$ and again increased uniformly to $30\\ \\mathrm{mT}$ in $40\\ \\mathrm{ms}$. If the emfs induced in the two time intervals are $e_1$ and $e_2$ respectively, then the value of $e_1/e_2$ is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","B":"$1$","C":"$2$","D":"$4$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare magnitudes of $N\\,\\Delta\\phi/\\Delta t$.","explanation":"Same flux change over $20$ ms and $40$ ms, so $e_1/e_2=40/20=2$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/2",4]]},{"id":"p0476","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following correctly represents the change in wave characteristics (all in vacuum) from microwaves to X-rays in electromagnetic spectrum?\nOption format: Speed; Wavelength; Frequency","options":{"A":"Speed: Remains same; Wavelength: Decreases; Frequency: Remains same","B":"Speed: Remains same; Wavelength: Decreases; Frequency: Increases","C":"Speed: Increases; Wavelength: Increases; Frequency: Decreases","D":"Speed: Remains same; Wavelength: Increases; Frequency: Remains same"},"answer":"B","hint":"All EM waves travel at $c$ in vacuum.","explanation":"Going to X-rays the wavelength decreases and frequency increases; speed stays $c$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/2",7]]},{"id":"p0477","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The speed of light in two media '1' and '2' are $v_1$ and $v_2\\ (> v_1)$ respectively. For a ray of light to undergo total internal reflection at the interface of these two media, it must be incident from","options":{"A":"medium '1' and at an angle greater than $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{v_1}{v_2}\\right)$","B":"medium '1' and at an angle greater than $\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{v_1}{v_2}\\right)$","C":"medium '2' and at an angle greater than $\\sin^{-1}\\left(\\frac{v_1}{v_2}\\right)$","D":"medium '2' and at an angle greater than $\\cos^{-1}\\left(\\frac{v_1}{v_2}\\right)$"},"answer":"A","hint":"TIR needs light going from the denser (slower) medium.","explanation":"Medium 1 is denser; critical angle $\\sin\\theta_c=n_2/n_1=v_1/v_2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/2",8]]},{"id":"p0478","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A source produces monochromatic light of frequency $5.0\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ and the power emitted is $3.31\\ \\mathrm{mW}$. The number of photons emitted per second by the source, on an average is","options":{"A":"$10^{16}$","B":"$10^{24}$","C":"$10^{10}$","D":"$10^{20}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photons per second equal power divided by $h\\nu$.","explanation":"$N=\\frac{3.31\\times10^{-3}}{6.62\\times10^{-34}\\times5\\times10^{14}}=10^{16}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/2",9]]},{"id":"p0479","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A charge Q is fixed in position. Another charge q is brought near charge Q and released from rest. Which of the following graphs is the correct representation of the acceleration of the charge q as a function of its distance r from charge Q ?","options":{"A":"Graph (A): a decreases steeply and then flattens, a hyperbola-like curve falling with r","B":"Graph (B): a stays nearly constant at small r and then drops sharply to a small value","C":"Graph (C): a decreases linearly with r","D":"Graph (D): a rises to a maximum at some r and then falls"},"answer":"A","hint":"Coulomb force falls with distance as an inverse power.","explanation":"$a=F/m\\propto 1/r^2$, which is the decaying curve of graph (A).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0479.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",1]]},{"id":"p0480","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two conductors A and B of the same material have their lengths in the ratio 1:2 and radii in the ratio 2:3. If they are connected in parallel across a battery, the ratio $\\frac{v_A}{v_B}$ of the drift velocities of electrons in them will be -","options":{"A":"$2$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{3}{2}$","D":"$\\frac{8}{9}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Parallel means same potential difference; drift velocity depends on the field $V/L$.","explanation":"$v_d=\\frac{e\\tau}{m}\\frac{V}{L}\\propto\\frac{1}{L}$, so $v_A/v_B=L_B/L_A=2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",2]]},{"id":"p0481","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the number of turns of the primary to the secondary coils in an ideal transformer is 20 : 1. If 240 V ac is applied from a source to the primary coil of transformer and a $6.0\\ \\Omega$ resistor is connected across the output terminals, then current drawn by the transformer from the source will be -","options":{"A":"$4.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$3.8\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$0.97\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$0.10\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Ideal transformer: input power equals output power.","explanation":"$V_s=12$ V, $I_s=2$ A, power 24 W, so $I_p=24/240=0.10$ A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",4]]},{"id":"p0482","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"X-rays are more harmful to human beings than ultraviolet radiations because X-rays -","options":{"A":"have frequency lower than that of ultraviolet radiations.","B":"have wavelength smaller than that of ultraviolet radiations.","C":"move faster than ultraviolet radiations in air.","D":"are mechanical waves but ultraviolet radiations are electro-magnetic waves."},"answer":"B","hint":"Harm relates to photon energy.","explanation":"X-rays have shorter wavelength, hence higher frequency and photon energy, than UV.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",7]]},{"id":"p0483","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A point source is placed at the bottom of a tank containing a transparent liquid (refractive index $n$) to a depth H. The area of the surface of the liquid through which light from the source can emerge out is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\pi H^2}{(n-1)}$","B":"$\\frac{\\pi H^2}{(n^2-1)}$","C":"$\\frac{\\pi H^2}{\\sqrt{n^2-1}}$","D":"$\\frac{\\pi H^2}{(n^2+1)}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Light escapes within a circle set by the critical angle.","explanation":"$r=H\\tan\\theta_c$ with $\\sin\\theta_c=1/n$ gives $r^2=H^2/(n^2-1)$, so area $=\\pi H^2/(n^2-1)$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",8]]},{"id":"p0484","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"In a photoelectric experiment with a material of work function 2.1 eV, the stopping potential is found to be 2.5 V. The maximum kinetic energy of ejected photoelectrons is","options":{"A":"$0.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"$2.1\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$2.5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"$4.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Stopping potential measures the maximum kinetic energy directly.","explanation":"$K_{max}=eV_0=2.5$ eV; the work function is a distractor.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",9]]},{"id":"p0485","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following figures correctly represent the shape of curve of binding energy per nucleon as a function of mass number ?","options":{"A":"Graph (A): rises steeply to a maximum at A = 56, then falls slowly","B":"Graph (B): rises to a plateau near A = 56 and stays flat","C":"Graph (C): symmetric hump with maximum at A = 80, then a dip and rise","D":"Graph (D): rises to a maximum at A = 80, then falls and rises again"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the peak position of the B.E. per nucleon curve.","explanation":"B.E. per nucleon peaks near A = 56 (iron) and falls gently for heavier nuclei, as in (A).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0485.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/1/3",12]]},{"id":"p0486","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two charges $-q$ each are placed at the vertices A and B of an equilateral triangle ABC. If M is the mid-point of AB, the net electric field at C will point along","options":{"A":"CA","B":"CB","C":"MC","D":"CM"},"answer":"D","hint":"Each field points toward its negative charge; add vectors.","explanation":"Components along AB cancel; the remaining components point from C toward M, i.e. along CM.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",1]]},{"id":"p0487","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A student has three resistors, each of resistance R. To obtain a resistance of $\\frac{2}{3}$ R, she should connect","options":{"A":"all the three resistors in series.","B":"all the three resistors in parallel.","C":"two resistors in series and then this combination in parallel with the third resistor.","D":"two resistors in parallel and then this combination in series with the third resistor."},"answer":"C","hint":"Combine series and parallel step by step.","explanation":"$2R\\parallel R=\\frac{2R\\cdot R}{3R}=\\frac{2}{3}R$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",2]]},{"id":"p0488","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A 1 cm straight segment of a conductor carrying 1 A current in $x$ direction lies symmetrically at origin of Cartesian coordinate system. The magnetic field due to this segment at point (1 m, 1 m, 0) is","options":{"A":"$1.0\\times10^{-9}\\,\\hat{k}\\ \\mathrm{T}$","B":"$-1.0\\times10^{-9}\\,\\hat{k}\\ \\mathrm{T}$","C":"$\\frac{5.0}{\\sqrt{2}}\\times10^{-10}\\,\\hat{k}\\ \\mathrm{T}$","D":"$-\\frac{5.0}{\\sqrt{2}}\\times10^{-10}\\,\\hat{k}\\ \\mathrm{T}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use Biot-Savart for a short element, $d\\vec{B}=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{I\\,d\\vec{l}\\times\\hat{r}}{r^2}$.","explanation":"$r^2=2$, $\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{r}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\hat{k}$, so $B=10^{-7}\\cdot\\frac{0.01}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt2}\\times10^{-10}$ T along $+\\hat{k}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",3],[2025,"R","55/2/2",6],[2025,"R","55/2/3",6]]},{"id":"p0489","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic field due to a small magnetic dipole of dipole moment 'M' at a distance 'r' from the centre along the axis of the dipole is given by","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\times\\frac{2M}{r^3}$","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\times\\frac{M}{r^3}$","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\times\\frac{M}{2r^3}$","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\times\\frac{2M}{r^2}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the axial field of a short bar magnet.","explanation":"Axial field is $\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{2M}{r^3}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",4]]},{"id":"p0490","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"In the figure X is a coil wound over a hollow wooden pipe.\nA permanent magnet is pushed at a constant speed $v$ from the right into the pipe and it comes out at the left end of the pipe. During the entry and the exit of the magnet, the current in the wire YZ will be from","options":{"A":"Y to Z and then Y to Z","B":"Z to Y and then Y to Z","C":"Y to Z and then Z to Y","D":"Z to Y and then Z to Y"},"answer":"B","hint":"Lenz's law: the coil opposes the change in flux, and entry and exit change flux oppositely.","explanation":"The induced current reverses between entry and exit, so the direction on entry (Z to Y) is followed by the opposite (Y to Z) on exit.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0490.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",5]]},{"id":"p0491","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The alternating current I in an inductor is observed to vary with time t as shown in the graph for a cycle (I rises linearly from 0 at t = 0 to a maximum at t = T/2 and falls linearly to 0 at t = T).\nWhich one of the following graphs is the correct representation of wave form of voltage V with time t ?","options":{"A":"Graph (A): triangular V rising to a peak at T/2 and falling to zero at T","B":"Graph (B): triangular wave, positive first half and negative second half","C":"Graph (C): square wave, constant positive for 0 to T/2 and constant negative for T/2 to T","D":"Graph (D): sinusoidal-looking wave, positive first half and negative second half"},"answer":"C","hint":"Inductor voltage is $L\\,dI/dt$, the slope of the current graph.","explanation":"Slope is constant positive for the first half and constant negative for the second, so V is a square wave: (C).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0491.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",6],[2025,"R","55/2/2",3],[2025,"R","55/2/3",8]]},{"id":"p0492","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A transformer is connected to a 200 V ac source. The transformer supplies 3000 V to a device. If the number of turns in the primary coil is 450, then the number of turns in its secondary coil is -","options":{"A":"$30$","B":"$450$","C":"$4500$","D":"$6750$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Voltage ratio equals turns ratio.","explanation":"$N_s=450\\times\\frac{3000}{200}=6750$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",7]]},{"id":"p0493","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following statements is correct ?\nElectric field due to static charges is","options":{"A":"conservative and field lines do not form closed loops.","B":"conservative and field lines form closed loops.","C":"non-conservative and field lines do not form closed loops.","D":"non-conservative and field lines form closed loops."},"answer":"A","hint":"Static electric fields are curl-free.","explanation":"Electrostatic force does zero work around a closed path and field lines start and end on charges, so no closed loops.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",8],[2025,"R","55/2/2",10],[2025,"R","55/2/3",3]]},{"id":"p0494","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A tub is filled with a transparent liquid to a height of 30.0 cm. The apparent depth of a coin lying at the bottom of the tub is found to be 16.0 cm. The speed of light in the liquid will be","options":{"A":"$1.6\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$","B":"$2.0\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$","C":"$3.0\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$","D":"$2.5\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Apparent depth gives refractive index, then $v=c/n$.","explanation":"$n=30/16=1.875$, so $v=3\\times10^8/1.875=1.6\\times10^8\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",9]]},{"id":"p0495","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Atomic spectral emission lines of hydrogen atom are incident on a zinc surface. The lines which can emit photoelectrons from the surface are members of","options":{"A":"Balmer series","B":"Paschen series","C":"Lyman series","D":"Neither Balmer, nor Paschen nor Lyman series"},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare photon energies with zinc's work function of about 4.3 eV.","explanation":"Balmer photons are at most 3.4 eV, Paschen less, while Lyman photons exceed 10.2 eV.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",10],[2025,"R","55/2/2",8],[2025,"R","55/2/3",11]]},{"id":"p0496","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of an electron in a hydrogen atom in ground state is $-13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. Its energy in an orbit corresponding to quantum number n is $-0.544\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. The value of n is","options":{"A":"$2$","B":"$3$","C":"$4$","D":"$5$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$E_n=-13.6/n^2$ eV.","explanation":"$n^2=13.6/0.544=25$, so $n=5$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",11],[2025,"R","55/2/2",12],[2025,"R","55/2/3",12]]},{"id":"p0497","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a semiconductor diode the thickness of depletion layer is not fixed.\nReason (R): Thickness of depletion layer in a semiconductor device depends upon many factors such as biasing of the semiconductor.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of how bias changes the depletion width.","explanation":"Width changes with forward or reverse bias and doping, so A and R are true and R explains A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",13],[2025,"R","55/2/2",16],[2025,"R","55/2/3",15]]},{"id":"p0498","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, the angular momentum of an electron in $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit is proportional to the square root of its orbit radius $r_n$.\nReason (R): According to Bohr model, electron can jump to its nearest orbits only.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $L=n\\hbar$ and $r_n\\propto n^2$.","explanation":"$L\\propto n\\propto\\sqrt{r_n}$ so A is true; an electron can jump between any allowed orbits, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",14],[2025,"R","55/2/2",15],[2025,"R","55/2/3",16]]},{"id":"p0499","chapter":8,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Out of Infrared and radio waves, the radio waves show more diffraction effect.\nReason (R): Radio waves have greater frequency than infrared waves.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Diffraction is stronger for longer wavelengths.","explanation":"Radio waves have longer wavelength so A is true; their frequency is lower than infrared, so R is false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",15],[2025,"R","55/2/2",13],[2025,"R","55/2/3",14]]},{"id":"p0500","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In an ideal step-down transformer, the electrical energy is not lost.\nReason (R): In a step-down transformer, voltage decreases but the current increases.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Ideal transformer conserves power: $V_pI_p=V_sI_s$.","explanation":"Both statements are true; R describes the result of power conservation, but the official scheme treats it as not the explanation of A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/1",16],[2025,"R","55/2/2",14],[2025,"R","55/2/3",13]]},{"id":"p0501","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two identical point charges are placed at the two vertices A and B of an equilateral triangle of side $l$. The magnitude of the electric field at the third vertex P is E. If a hollow conducting sphere of radius $(l/4)$ is placed at P, the magnitude of the electric field at point P now becomes","options":{"A":"$>E$","B":"$E$","C":"$\\frac{E}{2}$","D":"zero"},"answer":"D","hint":"Field inside a conductor cavity or body is shielded.","explanation":"P lies at the centre of the hollow conducting sphere, where the field due to outside charges is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/2",1]]},{"id":"p0502","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A battery of e.m.f. 12 V and internal resistance $0.5\\ \\Omega$ is connected to a $9.5\\ \\Omega$ resistor through a key. The ratio of potential difference between the two terminals of the battery, when the key is open to that when the key is closed, is","options":{"A":"$1.05$","B":"$1$","C":"$0.95$","D":"$1.1$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Open circuit terminal voltage is the emf; closed is $\\varepsilon-Ir$.","explanation":"$I=12/10=1.2$ A, $V=12-0.6=11.4$ V, ratio $=12/11.4\\approx1.05$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/2",2]]},{"id":"p0503","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A diamagnetic substance is brought, one by one, near the north pole and the south pole of a bar magnet. It is","options":{"A":"repelled by north pole and attracted by south pole.","B":"attracted by north pole and repelled by south pole.","C":"attracted by north pole as well as by south pole.","D":"repelled by north pole as well as by south pole."},"answer":"D","hint":"Diamagnets acquire magnetisation opposite to the applied field.","explanation":"A diamagnetic substance is weakly repelled by either pole.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/2",4]]},{"id":"p0504","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Two long solenoids of radii $r_1$ and $r_2$ ($>r_1$) and number of turns per unit length $n_1$ and $n_2$ respectively are co-axially wrapped one over the other. The ratio of self-inductance of inner solenoid to their mutual inductance is -","options":{"A":"$\\frac{n_1}{n_2}$","B":"$\\frac{n_2}{n_1}$","C":"$\\frac{n_1r_1^2}{n_2r_2^2}$","D":"$\\frac{n_2r_1^2}{n_1r_2^2}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Write $L_1$ and $M$ using flux through the inner area.","explanation":"$L_1=\\mu_0n_1^2\\pi r_1^2l$ and $M=\\mu_0n_1n_2\\pi r_1^2l$, so $L_1/M=n_1/n_2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/2",5]]},{"id":"p0505","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A coil of an ac generator, having 100 turns and area $0.1\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$ each, rotates at half a rotation per second in a magnetic field of 0.02 T. The maximum emf generated in the coil is","options":{"A":"$0.31\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$0.20\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$0.63\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$0.10\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$\\varepsilon_{max}=NAB\\omega$.","explanation":"$\\omega=2\\pi(0.5)=\\pi$, so $\\varepsilon_{max}=100\\times0.1\\times0.02\\times\\pi\\approx0.63$ V.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/2",7]]},{"id":"p0506","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The focal length of a concave mirror in air is f. When the mirror is immersed in a liquid of refractive index $\\frac{5}{3}$, its focal length will become","options":{"A":"$\\frac{5}{3}f$","B":"$\\frac{3}{5}f$","C":"$\\frac{2}{3}f$","D":"$f$"},"answer":"D","hint":"A mirror's focal length depends only on its radius of curvature.","explanation":"$f=R/2$ involves reflection only, so it is unchanged by the medium.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/2",9]]},{"id":"p0507","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider two identical dipoles $D_1$ and $D_2$. Charges $-q$ and $q$ of dipole $D_1$ are located at (0, 0) and (a, 0) and that of dipole $D_2$ at (0, a) and (0, 2a) in $x$-$y$ plane, respectively. The net dipole moment of the system is","options":{"A":"$qa(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j})$","B":"$-qa(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j})$","C":"$qa(\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$","D":"$-qa(\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Dipole moment points from the negative to the positive charge; add vectors.","explanation":"$\\vec{p}_1=qa\\hat{i}$ and $\\vec{p}_2=qa\\hat{j}$, so the total is $qa(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j})$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/3",1]]},{"id":"p0508","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which pair of readings of ideal voltmeter and ideal ammeter in the given circuit is possible when a suitable power source of $3\\ \\Omega$ internal resistance is connected between P and Q ? (An ammeter is in series with the source; a voltmeter is connected across a $24\\ \\Omega$ resistor.)","options":{"A":"$12.0\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ 2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ 0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$6.0\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ 2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$12\\ \\mathrm{V},\\ 0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Voltmeter reads $I\\times24\\ \\Omega$; the pair must satisfy Ohm's law.","explanation":"Only $12\\ \\mathrm{V}/0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}=24\\ \\Omega$ is consistent with the resistor.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0508.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/3",2]]},{"id":"p0509","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A material is pushed out when placed in a uniform magnetic field. The material is","options":{"A":"non-magnetic","B":"diamagnetic","C":"paramagnetic","D":"ferromagnetic"},"answer":"B","hint":"Which magnetic class is repelled from regions of strong field?","explanation":"Diamagnetic materials acquire opposite magnetisation and are expelled from the field.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/3",4]]},{"id":"p0510","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A soft iron rod X is allowed to fall on the two poles of a U shaped permanent magnet as shown in figure. A coil is wrapped over one arm of the U shaped magnet.\nDuring fall of the rod, the current in the coil will be","options":{"A":"clockwise current","B":"anticlockwise current","C":"alternating current","D":"zero"},"answer":"B","hint":"The flux through the coil changes as the rod approaches the poles.","explanation":"Flux through the arm increases as the rod nears, so the coil gives a one-directional induced current opposing the rise (anticlockwise per the official scheme).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0510.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/3",5]]},{"id":"p0511","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The number of turns between different pairs of output terminals are shown for a step-up transformer (primary A-B has 20 turns; secondary taps: P-Q 20 turns, Q-R 40 turns, R-S 60 turns).\nInput voltage of 20 V is applied between A and B. Between which two terminals will the output be 120 V ?","options":{"A":"P and Q","B":"Q and S","C":"P and R","D":"P and S"},"answer":"D","hint":"Voltage per turn is fixed by the primary.","explanation":"20 V on 20 turns gives 1 V per turn, so 120 V needs 120 turns, which is P to S (20+40+60).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0511.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/3",7]]},{"id":"p0512","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The plane face of a planoconvex lens is silvered. The refractive index of material and radius of curvature of the curved surface of the lens are n and R respectively. This lens will behave as a concave mirror of focal length","options":{"A":"$\\frac{R}{n}$","B":"$\\frac{R}{(n-1)}$","C":"$nR$","D":"$\\frac{R}{2(n-1)}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Light refracts through the curved face twice and reflects once.","explanation":"$\\frac1F=\\frac2f+\\frac1\\infty$ with $f=\\frac{R}{n-1}$, so $F=\\frac{R}{2(n-1)}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/2/3",9]]},{"id":"p0513","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A body acquires charge $8.0\\times10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{C}$. The mass of the body :","options":{"A":"increases by $4.5\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$","B":"decreases by $1.0\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$","C":"decreases by $4.55\\times10^{-23}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$","D":"increases by $9.1\\times10^{-23}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Positive charge means electrons are removed; count them.","explanation":"$n=8\\times10^{-12}/1.6\\times10^{-19}=5\\times10^7$ electrons lost, so $\\Delta m=5\\times10^7\\times9.1\\times10^{-31}=4.55\\times10^{-23}$ kg decrease.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",1]]},{"id":"p0514","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A current flows through a cylindrical conductor of radius R. The current density at a point in the conductor is $j=\\alpha r$ (along its axis), here $\\alpha$ is a constant and r is distance from the axis of the conductor. The current flowing through the portion of the conductor from $r=0$ to $r=\\frac{R}{2}$ is proportional to :","options":{"A":"$R$","B":"$R^2$","C":"$R^3$","D":"$R^4$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Integrate $j\\,dA$ over annular rings.","explanation":"$I=\\int_0^{R/2}\\alpha r\\,2\\pi r\\,dr=\\frac{2\\pi\\alpha}{3}\\frac{R^3}{8}\\propto R^3$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",2]]},{"id":"p0515","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle having charge $+q$ enters a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ as shown in the figure (field into the page above the x-axis; the particle moves along $+Y$ into the field). The particle will describe :","options":{"A":"a circular path in XZ plane","B":"a semicircular path in XY plane","C":"a helical path with its axis parallel to Y-axis","D":"a semicircular path in YZ plane"},"answer":"B","hint":"Velocity perpendicular to $\\vec{B}$ gives circular motion in the plane perpendicular to $\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$\\vec{v}\\perp\\vec{B}$ with $\\vec{B}$ along Z, so the path lies in the XY plane and is a semicircle before exiting the field region.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0515.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",3]]},{"id":"p0516","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A bar magnet is initially at right angles to a uniform magnetic field. The magnet is rotated till the torque acting on it becomes one-half of its initial value. The angle through which the bar magnet is rotated is :","options":{"A":"$30^\\circ$","B":"$45^\\circ$","C":"$60^\\circ$","D":"$75^\\circ$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Torque $\\tau=MB\\sin\\theta$, with $\\theta$ measured from the field.","explanation":"Initially $\\theta=90^\\circ$; $\\sin\\theta=\\frac12$ gives $\\theta=30^\\circ$, so the rotation is $60^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",4],[2025,"R","55/4/2",4],[2025,"R","55/4/3",4]]},{"id":"p0517","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one out of the following materials is not paramagnetic ?","options":{"A":"Aluminium","B":"Sodium Chloride","C":"Calcium","D":"Copper Chloride"},"answer":"B","hint":"Look for the material without unpaired electrons.","explanation":"NaCl has all electrons paired (diamagnetic), while Al, Ca and $\\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ salts are paramagnetic.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",5]]},{"id":"p0518","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ammeter connected in series in an ac circuit reads 10 A. The maximum value of current at any instant in the circuit is :","options":{"A":"$10\\sqrt{2}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$\\frac{10}{\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{2}\\,\\pi}\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"An ac ammeter reads the rms value.","explanation":"$I_0=\\sqrt2\\,I_{rms}=10\\sqrt2$ A.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",6]]},{"id":"p0519","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The amplitude of electric field in an electromagnetic wave in free space is $1000\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$. The amplitude of the magnetic field in this electromagnetic wave is :","options":{"A":"$3.0\\times10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{T}$","B":"$3.33\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{T}$","C":"$3.0\\times10^{11}\\ \\mathrm{T}$","D":"$3.33\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{T}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$B_0=E_0/c$.","explanation":"$B_0=1000/(3\\times10^8)=3.33\\times10^{-6}$ T.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",7]]},{"id":"p0520","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnification produced by a spherical mirror is $-2.0$. The mirror used and the nature of the image formed will be","options":{"A":"Convex and virtual","B":"Concave and real","C":"Concave and virtual","D":"Convex and real"},"answer":"B","hint":"Sign of magnification tells real or virtual.","explanation":"Negative magnification means an inverted real image, which only a concave mirror forms.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",8],[2025,"R","55/4/2",10],[2025,"R","55/4/3",9]]},{"id":"p0521","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Choose the correct statement :","options":{"A":"Photons of light show diffraction whereas electrons do not show diffraction.","B":"Electrons have momentum whereas photons do not have momentum.","C":"Photons of light and electrons both exhibit dual nature.","D":"All electromagnetic radiations do not have photons."},"answer":"C","hint":"Wave-particle duality applies to both light and matter.","explanation":"Light shows particle nature (photons) and wave nature; electrons also diffract, so both are dual.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",9],[2025,"R","55/4/2",11],[2025,"R","55/4/3",10]]},{"id":"p0522","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A beam of red light and a beam of blue light have equal intensities. Which of the following statements is true ?","options":{"A":"The blue beam has more number of photons than the red beam.","B":"The red beam has more number of photons than the blue beam.","C":"Wavelength of red light is lesser than wavelength of blue light.","D":"The blue light beam has lesser energy per photon than that in the red light beam."},"answer":"B","hint":"Intensity equals photon number rate times photon energy.","explanation":"Red photons have lower energy $h\\nu$, so equal intensity needs more red photons.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",10],[2025,"R","55/4/2",12],[2025,"R","55/4/3",11]]},{"id":"p0523","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following is an electrical conductor at room temperature ?","options":{"A":"Sn","B":"Mica","C":"Si","D":"C"},"answer":"A","hint":"Classify each material by band structure.","explanation":"Tin is a metal; mica is an insulator and Si is a semiconductor (carbon, as diamond, is an insulator).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",11],[2025,"R","55/4/2",9],[2025,"R","55/4/3",12]]},{"id":"p0524","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A long straight wire is held vertically and carries a steady current in upward direction. The shape of magnetic field lines produced by the current-carrying wire are :","options":{"A":"horizontal straight lines directed radially out from the wire.","B":"straight lines parallel to the current-carrying wire.","C":"concentric horizonal circles around the wire.","D":"coaxial helixes around the wire."},"answer":"C","hint":"Field of a straight current is azimuthal.","explanation":"Field lines are closed circles in horizontal planes, centred on the wire.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",12],[2025,"R","55/4/2",8],[2025,"R","55/4/3",8]]},{"id":"p0525","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): n-type semiconductor is not negatively charged.\nReason (R): Neutral pentavalent impurity atom doped in intrinsic semiconductor (neutral) donates its fifth unpaired electron to the crystal lattice and becomes a positive donor.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Doping adds as many protons as free electrons.","explanation":"Overall charge stays neutral since donor ions are positive; R explains why A holds.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",13],[2025,"R","55/4/2",16],[2025,"R","55/4/3",15]]},{"id":"p0526","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A series LCR circuit behaves as a pure resistive circuit at resonance.\nReason (R): At resonance, $X_L=X_C$ gives $\\omega=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"At resonance reactances cancel.","explanation":"$X_L=X_C$ makes net reactance zero, so $Z=R$; R is the condition causing this.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",14],[2025,"R","55/4/2",15],[2025,"R","55/4/3",16]]},{"id":"p0527","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): For monochromatic incident radiation, the emitted photoelectrons from a given metal have speed ranging from zero to a certain maximum value.\nReason (R): Each metal has a definite work function.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Electrons escape from different depths and lose different energy.","explanation":"Both are true, but a fixed work function only sets the maximum energy; the spread comes from energy losses inside the metal.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/1",16],[2025,"R","55/4/2",13],[2025,"R","55/4/3",14]]},{"id":"p0528","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole of dipole moment $1.0\\times10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{C\\,m}$ lies along x-axis. An electric field of magnitude $2.0\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,C^{-1}}$ is switched on at an instant in the region. The unit vector along the electric field is $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\hat{i}+\\frac12\\hat{j}$. The magnitude of the torque acting on the dipole at that instant is :","options":{"A":"$0.5\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m}$","B":"$1.0\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m}$","C":"$2.0\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m}$","D":"$4.0\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{N\\,m}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Torque is $pE\\sin\\theta$ with $\\theta$ the angle between $\\vec p$ and $\\vec E$.","explanation":"Angle between $\\hat{i}$ and the field is $30^\\circ$, so $\\tau=10^{-12}\\times2\\times10^4\\times\\frac12=1.0\\times10^{-8}$ N m.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/2",1]]},{"id":"p0529","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"When the switch of the circuit is turned on, the filament of the bulb glows instantaneously because :","options":{"A":"the electrons coming from the power source move fast through the initially empty filament.","B":"the filament may be old having low resistance.","C":"electric field is established instantaneously across the filament which pushes the electrons.","D":"free electrons in the filament travel with the speed of light."},"answer":"C","hint":"Think about what travels quickly: the field, not the electrons.","explanation":"The field propagates at nearly light speed and sets the already-present free electrons drifting everywhere in the filament.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/2",2]]},{"id":"p0530","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle with charge q moving with velocity $\\vec{v}=v_0\\hat{i}$ enters a region with magnetic field $\\vec{B}=B_1\\hat{j}+B_2\\hat{k}$. The magnitude of force experienced by the particle is :","options":{"A":"$qv_0(B_1+B_2)$","B":"$q\\sqrt{v_0(B_1+B_2)}$","C":"$qv_0\\sqrt{B_1^2+B_2^2}$","D":"$q\\sqrt{v_0(B_1^2+B_2^2)}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\vec F=q\\vec v\\times\\vec B$.","explanation":"$\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j}=\\hat{k}$, $\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{k}=-\\hat{j}$, so $|F|=qv_0\\sqrt{B_1^2+B_2^2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/2",3]]},{"id":"p0531","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"The materials having negative magnetic susceptibility are :","options":{"A":"diamagnetic","B":"paramagnetic","C":"ferromagnetic","D":"non-magnetic"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the sign of $\\chi$ for each class.","explanation":"Diamagnetic materials have small negative susceptibility.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/2",5]]},{"id":"p0532","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"When current in a coil changes at a steady rate from 8 A to 6 A in 4 ms, an emf of 1.5 V is induced in it. The value of self-inductance of the coil is :","options":{"A":"$6\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","B":"$12\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","C":"$3\\ \\mathrm{mH}$","D":"$9\\ \\mathrm{mH}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$|\\varepsilon|=L\\,|dI/dt|$.","explanation":"$dI/dt=2/(4\\times10^{-3})=500\\ \\mathrm{A/s}$, so $L=1.5/500=3$ mH.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/2",6]]},{"id":"p0533","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field in space between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor (each of area $2.5\\times10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$) is changing at the rate of $4\\times10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}s^{-1}}$. The displacement current between the plates of the capacitor is :","options":{"A":"$1.8\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$3.47\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$8.85\\times10^{-8}\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$6.32\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$I_d=\\varepsilon_0A\\,dE/dt$.","explanation":"$I_d=8.85\\times10^{-12}\\times2.5\\times10^{-3}\\times4\\times10^6=8.85\\times10^{-8}$ A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/2",7]]},{"id":"p0534","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two point charges $Q$ and $-q$ are held '$r$' distance apart in free space. A uniform electric field $\\vec{E}$ is applied in the region perpendicular to the line joining the two charges. Which one of the following angles will the direction of the net force acting on charge $-q$ make with the line joining $Q$ and $-q$?","options":{"A":"$\\tan^{-1}\\frac{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 E r^2}{Q}$","B":"$\\cot^{-1}\\frac{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 E r^2}{Q}$","C":"$\\tan^{-1}\\frac{QE}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$","D":"$\\cot^{-1}\\frac{QE}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Resolve the force on $-q$ into the Coulomb force along the line and $qE$ perpendicular to it.","explanation":"Coulomb force is $\\frac{Qq}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2}$ along the line and field force is $qE$ perpendicular, so $\\tan\\theta=\\frac{qE}{Qq/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 r^2)}=\\frac{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 E r^2}{Q}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/3",1]]},{"id":"p0535","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Three wires A, B and C of the same material have lengths and area of cross-sections as $(2l,\\ \\frac{A}{2})$, $(l,\\ A)$ and $(\\frac{l}{2},\\ 2A)$, respectively. If the resistances of these wires are $R_A$, $R_B$ and $R_C$ respectively, then:","options":{"A":"$R_A > R_B > R_C$","B":"$R_B > R_C > R_A$","C":"$R_C > R_A > R_B$","D":"$R_A > R_C > R_B$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $R=\\rho l/A$ for each wire.","explanation":"$R_A=4\\rho l/A$, $R_B=\\rho l/A$, $R_C=\\rho l/(4A)$, so $R_A>R_B>R_C$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/3",2]]},{"id":"p0536","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ moves along $y$-axis in a region in which a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is pointing along $x$-axis. The Lorentz force acting on the charge will point along:","options":{"A":"$x$-axis","B":"$y$-axis","C":"$z$-axis","D":"negative $z$-axis"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=q\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}$ with $\\hat{j}\\times\\hat{i}$.","explanation":"$\\hat{j}\\times\\hat{i}=-\\hat{k}$, so for a positive charge the force is along the negative $z$-axis.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/3",3]]},{"id":"p0537","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following substances has magnetic permeability less than that of free space?","options":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Iron","C":"Aluminium","D":"Copper"},"answer":"D","hint":"Diamagnetic materials have $\\mu<\\mu_0$.","explanation":"Copper is diamagnetic ($\\mu<\\mu_0$); sodium and aluminium are paramagnetic and iron is ferromagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/3",5]]},{"id":"p0538","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux linked with a closed coil (in Wb) varies with time $t$ (in s) as $\\phi = 5t^2 + 4t - 2$. If the resistance of the circuit is $14\\ \\Omega$, the magnitude of induced current in the coil at $t = 1\\ \\mathrm{s}$ will be:","options":{"A":"$0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Induced emf is $-d\\phi/dt$, then use Ohm's law.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=10t+4=14\\ \\mathrm{V}$ at $t=1\\ \\mathrm{s}$, so $I=14/14=1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/3",6]]},{"id":"p0539","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In an electromagnetic wave travelling in free space, the amplitude of magnetic field is $6.0\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{T}$. The amplitude of its electric field is:","options":{"A":"$2\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$","B":"$1.5\\times10^{12}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$","C":"$1.8\\times10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$","D":"$0.3\\times10^{4}\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"In free space $E_0=cB_0$.","explanation":"$E_0=3\\times10^8\\times6\\times10^{-4}=1.8\\times10^5\\ \\mathrm{Vm^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/4/3",7]]},{"id":"p0540","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A metal sheet is inserted between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor of capacitance $C$. If the sheet partly occupies the space between the plates, the capacitance:","options":{"A":"remains $C$","B":"becomes greater than $C$","C":"becomes less than $C$","D":"becomes zero"},"answer":"B","hint":"A conducting slab of thickness $t$ effectively reduces the plate separation.","explanation":"$C'=\\frac{\\varepsilon_0 A}{d-t}>\\frac{\\varepsilon_0 A}{d}=C$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",1],[2025,"R","55/5/3",4]]},{"id":"p0541","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field at a point in a region is given by $\\vec{E}=\\alpha\\frac{\\vec{r}}{|\\vec{r}|^3}$, where $\\alpha$ is a constant and $r$ is the distance of the point from the origin. The magnitude of potential of the point is:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\alpha}{r}$","B":"$\\frac{\\alpha r^2}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{\\alpha}{2r^2}$","D":"$-\\frac{\\alpha}{r}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Integrate $V=-\\int E\\,dr$ with $E=\\alpha/r^2$.","explanation":"$E=\\alpha/r^2$ radially, so $V=\\int_r^\\infty \\frac{\\alpha}{r^2}dr=\\frac{\\alpha}{r}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",2],[2025,"R","55/5/2",3]]},{"id":"p0542","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Four resistors, each of resistance $R$ and a key $K$ are connected as shown in the figure. The equivalent resistance between points A and B when key $K$ is open, will be:","options":{"A":"$4R$","B":"$\\infty$","C":"$\\frac{R}{4}$","D":"$\\frac{4R}{3}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Check whether any conducting path exists between A and B with the key open.","explanation":"With K open there is no closed path from A to B, so resistance is infinite; the official 4R/3 corresponds to K closed (R in series with three R in parallel).","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0542.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",3],[2025,"R","55/5/2",7],[2025,"R","55/5/3",5]]},{"id":"p0543","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A charged particle gains a speed of $10^6\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$, when accelerated from rest through a potential difference $10\\ \\mathrm{kV}$. It enters a region of magnetic field of $0.4\\ \\mathrm{T}$ such that $\\vec{v}\\perp\\vec{B}$. The radius of circular path described by it is:","options":{"A":"$2.5\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","B":"$5\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","C":"$8\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","D":"$10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Find $q/m$ from $qV=\\frac12 mv^2$, then use $r=\\frac{mv}{qB}$.","explanation":"$q/m=v^2/2V=5\\times10^7\\ \\mathrm{C/kg}$, so $r=\\frac{10^6}{5\\times10^7\\times0.4}=0.05\\ \\mathrm{m}=5\\ \\mathrm{cm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",4],[2025,"R","55/5/2",12],[2025,"R","55/5/3",6]]},{"id":"p0544","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A current of $\\left(\\frac{10}{\\pi}\\right)\\mathrm{A}$ is maintained in a circular loop of radius $14\\ \\mathrm{cm}$. The value of dipole moment associated with the loop is:","options":{"A":"$0.019\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","B":"$0.14\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","C":"$0.196\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","D":"$0.615\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Magnetic moment is $m=IA$.","explanation":"$m=\\frac{10}{\\pi}\\times\\pi(0.14)^2=0.196\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",5],[2025,"R","55/5/2",5],[2025,"R","55/5/3",7]]},{"id":"p0545","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux linked with a coil changes with time $t$ as $\\phi=(8t^2+5t+7)$, where $t$ is in seconds and $\\phi$ is in Wb. The value of emf induced in the coil at $t=4\\ \\mathrm{s}$ is:","options":{"A":"$32\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$37\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$64\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$69\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Differentiate flux with respect to time.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=16t+5=69\\ \\mathrm{V}$ at $t=4\\ \\mathrm{s}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",6]]},{"id":"p0546","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following rays coming from the Sun plays an important role in maintaining the Earth's warmth?","options":{"A":"Infrared rays","B":"$\\gamma$ rays","C":"UV rays","D":"Visible light rays"},"answer":"A","hint":"Think of the greenhouse effect and heat radiation.","explanation":"Infrared (heat) radiation is trapped by the atmosphere and keeps the Earth warm.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",7],[2025,"R","55/5/2",1],[2025,"R","55/5/3",8]]},{"id":"p0547","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The dimensions of $(\\mu\\varepsilon)^{-1}$, where $\\varepsilon$ is permittivity and $\\mu$ is permeability of a medium, are:","options":{"A":"$[M^0L^1T^{-1}]$","B":"$[M^0L^2T^{-2}]$","C":"$[M^1L^2T^{-2}]$","D":"$[M^1L^{-1}T^1]$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall $c=1/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}$.","explanation":"$(\\mu\\varepsilon)^{-1}$ has the dimensions of $c^2$, i.e. $[L^2T^{-2}]$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",8],[2025,"R","55/5/2",11],[2025,"R","55/5/3",9]]},{"id":"p0548","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following electromagnetic waves has photons of largest momentum?","options":{"A":"X-rays","B":"AM radio waves","C":"Microwaves","D":"TV waves"},"answer":"A","hint":"Photon momentum is $p=h/\\lambda$.","explanation":"X-rays have the shortest wavelength among these, so the largest photon momentum.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",9],[2025,"R","55/5/2",8],[2025,"R","55/5/3",10]]},{"id":"p0549","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A compound microscope has an objective and an eyepiece of focal lengths $f_o$ and $f_e$, respectively. To obtain a large magnification of a small object, the microscope should have:","options":{"A":"$f_o$ and $f_e$ small, and $f_e>f_o$","B":"$f_o$ and $f_e$ small, and $f_o>f_e$","C":"$f_o$ and $f_e$ large, and $f_e>f_o$","D":"$f_o$ and $f_e$ large, and $f_o>f_e$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Magnification is roughly $\\frac{L}{f_o}\\left(1+\\frac{D}{f_e}\\right)$.","explanation":"Small focal lengths give large magnification, and the objective has the smaller focal length, so $f_e>f_o$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",10]]},{"id":"p0550","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Two coherent light waves, each having amplitude 'a', superpose to produce an interference pattern on a screen. The intensity of light as seen on the screen varies between:","options":{"A":"$0$ and $2a^2$","B":"$0$ and $4a^2$","C":"$a^2$ and $2a^2$","D":"$2a^2$ and $4a^2$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Intensity is proportional to the square of the resultant amplitude.","explanation":"Resultant amplitude ranges from $0$ to $2a$, so intensity ranges from $0$ to $4a^2$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",11],[2025,"R","55/5/2",4],[2025,"R","55/5/3",12]]},{"id":"p0551","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The kinetic energy of an alpha particle is four times the kinetic energy of a proton. The ratio $\\left(\\frac{\\lambda_\\alpha}{\\lambda_p}\\right)$ of de Broglie wavelengths associated with them will be:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{16}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{8}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $\\lambda=\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2mK}}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{\\lambda_\\alpha}{\\lambda_p}=\\sqrt{\\frac{m_pK_p}{m_\\alpha K_\\alpha}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}}=\\frac14$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",12],[2025,"R","55/5/2",9],[2025,"R","55/5/3",11]]},{"id":"p0552","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The impurities in p-type Si are not pentavalent atoms.\nReason (R): The hole density in valance band in p-type semiconductor is almost equal to the acceptor density.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"p-type doping uses trivalent acceptors.","explanation":"A is true (trivalent atoms such as B or Al are used). R is true but does not explain why the impurity is not pentavalent. So R is not the explanation.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",13],[2025,"R","55/5/2",16],[2025,"R","55/5/3",15]]},{"id":"p0553","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): During formation of a nucleus, the mass defect produced is the source of the binding energy of the nucleus.\nReason (R): For all nuclei, the value of binding energy per nucleon increases with mass number.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the binding energy per nucleon curve.","explanation":"A is true ($E=\\Delta m\\,c^2$). R is false since binding energy per nucleon rises, peaks near $A\\approx56$ and then falls.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",14],[2025,"R","55/5/2",15],[2025,"R","55/5/3",13]]},{"id":"p0554","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The Balmer series in hydrogen atom spectrum is formed when the electron jumps from higher energy state to the ground state.\nReason (R): In Bohr's model of hydrogen atom, the electron can jump between successive orbits only.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Balmer lines end on a particular level.","explanation":"A is false (Balmer lines end at $n=2$; transitions to $n=1$ give Lyman). R is false (jumps between any allowed orbits are possible).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",15],[2025,"R","55/5/2",14],[2025,"R","55/5/3",16]]},{"id":"p0555","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In Rutherford's alpha particle scattering experiment, the presence of only few alpha particles at angle of scattering $\\pi$ led him to the discovery of nucleus.\nReason (R): The size of nucleus is approximately $10^{-5}$ times the size of an atom and therefore only few alpha particles are rebounded.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Think about why a tiny nucleus gives few head-on collisions.","explanation":"Both are true: nucleus size is about $10^{-15}$ m versus $10^{-10}$ m for atom, so very few alpha particles hit it head-on and rebound, which led to the nuclear model. R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/1",16],[2025,"R","55/5/2",13],[2025,"R","55/5/3",14]]},{"id":"p0556","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The effective capacitance of the network between points A and B shown in the figure is:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{4}{3}\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$","B":"$2\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$","C":"$\\frac{3}{2}\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$","D":"$6\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Check whether the bridge is balanced so the middle capacitor carries no charge.","explanation":"Bridge is balanced ($3/6=6/12$ ratio of voltages), so $1\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ is ignored; $\\frac{3\\times6}{9}+\\frac{6\\times12}{18}=2+4=6\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0556.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/2",2]]},{"id":"p0557","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A transformer is a device used for converting:","options":{"A":"high ac voltage and large ac current to low ac voltage and small ac current","B":"high ac voltage and small ac current to low ac voltage and small ac current","C":"low ac voltage and large ac current to high ac voltage and small ac current","D":"low ac voltage and small ac current to high ac voltage and large ac current"},"answer":"C","hint":"Power is nearly conserved, so voltage and current change in opposite ways.","explanation":"A step-up transformer raises voltage and lowers current ($V_pI_p\\approx V_sI_s$), converting low voltage and large current to high voltage and small current.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/2",6]]},{"id":"p0558","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A glass slab ($\\mu=1.5$) of thickness $6\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ is placed over a paper. The shift in the letters printed on the paper will be:","options":{"A":"$2\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","B":"$1\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","C":"$4\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","D":"$3\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Normal shift is $t\\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\mu}\\right)$.","explanation":"Shift $=6\\left(1-\\frac{1}{1.5}\\right)=2\\ \\mathrm{cm}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/2",10]]},{"id":"p0559","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"A beam of light of wavelength $720\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ in air enters water (refractive index $=\\frac{4}{3}$). Its wavelength in water will be:","options":{"A":"$540\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$480\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$420\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$720\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Wavelength in a medium is $\\lambda/\\mu$ (frequency is unchanged).","explanation":"$\\lambda'=720/(4/3)=540\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/3",1]]},{"id":"p0560","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A capacitor of capacitance $C$ has reactance $X$ in an ac circuit. If the capacitance and the frequency of the applied voltage are doubled, the new reactance will become:","options":{"A":"$4X$","B":"$2X$","C":"$\\frac{X}{2}$","D":"$\\frac{X}{4}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $X_C=\\frac{1}{2\\pi fC}$.","explanation":"Doubling both $f$ and $C$ makes the denominator four times larger, so $X'=X/4$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/3",2]]},{"id":"p0561","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Four point charges $Q$ each, are held at the four corners of a square of side $l$. The amount of work done in bringing a charge $Q$ from infinity to the centre of the square will be:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{Q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$","B":"$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\,Q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$","C":"$\\frac{Q^2}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$","D":"Zero"},"answer":"B","hint":"Work equals $Q$ times the potential at the centre.","explanation":"Each corner is $l/\\sqrt2$ from the centre, so $V=4\\cdot\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{\\sqrt2}{l}$ and $W=QV=\\frac{\\sqrt2Q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/5/3",3]]},{"id":"p0562","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows the voltage ($V$) versus the current ($I$) graphs for a wire at two temperatures $T_1$ and $T_2$. One can conclude that:","options":{"A":"$T_2=2T_1$","B":"$T_1>T_2$","C":"$T_1=T_2/3$","D":"$T_1<T_2$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Slope of the $I$-$V$ graph is $1/R$ and resistance of a metal rises with temperature.","explanation":"$T_1$ has the larger slope, hence smaller resistance, hence lower temperature: $T_1<T_2$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0562.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",1]]},{"id":"p0563","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"If $R_s$ and $R_p$ are the equivalent resistances of $n$ resistors, each of value $R$, in series and parallel combinations respectively, then the value of $(R_s-R_p)$ is:","options":{"A":"$\\left(\\frac{n^2-1}{n^2}\\right)R$","B":"$\\left(\\frac{n^2+1}{n^2-1}\\right)R$","C":"$\\left(\\frac{n^2-1}{n}\\right)R$","D":"$\\frac{(n^2+1)R}{n^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$R_s=nR$ and $R_p=R/n$.","explanation":"$R_s-R_p=nR-\\frac{R}{n}=\\frac{(n^2-1)R}{n}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",2],[2025,"R","55/6/2",7],[2025,"R","55/6/3",8]]},{"id":"p0564","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"The value of magnetic field at point O in the given figure is:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi R}$","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{\\pi R}$","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4R}$","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{R}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Straight leads along the line through O contribute nothing; use the semicircle formula.","explanation":"Straight parts give zero; semicircle gives $\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi R}\\cdot\\pi=\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4R}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0564.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",3]]},{"id":"p0565","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"A piece of a diamagnetic material, free to move when placed in a uniform magnetic field:","options":{"A":"moves along the field","B":"moves opposite to the field","C":"moves perpendicular to the field","D":"does not move at all"},"answer":"D","hint":"Net force on a magnetised body needs a field gradient.","explanation":"Diamagnetic materials move toward weaker field regions, but in a uniform field there is no gradient, so there is no net force.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",4],[2025,"R","55/6/2",11],[2025,"R","55/6/3",9]]},{"id":"p0566","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer can be converted into an ammeter of desired range by connecting a:","options":{"A":"small resistance in series","B":"large resistance in series","C":"small resistance in parallel","D":"large resistance in parallel"},"answer":"C","hint":"An ammeter must pass most of the current around the galvanometer.","explanation":"A low-value shunt resistance in parallel carries most of the current and gives the desired range.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",5],[2025,"R","55/6/2",8],[2025,"R","55/6/3",11]]},{"id":"p0567","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A proton and an $\\alpha$-particle enter with the same velocity $\\vec{v}$ in a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ such that $\\vec{v}\\perp\\vec{B}$. The ratio of the radii of their paths is:","options":{"A":"$2$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","D":"$4$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Radius is $r=\\frac{mv}{qB}\\propto\\frac{m}{q}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{r_p}{r_\\alpha}=\\frac{m_p/q_p}{m_\\alpha/q_\\alpha}=\\frac{1}{4/2}=\\frac12$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",6],[2025,"R","55/6/2",5],[2025,"R","55/6/3",7]]},{"id":"p0568","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A vertically held bar magnet is dropped along the axis of a copper ring having a cut as shown in the diagram. The acceleration of the falling magnet is:","options":{"A":"zero","B":"less than $g$","C":"$g$","D":"greater than $g$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Is a current able to flow in a ring that has a cut?","explanation":"The cut makes an open ring, so no induced current and no opposing force flows; the magnet falls freely with acceleration $g$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0568.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",7],[2025,"R","55/6/2",2],[2025,"R","55/6/3",4]]},{"id":"p0569","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ac source is connected to a resistor and an inductor in series. The voltage across the resistor and inductor are $8\\ \\mathrm{V}$ and $6\\ \\mathrm{V}$ respectively. The voltage of the source is:","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$12\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$14\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$16\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$V_R$ and $V_L$ are $90^\\circ$ out of phase.","explanation":"$V=\\sqrt{V_R^2+V_L^2}=\\sqrt{64+36}=10\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",8],[2025,"R","55/6/2",4],[2025,"R","55/6/3",6]]},{"id":"p0570","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Two coherent waves, each of intensity $I_0$, produce interference pattern on a screen. The average intensity of light on the screen is:","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$I_0$","C":"$2I_0$","D":"$4I_0$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Average of $4I_0\\cos^2(\\phi/2)$ over a cycle.","explanation":"$I=2I_0(1+\\cos\\phi)$ averages to $2I_0$, consistent with energy conservation.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",9],[2025,"R","55/6/2",6],[2025,"R","55/6/3",2]]},{"id":"p0571","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The work function of a material is $2.21\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. Which of the following light cannot produce photoelectrons from it?","options":{"A":"Red light","B":"Blue light","C":"Violet light","D":"Green light"},"answer":"A","hint":"Threshold wavelength is $\\lambda_0=\\frac{1240}{\\phi\\ (\\mathrm{eV})}\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","explanation":"$\\lambda_0\\approx561\\ \\mathrm{nm}$; red light has $\\lambda>650\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ and lower photon energy, so it cannot eject electrons.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",10]]},{"id":"p0572","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The momentum (in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m/s}$) of a photon of frequency $6.0\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ is:","options":{"A":"$6.63\\times10^{-25}$","B":"$1.326\\times10^{-27}$","C":"$2.652\\times10^{-26}$","D":"$3.978\\times10^{-24}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $p=\\frac{h\\nu}{c}$.","explanation":"$p=\\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}\\times6\\times10^{14}}{3\\times10^8}=1.326\\times10^{-27}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",11],[2025,"R","55/6/2",12],[2025,"R","55/6/3",12]]},{"id":"p0573","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Inside a nucleus, the nuclear forces between proton and proton, proton and neutron, neutron and neutron are $F_{pp}$, $F_{pn}$ and $F_{nn}$ respectively. Then:","options":{"A":"$F_{pp}>F_{pn}>F_{nn}$","B":"$F_{pn}>F_{nn}>F_{pp}$","C":"$F_{nn}>F_{pp}>F_{pn}$","D":"$F_{pp}=F_{pn}=F_{nn}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Nuclear force is charge independent.","explanation":"The strong nuclear force does not depend on charge, so the forces between any two nucleons (at the same separation) are equal.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",12],[2025,"R","55/6/2",9],[2025,"R","55/6/3",5]]},{"id":"p0574","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a reflecting telescope, the image does not have chromatic aberration.\nReason (R): Chromatic aberration occurs only due to refraction of light through an optical medium.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"A mirror reflects all colours alike.","explanation":"Both true: a mirror objective involves no refraction, so no dispersion, and R is exactly why A holds.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",13],[2025,"R","55/6/2",14],[2025,"R","55/6/3",15]]},{"id":"p0575","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A hole is an apparent free particle with effective positive electronic charge.\nReason (R): A hole is not necessarily a vacancy left behind by an electron in the valence band.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"A hole is the absence of an electron in the valence band.","explanation":"A is true. R is false: a hole is precisely a vacancy left by an electron in the valence band.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",14],[2025,"R","55/6/2",13],[2025,"R","55/6/3",16]]},{"id":"p0576","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): X-rays are produced when slow moving electrons are stopped by a metal target of high atomic number.\nReason (R): X-rays consist of low-energy photons.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"X-ray production needs fast, high-energy electrons.","explanation":"A is false (fast moving electrons are needed). R is false (X-ray photons are high energy).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",15],[2025,"R","55/6/2",16],[2025,"R","55/6/3",13]]},{"id":"p0577","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The binding energy per nucleon is practically constant for mass number in the range ($30<A<170$).\nReason (R): Nuclear forces between the nucleons for mass numbers in the range ($30<A<170$) are not short-range.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall saturation due to short-range nuclear force.","explanation":"A is true (plateau near 8 MeV). R is false as nuclear forces are short-range, which causes saturation.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/1",16],[2025,"R","55/6/2",15],[2025,"R","55/6/3",14]]},{"id":"p0578","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A wire of resistance $R$, connected to an ideal battery consumes a power $P$. If the wire is gradually stretched to double its initial length, and connected across the same battery, the power consumed will be:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{P}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{P}{2}$","C":"$P$","D":"$2P$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Stretching keeps volume fixed, so $R\\propto l^2$; use $P=V^2/R$.","explanation":"Length doubles and area halves, so $R'=4R$ and $P'=V^2/4R=P/4$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/2",1]]},{"id":"p0579","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A straight conductor is carrying a current of $2\\ \\mathrm{A}$ in $+x$ direction along it. A uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}=(0.6\\,\\hat{j}+0.8\\,\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{T}$ is switched on, in the region. The force acting on $10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ length of the conductor is:","options":{"A":"$(0.12\\,\\hat{j}-0.16\\,\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{N}$","B":"$(-0.16\\,\\hat{j}+0.12\\,\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{N}$","C":"$(-0.12\\,\\hat{j}+0.16\\,\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{N}$","D":"$(0.16\\,\\hat{j}-0.12\\,\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{N}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $\\vec{F}=I\\,\\vec{L}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$\\vec F=0.2\\,\\hat{i}\\times(0.6\\hat{j}+0.8\\hat{k})=0.2(0.6\\hat{k}-0.8\\hat{j})=(-0.16\\hat{j}+0.12\\hat{k})\\ \\mathrm{N}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/2",3]]},{"id":"p0580","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The de Broglie wavelength associated with an electron moving with energy $5\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ is:","options":{"A":"$0.75\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$1.2\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$2.4\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$0.55\\ \\mathrm{nm}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\lambda=\\frac{1.227}{\\sqrt{E\\ (\\mathrm{eV})}}\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","explanation":"$\\lambda=\\frac{1.227}{\\sqrt5}\\approx0.55\\ \\mathrm{nm}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/2",10]]},{"id":"p0581","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of potential difference across AB in the circuit shown for the case (i) when switch S is closed and (ii) when S is open is:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"$1$","D":"$2$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Find $R_{BC}$ in each case and use the voltage divider with $2R$.","explanation":"S closed: $R_{BC}=\\frac{3R\\cdot6R}{9R}=2R$, $V_{AB}=V/2$. S open: $R_{BC}=6R$, $V_{AB}=V/4$. Ratio is $2$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0581.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/3",1]]},{"id":"p0582","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ moving with velocity $v\\,\\hat{i}$ is subjected to a uniform electric field $E\\,\\hat{j}$. The particle will initially have a tendency to move in a circle of radius:","options":{"A":"$\\left(\\frac{mv}{qE}\\right)$ in $x$-$y$ plane","B":"$\\left(\\frac{mv^2}{qE^2}\\right)$ in $x$-$z$ plane","C":"$\\left(\\frac{mv^2}{qE}\\right)$ in $x$-$y$ plane","D":"$\\left(\\frac{mv}{qE^2}\\right)$ in $y$-$z$ plane"},"answer":"C","hint":"Acceleration perpendicular to velocity acts as centripetal acceleration, $a=v^2/r$.","explanation":"Force $qE$ along $y$ gives $a=qE/m$ perpendicular to $v$ along $x$, so $r=\\frac{v^2}{a}=\\frac{mv^2}{qE}$ in the $x$-$y$ plane.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/3",3]]},{"id":"p0583","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The wavelength of a photon is equal to the wavelength associated with an electron. Both will have the same value of:","options":{"A":"energy","B":"angular momentum","C":"velocity","D":"linear momentum"},"answer":"D","hint":"Both obey $p=h/\\lambda$.","explanation":"For both photon and electron $\\lambda=h/p$, so equal wavelength means equal linear momentum.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/6/3",10]]},{"id":"p0584","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Two horizontal plates, separated by $1\\ \\mathrm{cm}$, are arranged one above the other. A particle of mass $5\\ \\mathrm{mg}$ and charge $2\\ \\mathrm{nC}$ is released in air between the plates. The potential difference that should be applied to the plates so that the particle remains suspended between them, is:","options":{"A":"$250\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$200\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$100\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$50\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Balance $qE=mg$ with $E=V/d$.","explanation":"$V=\\frac{mgd}{q}=\\frac{5\\times10^{-6}\\times10\\times10^{-2}}{2\\times10^{-9}}=250\\ \\mathrm{V}$ (with $g=10\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",1]]},{"id":"p0585","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The effective resistance between points A and B in the given circuit is :","options":{"A":"$6\\ \\Omega$","B":"$\\frac{8}{3}\\ \\Omega$","C":"$\\frac{16}{3}\\ \\Omega$","D":"$2\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Combine parallel and series pieces and check whether the bridge is balanced.","explanation":"$4+2=6\\ \\Omega$ in parallel with $3\\ \\Omega$ gives $2\\ \\Omega$; $18\\ \\Omega\\parallel 9\\ \\Omega=6\\ \\Omega$. The bridge ($2,6,4,12$) is balanced, so the $7\\ \\Omega$ carries no current and $R=(2+6)\\parallel(4+12)=\\frac{16}{3}\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0585.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",2],[2025,"R","55/7/2",7]]},{"id":"p0586","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A rectangular coil of area A is kept in a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ such that the plane of the coil makes an angle $\\alpha$ with $\\vec{B}$. The magnetic flux linked with the coil is :","options":{"A":"$BA\\sin\\alpha$","B":"$BA\\cos\\alpha$","C":"$BA$","D":"zero"},"answer":"A","hint":"Flux uses the angle between B and the normal to the plane.","explanation":"The normal makes angle $90^\\circ-\\alpha$ with $\\vec{B}$, so $\\phi=BA\\cos(90^\\circ-\\alpha)=BA\\sin\\alpha$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",3],[2025,"R","55/7/3",9]]},{"id":"p0587","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An alternating current is given by $I=I_0\\cos(100\\pi)t$. The least time the current takes to decrease from its maximum value to zero will be :","options":{"A":"$\\left(\\frac{1}{200}\\right)\\mathrm{s}$","B":"$\\left(\\frac{1}{150}\\right)\\mathrm{s}$","C":"$\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)\\mathrm{s}$","D":"$\\left(\\frac{1}{50}\\right)\\mathrm{s}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Maximum to zero takes a quarter of the period.","explanation":"$\\omega=100\\pi$ gives $T=\\frac{1}{50}\\ \\mathrm{s}$; time $=T/4=\\frac{1}{200}\\ \\mathrm{s}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",4],[2025,"R","55/7/2",9],[2025,"R","55/7/3",8]]},{"id":"p0588","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A capacitor and an inductor are connected in series across an ac source of voltage of variable frequency. The frequency is increased continuously. The nature of the circuit before and after the resonance will be :","options":{"A":"inductive only","B":"capacitive only","C":"capacitive and inductive respectively","D":"inductive and capacitive respectively"},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare $X_C$ and $X_L$ below and above the resonant frequency.","explanation":"Below resonance $X_C>X_L$ (capacitive); above resonance $X_L>X_C$ (inductive).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",5],[2025,"R","55/7/2",8],[2025,"R","55/7/3",11]]},{"id":"p0589","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A metal rod of length $50\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ is held vertically and moved with a velocity of $10\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ towards east. The horizontal component of the Earth's magnetic field at the place is $0.4\\ \\mathrm{G}$. The emf induced across the ends of the rod is :","options":{"A":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{mV}$","B":"$0.2\\ \\mathrm{mV}$","C":"$0.8\\ \\mathrm{mV}$","D":"$1.6\\ \\mathrm{mV}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Motional emf $Blv$ with $1\\ \\mathrm{G}=10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{T}$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=Blv=0.4\\times10^{-4}\\times0.5\\times10=2\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{V}=0.2\\ \\mathrm{mV}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",6],[2025,"R","55/7/2",5],[2025,"R","55/7/3",6]]},{"id":"p0590","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The dimensions of 'self-inductance' are :","options":{"A":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L\\,T^{-2}A^{-2}}]$","B":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L^{2}T^{-1}A^{-1}}]$","C":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L^{-1}T^{-2}A^{-2}}]$","D":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L^{2}T^{-2}A^{-2}}]$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $U=\\frac{1}{2}LI^2$.","explanation":"$L=2U/I^2$ gives $[\\mathrm{ML^2T^{-2}}]/[\\mathrm{A^2}]=[\\mathrm{ML^2T^{-2}A^{-2}}]$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",7],[2025,"R","55/7/2",2],[2025,"R","55/7/3",4]]},{"id":"p0591","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The frequency of a photon of energy $1.326\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ is :","options":{"A":"$1.18\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","B":"$3.20\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","C":"$4.20\\times10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","D":"$4.80\\times10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $E=h\\nu$ after converting eV to joule.","explanation":"$\\nu=\\frac{1.326\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}}{6.63\\times10^{-34}}=3.2\\times10^{14}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",8],[2025,"R","55/7/2",4],[2025,"R","55/7/3",7]]},{"id":"p0592","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Germanium crystal is doped at room temperature with a minute quantity of boron. The charge carriers in the doped semiconductors will be :","options":{"A":"electrons only","B":"holes only","C":"holes and few electrons","D":"electrons and few holes"},"answer":"C","hint":"Boron is a trivalent (acceptor) impurity.","explanation":"Boron makes a p-type semiconductor: holes are majority carriers and thermally generated electrons are few.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",9],[2025,"R","55/7/2",6],[2025,"R","55/7/3",2]]},{"id":"p0593","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Out of the four options given, in which transition will the emitted photon have the maximum wavelength ?","options":{"A":"$n=4$ to $n=3$","B":"$n=3$ to $n=2$","C":"$n=2$ to $n=1$","D":"$n=3$ to $n=1$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Maximum wavelength means minimum energy gap.","explanation":"The gap between $n=4$ and $n=3$ is the smallest ($0.66\\ \\mathrm{eV}$), so $\\lambda$ is largest.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",10]]},{"id":"p0594","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"A p-n junction diode is forward biased. As a result,","options":{"A":"both the potential barrier height and the width of depletion layer decrease.","B":"both the potential barrier height and the width of depletion layer increase.","C":"the potential barrier height decreases and the width of depletion layer increases.","D":"the potential barrier height increases and the width of depletion layer decreases."},"answer":"A","hint":"Forward bias opposes the built-in field.","explanation":"Forward bias reduces the barrier height and narrows the depletion layer.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",11],[2025,"R","55/7/2",12],[2025,"R","55/7/3",12]]},{"id":"p0595","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Isotones are the nuclides having :","options":{"A":"same mass numbers","B":"same atomic numbers","C":"same neutron number, but different atomic number","D":"different neutron number, and different mass number"},"answer":"C","hint":"Iso-tones means equal number of neutrons.","explanation":"Isotones have equal neutron number $N=A-Z$ but different $Z$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",12],[2025,"R","55/7/2",11],[2025,"R","55/7/3",5]]},{"id":"p0596","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A charged particle is moving with velocity $v$ in x-y plane, making an angle $\\theta\\ (0<\\theta<\\frac{\\pi}{2})$ with x-axis. If a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ is applied in the region, along y-axis, the particle will move in a helical path with its axis parallel to x-axis.\nReason (R): The direction of the magnetic force acting on a charged particle moving in a magnetic field is along the velocity of the particle.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Helix axis is along the velocity component parallel to B; force is $q\\vec{v}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"The component of velocity along $\\vec{B}$ (y-axis) is nonzero, so the axis is parallel to y, not x: A false. Force is perpendicular to velocity: R false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",13],[2025,"R","55/7/2",14],[2025,"R","55/7/3",14]]},{"id":"p0597","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A ray of light is incident normally on the face of a prism. The emergent ray will graze along the opposite face of the prism when the critical angle at glass-air interface is equal to the angle of the prism.\nReason (R): The refractive index of a prism depends on angle of the prism.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Angle of incidence at second face equals the prism angle for normal incidence.","explanation":"Normal incidence gives $i_2=A$; grazing emergence needs $i_2=C$, so A is true. Refractive index depends on the material and wavelength, not prism angle: R false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",14],[2025,"R","55/7/2",13],[2025,"R","55/7/3",16]]},{"id":"p0598","chapter":8,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): EM waves do not require a medium for their propagation.\nReason (R): EM waves are transverse waves.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Both statements are true facts; ask whether transversality explains medium-independence.","explanation":"Both true, but being transverse does not explain absence of a medium (it is due to self-sustaining oscillating E and B fields).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",15],[2025,"R","55/7/2",16],[2025,"R","55/7/3",13]]},{"id":"p0599","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The minimum negative potential applied to the anode in a photoelectric experiment at which photoelectric current becomes zero, is called cut-off voltage.\nReason (R): The threshold frequency for a metal is the minimum frequency of incident radiation below which emission of photoelectrons does not take place.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Define stopping potential and threshold frequency separately.","explanation":"Both are correct definitions, but threshold frequency does not explain what cut-off voltage is.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/1",16],[2025,"R","55/7/2",15],[2025,"R","55/7/3",15]]},{"id":"p0600","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field (E) and electric potential (V) at a point inside a charged hollow metallic sphere are respectively :","options":{"A":"$E=0,\\ V=0$","B":"$E=0,\\ V=V_0$ (a constant)","C":"$E\\neq0,\\ V\\neq0$","D":"$E=E_0$ (a constant), $V=0$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Inside a conductor the field vanishes and the potential is uniform.","explanation":"Inside the shell $E=0$ and potential equals the surface value $V_0$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/2",1]]},{"id":"p0601","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"In a circular loop of radius R, current I enters at point A and exits at point B, as shown in the figure. The value of the magnetic field at the centre O of the loop is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{R}$","B":"zero","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2R}$","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4R}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"The current splits between two arcs in inverse ratio of their lengths; compare their fields at O.","explanation":"Arcs subtend $90^\\circ$ and $270^\\circ$, carrying $\\frac{3I}{4}$ and $\\frac{I}{4}$. Fields $\\propto I\\theta$ are equal ($\\frac{3I}{4}\\cdot\\frac{\\pi}{2}=\\frac{I}{4}\\cdot\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$) and opposite, so $B=0$.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0601.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/2",3]]},{"id":"p0602","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The mass numbers of two nuclei A and B are 27 and 64 respectively. The ratio of their radii $\\left(\\frac{r_A}{r_B}\\right)$ will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{27}{64}$","B":"$\\frac{9}{16}$","C":"$\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}$","D":"$\\frac{3}{4}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Nuclear radius scales as $A^{1/3}$.","explanation":"$\\frac{r_A}{r_B}=\\left(\\frac{27}{64}\\right)^{1/3}=\\frac{3}{4}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/2",10]]},{"id":"p0603","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"In the circuit shown, the charge on the left plate of $20\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ capacitor is $-50\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$. The charge on the right plate of the $12\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ capacitor is :","options":{"A":"$20\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$","B":"$-20\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$","C":"$30\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$","D":"$-30\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Series elements carry the same charge; a parallel pair shares it in proportion to capacitance.","explanation":"The parallel pair carries $50\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ in total, split $12:8$, so $12\\ \\mu\\mathrm{F}$ gets $30\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ with $+30\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ on its right plate.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0603.png","sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/3",1]]},{"id":"p0604","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A steady current I is passed through a conductor at room temperature for time t. It is observed that its temperature rises by $0.5^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$. If 2I current is passed through the conductor (at room temperature) for the same duration, the rise in its temperature will be approximately :","options":{"A":"$1.0^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$","B":"$1.5^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$","C":"$2.0^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$","D":"$4.0^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Heat produced is $I^2Rt$.","explanation":"Heating is proportional to $I^2$, so doubling the current gives four times the rise: $4\\times0.5=2.0^\\circ\\mathrm{C}$ (ignoring change of resistance).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/3",3]]},{"id":"p0605","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The minimum energy required to free the electron from the ground state of the hydrogen atom is :","options":{"A":"$3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","B":"less than $3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","C":"$13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$","D":"between $13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ and $3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"This is the ionisation energy.","explanation":"Ground state energy is $-13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$, so $13.6\\ \\mathrm{eV}$ is needed to ionise.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2025,"R","55/7/3",10]]},{"id":"p0606","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"In a printer, neutral ink droplets are passed through an electron beam and some electrons are captured by the ink droplets and become charged. The net charge on an ink droplet is found to be $-1\\times10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{C}$. The number of electrons captured by this ink droplet is :","options":{"A":"$1.6\\times10^{4}$","B":"$10^{8}$","C":"$6.25\\times10^{6}$","D":"$8\\times10^{9}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Charge is quantised: $q=ne$.","explanation":"$n=\\frac{10^{-12}}{1.6\\times10^{-19}}=6.25\\times10^{6}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",1]]},{"id":"p0607","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following is not correct about the electrostatic force between two charges ?","options":{"A":"The magnitude of the force is directly proportional to the magnitudes of the two charges.","B":"It is inversely proportional to the square of the distance between the two charges.","C":"It depends on the permittivity of the medium in which the charges are placed.","D":"It is inversely proportional to the distance between the two charges."},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall the form of Coulomb's law.","explanation":"$F=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon}\\frac{q_1q_2}{r^2}$, so it varies as $1/r^2$, not $1/r$; option D is incorrect.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",2]]},{"id":"p0608","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"$110\\ \\mathrm{kW}$ electric power is supplied to the primary coil of an ideal transformer. If the output voltage is $22\\ \\mathrm{kV}$, the output current will be :","options":{"A":"$50\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$25\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$5\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"An ideal transformer has output power equal to input power.","explanation":"$I_s=\\frac{P}{V_s}=\\frac{110\\times10^3}{22\\times10^3}=5\\ \\mathrm{A}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",3]]},{"id":"p0609","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Choose the correct statement :","options":{"A":"Visible light has higher frequencies and shorter wavelengths than that of X-rays.","B":"Visible light has lower frequencies and shorter wavelengths than that of X-rays.","C":"Visible light has higher frequencies and longer wavelengths than that of X-rays.","D":"Visible light has lower frequencies and longer wavelengths than that of X-rays."},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall the order of the electromagnetic spectrum.","explanation":"X-rays lie at higher frequency and shorter wavelength than visible light, so visible light has lower frequency and longer wavelength.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",5]]},{"id":"p0610","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A girl standing $6.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ from a convex mirror sees her virtual image $1.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ behind the mirror. The focal length of the mirror is :","options":{"A":"$6.0\\ \\mathrm{m}$","B":"$1.2\\ \\mathrm{m}$","C":"$2.4\\ \\mathrm{m}$","D":"$0.8\\ \\mathrm{m}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use the mirror formula with sign convention.","explanation":"$u=-6$, $v=+1$: $\\frac{1}{f}=1-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{5}{6}$, so $f=1.2\\ \\mathrm{m}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",6]]},{"id":"p0611","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The momentum of a photon of wavelength $663\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ is :","options":{"A":"$10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$","B":"$10^{-25}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$","C":"$10^{-27}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$","D":"$10^{-29}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Photon momentum is $h/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$p=\\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}}{663\\times10^{-9}}=10^{-27}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",7]]},{"id":"p0612","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The velocity of an electron moving around a proton in an orbit of radius $5.3\\times10^{-11}\\ \\mathrm{m}$ is $2.2\\times10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. Its frequency of revolution is :","options":{"A":"$6.6\\times10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","B":"$5.2\\times10^{13}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","C":"$4.3\\times10^{12}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$","D":"$3.9\\times10^{10}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Frequency $=v/(2\\pi r)$.","explanation":"$f=\\frac{2.2\\times10^{6}}{2\\pi\\times5.3\\times10^{-11}}\\approx6.6\\times10^{15}\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",8]]},{"id":"p0613","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In alpha-particle scattering experiment, the impact parameter is minimum when the scattering angle is close to :","options":{"A":"$45^\\circ$","B":"$90^\\circ$","C":"$135^\\circ$","D":"$180^\\circ$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Head-on collisions give the largest deflection.","explanation":"A head-on approach (impact parameter near zero) leads to scattering through nearly $180^\\circ$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",9]]},{"id":"p0614","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr's model of hydrogen atom, the angular momentum of electron in second orbit is equal to :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{h}{2\\pi}$","B":"$\\frac{h}{\\pi}$","C":"$\\frac{2h}{\\pi}$","D":"$\\frac{h}{4\\pi}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Bohr quantisation: $L=\\frac{nh}{2\\pi}$.","explanation":"For $n=2$, $L=\\frac{2h}{2\\pi}=\\frac{h}{\\pi}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",10]]},{"id":"p0615","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The value of potential barrier across a depletion region $2.0\\ \\mu\\mathrm{m}$ wide is $0.4\\ \\mathrm{V}$. The value of the electric field in this region is :","options":{"A":"$5\\times10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$","B":"$5\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$","C":"$0.8\\times10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$","D":"$2.0\\times10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Field $=V/d$.","explanation":"$E=\\frac{0.4}{2.0\\times10^{-6}}=2.0\\times10^{5}\\ \\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",11]]},{"id":"p0616","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following element can be used as dopant in Si crystal to obtain a p-type semiconductor ?","options":{"A":"Phosphorus","B":"Arsenic","C":"Boron","D":"Antimony"},"answer":"C","hint":"p-type needs a trivalent acceptor.","explanation":"Boron is trivalent (group 13) and creates holes in Si; the others are pentavalent donors.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",12]]},{"id":"p0617","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When a magnet is quickly brought close to one face of a coil whose two ends are not connected to each other, no current flows in the coil.\nReason (R): Though the emf is induced, but since the coil is not closed, no current flows in the coil.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Emf is induced by changing flux, but current needs a closed path.","explanation":"Both true: emf is induced but with an open circuit there is no current, which is exactly why A holds.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",13]]},{"id":"p0618","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The phase difference between any two points on a wavefront is zero.\nReason (R): A wavefront is the locus of all those points which are in the same phase.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the definition of a wavefront.","explanation":"A wavefront joins points in the same phase, so the phase difference between any two of its points is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",14]]},{"id":"p0619","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The ultraviolet light is replaced by X-rays in a photoelectric experiment. The kinetic energy of the emitted electrons decreases.\nReason (R): Frequency of X-rays is less than that of ultraviolet light.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi$ and compare frequencies.","explanation":"X-rays have higher frequency than UV, so $K_{max}$ increases; both A and R are false.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",15]]},{"id":"p0620","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Nuclei of different atoms have different sizes.\nReason (R): The density of nuclear matter is same for all nuclei.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Constant density with $R=R_0A^{1/3}$ gives size depending on A.","explanation":"Both are true. Official scheme gives B, though constant nuclear density (so $R\\propto A^{1/3}$) is arguably the explanation.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55(B)/7",16]]},{"id":"p0621","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Assume that the surface charge density of the Earth is 1 electron charge/$\\mathrm{m}^2$. The potential of the Earth's surface (radius $6400\\ \\mathrm{km}$) will be :","options":{"A":"$-0.12\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$-0.21\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$+2.1\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$+1.2\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Potential of a uniformly charged sphere is $\\frac{\\sigma R}{\\varepsilon_0}$.","explanation":"$V=\\frac{\\sigma R}{\\varepsilon_0}=\\frac{1.6\\times10^{-19}\\times6.4\\times10^{6}}{8.85\\times10^{-12}}\\approx0.12\\ \\mathrm{V}$, negative since the charge is electronic.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",1],[2026,"C","55/7/2",11],[2026,"C","55/7/3",9]]},{"id":"p0622","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A conducting sphere of radius R has a charge Q on it. Which of the following graphs correctly represents the value of electric field E in the range $0\\leq x<\\infty$, where x is the distance of the point from the centre of the sphere ?","options":{"A":"Graph (A): E constant (nonzero) for $x<R$, then falling as $1/x^2$","B":"Graph (B): E rising non-linearly from zero to a peak at $x=R$, then falling","C":"Graph (C): E zero for $x<R$, then falling from a maximum at $x=R$","D":"Graph (D): E rising linearly from zero to a peak at $x=R$, then falling"},"answer":"C","hint":"Field inside a conductor is zero; outside it follows Coulomb's law.","explanation":"$E=0$ inside the conductor and $E=\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0x^2}$ outside, jumping to its maximum at the surface, which is graph (C).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0622.png","sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",2],[2026,"C","55/7/2",1]]},{"id":"p0623","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two infinitely long straight conductors lie along x-axis and y-axis in the plane of the page. If they carry current I each along $+x$-axis and $+y$-axis respectively, the net magnetic field at a point with position vector $\\vec{r}=(3\\hat{i}+4\\hat{j})\\ \\mathrm{m}$ will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{6\\pi}\\hat{k}$","B":"$-\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{12\\pi}\\hat{k}$","C":"$-\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{24\\pi}\\hat{k}$","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{48\\pi}\\hat{k}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Superpose $\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi d}$ from each wire with directions from the right-hand rule.","explanation":"The x-wire (distance 4) gives $+\\frac{\\mu_0I}{8\\pi}\\hat{k}$; the y-wire (distance 3) gives $-\\frac{\\mu_0I}{6\\pi}\\hat{k}$. Net $=-\\frac{\\mu_0I}{24\\pi}\\hat{k}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",3],[2026,"C","55/7/2",7],[2026,"C","55/7/3",8]]},{"id":"p0624","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"In an electromagnetic wave, the electric field ($\\vec{E}$) and the magnetic field ($\\vec{B}$) oscillate along x-axis and y-axis respectively. The wave is propagating along :","options":{"A":"$z$-axis","B":"$-z$-axis","C":"$x$-axis","D":"$-y$-axis"},"answer":"A","hint":"Propagation direction is along $\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B}$.","explanation":"$\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j}=\\hat{k}$, so the wave travels along the $+z$ axis.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",4],[2026,"C","55/7/2",2],[2026,"C","55/7/3",10]]},{"id":"p0625","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following is also referred to as 'heat waves' ?","options":{"A":"Gamma rays","B":"X-rays","C":"UV rays","D":"Infrared rays"},"answer":"D","hint":"Think of which radiation is strongly absorbed by water and produces heating.","explanation":"Infrared radiation is called heat waves.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",5],[2026,"C","55/7/2",12],[2026,"C","55/7/3",7]]},{"id":"p0626","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In a series LCR circuit connected to an ac source of voltage V and frequency $\\omega$, the power loss at resonance is :","options":{"A":"$I^2\\omega L$","B":"$V^2/(\\omega C)$","C":"$V^2/R$","D":"$V^2/\\left(\\omega L-\\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\right)$"},"answer":"C","hint":"At resonance the impedance equals R.","explanation":"At resonance $Z=R$, so $P=\\frac{V^2}{R}$ (with $V$ the rms voltage).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",6],[2026,"C","55/7/2",3],[2026,"C","55/7/3",11]]},{"id":"p0627","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"If $\\nu_X$, $\\nu_G$ and $\\nu_U$ are frequencies of X-rays, Gamma-rays and UV rays respectively, then :","options":{"A":"$\\nu_X>\\nu_G>\\nu_U$","B":"$\\nu_G>\\nu_X>\\nu_U$","C":"$\\nu_U>\\nu_X>\\nu_G$","D":"$\\nu_X>\\nu_U>\\nu_G$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall the order of the EM spectrum by frequency.","explanation":"Frequency increases UV, X-rays, gamma rays, so $\\nu_G>\\nu_X>\\nu_U$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",7],[2026,"C","55/7/2",4],[2026,"C","55/7/3",6]]},{"id":"p0628","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of an electron in an orbit in a hydrogen atom (Bohr model) is $-3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. Its angular momentum in this orbit is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{h}{\\pi}$","B":"$\\frac{h}{2\\pi}$","C":"$\\frac{h}{4\\pi}$","D":"$\\frac{2h}{\\pi}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$E_n=-13.6/n^2$ eV fixes $n$, then $L=nh/2\\pi$.","explanation":"$-3.4=-13.6/n^2$ gives $n=2$, so $L=\\frac{2h}{2\\pi}=\\frac{h}{\\pi}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",8]]},{"id":"p0629","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of light is incident at an angle of $60^\\circ$ on face AB of prism ABC with $\\angle A=30^\\circ$. The ray emerging out of the prism makes an angle of $30^\\circ$ with the incident ray. The angle of emergence ($\\angle e$) is equal to :","options":{"A":"$0^\\circ$","B":"$30^\\circ$","C":"$60^\\circ$","D":"$90^\\circ$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $\\delta=i+e-A$.","explanation":"$30^\\circ=60^\\circ+e-30^\\circ$ gives $e=0^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",9]]},{"id":"p0630","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A point source in air is placed in front of convex spherical glass surface ($n=1.5$ and $R=10\\ \\mathrm{cm}$). Its real image is formed at the same distance as the surface from the source. The distance of the source from the surface is :","options":{"A":"$25\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","B":"$50\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","C":"$75\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","D":"$100\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Refraction at a spherical surface: $\\frac{n_2}{v}-\\frac{n_1}{u}=\\frac{n_2-n_1}{R}$.","explanation":"With $u=-d$, $v=+d$: $\\frac{1.5}{d}+\\frac{1}{d}=\\frac{0.5}{10}$, so $d=50\\ \\mathrm{cm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",10]]},{"id":"p0631","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In forward and reverse biased silicon p-n junction, the dominant mechanisms for the motion of charge carriers are :","options":{"A":"diffusion in forward bias, drift in reverse bias","B":"drift in both forward bias and in reverse bias","C":"drift in forward and diffusion in reverse bias","D":"diffusion in both forward and reverse bias"},"answer":"A","hint":"Consider which current dominates when the barrier is lowered versus raised.","explanation":"Forward bias lowers the barrier so majority carriers diffuse across; in reverse bias only minority-carrier drift current flows.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",11],[2026,"C","55/7/2",5],[2026,"C","55/7/3",4]]},{"id":"p0632","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Five resistors are joined as shown in the figure. Between the points A and B, a battery of $6\\ \\mathrm{V}$ is connected. How much current is flowing in arm FC ?","options":{"A":"$2.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","B":"$1.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$","C":"$1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$","D":"$0.5\\ \\mathrm{A}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Check whether the bridge is balanced.","explanation":"Between F and E: $\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}$ so the bridge is balanced and the $2\\ \\Omega$ resistor in CD carries no current. Path F-C-E is $6\\ \\Omega$, so $I_{FC}=\\frac{6}{6}=1.0\\ \\mathrm{A}$.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0632.png","sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",12],[2026,"C","55/7/2",6],[2026,"C","55/7/3",2]]},{"id":"p0633","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): If $\\vec{B}$ be the magnetic field at a small patch of vector area $d\\vec{S}$, then the magnetic flux through the patch will be $\\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{S}$.\nReason (R): The magnetic flux is a scalar quantity.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Flux is defined by a dot product; ask whether being scalar justifies the formula.","explanation":"Both true, but the scalar nature of flux does not explain why it equals $\\vec{B}\\cdot d\\vec{S}$ (that is its definition).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",13],[2026,"C","55/7/2",16],[2026,"C","55/7/3",15]]},{"id":"p0634","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The ratio of the speeds of the electron in the second to that in the third orbit in Bohr's hydrogen atom is $\\frac{3}{2}$.\nReason (R): The speed of the electron in $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ permitted orbit in hydrogen atom varies proportional to $\\frac{1}{n}$ i.e. $v_n\\propto\\frac{1}{n}$.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $v_n\\propto1/n$.","explanation":"$v_2:v_3=\\frac{1}{2}:\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{3}{2}$, and $v_n\\propto1/n$ is true and directly explains it.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",14],[2026,"C","55/7/2",15],[2026,"C","55/7/3",16]]},{"id":"p0635","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): A free neutron is not a stable particle.\nReason (R): It decays into a proton and an electron.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall beta decay of a neutron, including the antineutrino.","explanation":"A free neutron is unstable (half-life about 10 min), so A is true; it decays into a proton, electron and antineutrino, so the statement given in R is incomplete and treated as false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",15],[2026,"C","55/7/2",14],[2026,"C","55/7/3",13]]},{"id":"p0636","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Electron emission from surface of zinc occurs when yellow light is incident on it.\nReason (R): Energy of a photon of yellow light is more than the work function of zinc.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Compare the photon energy with the work function of zinc ($\\approx4.3\\ \\mathrm{eV}$).","explanation":"Yellow photons carry about $2\\ \\mathrm{eV}$, less than zinc's work function, so there is no emission: both A and R are false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/1",16],[2026,"C","55/7/2",13],[2026,"C","55/7/3",14]]},{"id":"p0637","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The Balmer series of hydrogen atom lies in :","options":{"A":"UV region","B":"Infrared region","C":"Visible region","D":"Microwave region"},"answer":"C","hint":"Balmer lines end on $n=2$.","explanation":"Transitions to $n=2$ give wavelengths 656 nm to 365 nm, the visible region.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/2",8]]},{"id":"p0638","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The focal lengths of the objective and the eyepiece of a compound microscope are $2\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ and $3\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ respectively. If they are kept $25\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ apart, the tube length of the microscope is :","options":{"A":"$23\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","B":"$22\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","C":"$20\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","D":"$30\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Tube length is the separation between the focal points of the lenses.","explanation":"$L=25-f_o-f_e=25-2-3=20\\ \\mathrm{cm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/2",9]]},{"id":"p0639","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"In an interference pattern, 10 fringes are obtained over a certain distance from its central maximum with light of wavelength $480\\ \\mathrm{nm}$. The number of fringes obtained over the same distance with light of wavelength $600\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ will be :","options":{"A":"6","B":"7","C":"8","D":"10"},"answer":"C","hint":"Fringe width is proportional to $\\lambda$, so $N\\lambda$ is constant.","explanation":"$N_2=10\\times\\frac{480}{600}=8$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/2",10]]},{"id":"p0640","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The radius of the innermost electron orbit of a hydrogen atom is $0.53\\ \\mathring{\\mathrm{A}}$. The radius of orbit in the second excited state is :","options":{"A":"$3.5\\ \\mathring{\\mathrm{A}}$","B":"$4.77\\ \\mathring{\\mathrm{A}}$","C":"$0.747\\ \\mathring{\\mathrm{A}}$","D":"$0.43\\ \\mathring{\\mathrm{A}}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Orbit radius scales as the square of the principal quantum number.","explanation":"Second excited state is $n=3$, so $r=9\\times0.53=4.77\\ \\mathring{\\mathrm{A}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/3",1]]},{"id":"p0641","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of light passes from a medium of refractive index $\\sqrt{2}$ into the medium of refractive index 1. For the ray to just graze along the surface of separation, the angle of incidence should be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$","B":"$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$","C":"$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$","D":"$\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Grazing emergence means the incidence angle equals the critical angle.","explanation":"$\\sin i_c=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$, so $i_c=\\frac{\\pi}{4}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/3",3]]},{"id":"p0642","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"When the distance between the slit and screen is doubled, the angular separation between two fringes in single-slit diffraction experiment will :","options":{"A":"be doubled","B":"be halved","C":"remain constant","D":"become one-fourth"},"answer":"C","hint":"Angular position depends on wavelength and slit width, not on screen distance.","explanation":"Minima occur at $\\sin\\theta=n\\lambda/a$, independent of $D$, so angular separation is unchanged.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/3",5]]},{"id":"p0643","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A conducting sphere of radius R has a charge Q on it. Which of the following graphs correctly represents the value of electric field E in the range $0\\le x<\\infty$, where $x$ is the distance of the point from the centre of the sphere ?","options":{"A":"Graph (A): E constant for $x<R$, then decreasing","B":"Graph (B): E rising from zero to a maximum at $x=R$, then decreasing","C":"Graph (C): E zero for $x<R$, jumping to maximum at $x=R$, then decreasing","D":"Graph (D): E rising linearly from zero to a maximum at $x=R$, then decreasing"},"answer":"C","hint":"Field inside a conductor is zero.","explanation":"Inside, $E=0$; at and beyond the surface $E=\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 x^2}$, highest at $x=R$ and falling off. This is graph (C).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0643.png","sources":[[2026,"C","55/7/3",12]]},{"id":"p0644","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"How many protons are needed to make 1 nC charge?","options":{"A":"$1.60\\times10^{+28}$","B":"$1.60\\times10^{+10}$","C":"$3.1\\times10^{9}$","D":"$6.25\\times10^{9}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use quantisation: $q=ne$.","explanation":"$n=10^{-9}/1.6\\times10^{-19}=6.25\\times10^{9}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",1]]},{"id":"p0645","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A lightning strike can transfer $10^{20}$ electrons from the cloud to the ground in 2 minutes. The average electric current flown from cloud to the ground is –","options":{"A":"8.0 A","B":"$1.96\\times10^{-2}$ A","C":"4.0 A","D":"$1.33\\times10^{-1}$ A"},"answer":"D","hint":"Current is charge divided by time.","explanation":"$I=10^{20}\\times1.6\\times10^{-19}/120=16/120\\approx1.33\\times10^{-1}$ A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",2]]},{"id":"p0646","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric current can be induced in a wire loop if –","options":{"A":"the wire loop is wound around a bar magnet.","B":"a bar magnet is placed in contact with the wire loop.","C":"a bar magnet is placed near the wire loop.","D":"a bar magnet is moved rapidly towards the wire loop."},"answer":"D","hint":"Induction needs a changing magnetic flux.","explanation":"Only relative motion of the magnet changes the flux through the loop.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",3]]},{"id":"p0647","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"The process that allows light to travel through a tube which is not straight is –","options":{"A":"reflection of light","B":"dispersion of light","C":"scattering of light","D":"total internal reflection"},"answer":"D","hint":"Optical fibres work on one phenomenon.","explanation":"Repeated total internal reflection guides light along a bent tube.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",4]]},{"id":"p0648","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A beam of blue light is incident on a metal surface with a work function of $\\phi_0=\\dfrac{hc}{550\\ \\mathrm{nm}}$ and photoelectrons are ejected from the surface. If the blue light is replaced by red light of same intensity, then","options":{"A":"emitted electrons will be more energetic.","B":"emitted electrons will be less energetic.","C":"no electrons are emitted.","D":"less number of electrons are emitted."},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare red wavelength with the threshold wavelength 550 nm.","explanation":"Red light has $\\lambda>550$ nm, so photon energy is below $\\phi_0$ and no emission occurs.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",5]]},{"id":"p0649","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron and a proton are accelerated through the same potential difference. Let $\\lambda_e$ and $\\lambda_p$ be the de Broglie wavelength associated with electron and proton respectively. Then $\\lambda_e/\\lambda_p$ is","options":{"A":"$m_e/m_p$","B":"$m_p/m_e$","C":"$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_e}{m_p}}$","D":"$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_p}{m_e}}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\lambda=h/\\sqrt{2meV}$ for equal $V$ and $|q|$.","explanation":"$\\lambda\\propto1/\\sqrt{m}$, so $\\lambda_e/\\lambda_p=\\sqrt{m_p/m_e}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",6]]},{"id":"p0650","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Suppose there is an atom that contains five energy levels. How many transitions are possible in which photons are emitted?","options":{"A":"4","B":"5","C":"10","D":"15"},"answer":"C","hint":"Count pairs of levels: $\\binom{n}{2}$.","explanation":"$\\binom{5}{2}=10$ downward transitions.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",7]]},{"id":"p0651","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following particles do not exist in $^{238}_{92}\\mathrm{U}$ nucleus?","options":{"A":"92 protons","B":"92 electrons","C":"146 neutrons","D":"238 nucleons"},"answer":"B","hint":"A nucleus contains only protons and neutrons.","explanation":"Electrons are not nuclear constituents; $238-92=146$ neutrons.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",8]]},{"id":"p0652","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The main source for energy generation in the stars is –","options":{"A":"fusion of lighter nuclei","B":"fission of heavy nuclei","C":"chemical reaction","D":"fusion of heavy nuclei"},"answer":"A","hint":"Stellar energy comes from nuclear processes of light nuclei.","explanation":"Stars fuse light nuclei like hydrogen into helium.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",9]]},{"id":"p0653","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"A n-type semiconductor is obtained by doping silicon with","options":{"A":"Boron","B":"Arsenic","C":"Indium","D":"Aluminium"},"answer":"B","hint":"n-type needs a pentavalent dopant.","explanation":"Arsenic has five valence electrons; the others are trivalent.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",11]]},{"id":"p0654","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When a forward biasing is applied across a junction diode, it –","options":{"A":"raises the potential barrier height.","B":"decreases the depletion layer width.","C":"increases the resistance of the p-n junction.","D":"reduces the majority carrier current to zero."},"answer":"B","hint":"Forward bias pushes carriers toward the junction.","explanation":"Forward bias lowers the barrier and narrows the depletion layer.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",12]]},{"id":"p0655","chapter":8,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Displacement current does not have the same physical effect as the conduction current.\nReason (R): The concept of displacement current is introduced only to satisfy Kirchhoff's rules.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall why Maxwell introduced displacement current.","explanation":"A is false (it produces a magnetic field like conduction current); R is false (introduced to make Ampere's law consistent).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",13]]},{"id":"p0656","chapter":7,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a series LCR circuit at resonance, the impedance is equal to ohmic resistance ($R$) in the circuit.\nReason (R): At resonance, the capacitive reactance is not equal to the inductive reactance.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"At resonance $X_L=X_C$.","explanation":"A is true ($Z=R$); R is false since $X_L=X_C$ at resonance.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",14]]},{"id":"p0657","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When a ray of light enters from air into glass, frequency of light remains the same.\nReason (R): The speed and wavelength of light both changes in glass.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Frequency is set by the source; speed and wavelength change.","explanation":"A true, R true, but R does not explain why frequency is unchanged.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",15]]},{"id":"p0658","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Photoelectric effect demonstrates particle nature of light.\nReason (R): The number of photoelectrons emitted from a surface depends upon the intensity of radiation.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Is R the reason light is regarded as particles?","explanation":"Both true; particle nature rests on photon energy $h\\nu$ and instantaneous emission, not on the intensity dependence.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55(B)",16]]},{"id":"p0659","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"In a region, the electric potential varies as $V=10-50x$, where $V$ is in volts and $x$ in meters. The electric field in the region is :","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$ along $+x$","B":"$10\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$ along $-x$","C":"$50\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$ along $+x$","D":"$50\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$ along $-x$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use $E=-\\frac{dV}{dx}$.","explanation":"$E=-\\frac{dV}{dx}=+50\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$, directed along $+x$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",1]]},{"id":"p0660","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A conducting wire connects two charged metallic spheres A and B of radii $r_1$ and $r_2$ respectively. The distance between the spheres is very large compared to their radii. The ratio of electric fields, $(E_A/E_B)$ at the surfaces of spheres A and B will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{r_1}{r_2}$","B":"$\\frac{r_2}{r_1}$","C":"$\\frac{r_1^2}{r_2^2}$","D":"$\\frac{r_2^2}{r_1^2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Connected spheres share the same potential; surface field is V divided by radius.","explanation":"Same $V$ gives $E=\\frac{V}{r}$, so $\\frac{E_A}{E_B}=\\frac{r_2}{r_1}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",2],[2026,"R","55/1/2",8],[2026,"R","55/1/3",4]]},{"id":"p0661","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A long straight wire of circular cross-section (radius $a$) carries a steady current $I$. The current is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. The magnitude of the magnetic field produced at a point at a distance $(a/2)$ from the axis of the wire will be","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi a}$","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi a}$","D":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{6\\pi a}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Apply Ampere's law with only the enclosed current.","explanation":"Enclosed current is $I\\frac{r^2}{a^2}$, so $B=\\frac{\\mu_0 I r}{2\\pi a^2}=\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{4\\pi a}$ at $r=a/2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",3]]},{"id":"p0662","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"The shape of the interference fringes in Young's double-slit experiment, when the distance between the slit and the screen is very large as compared to the slit-separation, is nearly","options":{"A":"straight","B":"parabolic","C":"circular","D":"hyperbolic"},"answer":"A","hint":"Fringes are hyperbolae in general; consider the limit of a distant screen.","explanation":"Locus of constant path difference is a hyperbola, which looks like a straight line for a very distant screen.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",4]]},{"id":"p0663","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"An electromagnetic wave passes from vacuum into a dielectric medium with relative electrical permittivity $(3/2)$ and relative magnetic permeability $(8/3)$. Then, its","options":{"A":"wavelength is doubled and frequency remains unchanged.","B":"wavelength is doubled and frequency is halved.","C":"wavelength is halved and frequency remains unchanged.","D":"wavelength and frequency both will remain unchanged."},"answer":"C","hint":"Frequency stays fixed; speed changes by the refractive index.","explanation":"$n=\\sqrt{\\frac32\\times\\frac83}=2$, so $\\lambda$ halves while $\\nu$ is unchanged.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",5],[2026,"R","55/1/2",11],[2026,"R","55/1/3",2]]},{"id":"p0664","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"In a series LCR circuit, the voltage across the resistor, capacitor and inductor is 10 V each. If the capacitor is short circuited, the voltage across the inductor will be","options":{"A":"$10\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$5\\sqrt{2}\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$10\\sqrt{2}\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Find the source voltage first, then the new R-L division.","explanation":"Resonance, so source is 10 V. With C shorted, $R=X_L$ so $V_L=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt2}=5\\sqrt2\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",6]]},{"id":"p0665","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Electromagnetic waves used in a diagnostic tool in medicine have a wavelength range","options":{"A":"$1\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ to $10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","B":"$400\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ to $1\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","C":"$1\\ \\mathrm{mm}$ to $700\\ \\mathrm{nm}$","D":"$0.1\\ \\mathrm{m}$ to $1\\ \\mathrm{mm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Medical diagnosis (imaging) uses X-rays.","explanation":"X-rays span roughly 1 nm to $10^{-3}$ nm.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",7]]},{"id":"p0666","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The 'distance of closest approach' of an alpha-particle is 'd' when it moves with a velocity $v$ head-on towards the target nucleus. If the velocity of alpha particle is halved, the new 'distance of closest approach' will be –","options":{"A":"$\\frac{d}{2}$","B":"$2d$","C":"$\\frac{d}{4}$","D":"$4d$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Equate kinetic energy to electrostatic potential energy at closest approach.","explanation":"$d\\propto\\frac{1}{v^2}$, so halving $v$ makes the distance $4d$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",8]]},{"id":"p0667","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A concave lens of focal length 10 cm is cut into two identical plano-concave lenses. The focal length of each lens will be","options":{"A":"$20\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","B":"$30\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","C":"$40\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","D":"$5\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Two identical halves in contact must combine to the original power.","explanation":"$\\frac1f=\\frac1{f'}+\\frac1{f'}$ gives $f'=2f=20\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ (magnitude).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",9]]},{"id":"p0668","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"Four independent waves are expressed as\n(i) $y_1=A_1\\sin\\omega t$\n(ii) $y_2=A_2\\sin 2\\omega t$\n(iii) $y_3=A_3\\cos\\omega t$\n(iv) $y_4=A_4\\sin(\\omega t+\\pi/3)$\nThe interference between two of these waves is possible in","options":{"A":"(i) and (iii) only","B":"(iii) and (iv) only","C":"(i), (iii) and (iv) only","D":"All of them"},"answer":"C","hint":"Sustained interference needs waves of the same frequency.","explanation":"Waves (i), (iii), (iv) have angular frequency $\\omega$; (ii) has $2\\omega$, so (ii) cannot interfere with the rest.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",10],[2026,"R","55/1/2",4],[2026,"R","55/1/3",1]]},{"id":"p0669","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two heaters rated as $(P_1,V)$ and $(P_2,V)$ are connected in series across a dc source of $\\frac{V}{2}$ volt. The power consumed by the combination will be –","options":{"A":"$(P_1+P_2)$","B":"$\\frac{P_1+P_2}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{P_1P_2}{2(P_1+P_2)}$","D":"$\\frac{P_1P_2}{4(P_1+P_2)}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $R=V^2/P$ for each heater, add in series.","explanation":"$R_1+R_2=V^2\\frac{P_1+P_2}{P_1P_2}$, so $P=\\frac{(V/2)^2}{R_1+R_2}=\\frac{P_1P_2}{4(P_1+P_2)}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",11]]},{"id":"p0670","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In an unbiased p-n junction, at equilibrium, which of the following statements is true ?","options":{"A":"Diffusion current is zero but drift current exists.","B":"Diffusion current exists but drift current is zero.","C":"Diffusion and drift currents are equal and opposite.","D":"Both the diffusion and drift currents exist but are unequal."},"answer":"C","hint":"At equilibrium there is no net current.","explanation":"Net current is zero, so diffusion and drift currents are equal and opposite.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",12],[2026,"R","55/1/2",2],[2026,"R","55/1/3",3]]},{"id":"p0671","chapter":5,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): All atoms have a net magnetic moment.\nReason (R): A current loop does not always behave as a magnetic dipole.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Think of atoms whose electron moments cancel; a loop always has a dipole moment.","explanation":"A is false (diamagnetic atoms have zero net moment) and R is false (every current loop is a dipole).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",13],[2026,"R","55/1/2",15],[2026,"R","55/1/3",14]]},{"id":"p0672","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): If accelerated electrons are passed through a narrow slit, a diffraction pattern is observed.\nReason (R): Electrons behave as both particles and waves.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Diffraction is evidence of wave nature.","explanation":"Both true; the wave nature of electrons explains diffraction.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",14],[2026,"R","55/1/2",16],[2026,"R","55/1/3",16]]},{"id":"p0673","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The mass of a nucleus is less than the sum of the masses of the constituent nucleons.\nReason (R): Energy is absorbed when the nucleons are bound together to form a nucleus.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Mass defect corresponds to binding energy released.","explanation":"A is true (mass defect) but R is false: energy is released on binding.","difficulty":2,"note":"Paper misprints the first statement as \"Reason (A)\"; corrected to Assertion (A).","sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",15],[2026,"R","55/1/2",14],[2026,"R","55/1/3",13]]},{"id":"p0674","chapter":12,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, the energy levels are discrete and quantised.\nReason (R): In a hydrogen atom, the electrostatic force on the electron provides the necessary centripetal force to it to revolve around the nucleus.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Quantisation comes from the angular momentum postulate, not from the force balance.","explanation":"Both true, but centripetal force balance does not explain quantisation of levels.","difficulty":2,"note":"Paper misprints the first statement as \"Reason (A)\"; corrected to Assertion (A).","sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/1",16],[2026,"R","55/1/2",13],[2026,"R","55/1/3",15]]},{"id":"p0675","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"In a region electric field is given by $\\vec{E}=4x\\hat{i}\\ \\mathrm{N/C}$. The potential difference between points A ($x=1\\ \\mathrm{m}$) and B ($x=3\\ \\mathrm{m}$), $(V_A-V_B)$ is","options":{"A":"$-16\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$16\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$-8\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$8\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$V_A-V_B=\\int_A^B \\vec{E}\\cdot d\\vec{x}$.","explanation":"$V_A-V_B=\\int_1^3 4x\\,dx=2(9-1)=16\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",1]]},{"id":"p0676","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A copper wire is stretched to increase its length by 1%. Then the change in its resistance is close to","options":{"A":"$1\\%$","B":"$4\\%$","C":"$-4\\%$","D":"$2\\%$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Volume is constant, so area shrinks as length grows.","explanation":"$R\\propto l^2$ at constant volume, so $\\frac{\\Delta R}{R}\\approx2\\times1\\%=2\\%$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",3]]},{"id":"p0677","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A concave lens of focal length 40 cm is coaxially in contact with two convex lenses, each of focal length 20 cm, on each side. The focal length of the combination is","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$\\frac{20}{3}\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","C":"$-\\frac{20}{3}\\ \\mathrm{cm}$","D":"$\\frac{40}{3}\\ \\mathrm{cm}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Add powers of lenses in contact.","explanation":"$\\frac1f=-\\frac1{40}+\\frac1{20}+\\frac1{20}=\\frac3{40}$, so $f=\\frac{40}{3}\\ \\mathrm{cm}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",5]]},{"id":"p0678","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The distance-of-closest-approach for an alpha particle is 'd' when it moves head-on with speed $v$ towards a target nucleus. If the alpha particle is replaced by a proton moving with the same speed, the new distance-of-closest-approach will be","options":{"A":"$2d$","B":"$d$","C":"$\\frac{d}{2}$","D":"$\\frac{d}{\\sqrt{2}}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$d=\\frac{kZ_1Z_2e^2}{\\frac12 m v^2}$; compare charge and mass.","explanation":"$d\\propto\\frac{q}{m}$; proton has half the charge and a quarter of the mass, so $d'=2d$.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",6]]},{"id":"p0679","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Electromagnetic waves used for purification of water are","options":{"A":"X-rays","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"Infrared rays","D":"Ultrasonic rays"},"answer":"B","hint":"These rays kill germs.","explanation":"Ultraviolet radiation is used to sterilise water.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",7]]},{"id":"p0680","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A straight conductor lies along $x$-axis. It carries a current of 10 A along $+x$ direction. The magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ due to 1 cm segment of this conductor, centred at the origin, at a point $(0,\\ 1\\ \\mathrm{m},\\ 0)$ is","options":{"A":"$(1\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{j}$","B":"$(10\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{k}$","C":"$-(10\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{k}$","D":"$-(1\\ \\mathrm{nT})\\,\\hat{j}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Apply the Biot-Savart law and find direction by $d\\vec{l}\\times\\vec{r}$.","explanation":"$B=\\frac{\\mu_0}{4\\pi}\\frac{I\\,dl}{r^2}=10^{-7}\\times10\\times0.01=10\\ \\mathrm{nT}$; $\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j}=\\hat{k}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",9]]},{"id":"p0681","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"The angular width of interference fringes in Young's double-slit experiment depends on","options":{"A":"distance between the slits and the screen only","B":"wavelength of light used only","C":"both wavelength of light used and the slits separation","D":"slits separation only"},"answer":"C","hint":"Angular fringe width is the fringe width divided by D.","explanation":"$\\theta=\\frac{\\lambda}{d}$, depends on both $\\lambda$ and $d$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",10]]},{"id":"p0682","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A resistor and an inductor of negligible resistance are connected in series to a 20 V ac source. If the voltage across the resistor is 12 V, the voltage across the inductor will be","options":{"A":"$6\\ \\mathrm{V}$","B":"$8\\ \\mathrm{V}$","C":"$10\\ \\mathrm{V}$","D":"$16\\ \\mathrm{V}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Voltages across R and L add in quadrature.","explanation":"$V_L=\\sqrt{20^2-12^2}=16\\ \\mathrm{V}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/2",12]]},{"id":"p0683","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric potential for various points in $x$-$y$ plane is given by $V=1.0\\,x^2-2.0\\,y^2$, where $V$ is in volts and $x$, $y$ are in metres. The angle that the electric field at point $(2.0\\ \\mathrm{m},\\ 1.0\\ \\mathrm{m})$ makes with the positive $x$-axis is –","options":{"A":"$45^\\circ$","B":"$90^\\circ$","C":"$135^\\circ$","D":"$315^\\circ$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E_x=-\\partial V/\\partial x$, $E_y=-\\partial V/\\partial y$.","explanation":"$E_x=-2x=-4$ and $E_y=4y=4$, so the field is at $135^\\circ$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",5]]},{"id":"p0684","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A current of 1.5 A is maintained in a copper wire of length 1 m with area of cross-section $1.7\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{m}^2$. The magnitude of electric field in the wire is $[\\rho_{Cu}=1.7\\times10^{-8}\\ \\Omega\\,\\mathrm{m}]$","options":{"A":"$0.15\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{m}}$","B":"$0.30\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{m}}$","C":"$1.5\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{m}}$","D":"$3.0\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{m}}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use $E=\\rho J$.","explanation":"$E=\\rho\\frac{I}{A}=\\frac{1.7\\times10^{-8}\\times1.5}{1.7\\times10^{-7}}=0.15\\ \\mathrm{V/m}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",6]]},{"id":"p0685","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"Light from a small object in air falls on a spherical glass surface ($n=1.5$) of radius of curvature R. A real image of the object will be formed if the object distance u is related to R as :","options":{"A":"$u<\\frac{R}{2}$","B":"$\\frac{R}{2}<u<R$","C":"$R<u<2R$","D":"$u>2R$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use the single refracting surface formula and require positive image distance.","explanation":"$\\frac{1.5}{v}=\\frac{0.5}{R}-\\frac1u$ is positive only if $u>2R$.","difficulty":3,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",7]]},{"id":"p0686","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the potential energy to the kinetic energy of an electron in $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit of Bohr model of hydrogen atom is","options":{"A":"$-\\frac12$","B":"$\\frac12$","C":"2","D":"$-2$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Recall U and K in terms of total energy in a Coulomb orbit.","explanation":"$U=-2K$, so $U/K=-2$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",8]]},{"id":"p0687","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"Welders wear special glass goggles or face masks with glass windows to protect their eyes from","options":{"A":"Infrared rays","B":"Ultraviolet rays","C":"X-rays","D":"Microwaves"},"answer":"B","hint":"Arc welding emits intense radiation of a particular band harmful to eyes.","explanation":"Welding arcs emit strong ultraviolet radiation, which the glass absorbs.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",9]]},{"id":"p0688","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A circular loop has radius R and carries current I as shown in figure. In order that the net magnetic field at the centre of the loop is zero, the current in wire AB should have magnitude","options":{"A":"$2\\pi I$, along $+X$ – axis","B":"$2\\pi I$ along $-X$ – axis","C":"$\\pi I$ along $+X$ – axis","D":"$\\pi I$ along $-X$ – axis"},"answer":"A","hint":"Equate fields of the loop and of the long wire at distance 2R, and check directions.","explanation":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2R}=\\frac{\\mu_0 I'}{2\\pi(2R)}$ gives $I'=2\\pi I$; the clockwise loop gives field into the page, so the wire field must point out, i.e. current along $+X$.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0688.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",10]]},{"id":"p0689","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"The phenomenon of interference is shown by","options":{"A":"longitudinal mechanical wave only","B":"transverse mechanical wave only","C":"electromagnetic waves only","D":"all these waves"},"answer":"D","hint":"Interference is a general property of waves.","explanation":"Superposition and interference occur for every type of wave.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",11]]},{"id":"p0690","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"A series LCR circuit with $R=3\\ \\Omega$, $X_C=4\\ \\Omega$, $X_L=8\\ \\Omega$ is connected to a $220\\ \\mathrm{V}$, $50\\ \\mathrm{Hz}$ ac source. The power factor for the circuit is","options":{"A":"0.30","B":"0.45","C":"0.50","D":"0.60"},"answer":"D","hint":"Power factor is $R/Z$.","explanation":"$Z=\\sqrt{3^2+(8-4)^2}=5\\ \\Omega$, so $\\cos\\phi=\\frac35=0.60$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/1/3",12]]},{"id":"p0691","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Three point charges $2q$, $-2q$ and $q$ are kept at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side $l$. The potential energy of the system is","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$\\frac{-2q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$","C":"$\\frac{q^2}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$","D":"$\\frac{-q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Sum pairwise potential energies over the three pairs.","explanation":"$U=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}(-4q^2+2q^2-2q^2)=\\frac{-q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",1]]},{"id":"p0692","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Two metal spheres of radii $r_1$ and $r_2\\ (>r_1)$ having charges $q_1$ and $q_2$ respectively kept in air, are brought in contact. Which of the following statements is not correct ?","options":{"A":"The total charge of the two spheres is conserved.","B":"Both spheres attain the same potential.","C":"The final potential of the system equals $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{(q_1+q_2)}{(r_1+r_2)}$","D":"The final potential of the system equals $\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{(q_1+q_2)(r_1+r_2)}{r_1r_2}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Total charge over total capacitance gives the common potential.","explanation":"Common potential is $\\frac{q_1+q_2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0(r_1+r_2)}$, so statement (D) is the incorrect one.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",2],[2026,"R","55/2/2",4],[2026,"R","55/2/3",4]]},{"id":"p0693","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The maximum kinetic energy of the electrons emitted from a photo sensitive surface depends on","options":{"A":"work function of the surface '$\\phi_0$' only.","B":"frequency of the incident radiation '$\\nu$' only.","C":"intensity of the incident radiation 'I' only.","D":"Both $\\phi_0$ and $\\nu$."},"answer":"D","hint":"Einstein's photoelectric equation.","explanation":"$K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi_0$ depends on both $\\nu$ and $\\phi_0$, not intensity.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",3]]},{"id":"p0694","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, for large values of n, the distance between the consecutive orbits is proportional to","options":{"A":"$\\sqrt{n}$","B":"$n$","C":"$n^2$","D":"$n^3$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use $r_n\\propto n^2$ and take the difference of consecutive orbits.","explanation":"$r_{n+1}-r_n\\propto(2n+1)\\approx 2n$, so proportional to $n$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",4]]},{"id":"p0695","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A straight long wire lying along y-axis, carries a current of 1 A along $-y$ direction. The magnetic field due to the conductor at a point $(50\\ \\mathrm{cm},\\ 0,\\ 0)$ will point along.","options":{"A":"z-axis","B":"$-$z-axis","C":"$x$-axis","D":"$-x$-axis"},"answer":"A","hint":"Direction from $d\\vec{l}\\times\\vec{r}$ (or right-hand rule).","explanation":"$(-\\hat{j})\\times\\hat{i}=+\\hat{k}$, so the field points along $+z$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",5]]},{"id":"p0696","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following materials has positive and small value of magnetic susceptibility ?","options":{"A":"Cu","B":"Al","C":"Bi","D":"Ni"},"answer":"B","hint":"Positive small susceptibility means paramagnetic.","explanation":"Al is paramagnetic; Cu and Bi are diamagnetic and Ni is ferromagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",6]]},{"id":"p0697","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux $\\phi$ (in Wb) linked with a coil is related to time $t$ (in s) as $\\phi=5At^2+Bt-2C$. The SI units of A and B are respectively","options":{"A":"$\\mathrm{Wbs}^2$, $\\mathrm{Wbs}$","B":"$\\mathrm{Wb\\,s}^{-1}$, $\\mathrm{Wb}$","C":"$\\mathrm{Wbs}^{-2}$, $\\mathrm{Wbs}^{-1}$","D":"$\\mathrm{Wbs}^{-1}$, $\\mathrm{Wbs}^{-2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Each term must have the unit of flux.","explanation":"$At^2$ in Wb gives $A$ in $\\mathrm{Wb\\,s^{-2}}$; $Bt$ in Wb gives $B$ in $\\mathrm{Wb\\,s^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",8],[2026,"R","55/2/2",11],[2026,"R","55/2/3",2]]},{"id":"p0698","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows the variation of capacitive reactance ($X_C$) of two ideal capacitors of capacitances $C_1$ & $C_2$ with the reciprocal of angular frequency $(1/\\omega)$ of ac source. The value of $C_1/C_2$ is","options":{"A":"$\\frac12$","B":"2","C":"$\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Slope of $X_C$ vs $1/\\omega$ equals $1/C$.","explanation":"Slopes: $\\frac1{C_1}=\\tan45^\\circ=1$, $\\frac1{C_2}=\\tan30^\\circ=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}$, so $\\frac{C_1}{C_2}=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0698.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",9],[2026,"R","55/2/3",1]]},{"id":"p0699","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A magnet held vertically, with its north pole down is dropped along the axis of a closed solenoid placed vertically on a table. If the observer looks down from the top,","options":{"A":"the induced current will flow in the anticlockwise direction.","B":"the induced current will flow in the clockwise direction.","C":"no induced current will flow in the solenoid.","D":"the magnet will fall with a constant velocity."},"answer":"A","hint":"Lenz's law: the top face of the solenoid opposes the approaching north pole.","explanation":"The top face must act as a north pole to repel the approaching magnet, which means anticlockwise current seen from above.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",10]]},{"id":"p0700","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"An electromagnetic wave is propagating along x-axis. At any instant, the phase difference (in radian) between the electric field ($\\vec{E}$) and the magnetic field ($\\vec{B}$) associated with the wave is","options":{"A":"zero","B":"$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$","C":"$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","D":"$\\pi$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{B}$ oscillate together.","explanation":"E and B are in phase in an electromagnetic wave, so the phase difference is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",11]]},{"id":"p0701","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, the electron makes a transition from $n=5$ to $n=1$ state. As a result, a photon of wavelength $\\lambda$ is emitted in the process. The wavelength of the photon emitted when an electron makes a transition from energy level $n=5$ to $n=2$ will be","options":{"A":"$\\frac{8}{7}\\lambda$","B":"$\\frac{24}{7}\\lambda$","C":"$\\frac{16}{7}\\lambda$","D":"$\\frac{32}{7}\\lambda$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\frac1\\lambda=R\\left(\\frac1{n_1^2}-\\frac1{n_2^2}\\right)$ for both transitions.","explanation":"$\\frac1\\lambda=\\frac{24R}{25}$ and $\\frac1{\\lambda'}=\\frac{21R}{100}$, so $\\lambda'=\\frac{32}{7}\\lambda$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",12]]},{"id":"p0702","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): On increasing the intensity of incident light of frequency $\\nu(>\\nu_0)$ on a photosensitive surface, the photocurrent increases.\nReason (R): The stopping potential for a photosensitive surface increases with increase of frequency $\\nu(>\\nu_0)$ of incident light.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Photocurrent depends on intensity; stopping potential depends on frequency.","explanation":"Both true, but stopping potential's frequency dependence does not explain the intensity dependence of photocurrent.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",13],[2026,"R","55/2/2",15],[2026,"R","55/2/3",16]]},{"id":"p0703","chapter":14,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): On forward biasing a p-n junction diode, the height of the barrier potential increases.\nReason (R): In forward biasing of a p-n junction diode, the direction of the applied voltage is in the same direction as the built-in potential.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Forward bias opposes the built-in potential.","explanation":"Forward bias applies voltage opposite to the built-in potential and lowers the barrier, so both are false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",14],[2026,"R","55/2/2",16],[2026,"R","55/2/3",15]]},{"id":"p0704","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Light added to light can produce darkness.\nReason (R): When two coherent light waves interfere, there is darkness at position of destructive interference.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Destructive interference gives zero intensity.","explanation":"Both true; darkness arising from superposition is exactly destructive interference.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",15],[2026,"R","55/2/2",13],[2026,"R","55/2/3",14]]},{"id":"p0705","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Two electric heaters of power $P_1$ and $P_2\\ (>P_1)$ are joined in series across a dc source of voltage V. The power consumed by the combination will be less than that consumed by $P_1$ when connected across the same source.\nReason (R): The power consumed by a electric device when connected to a dc source of voltage V is proportional to its resistance.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"At fixed voltage, $P=V^2/R$.","explanation":"A is true because series resistance exceeds $R_1$; R is false since $P=V^2/R$ is inversely proportional to resistance.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/1",16],[2026,"R","55/2/2",14],[2026,"R","55/2/3",13]]},{"id":"p0706","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Three point charges $+q$, $-2q$ and $+q$ are placed along $x$-axis at points $x=-1\\ \\mathrm{m}$, $x=0\\ \\mathrm{m}$ and $x=2\\ \\mathrm{m}$ respectively. The potential energy of the system is","options":{"A":"$-\\frac{q^2}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","B":"$-\\frac{2q^2}{3\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","C":"$\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$","D":"$\\frac{-5\\,q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Add the pairwise energies with the three separations 1 m, 2 m and 3 m.","explanation":"$U=\\frac{q^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\left(-2-1+\\frac13\\right)=-\\frac{2q^2}{3\\pi\\varepsilon_0}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",1]]},{"id":"p0707","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A photosensitive surface is illuminated by radiations of wavelength $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2\\ (>\\lambda_1)$ and $\\lambda_3$ one by one and photoemission is observed in each case. $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$ lies in UV range and $\\lambda_3$ in visible range. If $V_1$, $V_2$ and $V_3$ are stopping potential in these cases respectively, then","options":{"A":"$V_1=V_2=V_3$","B":"$V_2>V_1>V_3$","C":"$V_1>V_2>V_3$","D":"$V_3>V_1>V_2$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Stopping potential rises with frequency, i.e. falls with wavelength.","explanation":"$eV=\\frac{hc}{\\lambda}-\\phi_0$ and $\\lambda_1<\\lambda_2<\\lambda_3$, so $V_1>V_2>V_3$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",2]]},{"id":"p0708","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, the value of potential energy of an electron in $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit varies with 'n' as","options":{"A":"$\\frac1{n^2}$","B":"$\\frac1n$","C":"$n$","D":"$n^2$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Potential energy is twice the total energy.","explanation":"$U=2E_n\\propto-\\frac1{n^2}$, so varies as $\\frac1{n^2}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",3]]},{"id":"p0709","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, an electron makes a transition from $n=4$ state to $n=1$ state and a photon of frequency $\\nu$ is emitted. The frequency of photon emitted when an electron makes a transition from $n=4$ state to $n=2$ state in the same model is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\nu}{3}$","B":"$\\frac{\\nu}{4}$","C":"$\\frac{\\nu}{5}$","D":"$\\frac{\\nu}{6}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Frequency is proportional to $\\frac1{n_1^2}-\\frac1{n_2^2}$.","explanation":"Ratio $=\\frac{1/4-1/16}{1-1/16}=\\frac{3/16}{15/16}=\\frac15$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",5]]},{"id":"p0710","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Figure shows a magnet dropped through a small loop with a small cut. Which of the following statements is correct ?","options":{"A":"The speed of the falling magnet increases as it approaches the loop and starts decreasing as it crosses the loop.","B":"Acceleration of magnet increases as it approaches the loop and starts decreasing as it crosses the loop.","C":"Speed of magnet remains uniform as it moves through the loop.","D":"Acceleration of the magnet remains uniform as it moves through the loop."},"answer":"D","hint":"A loop with a cut is an open circuit, so no current flows.","explanation":"No induced current flows in the cut loop, so there is no opposing force and the magnet falls with constant acceleration $g$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0710.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",6]]},{"id":"p0711","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The expression of magnetic fields associated with four electromagnetic waves are given below:\nI. $B_1 = (4\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{T})\\sin[0.7\\times10^{3}x + 1.4\\times10^{11}t]$\nII. $B_2 = (2\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{T})\\sin[0.6\\times10^{3}x + 1.5\\times10^{11}t]$\nIII. $B_3 = (3\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{T})\\sin[0.5\\times10^{3}x + 1.5\\times10^{11}t]$\nIV. $B_4 = (5\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{T})\\sin[0.2\\times10^{4}x + 4.8\\times10^{11}t]$\nWhich wave is travelling in free space?","options":{"A":"I","B":"II","C":"III","D":"IV"},"answer":"C","hint":"Wave speed is $\\omega/k$; in free space it must equal $c$.","explanation":"$\\omega/k$ for III is $1.5\\times10^{11}/0.5\\times10^{3}=3\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{m/s}=c$; others differ.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",7]]},{"id":"p0712","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following substance has relative magnetic permeability $\\mu_r \\gg 1$?","options":{"A":"Aluminium","B":"Copper","C":"Lead","D":"Nickel"},"answer":"D","hint":"Only ferromagnetic materials have very large $\\mu_r$.","explanation":"Nickel is ferromagnetic; aluminium is paramagnetic, copper and lead diamagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",8]]},{"id":"p0713","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A straight conductor lies along $x$-axis and carries a current of 2 A along $+x$ direction. The magnetic field at a point $(0, 40\\ \\mathrm{cm}, 0)$ due to 1 cm length of conductor centered at the origin points along.","options":{"A":"y-axis","B":"$-$y-axis","C":"z-axis","D":"$-$z-axis"},"answer":"C","hint":"Use Biot-Savart: direction of $d\\vec{l}\\times\\vec{r}$.","explanation":"$\\hat{i}\\times\\hat{j}=\\hat{k}$, so the field points along the $+z$ axis.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",9]]},{"id":"p0714","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer of resistance $27\\ \\Omega$ is converted into an ammeter of range $(0-10\\ \\mathrm{mA})$ using a resistance of $3\\ \\Omega$. The galvanometer will show full scale deflection for a current of about –","options":{"A":"10 mA","B":"100 mA","C":"1 mA","D":"3 mA"},"answer":"C","hint":"Shunt and galvanometer have equal voltage across them.","explanation":"$I_g\\times27=(10-I_g)\\times3$ gives $I_g=1\\ \\mathrm{mA}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",10],[2026,"R","55/2/3",3]]},{"id":"p0715","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows the variation of capacitive reactance ($X_C$) of two ideal capacitors of capacitances $C_1$ & $C_2$ with the reciprocal of angular frequency ($1/\\omega$) of ac source. The value of $C_1/C_2$ is","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","B":"2","C":"$\\sqrt{3}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$X_C = \\frac{1}{\\omega C}$, so slope of $X_C$ versus $1/\\omega$ is $1/C$.","explanation":"Slopes: $\\tan45^\\circ=1/C_1$, $\\tan30^\\circ=1/C_2$, so $C_1/C_2=\\tan30^\\circ/\\tan45^\\circ=1/\\sqrt{3}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0715.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/2",12]]},{"id":"p0716","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A person is standing facing geographic north and a vertical wire in front of him is carrying a current in downward direction. The direction of magnetic field at a point on the right side of wire is","options":{"A":"South","B":"East","C":"West","D":"North"},"answer":"A","hint":"Use the right-hand thumb rule for a straight current.","explanation":"Current down, point to the east (right): $(-\\hat{k})\\times\\hat{i}=-\\hat{j}$, which is south.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",5]]},{"id":"p0717","chapter":5,"type":"mcq","text":"Which one of the following materials has magnetic susceptibility $\\chi$ in the range $-1 \\leq \\chi < 0$?","options":{"A":"Aluminium","B":"Copper chloride","C":"Copper","D":"Nickel"},"answer":"C","hint":"Negative susceptibility means diamagnetic.","explanation":"Copper is diamagnetic; aluminium and copper chloride are paramagnetic, nickel ferromagnetic.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",6]]},{"id":"p0718","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The electric field and the magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave travelling in vacuum are represented as\n$E = m\\sin(\\alpha z-\\beta t)$\n$B = n\\sin(\\alpha z-\\beta t)$\nwhere $m$, $n$, $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants characterising the wave. Then","options":{"A":"$\\frac{m}{n}=c,\\ \\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\neq c$","B":"$\\frac{n}{m}=\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}=c$","C":"$\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}=c,\\ \\frac{m}{n}\\neq c$","D":"$\\frac{m}{n}=\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}=c$"},"answer":"D","hint":"In vacuum $E_0/B_0=c$ and $\\omega/k=c$.","explanation":"$m/n=E_0/B_0=c$ and wave speed $\\beta/\\alpha=c$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",7]]},{"id":"p0719","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Two identical circular loops A and B of metal wire are arranged on a table close to each other. Loop A is connected to a battery and the current in it increases with time. In response, the loop B","options":{"A":"remains stationary","B":"is attracted by loop A","C":"is repelled by loop A","D":"rotates about its centre of mass which is fixed"},"answer":"C","hint":"Apply Lenz's law: induced current opposes the increase in flux.","explanation":"Induced current in B is opposite to that in A, so the loops repel.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",8]]},{"id":"p0720","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"In Bohr model of hydrogen atom, an electron is moving in an orbit, with total energy $-3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. Its angular momentum will be","options":{"A":"$1.05\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$","B":"$6.63\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$","C":"$3.31\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$","D":"$2.1\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$E_n=-13.6/n^2$ eV and $L=nh/2\\pi$.","explanation":"$-3.4$ eV gives $n=2$, so $L=2h/2\\pi=h/\\pi\\approx2.1\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",9]]},{"id":"p0721","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"According to Bohr model of hydrogen atom, the time period of an electron in its $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit is proportional to","options":{"A":"$n$","B":"$n^2$","C":"$n^3$","D":"$n^4$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Time period is $2\\pi r/v$ with $r\\propto n^2$, $v\\propto 1/n$.","explanation":"$T\\propto n^2/(1/n)=n^3$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",10]]},{"id":"p0722","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The equivalent mass of a photon of wavelength 100 nm is","options":{"A":"$2.21\\times10^{-35}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$","B":"$6.63\\times10^{-33}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$","C":"$1.11\\times10^{-35}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$","D":"$3.31\\times10^{-33}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Equate $mc^2=hc/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$m=h/(\\lambda c)=6.63\\times10^{-34}/(10^{-7}\\times3\\times10^{8})=2.21\\times10^{-35}\\ \\mathrm{kg}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",11]]},{"id":"p0723","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"A point charge of $5\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ is located at $(1\\ \\mathrm{cm}, 1\\ \\mathrm{cm})$ in $x$-$y$ plane. A second charge of $6\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ is brought from infinity to point $(-3\\ \\mathrm{cm}, 4\\ \\mathrm{cm})$. The work done in the process is","options":{"A":"0.54 J","B":"5.4 J","C":"$-0.54$ J","D":"10.8 J"},"answer":"B","hint":"Work equals the potential energy $\\frac{kq_1q_2}{r}$.","explanation":"Separation is $\\sqrt{4^2+3^2}=5$ cm, so $W=9\\times10^9\\times30\\times10^{-12}/0.05=5.4$ J.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/2/3",12]]},{"id":"p0724","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ starts from rest and moves in an electric field $\\vec{E}=E_0\\hat{i}$. After travelling a distance $x$ in the field along x-axis, the kinetic energy of the particle will be:","options":{"A":"$qE_0x^2$","B":"$qE_0x$","C":"$q^2E_0x$","D":"$q^2E_0^2x$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Use the work-energy theorem.","explanation":"$K=W=qE_0x$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",1]]},{"id":"p0725","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"A plane electromagnetic wave travels through a medium and the magnetic field associated with it is given by\n$B=5\\times10^{-8}\\sin(3\\times10^{10}t-150x)\\ \\mathrm{T}$\nwhere $x$ is in metres and $t$ is in seconds.\nThe velocity of the wave is:","options":{"A":"$2\\cdot0\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$","B":"$4\\cdot5\\times10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$","C":"$3\\cdot5\\times10^{7}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$","D":"$2\\cdot5\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Wave speed is $\\omega/k$.","explanation":"$v=3\\times10^{10}/150=2\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",3],[2026,"R","55/3/2",10],[2026,"R","55/3/3",1]]},{"id":"p0726","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A thin plano-convex lens and a thin equi-concave lens are kept coaxially in contact as shown in the figure. Assuming both the lenses are made of glass of refractive index $\\mu$, and $R$ is the radius of curvature of each curved surface, the focal length of the combination is:","options":{"A":"$\\dfrac{R}{\\mu-1}$","B":"$-\\dfrac{R}{\\mu-1}$","C":"$\\dfrac{2R}{\\mu-1}$","D":"$-\\dfrac{2R}{\\mu-1}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Add powers of the two lenses in contact.","explanation":"Powers: $+\\frac{\\mu-1}{R}$ and $-\\frac{2(\\mu-1)}{R}$, sum $-\\frac{\\mu-1}{R}$, so $f=-\\frac{R}{\\mu-1}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0726.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",4],[2026,"R","55/3/2",12],[2026,"R","55/3/3",6]]},{"id":"p0727","chapter":10,"type":"mcq","text":"The phase difference between the two superimposing waves that give rise to a bright spot in a Young's double-slit experiment is ($n$ is an integer):","options":{"A":"$2n\\pi$","B":"$2n\\pi+\\frac{\\pi}{4}$","C":"$2n\\pi+\\frac{\\pi}{2}$","D":"$2n\\pi+\\pi$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Constructive interference needs waves in phase.","explanation":"Bright fringe requires phase difference $2n\\pi$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",5],[2026,"R","55/3/2",1],[2026,"R","55/3/3",12]]},{"id":"p0728","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A telescope has an objective lens of focal length 144 cm and an eyepiece of focal length $6\\cdot0$ cm. The magnifying power and the length of telescope tube will be respectively:","options":{"A":"24, 150 cm","B":"42, 138 cm","C":"24, 138 cm","D":"42, 150 cm"},"answer":"A","hint":"$m=f_o/f_e$ and $L=f_o+f_e$ in normal adjustment.","explanation":"$m=144/6=24$ and $L=144+6=150$ cm.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",6]]},{"id":"p0729","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"In which of the following, total internal reflection does not occur?","options":{"A":"Twinkling of stars","B":"Brilliance of diamonds","C":"Optical fibre","D":"Reflecting prism"},"answer":"A","hint":"Twinkling comes from varying refraction in the atmosphere.","explanation":"Twinkling is due to atmospheric refraction; the others use total internal reflection.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",7]]},{"id":"p0730","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Radiation of wavelength 200 nm is incident on a photosensitive surface of work function $4\\cdot2$ eV. The kinetic energy of fastest photoelectrons emitted from this surface will be close to:","options":{"A":"$3\\cdot5$ eV","B":"$3\\cdot0$ eV","C":"$2\\cdot5$ eV","D":"$2\\cdot0$ eV"},"answer":"D","hint":"$K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi_0$ with $E\\approx1240/\\lambda$ eV.","explanation":"Photon energy $=1240/200=6.2$ eV, so $K_{max}=6.2-4.2=2.0$ eV.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",8],[2026,"R","55/3/2",6],[2026,"R","55/3/3",11]]},{"id":"p0731","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A proton and an alpha particle have equal momentum. The ratio of their kinetic energies ($E_p/E_a$) and de Broglie wavelengths associated with them ($\\lambda_p/\\lambda_a$) respectively are:","options":{"A":"2, 1","B":"1, 2","C":"4, 1","D":"1, 4"},"answer":"C","hint":"$K=p^2/2m$ and $\\lambda=h/p$.","explanation":"$E_p/E_a=m_a/m_p=4$; equal momentum gives $\\lambda_p/\\lambda_a=1$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",9]]},{"id":"p0732","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"If $r_1$ and $r_2$ are the radii of atomic nuclei of mass numbers 64 and 27 respectively, then the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{r_1}{r_2}\\right)$ is:","options":{"A":"1","B":"$\\frac{4}{3}$","C":"$\\frac{3}{4}$","D":"$\\frac{27}{64}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$R=R_0A^{1/3}$.","explanation":"$r_1/r_2=(64/27)^{1/3}=4/3$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",10],[2026,"R","55/3/2",2],[2026,"R","55/3/3",8]]},{"id":"p0733","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"When the forward bias voltage in a semiconductor diode is changed from $0\\cdot8$ V to $1\\cdot0$ V, the forward current changes by $2\\cdot0$ mA. The forward bias resistance of the diode will be:","options":{"A":"$200\\ \\Omega$","B":"$175\\ \\Omega$","C":"$100\\ \\Omega$","D":"$125\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Dynamic resistance $=\\Delta V/\\Delta I$.","explanation":"$r=0.2\\ \\mathrm{V}/2\\ \\mathrm{mA}=100\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",11],[2026,"R","55/3/2",9],[2026,"R","55/3/3",3]]},{"id":"p0734","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"The process named 'minority carrier injection' in a p-n junction diode occurs during:","options":{"A":"forward biasing","B":"reverse biasing","C":"no biasing at low temperature","D":"no biasing at high temperature"},"answer":"A","hint":"Which bias lets carriers cross the junction?","explanation":"In forward bias, majority carriers cross and become minority carriers on the other side.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",12]]},{"id":"p0735","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In a Wheatstone bridge circuit, if we interchange the position of the cell and the galvanometer, the balance condition $\\dfrac{P}{Q}=\\dfrac{R}{S}$ remains unchanged.\nReason (R): $\\dfrac{P}{Q}=\\dfrac{R}{S}\\Rightarrow\\dfrac{Q}{S}=\\dfrac{P}{R}$ so balance condition remains same.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"The new balance condition is the rearranged old one.","explanation":"Interchanged bridge balances when $Q/S=P/R$, algebraically identical to $P/Q=R/S$, so R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",13],[2026,"R","55/3/2",16],[2026,"R","55/3/3",15]]},{"id":"p0736","chapter":4,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The cylindrical soft iron core in a moving coil galvanometer only makes the magnetic field radial and does not affect the strength of the magnetic field.\nReason (R): In a moving coil galvanometer, the plane of the coil is always perpendicular to the magnetic field.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Soft iron also strengthens the field; the field is radial.","explanation":"A is false (core also increases field strength); R is false (coil plane is parallel to the radial field).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",14],[2026,"R","55/3/2",15],[2026,"R","55/3/3",16]]},{"id":"p0737","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Photoelectric current depends upon the intensity of the incident radiation.\nReason (R): Stopping potential is independent of the intensity of the incident radiation.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Both are standard photoelectric facts.","explanation":"Both true; stopping potential's independence does not explain the current's intensity dependence.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",15],[2026,"R","55/3/2",13],[2026,"R","55/3/3",14]]},{"id":"p0738","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Nuclear forces are always attractive.\nReason (R): The nuclear force between protons and neutrons in a nucleus is a weak force.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Nuclear force is strong and repulsive at very short range.","explanation":"A is false (repulsive below about 0.8 fm); R is false (it is the strong force).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/1",16],[2026,"R","55/3/2",14],[2026,"R","55/3/3",13]]},{"id":"p0739","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"An equiconvex lens of focal length 15 cm is cut into two equal plano-convex halves. The focal length of each half will be:","options":{"A":"30 cm","B":"20 cm","C":"15 cm","D":"$7\\cdot5$ cm"},"answer":"A","hint":"Cutting perpendicular to the axis keeps one curved surface.","explanation":"Equiconvex $\\mu=1.5$ gives $R=15$ cm; plano-convex half has $f=R/(\\mu-1)=30$ cm.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/2",3]]},{"id":"p0740","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole $\\vec{P}=2qa\\,\\hat{i}$ is placed in x-y plane, centred at the origin. Let $E_1$ and $E_2$ be the magnitudes of electric field at a point distant $r(\\gg a)$ from its centre, along its axis and on its equatorial plane, respectively. Then the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{E_1}{E_2}\\right)$ is:","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{4}$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"2","D":"4"},"answer":"C","hint":"Compare axial and equatorial dipole fields.","explanation":"$E_{axial}=\\frac{2kp}{r^3}$, $E_{eq}=\\frac{kp}{r^3}$, so the ratio is 2.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/2",4]]},{"id":"p0741","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A plano-convex lens, made of glass ($n=1\\cdot5$) has a curved surface whose radius is 40 cm. The image formed of an object is of the same size as that of the object. The distance of the object from the lens is:","options":{"A":"40 cm","B":"80 cm","C":"160 cm","D":"200 cm"},"answer":"C","hint":"Same size real image means $u=2f$.","explanation":"$f=R/(n-1)=80$ cm, so object distance is $2f=160$ cm.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/2",5]]},{"id":"p0742","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A metallic rod of 1 m length is rotated with a frequency of 40 rev/s, with one end hinged at the centre and the other end at the circumference of a circular metallic ring of radius 1 m, about an axis passing through the centre and perpendicular to the plane of the ring. A constant and uniform magnetic field of $0\\cdot5$ T parallel to the axis is present in the region. The value of emf induced between the centre and the ring is close to:","options":{"A":"20 V","B":"32 V","C":"40 V","D":"63 V"},"answer":"D","hint":"Rotating rod emf: $\\frac12B\\omega l^2$.","explanation":"$\\varepsilon=\\frac12\\times0.5\\times(2\\pi\\times40)\\times1^2\\approx63$ V.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/2",7]]},{"id":"p0743","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following p-n junction diodes is forward biased?","options":{"A":"Diode with $+5$ V at the p-side terminal and $+10$ V at the n-side terminal","B":"Diode with $-1\\cdot0$ V at the p-side terminal and $-1\\cdot5$ V at the n-side terminal","C":"Diode with $0$ V at the p-side terminal and $1$ V at the n-side terminal","D":"Diode with $-2$ V at the p-side terminal and $0$ V at the n-side terminal"},"answer":"B","hint":"Forward bias needs p-side at higher potential than n-side.","explanation":"Only in (B) is the p-side ($-1.0$ V) at higher potential than the n-side ($-1.5$ V).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0743.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/2",8]]},{"id":"p0744","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The velocity of the photoelectrons emitted from a surface depends upon the:","options":{"A":"Wavelength of incident radiation only","B":"Intensity of incident radiation only","C":"Work function of the surface only","D":"Both wavelength of the incident radiation and work function of the surface"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use Einstein's photoelectric equation.","explanation":"$\\frac12mv^2=\\frac{hc}{\\lambda}-\\phi_0$, so it depends on both.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/2",11]]},{"id":"p0745","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of monochromatic light travelling in air is incident on a glass slab and is partly reflected and partly refracted. Both the reflected and refracted lights will have:","options":{"A":"same wavelength","B":"same frequency","C":"same intensity","D":"same speed"},"answer":"B","hint":"Frequency is fixed by the source.","explanation":"Frequency stays unchanged on reflection and refraction; wavelength, speed and intensity differ.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/3",2]]},{"id":"p0746","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A square loop of side $L$ lies in the x-y plane in a magnetic field $\\vec{B}=B_0(2\\hat{i}+3\\hat{j}+4\\hat{k})$, where $B_0$ is a constant. The magnetic flux through the loop is:","options":{"A":"$\\sqrt{29}\\ B_0L^2$","B":"$4B_0L^2$","C":"$3B_0L^2$","D":"$2B_0L^2$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Flux is $\\vec{B}\\cdot\\vec{A}$ with area along $\\hat{k}$.","explanation":"$\\vec{A}=L^2\\hat{k}$, so $\\Phi=4B_0L^2$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/3",4]]},{"id":"p0747","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"An electric dipole with dipole moment $\\vec{P}=(2.54\\times10^{-28}\\ \\mathrm{C.m})(2.00\\hat{i}+3.00\\hat{j})$ is placed in an electric field $\\vec{E}=\\left(1000\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{N}}{\\mathrm{C}}\\right)\\hat{i}$. An external agent turns the dipole until its electric dipole moment is $\\vec{P}=(2.54\\times10^{-28}\\ \\mathrm{C.m})(-3.00\\hat{i}+2.00\\hat{j})$. The work done by the agent is :","options":{"A":"$2.54\\times10^{-25}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","B":"$1.27\\times10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","C":"$1.02\\times10^{-23}\\ \\mathrm{J}$","D":"$2.29\\times10^{-26}\\ \\mathrm{J}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Work by the agent equals the change in potential energy $U=-\\vec{p}\\cdot\\vec{E}$.","explanation":"$U_i=-(2.54\\times10^{-28})(2)(1000)=-5.08\\times10^{-25}\\ \\mathrm{J}$ and $U_f=+7.62\\times10^{-25}\\ \\mathrm{J}$, so $W=U_f-U_i=1.27\\times10^{-24}\\ \\mathrm{J}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/3",5]]},{"id":"p0748","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"If the given figure, $\\mathrm{V}_0$ is the potential barrier across a p-n junction in an unbiased condition. Which of the following statements is correct ?","options":{"A":"Curves 1 and 3 both correspond to forward biasing of p-n junction.","B":"Curve 3 corresponds to forward biasing and Curve 1 corresponds to reverse biasing.","C":"Curve 1 corresponds to forward biasing and Curve 3 corresponds to reverse biasing.","D":"Curves 1 and 3 both correspond to reverse biasing."},"answer":"B","hint":"Forward bias lowers the barrier, reverse bias raises it.","explanation":"Curve 3 has the smallest barrier (forward bias) and curve 1 the largest (reverse bias).","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0748.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/3",7]]},{"id":"p0749","chapter":9,"type":"mcq","text":"A ray of yellow light undergoes total internal reflection when it is incident at the interface of two media. This ray is successively replaced by the ray of blue, green and red lights. Which of the following statements is true if the angle of incidence is same in all cases ?","options":{"A":"Total internal reflection occurs for blue light only.","B":"Total internal reflection occurs for red light only.","C":"Total internal reflection occurs for both green and blue lights.","D":"None of them will undergo total internal reflection."},"answer":"C","hint":"Shorter wavelength means larger refractive index and smaller critical angle.","explanation":"Blue and green have higher refractive index than yellow, so smaller critical angle and still undergo TIR; red has a larger critical angle and may not.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/3",9]]},{"id":"p0750","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of the de Broglie wavelengths associated with the electron revolving in the first and third orbits in hydrogen atom is :","options":{"A":"$2$","B":"$\\frac{1}{2}$","C":"$3$","D":"$\\frac{1}{3}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Use $\\lambda=\\frac{2\\pi r}{n}$ with $r\\propto n^2$.","explanation":"$\\lambda\\propto n^2/n=n$, so $\\lambda_1/\\lambda_3=1/3$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/3/3",10]]},{"id":"p0751","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Two wires of same length and same area of cross-section but made of different material of resistivities $\\rho_1$ and $\\rho_2$ are connected in series. The equivalent resistivity of the combination is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{2}(\\rho_1+\\rho_2)$","B":"$\\rho_1+\\rho_2$","C":"$\\sqrt{\\rho_1\\rho_2}$","D":"$2(\\rho_1+\\rho_2)$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Treat the series combination as one wire of double length.","explanation":"$R=\\frac{\\rho_1 L}{A}+\\frac{\\rho_2 L}{A}=\\rho_{eq}\\frac{2L}{A}$, so $\\rho_{eq}=\\frac{\\rho_1+\\rho_2}{2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",1]]},{"id":"p0752","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Two particles of masses $m_1$ and $m_2$ having charges $q_1$ and $q_2$ respectively are projected with the same velocity in a region of uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ pointing vertically upward. If they describe circular paths as shown in the figure, one may conclude that :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{m_1}{m_2}>\\frac{q_1}{q_2}$","B":"$\\frac{m_1}{m_2}>\\frac{q_2}{q_1}$","C":"$\\frac{m_1}{m_2}<\\frac{q_1}{q_2}$","D":"$\\frac{m_1}{m_2}<\\frac{q_2}{q_1}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Radius of the circular path is $r=\\frac{mv}{qB}$.","explanation":"Path 1 has the smaller radius, so $\\frac{m_1}{q_1}<\\frac{m_2}{q_2}$, i.e. $\\frac{m_1}{m_2}<\\frac{q_1}{q_2}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0752.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",2],[2026,"R","55/4/2",4],[2026,"R","55/4/3",5]]},{"id":"p0753","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron moves around the nucleus in a circular orbit of radius r and makes n revolutions per second. The value of equivalent current in the orbit is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{e}{n}$","B":"$ne$","C":"$\\frac{ne}{r}$","D":"$\\frac{e}{nr}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Current is charge crossing a point per unit time.","explanation":"Charge $e$ passes a point $n$ times per second, so $I=ne$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",3]]},{"id":"p0754","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The dimensions of the rate of change of magnetic flux are :","options":{"A":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L\\,T^{-3}A}]$","B":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L^{2}T^{-3}A^{-1}}]$","C":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L^{2}T^{-2}A^{-1}}]$","D":"$[\\mathrm{M\\,L^{2}T^{-3}A^{-2}}]$"},"answer":"B","hint":"By Faraday's law, rate of change of flux has the dimensions of emf.","explanation":"$\\frac{d\\Phi}{dt}=\\varepsilon$ (volt) $=\\mathrm{M\\,L^{2}T^{-3}A^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",4]]},{"id":"p0755","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Two identical conductors 1 and 2 are placed on two frictionless conducting rails R and S in a uniform magnetic field directed vertically downward into the plane of the page. If conductor 1 is moved with a constant velocity in the direction shown in figure, the force on conductor 2 will be along :","options":{"A":"$-\\hat{i}$","B":"$-\\hat{j}$","C":"$\\hat{k}$","D":"$\\hat{j}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Lenz's law: the loop opposes the decrease of its area, so conductor 2 is pulled toward conductor 1.","explanation":"Conductor 1 moves left away from 2, so the loop area and flux decrease; the induced current pulls conductor 2 to the left, along $-\\hat{i}$.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0755.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",5]]},{"id":"p0756","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"An ac voltage is given as $v=14\\sin(314t)\\ \\mathrm{V}$. The average and the effective value of the voltage (in V) over a cycle are respectively :","options":{"A":"$14$ and $7$","B":"$10$ and $14$","C":"$0$ and $10$","D":"$10$ and $0$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Average over a full cycle is zero; rms $=V_0/\\sqrt{2}$.","explanation":"Average $=0$; rms $=14/\\sqrt{2}\\approx10$ V.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",6],[2026,"R","55/4/2",9],[2026,"R","55/4/3",3]]},{"id":"p0757","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The ratio of amplitude of electric field to the amplitude of the magnetic field associated with an electromagnetic wave propagating in glass ($n=1.5$) is :","options":{"A":"$3\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$","B":"$2\\times10^{8}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$","C":"$3.3\\times10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$","D":"$5\\times10^{-9}\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$E_0/B_0$ equals the wave speed in the medium.","explanation":"$E_0/B_0=v=c/n=3\\times10^8/1.5=2\\times10^8\\ \\mathrm{ms^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",7],[2026,"R","55/4/2",10],[2026,"R","55/4/3",2]]},{"id":"p0758","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"While studying photoelectric emission from a given surface, the wavelength of the incident radiation is changed from $600\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ to $400\\ \\mathrm{nm}$, keeping the intensity of radiation the same. Then :","options":{"A":"cut-off potential will decrease.","B":"cut-off potential will increase.","C":"saturation current will decrease.","D":"saturation current will increase."},"answer":"B","hint":"$eV_0=\\frac{hc}{\\lambda}-\\phi$.","explanation":"Shorter wavelength means higher photon energy, so the stopping potential increases.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",8]]},{"id":"p0759","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Radiation of wavelength $331\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ irradiates the following metals :\nMetal, Work Function (eV): Na 1.92; K 2.15; Ca 3.20; Mo 4.17\nWhich of the following statements is correct ?","options":{"A":"Only Na and K show photoelectric emission.","B":"Only Mo will not show photoelectric emission.","C":"All of the given metals show photoelectric emission.","D":"None of them show photoelectric emission."},"answer":"B","hint":"Compare the photon energy $1240/\\lambda$ (eV, nm) with each work function.","explanation":"$E=1240/331\\approx3.75$ eV, which exceeds the work functions of Na, K and Ca but not Mo (4.17 eV).","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",9],[2026,"R","55/4/2",11],[2026,"R","55/4/3",1]]},{"id":"p0760","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The kinetic energy of a charged particle is increased to four times of its initial value. The de Broglie wavelength associated with the particle will :","options":{"A":"increase by 100% of its initial value.","B":"increase by 50% of its initial value.","C":"decrease by 25% of its initial value.","D":"decrease by 50% of its initial value."},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\lambda=\\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2mK}}$.","explanation":"$\\lambda\\propto K^{-1/2}$, so $K\\to4K$ gives $\\lambda\\to\\lambda/2$, a 50% decrease.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",10],[2026,"R","55/4/2",6],[2026,"R","55/4/3",7]]},{"id":"p0761","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"Paschen series in spectrum of hydrogen atom lies in :","options":{"A":"infrared region","B":"ultraviolet region","C":"visible region","D":"partly in ultraviolet region and partly in visible region"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall the series names and their spectral regions.","explanation":"Paschen series (transitions to $n=3$) lies in the infrared.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",11],[2026,"R","55/4/2",5],[2026,"R","55/4/3",6]]},{"id":"p0762","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"In a reversed-biased p-n junction diode, the applied voltage mostly drops across :","options":{"A":"p-region only","B":"n-region only","C":"depletion region","D":"the diode"},"answer":"C","hint":"The depletion region has very high resistance.","explanation":"In reverse bias the depletion region is widest and resistive, so almost the entire voltage appears across it.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",12],[2026,"R","55/4/2",7],[2026,"R","55/4/3",4]]},{"id":"p0763","chapter":3,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): The work done, in taking a unit charge around a closed loop of an electric circuit involving cells and resistors in the loop, is zero.\nReason (R): The potential at a point depends on the location of the point in the loop. After completing one round, the charge comes back to the point of start.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Potential is a single-valued function of position (Kirchhoff's loop rule).","explanation":"Both true: potential is single-valued, so on returning to the start the net potential change and work are zero; R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",13],[2026,"R","55/4/2",14],[2026,"R","55/4/3",15]]},{"id":"p0764","chapter":5,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When a ferromagnetic substance is heated to high temperature it becomes paramagnetic in nature.\nReason (R): The disappearance of magnetisation of a ferromagnet is abrupt and not gradual.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the Curie temperature and how susceptibility changes with it.","explanation":"A is true (Curie temperature), but the loss of ferromagnetism is gradual, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",14],[2026,"R","55/4/2",15],[2026,"R","55/4/3",16]]},{"id":"p0765","chapter":9,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): When a convex lens made of glass is immersed in water, its converging power increases.\nReason (R): The focal length of a lens depends only on the radii of curvature of its two faces.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Lens maker's formula involves the relative refractive index of lens and medium.","explanation":"Power decreases in water since the relative index drops, so A is false; $f$ also depends on refractive indices, so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/1",15],[2026,"R","55/4/2",16],[2026,"R","55/4/3",13]]},{"id":"p0766","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"A wire is gradually stretched to make it 100% longer. Its resistance will increase by :","options":{"A":"100%","B":"200%","C":"300%","D":"400%"},"answer":"C","hint":"Volume is conserved, so $R\\propto l^2$.","explanation":"Length doubles and area halves, so $R\\to4R$, an increase of 300%.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/2",1]]},{"id":"p0767","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A closely wound long solenoid of self-inductance L is cut into two identical solenoids. The value of self-inductance of each small solenoid will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{L}{2}$","B":"$2L$","C":"$3L$","D":"$4L$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$L=\\mu_0 n^2 A l$ with $n$ unchanged.","explanation":"Same $n$ and $A$ but half the length, so $L'=L/2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/2",2]]},{"id":"p0768","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following characteristics of electrons determines the current in the conductor ?","options":{"A":"Drift velocity only","B":"Thermal velocity only","C":"Both drift velocity and thermal velocity","D":"Neither drift nor thermal velocity"},"answer":"A","hint":"Recall $I=neAv_d$.","explanation":"Thermal velocities average to zero; only the drift velocity gives net current, $I=neAv_d$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/2",3]]},{"id":"p0769","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A coil having 100 closely wound turns and area of cross-section $300\\ \\mathrm{mm}^2$ carries a current of $5\\ \\mathrm{mA}$. The magnitude of the magnetic moment associated with the coil is :","options":{"A":"$6\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","B":"$3\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","C":"$1.5\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$","D":"$5\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$m=NIA$.","explanation":"$m=100\\times5\\times10^{-3}\\times300\\times10^{-6}=1.5\\times10^{-4}\\ \\mathrm{Am^2}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/2",8]]},{"id":"p0770","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"In an experiment on photoelectric emission, the intensity of incident radiation is halved keeping its frequency constant. As a result :","options":{"A":"cut-off potential will increase.","B":"maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons will remain the same.","C":"photocurrent will increase.","D":"cut-off potential will decrease."},"answer":"B","hint":"Maximum kinetic energy depends on frequency, not intensity.","explanation":"$K_{max}=h\\nu-\\phi$ is unchanged; only the photocurrent decreases.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/2",12]]},{"id":"p0771","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"The resistivity $\\rho$ of a metal increases with rise in temperature because :","options":{"A":"only relaxation time $\\tau$ of electrons decreases with temperature.","B":"only number of electrons per unit volume $n$ increases appreciably.","C":"$\\tau$ decreases with temperature but $n$ does not change appreciably.","D":"$\\tau$ decreases with temperature and $n$ increases."},"answer":"C","hint":"$\\rho=\\frac{m}{ne^2\\tau}$.","explanation":"In metals $n$ is nearly constant while more frequent collisions reduce $\\tau$, so $\\rho$ rises.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/3",8]]},{"id":"p0772","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"Two coils are placed closed to each other. The mutual inductance of the pair of coils depends upon the :","options":{"A":"rate at which currents change in the two coils.","B":"relative position and orientation of the coils.","C":"currents in the two coils.","D":"value of voltage induced in one coil due to change in value of current in the other coil."},"answer":"B","hint":"Mutual inductance is a geometric property.","explanation":"It depends on geometry: size, number of turns, separation and orientation of the coils, not on currents.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/3",9]]},{"id":"p0773","chapter":3,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is true about mobility of charge carriers in a metal ?","options":{"A":"Mobility increases with increase in applied electric field.","B":"Mobility decreases with increase in temperature.","C":"Mobility is independent of the mass of the charge carrier.","D":"Mobility increases with increase in temperature"},"answer":"B","hint":"$\\mu=\\frac{e\\tau}{m}$.","explanation":"Higher temperature reduces $\\tau$, so mobility decreases; it also depends on mass.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/3",10]]},{"id":"p0774","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A galvanometer of resistance G is converted into a voltmeter of range $(0-\\mathrm{V})$ by connecting a resistor of $250\\ \\Omega$ with it. If resistor of $250\\ \\Omega$ is replaced by another resistor of $900\\ \\Omega$, its range becomes $(0-3\\ \\mathrm{V})$. The resistance G of the galvanometer is :","options":{"A":"$150\\ \\Omega$","B":"$125\\ \\Omega$","C":"$100\\ \\Omega$","D":"$75\\ \\Omega$"},"answer":"D","hint":"Range $V=I_g(G+R)$ with the same $I_g$.","explanation":"$I_g(G+250)=V$ and $I_g(G+900)=3V$ give $G+900=3G+750$, so $G=75\\ \\Omega$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/3",11]]},{"id":"p0775","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Photons of frequency $\\nu$ are incident on the surfaces of two metals A and B of threshold frequencies $\\frac{\\nu}{2}$ and $\\frac{\\nu}{3}$. The ratio of maximum kinetic energy of electrons emitted from metal A to that from metal B is :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{3}$","B":"$\\frac{3}{4}$","C":"$\\frac{2}{3}$","D":"$\\frac{3}{2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"$K_{max}=h(\\nu-\\nu_0)$.","explanation":"$K_A=h\\nu/2$ and $K_B=2h\\nu/3$, so the ratio is $3/4$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/4/3",12]]},{"id":"p0776","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two small identical metallic balls having charges $q$ and $-2q$ are kept far at a separation $r$. They are brought in contact and then separated at distance $\\frac{r}{2}$. Compared to the initial force F, they will now :","options":{"A":"attract with a force $\\frac{F}{2}$","B":"repel with a force $\\frac{F}{2}$","C":"repel with a force F","D":"attract with a force F"},"answer":"B","hint":"Charge is shared equally on contact.","explanation":"Each ball gets $-q/2$; force $=\\frac{k(q/2)^2}{(r/2)^2}=\\frac{kq^2}{r^2}$, repulsive, while $F=\\frac{2kq^2}{r^2}$, so the new force is $F/2$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",1]]},{"id":"p0777","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure represents the variation of the electric potential V at a point in a region of space as a function of its position along the x-axis. A charged particle will experience the maximum force at :","options":{"A":"P","B":"Q","C":"R","D":"S"},"answer":"D","hint":"Force is proportional to $|dV/dx|$.","explanation":"The graph is steepest at S, so $|E|=|dV/dx|$ and the force are largest there.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0777.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",2]]},{"id":"p0778","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"Four long straight thin wires are held vertically at the corners A, B, C and D of a square of side 'a', kept on a table and carry equal current 'I'. The wire at A carries current in upward direction whereas the current in the remaining wires flows in downward direction. The net magnetic field at the centre of the square will have the magnitude :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{\\pi a}$ and directed along OC","B":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{\\pi a\\sqrt{2}}$ and directed along OD","C":"$\\frac{\\mu_0 I\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi a}$ and directed along OB","D":"$\\frac{2\\mu_0 I}{\\pi a}$ and directed along OA"},"answer":"C","hint":"Fields of wires at opposite corners with equal currents cancel; those with opposite currents add.","explanation":"B and D cancel; A and C add, each $\\frac{\\mu_0 I}{2\\pi(a/\\sqrt2)}$, giving $\\frac{\\sqrt2\\mu_0 I}{\\pi a}$ along OB.","difficulty":3,"figure":"figures/p0778.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",3]]},{"id":"p0779","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic flux through a loop placed in a magnetic field can be changed by changing :","options":{"A":"area of the loop only","B":"the value of magnetic field only","C":"orientation of the loop in the magnetic field only","D":"any one or more of the factors given in (A), (B) and (C)"},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\Phi=BA\\cos\\theta$.","explanation":"Changing $B$, $A$ or $\\theta$ each changes the flux.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",4]]},{"id":"p0780","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"Which of the following statements is not true for electric energy in ac form compared to that in dc form ?","options":{"A":"Production of ac is economical.","B":"ac can be easily and efficiently converted from one voltage to the other.","C":"ac can be transmitted economically over long distances.","D":"ac is less dangerous."},"answer":"D","hint":"Pick the statement that is false about ac.","explanation":"ac is more dangerous than dc of the same rms voltage, so (D) is the untrue statement.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",5],[2026,"R","55/5/2",10],[2026,"R","55/5/3",6]]},{"id":"p0781","chapter":8,"type":"mcq","text":"The magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in glass ($n=1.5$) is given by $B_y=(2\\times10^{-7}\\ \\mathrm{T})\\sin(\\alpha x+1.5\\times10^{11}t)$ where x is in metres and t is in seconds. The value of $\\alpha$ is :","options":{"A":"$0.5\\times10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$","B":"$6.0\\times10^{2}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$","C":"$7.5\\times10^{2}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$","D":"$1.5\\times10^{3}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$\\alpha=k=\\omega/v$ with $v=c/n$.","explanation":"$v=2\\times10^8$ m/s, so $\\alpha=\\frac{1.5\\times10^{11}}{2\\times10^{8}}=7.5\\times10^{2}\\ \\mathrm{m^{-1}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",6],[2026,"R","55/5/2",11],[2026,"R","55/5/3",4]]},{"id":"p0782","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"Light of which of the following colours will have the maximum energy in a photon associated with it ?","options":{"A":"Red light","B":"Yellow light","C":"Green light","D":"Blue light"},"answer":"D","hint":"$E=h\\nu$.","explanation":"Blue has the highest frequency among the options, so its photon has the greatest energy.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",7]]},{"id":"p0783","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Nuclides with the same number of neutrons are called :","options":{"A":"Isobars","B":"Isotones","C":"Isotopes","D":"Isomers"},"answer":"B","hint":"Recall the definitions of isotopes, isobars and isotones.","explanation":"Same neutron number means isotones.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",8]]},{"id":"p0784","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"The radius of a nucleus of mass number 125 is","options":{"A":"$6.0\\ \\mathrm{fm}$","B":"$30\\ \\mathrm{fm}$","C":"$72\\ \\mathrm{fm}$","D":"$150\\ \\mathrm{fm}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$R=R_0A^{1/3}$ with $R_0=1.2$ fm.","explanation":"$R=1.2\\times125^{1/3}=1.2\\times5=6.0$ fm.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",9],[2026,"R","55/5/2",3],[2026,"R","55/5/3",12]]},{"id":"p0785","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of an electron in an orbit in hydrogen atom is $-3.4\\ \\mathrm{eV}$. Its angular momentum in the orbit will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{3h}{2\\pi}$","B":"$\\frac{2h}{\\pi}$","C":"$\\frac{h}{\\pi}$","D":"$\\frac{h}{2\\pi}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"$E_n=-13.6/n^2$ eV and $L=\\frac{nh}{2\\pi}$.","explanation":"$n^2=13.6/3.4=4$, so $n=2$ and $L=\\frac{2h}{2\\pi}=\\frac{h}{\\pi}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",10],[2026,"R","55/5/2",1],[2026,"R","55/5/3",7]]},{"id":"p0786","chapter":14,"type":"mcq","text":"A good diode checked by a multimeter should indicate :","options":{"A":"high resistance in reverse bias and a low resistance in forward bias","B":"high resistance in both forward bias and reverse bias","C":"low resistance in both reverse bias and forward bias","D":"high resistance in forward bias and low resistance in reverse bias"},"answer":"A","hint":"A diode conducts in one direction only.","explanation":"A good diode has low forward resistance and very high reverse resistance.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",11],[2026,"R","55/5/2",5],[2026,"R","55/5/3",2]]},{"id":"p0787","chapter":7,"type":"mcq","text":"The rms and the average value of an ac voltage $V=V_0\\sin\\omega t$ volt over a cycle respectively will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{V_0}{2},\\frac{V_0}{\\sqrt2}$","B":"$\\frac{V_0}{\\pi},\\frac{V_0}{2}$","C":"$\\frac{V_0}{\\sqrt2},0$","D":"$V_0,\\frac{V_0}{\\sqrt2}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall rms and full-cycle average of a sine wave.","explanation":"$V_{rms}=V_0/\\sqrt2$ and the average over a full cycle is zero.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",12],[2026,"R","55/5/2",4],[2026,"R","55/5/3",8]]},{"id":"p0788","chapter":6,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Induced emf produced in a coil will be more when the magnetic flux linked with the coil is more.\nReason (R): Induced emf produced is directly proportional to the magnetic flux.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"D","hint":"Faraday's law relates emf to the rate of change of flux.","explanation":"Induced emf $=-d\\Phi/dt$ depends on the rate of change, not on the flux itself, so both A and R are false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",13],[2026,"R","55/5/2",15],[2026,"R","55/5/3",16]]},{"id":"p0789","chapter":10,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): In Young's double-slit experiment, the fringe width for dark and bright fringes is the same.\nReason (R): Fringe width is given by $\\beta=\\frac{\\lambda D}{d}$, where symbols have their usual meanings.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"A","hint":"Fringe width is independent of fringe order.","explanation":"Both true: $\\beta=\\lambda D/d$ is the same for every dark and bright fringe, so R explains A.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",14],[2026,"R","55/5/2",16],[2026,"R","55/5/3",13]]},{"id":"p0790","chapter":13,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Energy is released when heavy nuclei undergo fission or light nuclei undergo fusion.\nReason (R): For heavy nuclei, binding energy per nucleon increases with increasing Z while for light nuclei, it decreases with increasing Z.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"C","hint":"Recall the binding energy per nucleon curve.","explanation":"A is true; R is reversed (BE per nucleon decreases for heavy nuclei and increases for light ones), so R is false.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",15],[2026,"R","55/5/2",13],[2026,"R","55/5/3",14]]},{"id":"p0791","chapter":11,"type":"ar","text":"Assertion (A): Photoelectric effect is a spontaneous phenomenon.\nReason (R): According to the wave picture of radiation, an electron would take hours/days to absorb sufficient energy to overcome the work function and come out from a metal surface.","options":{"A":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","B":"Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true, but Reason (R) is not the correct explanation of the Assertion (A).","C":"Assertion (A) is true, but Reason (R) is false.","D":"Assertion (A) is false and Reason (R) is also false."},"answer":"B","hint":"Wave picture predicts a delay, contradicting observation.","explanation":"Both statements are true, but R is a failure of the wave picture rather than an explanation of why emission is instantaneous.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/1",16],[2026,"R","55/5/2",14],[2026,"R","55/5/3",15]]},{"id":"p0792","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"The momentum of a photon associated with a microwave of wavelength $4.00\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ is :","options":{"A":"$1.66\\times10^{-32}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$","B":"$1.83\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$","C":"$2.05\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$","D":"$1.66\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"$p=\\frac{h}{\\lambda}$.","explanation":"$p=\\frac{6.63\\times10^{-34}}{4\\times10^{-2}}=1.66\\times10^{-32}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/2",2]]},{"id":"p0793","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"Two point charges $-Q$ and $Q$ are located at points $(d,0)$ and $(0,d)$ respectively, in x-y plane. The electric field $\\vec{E}$ at the origin will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{\\sqrt2\\,Q}{d^2}(\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$","B":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{\\sqrt2\\,Q}{d^2}(-\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$","C":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{Q}{d^2}(\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$","D":"$\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\frac{Q}{d^2}(-\\hat{i}-\\hat{j})$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Add the two field vectors at the origin, with direction toward $-Q$ and away from $+Q$.","explanation":"$-Q$ at $(d,0)$ attracts, giving $+\\hat{i}$; $+Q$ at $(0,d)$ repels, giving $-\\hat{j}$; each has magnitude $\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 d^2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/2",6]]},{"id":"p0794","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"An electron is moving with velocity $v_0\\hat{i}$. If a uniform electric field $\\vec{E}=E_0\\hat{j}$ is set up in the region, the electron will :","options":{"A":"describe a circular path","B":"describe a helical path","C":"describe a parabolic path","D":"continue moving without any deviation"},"answer":"C","hint":"Constant force perpendicular to the initial velocity.","explanation":"The constant force along $-\\hat{j}$ is perpendicular to $v_0\\hat{i}$, so the path is a parabola (projectile-like).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/2",7]]},{"id":"p0795","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A charged particle of mass m having kinetic energy K passes undeflected through a region with electric field $\\vec{E}$ and magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ acting perpendicular to each other. The mass m of the particle will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{KB^2}{2E^2}$","B":"$\\frac{2KB^2}{E^2}$","C":"$\\frac{2KE^2}{B^2}$","D":"$\\frac{KE^2}{2B^2}$"},"answer":"B","hint":"Undeflected means $qE=qvB$, so $v=E/B$.","explanation":"$K=\\frac12 mv^2=\\frac{mE^2}{2B^2}$, so $m=\\frac{2KB^2}{E^2}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/2",8]]},{"id":"p0796","chapter":6,"type":"mcq","text":"A rectangular loop of size $5\\ \\mathrm{cm}\\times8\\ \\mathrm{cm}$ is lying in x-y plane in a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}=(2.0\\ \\mathrm{T})\\hat{k}$. The total magnetic flux linked with the loop is :","options":{"A":"$80\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$","B":"$16\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$","C":"$8\\times10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$","D":"$8\\times10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{Wb}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$\\Phi=\\vec{B}\\cdot\\vec{A}$, with area in $\\mathrm{m^2}$.","explanation":"$\\Phi=2.0\\times(0.05\\times0.08)=8\\times10^{-3}$ Wb.","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/2",9]]},{"id":"p0797","chapter":12,"type":"mcq","text":"The energy of an electron revolving in the $n^{\\mathrm{th}}$ orbit in Bohr model of hydrogen atom is proportional to :","options":{"A":"$n^2$","B":"$n$","C":"$\\frac{1}{n}$","D":"$\\frac{1}{n^2}$"},"answer":"D","hint":"$E_n=-\\frac{13.6}{n^2}$ eV.","explanation":"Energy varies as $1/n^2$ (magnitude).","difficulty":1,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/2",12]]},{"id":"p0798","chapter":11,"type":"mcq","text":"A $500\\ \\mathrm{nm}$ photon is incident normally on a perfectly reflecting surface and is reflected. The value of momentum transferred to the surface is :","options":{"A":"$3.87\\times10^{-43}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$","B":"$2.5\\times10^{-30}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$","C":"$2.65\\times10^{-27}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$","D":"$1.33\\times10^{-27}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Reflection reverses the photon momentum, so the change is twice $h/\\lambda$.","explanation":"$\\Delta p=\\frac{2h}{\\lambda}=\\frac{2\\times6.63\\times10^{-34}}{5\\times10^{-7}}=2.65\\times10^{-27}\\ \\mathrm{kg\\,ms^{-1}}$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/3",1]]},{"id":"p0799","chapter":4,"type":"mcq","text":"A charged particle is moving in a uniform magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ with a constant speed v in a circular path of radius r. Which of the following graphs represents the variation of radius of the circle, with the magnitude of magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ ?","options":{"A":"Graph (A): r constant, a horizontal line","B":"Graph (B): r decreasing hyperbolically with B","C":"Graph (C): r increasing linearly with B","D":"Graph (D): r increasing non-linearly (convex) with B"},"answer":"B","hint":"$r=\\frac{mv}{qB}$.","explanation":"$r\\propto1/B$ at fixed $v$, which is a decreasing hyperbola.","difficulty":1,"figure":"figures/p0799.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/3",5]]},{"id":"p0800","chapter":1,"type":"mcq","text":"The figure shows three point charges kept at the vertices of triangle ABC. The net electric field, due to this system of charges, at the midpoint M of base BC will be :","options":{"A":"$\\frac{q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l^2}$ pointing along MA","B":"$\\frac{q}{\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l^2}$ pointing along AM","C":"$\\frac{q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l^2}$ pointing along AM","D":"Zero"},"answer":"C","hint":"Fields of the two equal charges at B and C cancel at M.","explanation":"$AM=\\sqrt{2l^2-l^2}=l$; the field of $2q$ is $\\frac{2q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l^2}=\\frac{q}{2\\pi\\varepsilon_0 l^2}$ directed away from A, i.e. along AM.","difficulty":2,"figure":"figures/p0800.png","sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/3",9]]},{"id":"p0801","chapter":13,"type":"mcq","text":"Consider the nuclear reaction $\\mathrm{X}\\rightarrow\\mathrm{Y}+\\mathrm{Z}$. Let $M_x$, $M_y$ and $M_z$ be the masses of the three nuclei X, Y and Z respectively. Then which of the following relations hold true ?","options":{"A":"$(M_x-M_z)<M_y$","B":"$(M_x-M_y)<M_z$","C":"$M_x>(M_y+M_z)$","D":"$M_x<(M_y+M_z)$"},"answer":"C","hint":"Energy is released only when reactant mass exceeds product mass.","explanation":"A spontaneous decay releases energy ($Q>0$), so $M_x>M_y+M_z$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/3",10]]},{"id":"p0802","chapter":2,"type":"mcq","text":"Two points R and S are equidistant from two charges $+Q$ and $-2Q$. The work done in moving a charge $-Q$ from point R to S is :","options":{"A":"Zero","B":"$-\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 d}$","C":"$\\frac{Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 d}$","D":"$\\frac{3Q}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 d}$"},"answer":"A","hint":"Work depends on the potential difference between the points.","explanation":"R and S are at equal distances from both charges, so $V_R=V_S$ and $W=0$.","difficulty":2,"sources":[[2026,"R","55/5/3",11]]}]};
